        <UserObject label="mailbox: [m1]" mermaidId="n:note0" mermaidBaseStyle="html=1;fillColor=light-dark(#fff5ad,#2a2a2a);strokeColor=light-dark(#aaaa33,#cccccc);fontColor=light-dark(#333333,#cccccc);fontFamily=Trebuchet MS,Verdana,Arial,sans-serif;fontSize=16;" mermaidBaseValue="mailbox: [m1]" id="m8">
          <mxCell parent="1" style="html=1;fillColor=light-dark(#fff5ad,#2a2a2a);strokeColor=light-dark(#aaaa33,#cccccc);fontColor=light-dark(#333333,#cccccc);fontFamily=Trebuchet MS,Verdana,Arial,sans-serif;fontSize=16;" vertex="1">
            <mxGeometry height="57.35" width="232.5" x="375.0" y="342.55" as="geometry" />
          </mxCell>
        </UserObject>
        <UserObject label="mailbox: [m2]" mermaidId="n:note1" mermaidBaseStyle="html=1;fillColor=light-dark(#fff5ad,#2a2a2a);strokeColor=light-dark(#aaaa33,#cccccc);fontColor=light-dark(#333333,#cccccc);fontFamily=Trebuchet MS,Verdana,Arial,sans-serif;fontSize=16;" mermaidBaseValue="mailbox: [m2]" id="m9">
          <mxCell parent="1" style="html=1;fillColor=light-dark(#fff5ad,#2a2a2a);strokeColor=light-dark(#aaaa33,#cccccc);fontColor=light-dark(#333333,#cccccc);fontFamily=Trebuchet MS,Verdana,Arial,sans-serif;fontSize=16;" vertex="1">
            <mxGeometry height="57.35" width="232.5" x="685.0" y="604.5" as="geometry" />
          </mxCell>
        </UserObject>
        <UserObject label="mailbox: [m3]" mermaidId="n:note2" mermaidBaseStyle="html=1;fillColor=light-dark(#fff5ad,#2a2a2a);strokeColor=light-dark(#aaaa33,#cccccc);fontColor=light-dark(#333333,#cccccc);fontFamily=Trebuchet MS,Verdana,Arial,sans-serif;fontSize=16;" mermaidBaseValue="mailbox: [m3]" id="m10">
          <mxCell parent="1" style="html=1;fillColor=light-dark(#fff5ad,#2a2a2a);strokeColor=light-dark(#aaaa33,#cccccc);fontColor=light-dark(#333333,#cccccc);fontFamily=Trebuchet MS,Verdana,Arial,sans-serif;fontSize=16;" vertex="1">
            <mxGeometry height="57.35" width="232.5" y="748.65" as="geometry" />
          </mxCell>
        </UserObject>
        <mxCell id="s0" value="&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:15px&quot;&gt;&lt;b&gt;INTENT&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:13px;line-height:1.35&quot;&gt;Implement each agent as an independent actor with its own mailbox, processing asynchronous messages one at a time and never sharing mutable state with peers.&lt;/div&gt;" style="rounded=1;whiteSpace=wrap;html=1;fillColor=#ffffff;strokeColor=#c5d0db;spacing=11;align=left;verticalAlign=top;" vertex="1" parent="1">
          <mxGeometry x="30" y="982.75" width="755" height="92" as="geometry" />
        </mxCell>
        <mxCell id="s1" value="&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:15px&quot;&gt;&lt;b&gt;CONTEXT&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:13px;line-height:1.35&quot;&gt;A team is building a multi-agent system where several agents must run at the same time, react to events as they arrive, and keep going even when one of them crashes. There is no single conversational chair driving turn order, and the agents may live in different processes or on different machines.&lt;/div&gt;" style="rounded=1;whiteSpace=wrap;html=1;fillColor=#ffffff;strokeColor=#c5d0db;spacing=11;align=left;verticalAlign=top;" vertex="1" parent="1">
          <mxGeometry x="815" y="982.75" width="755" height="111" as="geometry" />
        </mxCell>
        <mxCell id="s2" value="&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:15px&quot;&gt;&lt;b&gt;PROBLEM&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:13px;line-height:1.35&quot;&gt;If the agents are modelled as a request-and-response conversation, they are pinned to one thread of control and cannot easily run concurrently. If they share mutable state — a common dictionary, a shared queue, a global cache — concurrent reads and writes produce race conditions, and a crash in one agent corrupts state the others were relying on. Ad-hoc locking solves neither problem cleanly: it slows the system down and still leaves failure containment as an afterthought.&lt;/div&gt;" style="rounded=1;whiteSpace=wrap;html=1;fillColor=#ffffff;strokeColor=#c5d0db;spacing=11;align=left;verticalAlign=top;" vertex="1" parent="1">
          <mxGeometry x="30" y="1088.75" width="755" height="149" as="geometry" />
        </mxCell>
        <mxCell id="s3" value="&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:15px&quot;&gt;&lt;b&gt;FORCES&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:13px;line-height:1.35&quot;&gt;&amp;bull; Concurrency and asynchrony are natural to agent systems but hostile to shared-state programming.&lt;br&gt;&amp;bull; Actor-style isolation makes per-agent failure containment straightforward.&lt;br&gt;&amp;bull; Sequential conversations are easier to reason about than concurrent mailboxes — but they do not scale to many agents.&lt;br&gt;&amp;bull; A mailbox queue per agent costs memory and needs back-pressure rules.&lt;/div&gt;" style="rounded=1;whiteSpace=wrap;html=1;fillColor=#ffffff;strokeColor=#c5d0db;spacing=11;align=left;verticalAlign=top;" vertex="1" parent="1">
          <mxGeometry x="815" y="1107.75" width="755" height="149" as="geometry" />
        </mxCell>
        <mxCell id="s4" value="&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:15px&quot;&gt;&lt;b&gt;THEREFORE&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:13px;line-height:1.35&quot;&gt;Therefore: give each agent a mailbox, process its messages one at a time, and forbid shared mutable state across agents, so that concurrency, isolation, and partial-failure handling come from the actor discipline rather than ad-hoc locking.&lt;/div&gt;" style="rounded=1;whiteSpace=wrap;html=1;fillColor=#ffffff;strokeColor=#c5d0db;spacing=11;align=left;verticalAlign=top;" vertex="1" parent="1">
          <mxGeometry x="30" y="1251.75" width="755" height="111" as="geometry" />
        </mxCell>
        <mxCell id="s5" value="&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:15px&quot;&gt;&lt;b&gt;SOLUTION&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:13px;line-height:1.35&quot;&gt;Model each agent as an actor: a process or coroutine with its own mailbox, its own local state, and a message-handler that runs messages in receive order. Agents communicate only by sending messages — directly to a known agent id, or by publishing to a topic (see topic-based-routing). The runtime supervises actor lifecycles, restarts on crash, and routes messages across processes or machines. Pair with role-assignment when agents do have stable personas, and with supervisor when a coordinator is needed.&lt;/div&gt;" style="rounded=1;whiteSpace=wrap;html=1;fillColor=#ffffff;strokeColor=#c5d0db;spacing=11;align=left;verticalAlign=top;" vertex="1" parent="1">
          <mxGeometry x="815" y="1270.75" width="755" height="168" as="geometry" />
        </mxCell>
        <mxCell id="s6" value="&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:15px&quot;&gt;&lt;b&gt;STRUCTURE&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:13px;line-height:1.35&quot;&gt;&lt;font face=&quot;monospace&quot;&gt;Agent A (mailbox A) ↔ Agent B (mailbox B) ↔ Agent C (mailbox C). All communication via send(agent_id, message); no shared state.&lt;/font&gt;&lt;/div&gt;" style="rounded=1;whiteSpace=wrap;html=1;fillColor=#ffffff;strokeColor=#c5d0db;spacing=11;align=left;verticalAlign=top;" vertex="1" parent="1">
          <mxGeometry x="30" y="1376.75" width="755" height="111" as="geometry" />
        </mxCell>
        <mxCell id="s7" value="&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:15px&quot;&gt;&lt;b&gt;EXAMPLE SCENARIO&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:13px;line-height:1.35&quot;&gt;A monitoring system has a perception agent that ingests telemetry, an analysis agent that hypothesises causes, and a remediation agent that proposes actions. Each runs as its own actor with a mailbox. Telemetry arrives as messages to perception; perception emits analysis-request messages; analysis emits remediation-proposal messages. When the analysis actor crashes on a malformed input the supervisor restarts it with an empty mailbox; perception and remediation keep running. None of the three actors shares mutable state.&lt;/div&gt;" style="rounded=1;whiteSpace=wrap;html=1;fillColor=#ffffff;strokeColor=#c5d0db;spacing=11;align=left;verticalAlign=top;" vertex="1" parent="1">
          <mxGeometry x="815" y="1452.75" width="755" height="168" as="geometry" />
        </mxCell>
        <mxCell id="s8" value="&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:15px&quot;&gt;&lt;b&gt;CONSEQUENCES&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:13px;line-height:1.35&quot;&gt;&lt;b&gt;Benefits&lt;/b&gt;&lt;br&gt;&lt;br&gt;&amp;bull; Concurrent agents without ad-hoc locks or shared-state hazards.&lt;br&gt;&amp;bull; Per-actor crash recovery — one agent&#39;s failure does not corrupt peers.&lt;br&gt;&amp;bull; Distributable across processes and machines under the same programming model.&lt;br&gt;&amp;bull; Fits event-driven and pub/sub shapes naturally.&lt;br&gt;&lt;br&gt;&lt;b&gt;Liabilities&lt;/b&gt;&lt;br&gt;&lt;br&gt;&amp;bull; Message-driven debugging is harder to follow than a linear conversation.&lt;br&gt;&amp;bull; Each agent needs its own mailbox queue with back-pressure rules.&lt;br&gt;&amp;bull; Cross-agent transactions are not first-class — saga-style compensation is required.&lt;/div&gt;" style="rounded=1;whiteSpace=wrap;html=1;fillColor=#ffffff;strokeColor=#c5d0db;spacing=11;align=left;verticalAlign=top;" vertex="1" parent="1">
          <mxGeometry x="30" y="1501.75" width="755" height="282" as="geometry" />
        </mxCell>
        <mxCell id="s9" value="&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:15px&quot;&gt;&lt;b&gt;WHAT THIS PATTERN CONSTRAINS&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:13px;line-height:1.35&quot;&gt;Agents do not share mutable state and may not call each other synchronously; all cross-agent interaction must go through asynchronous mailbox messages.&lt;/div&gt;" style="rounded=1;whiteSpace=wrap;html=1;fillColor=#ffffff;strokeColor=#c5d0db;spacing=11;align=left;verticalAlign=top;" vertex="1" parent="1">
          <mxGeometry x="815" y="1634.75" width="755" height="92" as="geometry" />
        </mxCell>
        <mxCell id="s10" value="&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:15px&quot;&gt;&lt;b&gt;USE WHEN&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:13px;line-height:1.35&quot;&gt;&amp;bull; Agents must run concurrently with isolated state.&lt;br&gt;&amp;bull; The system must survive partial failures of individual agents.&lt;br&gt;&amp;bull; Communication is naturally event- or message-driven rather than turn-based dialogue.&lt;br&gt;&amp;bull; The agent population is expected to scale to dozens or more participants.&lt;/div&gt;" style="rounded=1;whiteSpace=wrap;html=1;fillColor=#ffffff;strokeColor=#c5d0db;spacing=11;align=left;verticalAlign=top;" vertex="1" parent="1">
          <mxGeometry x="30" y="1797.75" width="755" height="130" as="geometry" />
        </mxCell>
        <mxCell id="s11" value="&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:15px&quot;&gt;&lt;b&gt;DO NOT USE WHEN&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:13px;line-height:1.35&quot;&gt;&amp;bull; The interaction is a strict two-agent dialogue with a single thread of control (see autogen-conversational).&lt;br&gt;&amp;bull; The team has no actor-runtime experience and the application is small enough that a sequential loop suffices.&lt;br&gt;&amp;bull; Strong cross-agent transactions are required and saga-style compensation is not acceptable.&lt;/div&gt;" style="rounded=1;whiteSpace=wrap;html=1;fillColor=#ffffff;strokeColor=#c5d0db;spacing=11;align=left;verticalAlign=top;" vertex="1" parent="1">
          <mxGeometry x="815" y="1740.75" width="755" height="149" as="geometry" />
        </mxCell>
        <mxCell id="s12" value="&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:15px&quot;&gt;&lt;b&gt;COMPONENTS&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:13px;line-height:1.35&quot;&gt;&amp;bull; Actor agent — autonomous unit with private local state and a single-threaded message handler&lt;br&gt;&amp;bull; Mailbox queue — per-actor inbox that serialises incoming messages and applies back-pressure&lt;br&gt;&amp;bull; Actor runtime — supervises lifecycles, restarts crashed actors, and routes messages across processes&lt;br&gt;&amp;bull; Message envelope — typed payload carrying sender, target actor id, and the request&lt;/div&gt;" style="rounded=1;whiteSpace=wrap;html=1;fillColor=#ffffff;strokeColor=#c5d0db;spacing=11;align=left;verticalAlign=top;" vertex="1" parent="1">
          <mxGeometry x="30" y="1941.75" width="755" height="149" as="geometry" />
        </mxCell>
        <mxCell id="s13" value="&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:15px&quot;&gt;&lt;b&gt;TOOLS&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:13px;line-height:1.35&quot;&gt;&amp;bull; Actor framework — AutoGen Core, Akka, or Pekko providing mailbox, scheduling, and supervision primitives&lt;br&gt;&amp;bull; Message bus — transport that delivers envelopes across processes or machines&lt;br&gt;&amp;bull; LLM API — invoked inside an actor&#39;s handler to compute its next response&lt;/div&gt;" style="rounded=1;whiteSpace=wrap;html=1;fillColor=#ffffff;strokeColor=#c5d0db;spacing=11;align=left;verticalAlign=top;" vertex="1" parent="1">
          <mxGeometry x="815" y="1903.75" width="755" height="130" as="geometry" />
        </mxCell>
        <mxCell id="s14" value="&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:15px&quot;&gt;&lt;b&gt;FAILURE MODES&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:13px;line-height:1.35&quot;&gt;&amp;bull; Unbounded mailbox — a slow actor keeps receiving messages with no back-pressure rule, its queue grows without limit, and replies arrive long after they matter.&lt;br&gt;&amp;bull; Lost message trail — a bug spans three actors, and nobody can reconstruct which message caused which reaction because there is no single linear log.&lt;br&gt;&amp;bull; Half-applied cross-agent change — two actors must both update for a task to be correct, one crashes after the other has committed, and no compensation step undoes the first.&lt;br&gt;&amp;bull; Hidden shared state — a team slips a common cache back in for speed, and the race conditions the actor model was meant to remove come back.&lt;/div&gt;" style="rounded=1;whiteSpace=wrap;html=1;fillColor=#ffffff;strokeColor=#c5d0db;spacing=11;align=left;verticalAlign=top;" vertex="1" parent="1">
          <mxGeometry x="30" y="2104.75" width="755" height="206" as="geometry" />
        </mxCell>
        <mxCell id="s15" value="&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:15px&quot;&gt;&lt;b&gt;EVALUATION METRICS&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:13px;line-height:1.35&quot;&gt;&amp;bull; Mailbox depth distribution — how far behind each actor falls and where back-pressure kicks in&lt;br&gt;&amp;bull; Per-actor crash and restart rate — isolation effectiveness when one agent fails&lt;br&gt;&amp;bull; Cross-actor message latency — wall-clock time from send to handler entry on the target&lt;br&gt;&amp;bull; Throughput per actor — messages handled per second under realistic load&lt;br&gt;&amp;bull; Saga compensation rate — share of cross-actor workflows that needed rollback due to no transactions&lt;/div&gt;" style="rounded=1;whiteSpace=wrap;html=1;fillColor=#ffffff;strokeColor=#c5d0db;spacing=11;align=left;verticalAlign=top;" vertex="1" parent="1">
          <mxGeometry x="815" y="2047.75" width="755" height="168" as="geometry" />
        </mxCell>
        <mxCell id="s16" value="&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:15px&quot;&gt;&lt;b&gt;KNOWN USES&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:13px;line-height:1.35&quot;&gt;&amp;bull; &lt;b&gt;&lt;a href=&quot;https://microsoft.github.io/autogen/stable/user-guide/core-user-guide/index.html&quot;&gt;AutoGen Core&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/b&gt; &lt;i&gt;available&lt;/i&gt; — AutoGen Core documents explicitly that agents are developed using the Actor model.&lt;br&gt;&amp;bull; &lt;b&gt;&lt;a href=&quot;https://doc.akka.io/libraries/akka-core/current/typed/actors.html&quot;&gt;Akka / Pekko + LLM tool integrations&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/b&gt; &lt;i&gt;available&lt;/i&gt; — JVM actor runtimes used as the substrate for multi-agent LLM systems.&lt;br&gt;&amp;bull; &lt;b&gt;&lt;a href=&quot;https://marco-nissen.com/sparrot/&quot;&gt;Sparrot&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/b&gt; &lt;i&gt;available&lt;/i&gt; — Peer messaging is mailbox-based: each peer (atelier, others) has an inbox folder and the agent processes messages one at a time with no shared mutable state across peers.&lt;/div&gt;" style="rounded=1;whiteSpace=wrap;html=1;fillColor=#ffffff;strokeColor=#c5d0db;spacing=11;align=left;verticalAlign=top;" vertex="1" parent="1">
          <mxGeometry x="30" y="2324.75" width="755" height="187" as="geometry" />
        </mxCell>
        <mxCell id="s17" value="&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:15px&quot;&gt;&lt;b&gt;RELATED PATTERNS&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:13px;line-height:1.35&quot;&gt;&amp;bull; &lt;i&gt;complements&lt;/i&gt; &lt;b&gt;Topic-Based Routing&lt;/b&gt;&lt;br&gt;&amp;bull; &lt;i&gt;complements&lt;/i&gt; &lt;b&gt;Event-Driven Agent&lt;/b&gt;&lt;br&gt;&amp;bull; &lt;i&gt;specialises&lt;/i&gt; &lt;b&gt;Inter-Agent Communication&lt;/b&gt;&lt;br&gt;&amp;bull; &lt;i&gt;complements&lt;/i&gt; &lt;b&gt;Supervisor&lt;/b&gt;&lt;br&gt;&amp;bull; &lt;i&gt;alternative-to&lt;/i&gt; &lt;b&gt;Conversational Multi-Agent&lt;/b&gt;&lt;br&gt;&amp;bull; &lt;i&gt;complements&lt;/i&gt; &lt;b&gt;Cellular-Automata Agents&lt;/b&gt;&lt;br&gt;&amp;bull; &lt;i&gt;complements&lt;/i&gt; &lt;b&gt;Contract Net Protocol&lt;/b&gt;&lt;br&gt;&amp;bull; &lt;i&gt;complements&lt;/i&gt; &lt;b&gt;Performative Message&lt;/b&gt;&lt;br&gt;&amp;bull; &lt;i&gt;alternative-to&lt;/i&gt; &lt;b&gt;Stigmergic Coordination&lt;/b&gt;&lt;br&gt;&amp;bull; &lt;i&gt;complements&lt;/i&gt; &lt;b&gt;Async Tool Handle&lt;/b&gt; — The background worker behind the handle is naturally an actor with its own mailbox; the start tool sends it a message and the poll tool reads its outcome.&lt;/div&gt;" style="rounded=1;whiteSpace=wrap;html=1;fillColor=#ffffff;strokeColor=#c5d0db;spacing=11;align=left;verticalAlign=top;" vertex="1" parent="1">
