        <mxCell id="arch-caption" value="&lt;i&gt;Each candidate action is scored for affordance against the current scene; only feasible actions reach the controller.&lt;/i&gt;" style="text;html=1;align=center;verticalAlign=middle;strokeColor=none;fillColor=none;fontSize=12;fontColor=#52606d;" vertex="1" parent="1">
          <mxGeometry x="65" y="1232.05" width="1470" height="32" as="geometry" />
        </mxCell>
        <mxCell id="s0" value="&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:15px&quot;&gt;&lt;b&gt;INTENT&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:13px;line-height:1.35&quot;&gt;Have a vision-language model ground each candidate action against the current scene and predict its affordance, so that actions the environment cannot physically support are discarded before any reach the controller.&lt;/div&gt;" style="rounded=1;whiteSpace=wrap;html=1;fillColor=#ffffff;strokeColor=#c5d0db;spacing=11;align=left;verticalAlign=top;" vertex="1" parent="1">
          <mxGeometry x="30" y="1307.05" width="755" height="111" as="geometry" />
        </mxCell>
        <mxCell id="s1" value="&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:15px&quot;&gt;&lt;b&gt;CONTEXT&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:13px;line-height:1.35&quot;&gt;An embodied agent — a robot arm, a mobile manipulator, a GUI or device controller — plans an action from a high-level goal and a view of the scene. The planner reasons in language about what to do next, but language plans drift from what the body and the scene actually allow. A target may sit out of reach, an object may be too large for the gripper, a surface may not be graspable, or a referenced widget may not exist on screen. Executing such an action wastes a real interaction step and can leave the world in a worse state.&lt;/div&gt;" style="rounded=1;whiteSpace=wrap;html=1;fillColor=#ffffff;strokeColor=#c5d0db;spacing=11;align=left;verticalAlign=top;" vertex="1" parent="1">
          <mxGeometry x="815" y="1307.05" width="755" height="168" as="geometry" />
        </mxCell>
        <mxCell id="s2" value="&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:15px&quot;&gt;&lt;b&gt;PROBLEM&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:13px;line-height:1.35&quot;&gt;A language planner proposes actions from intent, not from what the scene affords, so it readily emits commands the agent cannot carry out: grasp an object beyond reach, place on a surface that does not exist, click a control that is off screen. Checking feasibility only after execution is slow and sometimes destructive, while encoding every physical pre-condition by hand is brittle across scenes and embodiments. The agent needs to know, from the current perception, whether each proposed action is even possible before it spends a real step on it.&lt;/div&gt;" style="rounded=1;whiteSpace=wrap;html=1;fillColor=#ffffff;strokeColor=#c5d0db;spacing=11;align=left;verticalAlign=top;" vertex="1" parent="1">
          <mxGeometry x="30" y="1432.05" width="755" height="168" as="geometry" />
        </mxCell>
        <mxCell id="s3" value="&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:15px&quot;&gt;&lt;b&gt;FORCES&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:13px;line-height:1.35&quot;&gt;&amp;bull; A language planner reasons about goals and steps but has weak grounding in the geometry, reachability, and physics of the specific scene in front of it.&lt;br&gt;&amp;bull; Validating an action by executing it costs a real interaction step and can be irreversible, so failed actions are expensive.&lt;br&gt;&amp;bull; Hand-coding pre-conditions per object and per embodiment does not transfer; a learned visual predictor generalises but adds latency and can mis-score.&lt;/div&gt;" style="rounded=1;whiteSpace=wrap;html=1;fillColor=#ffffff;strokeColor=#c5d0db;spacing=11;align=left;verticalAlign=top;" vertex="1" parent="1">
          <mxGeometry x="815" y="1489.05" width="755" height="168" as="geometry" />
        </mxCell>
        <mxCell id="s4" value="&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:15px&quot;&gt;&lt;b&gt;THEREFORE&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:13px;line-height:1.35&quot;&gt;Therefore: insert a perception-side gate where a vision-language model scores each candidate action&#39;s affordance against the current image, and only actions that pass the feasibility threshold are forwarded to the controller.&lt;/div&gt;" style="rounded=1;whiteSpace=wrap;html=1;fillColor=#ffffff;strokeColor=#c5d0db;spacing=11;align=left;verticalAlign=top;" vertex="1" parent="1">
          <mxGeometry x="30" y="1614.05" width="755" height="111" as="geometry" />
        </mxCell>
        <mxCell id="s5" value="&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:15px&quot;&gt;&lt;b&gt;SOLUTION&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:13px;line-height:1.35&quot;&gt;For each candidate action the planner proposes, render a grounded query to a vision-language model that pairs the action with the current scene image and asks whether the agent&#39;s body can perform it here — is the target reachable, graspable, clickable, large enough, on a valid surface. The model returns an affordance score or a yes/no feasibility judgement, optionally with the grounded location. Candidates that fall below the threshold are filtered out and the planner is asked to revise; candidates that pass are forwarded to the low-level controller for execution. The check is pure perception: it reads the scene as it is and predicts feasibility, without rolling out the action&#39;s downstream consequences or maintaining a simulator of the environment.&lt;/div&gt;" style="rounded=1;whiteSpace=wrap;html=1;fillColor=#ffffff;strokeColor=#c5d0db;spacing=11;align=left;verticalAlign=top;" vertex="1" parent="1">
          <mxGeometry x="815" y="1671.05" width="755" height="206" as="geometry" />
        </mxCell>
        <mxCell id="s6" value="&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:15px&quot;&gt;&lt;b&gt;STRUCTURE&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:13px;line-height:1.35&quot;&gt;&lt;font face=&quot;monospace&quot;&gt;Planner -&amp;gt; candidate actions -&amp;gt; VLM affordance scorer (action + scene image) -&amp;gt; filter below threshold -&amp;gt; feasible actions -&amp;gt; controller&lt;/font&gt;&lt;/div&gt;" style="rounded=1;whiteSpace=wrap;html=1;fillColor=#ffffff;strokeColor=#c5d0db;spacing=11;align=left;verticalAlign=top;" vertex="1" parent="1">
          <mxGeometry x="30" y="1739.05" width="755" height="111" as="geometry" />
        </mxCell>
        <mxCell id="s7" value="&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:15px&quot;&gt;&lt;b&gt;EXAMPLE SCENARIO&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:13px;line-height:1.35&quot;&gt;A tabletop robot is told to put the mug on the top shelf. Its planner proposes grasping the mug and placing it on the shelf. Before either runs, a vision-language model looks at the camera image and scores each action: the grasp is feasible because the mug is in reach and gripper-sized, but the place is scored infeasible because the top shelf is above the arm&#39;s reach. The place action is filtered out, and the planner is asked to revise toward a reachable shelf instead of wasting a move the arm could never complete.&lt;/div&gt;" style="rounded=1;whiteSpace=wrap;html=1;fillColor=#ffffff;strokeColor=#c5d0db;spacing=11;align=left;verticalAlign=top;" vertex="1" parent="1">
          <mxGeometry x="815" y="1891.05" width="755" height="168" as="geometry" />
        </mxCell>
        <mxCell id="s8" value="&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:15px&quot;&gt;&lt;b&gt;CONSEQUENCES&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:13px;line-height:1.35&quot;&gt;&lt;b&gt;Benefits&lt;/b&gt;&lt;br&gt;&lt;br&gt;&amp;bull; Physically impossible actions are caught from perception before they cost a real interaction step or damage the scene.&lt;br&gt;&amp;bull; The visual feasibility predictor transfers across objects and layouts better than hand-coded pre-conditions.&lt;br&gt;&amp;bull; The planner stays focused on intent while a separate grounded check enforces what the body and scene allow.&lt;br&gt;&lt;br&gt;&lt;b&gt;Liabilities&lt;/b&gt;&lt;br&gt;&lt;br&gt;&amp;bull; A miscalibrated scorer either blocks valid actions, stalling the agent, or passes infeasible ones, defeating the gate.&lt;br&gt;&amp;bull; Each candidate adds a vision-language inference, raising per-step latency and cost.&lt;br&gt;&amp;bull; The gate screens feasibility now, not safety or downstream effects; a feasible action can still be the wrong or harmful one.&lt;/div&gt;" style="rounded=1;whiteSpace=wrap;html=1;fillColor=#ffffff;strokeColor=#c5d0db;spacing=11;align=left;verticalAlign=top;" vertex="1" parent="1">
          <mxGeometry x="30" y="1864.05" width="755" height="358" as="geometry" />
        </mxCell>
        <mxCell id="s9" value="&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:15px&quot;&gt;&lt;b&gt;WHAT THIS PATTERN CONSTRAINS&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:13px;line-height:1.35&quot;&gt;Only actions the scene affords reach the controller; the agent may not execute a candidate action until the vision-language affordance check passes the feasibility threshold, and a candidate scored below threshold must be filtered or revised rather than attempted.&lt;/div&gt;" style="rounded=1;whiteSpace=wrap;html=1;fillColor=#ffffff;strokeColor=#c5d0db;spacing=11;align=left;verticalAlign=top;" vertex="1" parent="1">
          <mxGeometry x="815" y="2073.05" width="755" height="111" as="geometry" />
        </mxCell>
        <mxCell id="s10" value="&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:15px&quot;&gt;&lt;b&gt;USE WHEN&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:13px;line-height:1.35&quot;&gt;&amp;bull; An embodied or device agent plans actions in language but executes against a physical or on-screen scene with reach, geometry, or existence constraints.&lt;br&gt;&amp;bull; Failed actions are expensive or irreversible, so screening infeasible candidates from perception is worth a per-step inference.&lt;br&gt;&amp;bull; A vision-language model can ground the action against the scene and predict feasibility better than hand-coded pre-conditions.&lt;/div&gt;" style="rounded=1;whiteSpace=wrap;html=1;fillColor=#ffffff;strokeColor=#c5d0db;spacing=11;align=left;verticalAlign=top;" vertex="1" parent="1">
          <mxGeometry x="30" y="2236.05" width="755" height="168" as="geometry" />
        </mxCell>
        <mxCell id="s11" value="&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:15px&quot;&gt;&lt;b&gt;DO NOT USE WHEN&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:13px;line-height:1.35&quot;&gt;&amp;bull; The action space is fully reliable, so every proposed action is already known to be executable.&lt;br&gt;&amp;bull; The real concern is an action&#39;s downstream consequences or safety, where a side-effect simulator or a policy gate fits better than a feasibility screen.&lt;br&gt;&amp;bull; Per-candidate vision-language latency is unaffordable and a cheap geometric reachability check would suffice.&lt;/div&gt;" style="rounded=1;whiteSpace=wrap;html=1;fillColor=#ffffff;strokeColor=#c5d0db;spacing=11;align=left;verticalAlign=top;" vertex="1" parent="1">
          <mxGeometry x="815" y="2198.05" width="755" height="149" as="geometry" />
        </mxCell>
        <mxCell id="s12" value="&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:15px&quot;&gt;&lt;b&gt;COMPONENTS&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:13px;line-height:1.35&quot;&gt;&amp;bull; Planner — proposes candidate actions from the goal and the scene, without strong physical grounding&lt;br&gt;&amp;bull; VLM affordance scorer — pairs each candidate action with the current scene image and predicts a feasibility score or judgement&lt;br&gt;&amp;bull; Feasibility gate — filters out candidates below the threshold and forwards passing ones to the controller&lt;br&gt;&amp;bull; Revision loop — asks the planner for an alternative when a candidate is screened out&lt;br&gt;&amp;bull; Low-level controller — executes only the actions that pass the affordance check&lt;/div&gt;" style="rounded=1;whiteSpace=wrap;html=1;fillColor=#ffffff;strokeColor=#c5d0db;spacing=11;align=left;verticalAlign=top;" vertex="1" parent="1">
          <mxGeometry x="30" y="2418.05" width="755" height="206" as="geometry" />
        </mxCell>
        <mxCell id="s13" value="&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:15px&quot;&gt;&lt;b&gt;TOOLS&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:13px;line-height:1.35&quot;&gt;&amp;bull; Vision-language model — grounds a candidate action against the scene image and predicts its affordance&lt;br&gt;&amp;bull; Scene camera / screenshot capture — supplies the current visual observation the scorer reads&lt;br&gt;&amp;bull; Affordance / grounding head — returns the feasible location or score for the action on the perceived object&lt;/div&gt;" style="rounded=1;whiteSpace=wrap;html=1;fillColor=#ffffff;strokeColor=#c5d0db;spacing=11;align=left;verticalAlign=top;" vertex="1" parent="1">
          <mxGeometry x="815" y="2361.05" width="755" height="149" as="geometry" />
        </mxCell>
        <mxCell id="s14" value="&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:15px&quot;&gt;&lt;b&gt;FAILURE MODES&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:13px;line-height:1.35&quot;&gt;&amp;bull; Over-blocking — the scorer marks reachable, graspable actions as infeasible and the agent never makes progress.&lt;br&gt;&amp;bull; False affordance — the scorer passes an action the body cannot execute, so the failure surfaces only at the controller.&lt;br&gt;&amp;bull; Grounding mismatch — the predicted affordance location does not match the object the planner meant, gating the wrong action.&lt;br&gt;&amp;bull; Threshold drift — a fixed feasibility threshold tuned on one scene type silently mis-screens on a different one.&lt;/div&gt;" style="rounded=1;whiteSpace=wrap;html=1;fillColor=#ffffff;strokeColor=#c5d0db;spacing=11;align=left;verticalAlign=top;" vertex="1" parent="1">
          <mxGeometry x="30" y="2638.05" width="755" height="206" as="geometry" />
        </mxCell>
        <mxCell id="s15" value="&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:15px&quot;&gt;&lt;b&gt;EVALUATION METRICS&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:13px;line-height:1.35&quot;&gt;&amp;bull; Infeasible-action catch rate — fraction of physically impossible actions screened out before execution&lt;br&gt;&amp;bull; Over-block rate — fraction of feasible actions wrongly rejected by the gate&lt;br&gt;&amp;bull; Wasted-step reduction — drop in executed-then-failed actions versus a no-gate baseline&lt;br&gt;&amp;bull; Per-step gate latency — added inference time the affordance check costs each step&lt;/div&gt;" style="rounded=1;whiteSpace=wrap;html=1;fillColor=#ffffff;strokeColor=#c5d0db;spacing=11;align=left;verticalAlign=top;" vertex="1" parent="1">
          <mxGeometry x="815" y="2524.05" width="755" height="149" as="geometry" />
        </mxCell>
        <mxCell id="s16" value="&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:15px&quot;&gt;&lt;b&gt;KNOWN USES&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:13px;line-height:1.35&quot;&gt;&amp;bull; &lt;b&gt;&lt;a href=&quot;https://arxiv.org/abs/2404.11027&quot;&gt;LLM+A (Affordance Prompting)&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/b&gt; &lt;i&gt;pure-future&lt;/i&gt; — Prompts a vision-language model to predict affordance values for candidate robot manipulation actions against the current scene, screening physically infeasible actions before execution.&lt;br&gt;&amp;bull; &lt;b&gt;&lt;a href=&quot;https://arxiv.org/abs/2404.11000&quot;&gt;OVAL-Prompt&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/b&gt; &lt;i&gt;pure-future&lt;/i&gt; — Open-vocabulary affordance localisation that prompts a VLM to ground where on an object an action is feasible, supplying the grounded affordance the gate screens on.&lt;br&gt;&amp;bull; &lt;b&gt;&lt;a href=&quot;https://say-can.github.io/&quot;&gt;SayCan&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/b&gt; &lt;i&gt;available&lt;/i&gt; — A language model proposes candidate skills while learned affordance functions score each one for what the robot can actually execute in the current state, so only feasible actions are selected — the canonical affordance gate.&lt;br&gt;&amp;bull; &lt;b&gt;&lt;a href=&quot;https://voxposer.github.io/&quot;&gt;VoxPoser&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/b&gt; &lt;i&gt;available&lt;/i&gt; — An LLM and a VLM compose 3D value maps that ground affordances and constraints from the scene observation, steering trajectory synthesis toward what the environment physically affords.&lt;br&gt;&amp;bull; &lt;b&gt;&lt;a href=&quot;https://rekep-robot.github.io/&quot;&gt;ReKep&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/b&gt; &lt;i&gt;available&lt;/i&gt; — Large vision models and VLMs ground RGB-D observations into relational keypoint constraints that encode what manipulation a scene affords, screening actions against perceived feasibility before motion.&lt;/div&gt;" style="rounded=1;whiteSpace=wrap;html=1;fillColor=#ffffff;strokeColor=#c5d0db;spacing=11;align=left;verticalAlign=top;" vertex="1" parent="1">
          <mxGeometry x="30" y="2858.05" width="755" height="339" as="geometry" />
        </mxCell>
        <mxCell id="s17" value="&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:15px&quot;&gt;&lt;b&gt;RELATED PATTERNS&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:13px;line-height:1.35&quot;&gt;&amp;bull; &lt;i&gt;complements&lt;/i&gt; &lt;b&gt;Simulate Before Actuate&lt;/b&gt; — Both screen actions before execution, but simulate-before-actuate runs a deterministic side-effect simulation; the affordance gate is a perception-side feasibility check with no simulator.&lt;br&gt;&amp;bull; &lt;i&gt;alternative-to&lt;/i&gt; &lt;b&gt;World Model as Tool&lt;/b&gt; — World-model-as-tool rolls out an action&#39;s future consequences via a generative simulator; the affordance gate predicts present feasibility from the scene image without any rollout.&lt;br&gt;&amp;bull; &lt;i&gt;alternative-to&lt;/i&gt; &lt;b&gt;Mental-Model-In-The-Loop Simulator&lt;/b&gt; — The simulator scores multi-step strategy outcomes; the affordance gate scores whether a single candidate action is physically possible right now.&lt;br&gt;&amp;bull; &lt;i&gt;complements&lt;/i&gt; &lt;b&gt;Canonical-Entity Grounding&lt;/b&gt; — Both ground a proposed action against an authority before acting; canonical-entity-grounding resolves business identifiers, the affordance gate resolves physical feasibility against the scene.&lt;br&gt;&amp;bull; &lt;i&gt;alternative-to&lt;/i&gt; &lt;b&gt;Physical Hallucination&lt;/b&gt; — Affordance grounding is the corrective — discard actions the scene cannot physically support before the controller; physical hallucination is the failure when no such gate exists.&lt;br&gt;&amp;bull; &lt;i&gt;complements&lt;/i&gt; &lt;b&gt;Action-Admissibility Tiering&lt;/b&gt; — Screens whether the scene physically supports an action; this ladder asks whether the operation permits it, which is a separate question from whether the body can do it.&lt;br&gt;&amp;bull; &lt;i&gt;complements&lt;/i&gt; &lt;b&gt;Feasibility-Negotiated Intent Contract&lt;/b&gt; — That gate screens whether one candidate action is physically supported by the current scene; this negotiates a whole multi-expectation objective and returns a quantified shortfall rather than filtering a candidate.&lt;br&gt;&amp;bull; &lt;i&gt;complements&lt;/i&gt; &lt;b&gt;Physical-Evidence Progress Gate&lt;/b&gt; — The affordance gate asks before actuation whether the scene can support the action; this gate asks after actuation whether the effect occurred, and passing a feasibility check says nothing about whether the grasp closed on the object.&lt;/div&gt;" style="rounded=1;whiteSpace=wrap;html=1;fillColor=#ffffff;strokeColor=#c5d0db;spacing=11;align=left;verticalAlign=top;" vertex="1" parent="1">
          <mxGeometry x="815" y="2687.05" width="755" height="491" as="geometry" />
        </mxCell>
        <mxCell id="s18" value="&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:15px&quot;&gt;&lt;b&gt;REFERENCES&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:13px;line-height:1.35&quot;&gt;&amp;bull; &lt;a href=&quot;https://arxiv.org/abs/2404.11027&quot;&gt;Empowering Large Language Models on Robotic Manipulation with Affordance Prompting&lt;/a&gt; — 2024&lt;br&gt;&amp;bull; &lt;a href=&quot;https://arxiv.org/abs/2404.11000&quot;&gt;OVAL-Prompt: Open-Vocabulary Affordance Localization for Robot Manipulation through LLM Affordance-Grounding&lt;/a&gt; — 2024&lt;br&gt;&amp;bull; &lt;a href=&quot;https://arxiv.org/abs/2204.01691&quot;&gt;Do As I Can, Not As I Say: Grounding Language in Robotic Affordances&lt;/a&gt; — Michael Ahn, Anthony Brohan, Noah Brown, et al., 2022&lt;br&gt;&amp;bull; &lt;a href=&quot;https://arxiv.org/abs/2307.05973&quot;&gt;VoxPoser: Composable 3D Value Maps for Robotic Manipulation with Language Models&lt;/a&gt; — Wenlong Huang, Chen Wang, Ruohan Zhang, Yunzhu Li, Jiajun Wu, Li Fei-Fei, 2023&lt;br&gt;&amp;bull; &lt;a href=&quot;https://arxiv.org/abs/2409.01652&quot;&gt;ReKep: Spatio-Temporal Reasoning of Relational Keypoint Constraints for Robotic Manipulation&lt;/a&gt; — Wenlong Huang, Chen Wang, Yunzhu Li, Ruohan Zhang, Li Fei-Fei, 2024&lt;/div&gt;" style="rounded=1;whiteSpace=wrap;html=1;fillColor=#ffffff;strokeColor=#c5d0db;spacing=11;align=left;verticalAlign=top;" vertex="1" parent="1">
          <mxGeometry x="30" y="3211.05" width="755" height="244" as="geometry" />
        </mxCell>
      </root>
    </mxGraphModel>
  </diagram>
