              <Array as="points">
                <mxPoint x="494.35" y="438.65" />
              </Array>
            </mxGeometry>
          </mxCell>
        </UserObject>
        <UserObject label="" mermaidId="e:A-&gt;Esc#0" mermaidBaseStyle="curved=1;startArrow=none;endArrow=block;endSize=7;strokeColor=light-dark(#333333,#cccccc);exitX=1;exitY=0.76;entryX=0.5;entryY=0;" mermaidBaseValue="" id="m12">
          <mxCell edge="1" parent="1" source="m1" style="curved=1;startArrow=none;endArrow=block;endSize=7;strokeColor=light-dark(#333333,#cccccc);exitX=1;exitY=0.76;entryX=0.5;entryY=0;" target="m6">
            <mxGeometry relative="1" as="geometry">
              <Array as="points">
                <mxPoint x="886.5" y="438.65" />
              </Array>
            </mxGeometry>
          </mxCell>
        </UserObject>
        <mxCell id="s0" value="&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:15px&quot;&gt;&lt;b&gt;INTENT&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:13px;line-height:1.35&quot;&gt;Borrow the behavior-tree formalism: leaves are LLM calls or tools that return success/failure; a tree of selectors and sequences orchestrates control flow.&lt;/div&gt;" style="rounded=1;whiteSpace=wrap;html=1;fillColor=#ffffff;strokeColor=#c5d0db;spacing=11;align=left;verticalAlign=top;" vertex="1" parent="1">
          <mxGeometry x="30" y="606.1" width="755" height="92" as="geometry" />
        </mxCell>
        <mxCell id="s1" value="&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:15px&quot;&gt;&lt;b&gt;CONTEXT&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:13px;line-height:1.35&quot;&gt;An agent needs structured orchestration with clear fallback semantics — try one approach; if it fails, try the next; if all fail, escalate. Pure prompt chains and free-form ReAct loops have no first-class concept of &#39;failure of a sub-task triggers the sibling branch&#39;. Behavior trees, widely used in game design and robotics, are the canonical formalism for this shape.&lt;/div&gt;" style="rounded=1;whiteSpace=wrap;html=1;fillColor=#ffffff;strokeColor=#c5d0db;spacing=11;align=left;verticalAlign=top;" vertex="1" parent="1">
          <mxGeometry x="815" y="606.1" width="755" height="130" as="geometry" />
        </mxCell>
        <mxCell id="s2" value="&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:15px&quot;&gt;&lt;b&gt;PROBLEM&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:13px;line-height:1.35&quot;&gt;Free-form ReAct gives the LLM total freedom over control flow, which is brittle on tasks where the design intent is exactly a structured sequence of try-then-fallback. Prompt chains hard-code one path with no fallback. Custom orchestrators reinvent BT semantics ad-hoc per project. Without a first-class BT layer, the team rebuilds the same selector/sequence/decorator vocabulary every time, with diverging implementations and no shared mental model.&lt;/div&gt;" style="rounded=1;whiteSpace=wrap;html=1;fillColor=#ffffff;strokeColor=#c5d0db;spacing=11;align=left;verticalAlign=top;" vertex="1" parent="1">
          <mxGeometry x="30" y="712.1" width="755" height="149" as="geometry" />
        </mxCell>
        <mxCell id="s3" value="&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:15px&quot;&gt;&lt;b&gt;FORCES&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:13px;line-height:1.35&quot;&gt;&amp;bull; Selector (try children until one succeeds) and Sequence (run all children, fail on first failure) are the core BT primitives.&lt;br&gt;&amp;bull; Leaves can be LLM calls, tool invocations, or even sub-agents.&lt;br&gt;&amp;bull; Success/failure must propagate cleanly upward.&lt;br&gt;&amp;bull; Retries, timeouts, and decorators (e.g. invert, always-succeed) are standard BT extensions.&lt;/div&gt;" style="rounded=1;whiteSpace=wrap;html=1;fillColor=#ffffff;strokeColor=#c5d0db;spacing=11;align=left;verticalAlign=top;" vertex="1" parent="1">
          <mxGeometry x="815" y="750.1" width="755" height="149" as="geometry" />
        </mxCell>
        <mxCell id="s4" value="&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:15px&quot;&gt;&lt;b&gt;THEREFORE&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:13px;line-height:1.35&quot;&gt;Therefore: orchestrate the agent as a behavior tree whose leaves are LLM calls or tool invocations and whose interior nodes are selectors and sequences, so control flow is explicit, retry/fallback is first-class, and the structure is reviewable.&lt;/div&gt;" style="rounded=1;whiteSpace=wrap;html=1;fillColor=#ffffff;strokeColor=#c5d0db;spacing=11;align=left;verticalAlign=top;" vertex="1" parent="1">
          <mxGeometry x="30" y="875.1" width="755" height="111" as="geometry" />
        </mxCell>
        <mxCell id="s5" value="&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:15px&quot;&gt;&lt;b&gt;SOLUTION&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:13px;line-height:1.35&quot;&gt;Build the agent as a tree. Interior nodes are Selectors (try children left-to-right, succeed on first success) and Sequences (run children left-to-right, fail on first failure), plus standard decorators (Retry, Timeout, Invert). Leaves call the LLM or a tool and return SUCCESS or FAILURE. The tree executes top-down per tick; status propagates up. The tree itself is a versioned artifact reviewers can read. Distinct from [[plan-and-execute]] (one-shot plan + sequential run): a behavior tree is the structure of the controller across runs.&lt;/div&gt;" style="rounded=1;whiteSpace=wrap;html=1;fillColor=#ffffff;strokeColor=#c5d0db;spacing=11;align=left;verticalAlign=top;" vertex="1" parent="1">
          <mxGeometry x="815" y="913.1" width="755" height="168" as="geometry" />
        </mxCell>
        <mxCell id="s6" value="&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:15px&quot;&gt;&lt;b&gt;STRUCTURE&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:13px;line-height:1.35&quot;&gt;&lt;font face=&quot;monospace&quot;&gt;Tick --&amp;gt; Root Sequence --&amp;gt; Selector (leaf A --fail--&amp;gt; leaf B --fail--&amp;gt; escalate leaf) --SUCCESS--&amp;gt; next Sequence child (tool or LLM leaf, wrapped in Retry/Timeout) --&amp;gt; status propagates up --&amp;gt; Root SUCCESS or FAILURE&lt;/font&gt;&lt;/div&gt;" style="rounded=1;whiteSpace=wrap;html=1;fillColor=#ffffff;strokeColor=#c5d0db;spacing=11;align=left;verticalAlign=top;" vertex="1" parent="1">
          <mxGeometry x="30" y="1000.1" width="755" height="130" as="geometry" />
        </mxCell>
        <mxCell id="s7" value="&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:15px&quot;&gt;&lt;b&gt;EXAMPLE SCENARIO&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:13px;line-height:1.35&quot;&gt;A customer-onboarding agent&#39;s behavior tree at the top level is a Sequence: validate identity → set up account → send welcome. The validate-identity child is a Selector: try OAuth → fall back to email-verify → fall back to escalate-to-human. Each leaf is an LLM call or tool. If OAuth fails, the agent moves to email-verify without the LLM having to reason about fallback structure.&lt;/div&gt;" style="rounded=1;whiteSpace=wrap;html=1;fillColor=#ffffff;strokeColor=#c5d0db;spacing=11;align=left;verticalAlign=top;" vertex="1" parent="1">
          <mxGeometry x="815" y="1095.1" width="755" height="130" as="geometry" />
        </mxCell>
        <mxCell id="s8" value="&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:15px&quot;&gt;&lt;b&gt;CONSEQUENCES&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:13px;line-height:1.35&quot;&gt;&lt;b&gt;Benefits&lt;/b&gt;&lt;br&gt;&lt;br&gt;&amp;bull; Retry, fallback, and escalation are first-class structural choices.&lt;br&gt;&amp;bull; Reviewable as a tree, not a prompt.&lt;br&gt;&amp;bull; Composes naturally with sub-agents at leaves.&lt;br&gt;&lt;br&gt;&lt;b&gt;Liabilities&lt;/b&gt;&lt;br&gt;&lt;br&gt;&amp;bull; Tree authoring is up-front design work; ad-hoc cases want to bypass the tree.&lt;br&gt;&amp;bull; Mixing LLM leaves with deterministic ones complicates timing and cost reasoning.&lt;br&gt;&amp;bull; Authors may overuse decorators to paper over leaf flakiness.&lt;/div&gt;" style="rounded=1;whiteSpace=wrap;html=1;fillColor=#ffffff;strokeColor=#c5d0db;spacing=11;align=left;verticalAlign=top;" vertex="1" parent="1">
          <mxGeometry x="30" y="1144.1" width="755" height="263" as="geometry" />
        </mxCell>
        <mxCell id="s9" value="&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:15px&quot;&gt;&lt;b&gt;WHAT THIS PATTERN CONSTRAINS&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:13px;line-height:1.35&quot;&gt;Control flow with structured fallback must not be left entirely to LLM reasoning; selector/sequence/decorator semantics are explicit in the tree.&lt;/div&gt;" style="rounded=1;whiteSpace=wrap;html=1;fillColor=#ffffff;strokeColor=#c5d0db;spacing=11;align=left;verticalAlign=top;" vertex="1" parent="1">
          <mxGeometry x="815" y="1239.1" width="755" height="92" as="geometry" />
        </mxCell>
        <mxCell id="s10" value="&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:15px&quot;&gt;&lt;b&gt;USE WHEN&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:13px;line-height:1.35&quot;&gt;&amp;bull; Control flow has structured retries, fallbacks, and escalations.&lt;br&gt;&amp;bull; Reviewing the agent&#39;s structure is a first-class need.&lt;br&gt;&amp;bull; Multiple leaf implementations (LLM, tool, sub-agent) need uniform success/failure semantics.&lt;/div&gt;" style="rounded=1;whiteSpace=wrap;html=1;fillColor=#ffffff;strokeColor=#c5d0db;spacing=11;align=left;verticalAlign=top;" vertex="1" parent="1">
          <mxGeometry x="30" y="1421.1" width="755" height="111" as="geometry" />
        </mxCell>
        <mxCell id="s11" value="&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:15px&quot;&gt;&lt;b&gt;DO NOT USE WHEN&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:13px;line-height:1.35&quot;&gt;&amp;bull; The agent&#39;s control flow is genuinely open-ended exploration — ReAct fits better.&lt;br&gt;&amp;bull; Task is one-shot; tree authoring overhead is not justified.&lt;br&gt;&amp;bull; Team has no BT vocabulary and the tree becomes a custom DAG no one can read.&lt;/div&gt;" style="rounded=1;whiteSpace=wrap;html=1;fillColor=#ffffff;strokeColor=#c5d0db;spacing=11;align=left;verticalAlign=top;" vertex="1" parent="1">
          <mxGeometry x="815" y="1345.1" width="755" height="111" as="geometry" />
        </mxCell>
        <mxCell id="s12" value="&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:15px&quot;&gt;&lt;b&gt;COMPONENTS&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:13px;line-height:1.35&quot;&gt;&amp;bull; Selector node — tries children left-to-right, succeeds on first success&lt;br&gt;&amp;bull; Sequence node — runs children left-to-right, fails on first failure&lt;br&gt;&amp;bull; Decorator nodes — Retry, Timeout, Invert, AlwaysSucceed&lt;br&gt;&amp;bull; Leaf nodes — LLM call, tool call, or sub-agent&lt;/div&gt;" style="rounded=1;whiteSpace=wrap;html=1;fillColor=#ffffff;strokeColor=#c5d0db;spacing=11;align=left;verticalAlign=top;" vertex="1" parent="1">
          <mxGeometry x="30" y="1546.1" width="755" height="130" as="geometry" />
        </mxCell>
        <mxCell id="s13" value="&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:15px&quot;&gt;&lt;b&gt;TOOLS&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:13px;line-height:1.35&quot;&gt;&amp;bull; py_trees or equivalent BT runtime — schedules ticks&lt;br&gt;&amp;bull; Trace logger — records per-node status per tick&lt;br&gt;&amp;bull; Tree editor — visual or text authoring of tree structure&lt;/div&gt;" style="rounded=1;whiteSpace=wrap;html=1;fillColor=#ffffff;strokeColor=#c5d0db;spacing=11;align=left;verticalAlign=top;" vertex="1" parent="1">
          <mxGeometry x="815" y="1470.1" width="755" height="111" as="geometry" />
        </mxCell>
        <mxCell id="s14" value="&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:15px&quot;&gt;&lt;b&gt;FAILURE MODES&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:13px;line-height:1.35&quot;&gt;&amp;bull; Decorator padding — authors wrap a flaky leaf in Retry and Timeout until the tree passes, and the real leaf defect stays hidden.&lt;br&gt;&amp;bull; Tree bypass — an ad-hoc case is handled outside the tree, so the reviewed control flow no longer matches what the agent does.&lt;br&gt;&amp;bull; Silent fallback chain — a Selector keeps falling through to its last child, such as the escalation leaf, and nobody notices the earlier leaves stopped succeeding.&lt;br&gt;&amp;bull; Cost blind spot — LLM leaves sit next to cheap deterministic leaves, and one tick of the tree costs far more time and money than the authors expected.&lt;/div&gt;" style="rounded=1;whiteSpace=wrap;html=1;fillColor=#ffffff;strokeColor=#c5d0db;spacing=11;align=left;verticalAlign=top;" vertex="1" parent="1">
          <mxGeometry x="30" y="1690.1" width="755" height="206" as="geometry" />
        </mxCell>
        <mxCell id="s15" value="&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:15px&quot;&gt;&lt;b&gt;EVALUATION METRICS&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:13px;line-height:1.35&quot;&gt;&amp;bull; Per-node success rate — diagnostic on which leaves dominate failure&lt;br&gt;&amp;bull; Fallback-firing rate — share of selectors that fall through to later children&lt;br&gt;&amp;bull; Tree-shape changes per release — proxy for instability in design&lt;/div&gt;" style="rounded=1;whiteSpace=wrap;html=1;fillColor=#ffffff;strokeColor=#c5d0db;spacing=11;align=left;verticalAlign=top;" vertex="1" parent="1">
          <mxGeometry x="815" y="1595.1" width="755" height="111" as="geometry" />
        </mxCell>
        <mxCell id="s16" value="&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:15px&quot;&gt;&lt;b&gt;KNOWN USES&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:13px;line-height:1.35&quot;&gt;&amp;bull; &lt;b&gt;&lt;a href=&quot;https://livebook.manning.com/book/ai-agents-in-action/chapter-6&quot;&gt;AI Agents in Action (Lanham) — Agentic Behavior Trees&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/b&gt; &lt;i&gt;available&lt;/i&gt;&lt;br&gt;&amp;bull; &lt;b&gt;&lt;a href=&quot;https://arxiv.org/abs/2404.07439&quot;&gt;Game/robotics BT libraries adapted to LLM agents (py_trees + LLM leaves)&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/b&gt; &lt;i&gt;available&lt;/i&gt;&lt;/div&gt;" style="rounded=1;whiteSpace=wrap;html=1;fillColor=#ffffff;strokeColor=#c5d0db;spacing=11;align=left;verticalAlign=top;" vertex="1" parent="1">
          <mxGeometry x="30" y="1910.1" width="755" height="130" as="geometry" />
        </mxCell>
        <mxCell id="s17" value="&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:15px&quot;&gt;&lt;b&gt;RELATED PATTERNS&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:13px;line-height:1.35&quot;&gt;&amp;bull; &lt;i&gt;alternative-to&lt;/i&gt; &lt;b&gt;Plan-and-Execute&lt;/b&gt;&lt;br&gt;&amp;bull; &lt;i&gt;alternative-to&lt;/i&gt; &lt;b&gt;ReAct&lt;/b&gt;&lt;br&gt;&amp;bull; &lt;i&gt;complements&lt;/i&gt; &lt;b&gt;Behavior Tree Back Chaining&lt;/b&gt; — Back-chaining is one way to construct an ABT.&lt;br&gt;&amp;bull; &lt;i&gt;uses&lt;/i&gt; &lt;b&gt;Fallback Chain&lt;/b&gt;&lt;br&gt;&amp;bull; &lt;i&gt;composes-with&lt;/i&gt; &lt;b&gt;Agent-as-Tool Embedding&lt;/b&gt;&lt;br&gt;&amp;bull; &lt;i&gt;complements&lt;/i&gt; &lt;b&gt;Circuit Breaker&lt;/b&gt;&lt;br&gt;&amp;bull; &lt;i&gt;complements&lt;/i&gt; &lt;b&gt;Degenerate-Output Detection&lt;/b&gt;&lt;br&gt;&amp;bull; &lt;i&gt;composes-with&lt;/i&gt; &lt;b&gt;Semantic Decision Node&lt;/b&gt; — A behavior tree supplies the explicit selectors and sequences; semantic decision nodes supply the condition leaves whose truth needs judgement, returning a probability that the tree thresholds into success or failure.&lt;/div&gt;" style="rounded=1;whiteSpace=wrap;html=1;fillColor=#ffffff;strokeColor=#c5d0db;spacing=11;align=left;verticalAlign=top;" vertex="1" parent="1">
          <mxGeometry x="815" y="1720.1" width="755" height="244" as="geometry" />
        </mxCell>
        <mxCell id="s18" value="&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:15px&quot;&gt;&lt;b&gt;REFERENCES&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:13px;line-height:1.35&quot;&gt;&amp;bull; &lt;a href=&quot;https://www.manning.com/books/ai-agents-in-action&quot;&gt;AI Agents in Action&lt;/a&gt; — Micheal Lanham, 2025&lt;br&gt;&amp;bull; &lt;a href=&quot;https://medium.com/@Micheal-Lanham/introduction-to-autonomous-assistants-with-behaviour-trees-b79ec24fc346&quot;&gt;Introduction to Autonomous Assistants with Behaviour Trees&lt;/a&gt; — Micheal Lanham&lt;/div&gt;" style="rounded=1;whiteSpace=wrap;html=1;fillColor=#ffffff;strokeColor=#c5d0db;spacing=11;align=left;verticalAlign=top;" vertex="1" parent="1">
          <mxGeometry x="30" y="2054.1" width="755" height="111" as="geometry" />
        </mxCell>
      </root>
    </mxGraphModel>
  </diagram>
  <diagram id="agentic-context-engineering-playbook" name="Agentic Context Engineering Playbook">
