        <mxCell id="arch-caption" value="&lt;i&gt;The router activates the minimal disjoint subset of subsystems per query; the effective architecture emerges query by query.&lt;/i&gt;" style="text;html=1;align=center;verticalAlign=middle;strokeColor=none;fillColor=none;fontSize=12;fontColor=#52606d;" vertex="1" parent="1">
          <mxGeometry x="65" y="1645.9" width="1470" height="32" as="geometry" />
        </mxCell>
        <mxCell id="s0" value="&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:15px&quot;&gt;&lt;b&gt;INTENT&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:13px;line-height:1.35&quot;&gt;Treat a deployed agent as a space of behaviors over a pool of subsystems and let a router pick, per query, the minimal disjoint subset that query needs, so the effective architecture emerges per query.&lt;/div&gt;" style="rounded=1;whiteSpace=wrap;html=1;fillColor=#ffffff;strokeColor=#c5d0db;spacing=11;align=left;verticalAlign=top;" vertex="1" parent="1">
          <mxGeometry x="30" y="1720.9" width="755" height="111" as="geometry" />
        </mxCell>
        <mxCell id="s1" value="&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:15px&quot;&gt;&lt;b&gt;CONTEXT&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:13px;line-height:1.35&quot;&gt;A team assembles an agent from several heavyweight subsystems: a plain tool loop, a retrieval-augmented pipeline, a knowledge graph, a multi-agent sub-team, a cache, and tiered memory. Most production traffic is mixed: a greeting needs none of them, a lookup needs only retrieval, an audit query needs the graph and memory, and a planning request needs the sub-team. Wiring every query through the full stack is the default, and it is the source of latency, cost, and brittleness.&lt;/div&gt;" style="rounded=1;whiteSpace=wrap;html=1;fillColor=#ffffff;strokeColor=#c5d0db;spacing=11;align=left;verticalAlign=top;" vertex="1" parent="1">
          <mxGeometry x="815" y="1720.9" width="755" height="149" as="geometry" />
        </mxCell>
        <mxCell id="s2" value="&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:15px&quot;&gt;&lt;b&gt;PROBLEM&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:13px;line-height:1.35&quot;&gt;A single fixed pipeline forces every request through every subsystem it bundles, which pays for retrieval, graph traversal, and multi-agent fan-out even when a query needs none of them. Each extra layer is a feature and a failure surface at once, so a maximal architecture maximises both spend and the ways a simple query can break. The system needs a way to spend only the subsystems a given query actually requires.&lt;/div&gt;" style="rounded=1;whiteSpace=wrap;html=1;fillColor=#ffffff;strokeColor=#c5d0db;spacing=11;align=left;verticalAlign=top;" vertex="1" parent="1">
          <mxGeometry x="30" y="1845.9" width="755" height="149" as="geometry" />
        </mxCell>
        <mxCell id="s3" value="&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:15px&quot;&gt;&lt;b&gt;FORCES&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:13px;line-height:1.35&quot;&gt;&amp;bull; A richer subsystem pool answers more query types, but invoking the whole pool per query multiplies latency, cost, and the number of components that can fail.&lt;br&gt;&amp;bull; The most-tuned subsystem is rarely the most-invoked one, so optimisation effort and routing decisions are usually misaligned.&lt;br&gt;&amp;bull; A deterministic per-query selector is inspectable and testable, but it must be kept correct as subsystems are added or retired or it routes to the wrong subset.&lt;/div&gt;" style="rounded=1;whiteSpace=wrap;html=1;fillColor=#ffffff;strokeColor=#c5d0db;spacing=11;align=left;verticalAlign=top;" vertex="1" parent="1">
          <mxGeometry x="815" y="1883.9" width="755" height="168" as="geometry" />
        </mxCell>
        <mxCell id="s4" value="&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:15px&quot;&gt;&lt;b&gt;THEREFORE&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:13px;line-height:1.35&quot;&gt;Therefore: design a deterministic router that maps each query to the minimal disjoint subset of subsystems it needs, treat the deliverable as that space of behaviors rather than one wired pipeline, and add a subsystem only once a query class actually routes through it.&lt;/div&gt;" style="rounded=1;whiteSpace=wrap;html=1;fillColor=#ffffff;strokeColor=#c5d0db;spacing=11;align=left;verticalAlign=top;" vertex="1" parent="1">
          <mxGeometry x="30" y="2008.9" width="755" height="111" as="geometry" />
        </mxCell>
        <mxCell id="s5" value="&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:15px&quot;&gt;&lt;b&gt;SOLUTION&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:13px;line-height:1.35&quot;&gt;Model the agent as a pool of independent subsystems and a router rather than a wired graph of layers. For each incoming query the router classifies intent and required capabilities, then activates the minimal subset of subsystems that query needs and bypasses the rest; a trivial query may activate the bare loop alone, a complex one a retrieval-plus-graph-plus-memory subset. The subsets are disjoint and non-nested across query classes, so the architecture that actually runs is a property that emerges query by query instead of a shape chosen once at build time. Design effort concentrates on the routing heuristics and their evaluation, and a new subsystem is built only when a query class is shown to route through it, so the pool grows on demand rather than upfront.&lt;/div&gt;" style="rounded=1;whiteSpace=wrap;html=1;fillColor=#ffffff;strokeColor=#c5d0db;spacing=11;align=left;verticalAlign=top;" vertex="1" parent="1">
          <mxGeometry x="815" y="2065.9" width="755" height="206" as="geometry" />
        </mxCell>
        <mxCell id="s6" value="&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:15px&quot;&gt;&lt;b&gt;STRUCTURE&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:13px;line-height:1.35&quot;&gt;&lt;font face=&quot;monospace&quot;&gt;Query --&amp;gt; Router (classify intent + capabilities) --&amp;gt; activate minimal disjoint subset {loop | RAG | knowledge-graph | multi-agent | cache | memory} --&amp;gt; compose active subsystems --&amp;gt; Response&lt;/font&gt;&lt;/div&gt;" style="rounded=1;whiteSpace=wrap;html=1;fillColor=#ffffff;strokeColor=#c5d0db;spacing=11;align=left;verticalAlign=top;" vertex="1" parent="1">
          <mxGeometry x="30" y="2133.9" width="755" height="111" as="geometry" />
        </mxCell>
        <mxCell id="s7" value="&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:15px&quot;&gt;&lt;b&gt;EXAMPLE SCENARIO&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:13px;line-height:1.35&quot;&gt;An enterprise support agent fronts a tool loop, a vector retriever, a knowledge graph of entitlements, and a planning sub-team. A user typing &#39;thanks&#39; activates the bare loop and nothing else. &#39;What&#39;s my plan limit?&#39; activates retrieval alone. &#39;Was my account ever over its quota, and what changed?&#39; activates the graph plus memory. &#39;Draft a migration plan for my workspace&#39; activates the planning sub-team. No query ever runs the full stack, and a new subsystem is added only when a recurring query class needs one.&lt;/div&gt;" style="rounded=1;whiteSpace=wrap;html=1;fillColor=#ffffff;strokeColor=#c5d0db;spacing=11;align=left;verticalAlign=top;" vertex="1" parent="1">
          <mxGeometry x="815" y="2285.9" width="755" height="168" as="geometry" />
        </mxCell>
        <mxCell id="s8" value="&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:15px&quot;&gt;&lt;b&gt;CONSEQUENCES&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:13px;line-height:1.35&quot;&gt;&lt;b&gt;Benefits&lt;/b&gt;&lt;br&gt;&lt;br&gt;&amp;bull; A simple query pays only for the subsystems it activates, so common cheap traffic stops subsidising the cost and latency of rarely-needed heavy layers.&lt;br&gt;&amp;bull; Each query class exercises a small, named subset, which shrinks the failure surface and makes a failed query trace back to one active subsystem.&lt;br&gt;&amp;bull; The subsystem pool grows only when a query class demands it, so unused layers are never built or maintained.&lt;br&gt;&lt;br&gt;&lt;b&gt;Liabilities&lt;/b&gt;&lt;br&gt;&lt;br&gt;&amp;bull; Routing quality becomes the dominant determinant of system quality, so a mis-tuned router degrades every query class at once.&lt;br&gt;&amp;bull; Maintaining correct disjoint subsets as subsystems are added or retired is ongoing work, and a stale mapping silently routes to the wrong subset.&lt;br&gt;&amp;bull; A heterogeneous pool of subsystems is harder to observe and reason about end to end than a single fixed pipeline.&lt;/div&gt;" style="rounded=1;whiteSpace=wrap;html=1;fillColor=#ffffff;strokeColor=#c5d0db;spacing=11;align=left;verticalAlign=top;" vertex="1" parent="1">
          <mxGeometry x="30" y="2258.9" width="755" height="377" as="geometry" />
        </mxCell>
        <mxCell id="s9" value="&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:15px&quot;&gt;&lt;b&gt;WHAT THIS PATTERN CONSTRAINS&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:13px;line-height:1.35&quot;&gt;A subsystem runs for a query only when the router selects it; no subsystem may self-activate or be invoked outside the router&#39;s per-query decision, and a layer must not be added to the pool before a query class routes through it.&lt;/div&gt;" style="rounded=1;whiteSpace=wrap;html=1;fillColor=#ffffff;strokeColor=#c5d0db;spacing=11;align=left;verticalAlign=top;" vertex="1" parent="1">
          <mxGeometry x="815" y="2467.9" width="755" height="111" as="geometry" />
        </mxCell>
        <mxCell id="s10" value="&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:15px&quot;&gt;&lt;b&gt;USE WHEN&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:13px;line-height:1.35&quot;&gt;&amp;bull; Production traffic is mixed, so different query classes need genuinely different and largely disjoint capabilities.&lt;br&gt;&amp;bull; Heavyweight subsystems (retrieval, knowledge graph, multi-agent) are expensive or slow enough that running them on every query is wasteful.&lt;br&gt;&amp;bull; Routing decisions can be made deterministic, inspectable, and evaluated against labelled query classes.&lt;/div&gt;" style="rounded=1;whiteSpace=wrap;html=1;fillColor=#ffffff;strokeColor=#c5d0db;spacing=11;align=left;verticalAlign=top;" vertex="1" parent="1">
          <mxGeometry x="30" y="2649.9" width="755" height="168" as="geometry" />
        </mxCell>
        <mxCell id="s11" value="&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:15px&quot;&gt;&lt;b&gt;DO NOT USE WHEN&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:13px;line-height:1.35&quot;&gt;&amp;bull; Almost every query needs the same subsystems, so a single fixed pipeline is simpler and the router adds only overhead.&lt;br&gt;&amp;bull; Query intent cannot be classified reliably enough to pick the right subset, so misrouting would dominate the savings.&lt;br&gt;&amp;bull; The subsystem pool is small and cheap, so activating all of it costs less than building and maintaining the router.&lt;/div&gt;" style="rounded=1;whiteSpace=wrap;html=1;fillColor=#ffffff;strokeColor=#c5d0db;spacing=11;align=left;verticalAlign=top;" vertex="1" parent="1">
          <mxGeometry x="815" y="2592.9" width="755" height="168" as="geometry" />
        </mxCell>
        <mxCell id="s12" value="&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:15px&quot;&gt;&lt;b&gt;COMPONENTS&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:13px;line-height:1.35&quot;&gt;&amp;bull; Subsystem pool — the independent capabilities the agent can draw on (tool loop, retrieval, knowledge graph, multi-agent sub-team, cache, tiered memory)&lt;br&gt;&amp;bull; Router — classifies each query&#39;s intent and required capabilities and selects the minimal disjoint subset of subsystems to activate&lt;br&gt;&amp;bull; Capability map — the maintained mapping from query class to the subset of subsystems it requires&lt;br&gt;&amp;bull; Composer — wires the activated subsystems for the current query and assembles their outputs into one response&lt;br&gt;&amp;bull; Router evaluation harness — scores routing decisions against labelled query classes so the selector stays correct as the pool changes&lt;/div&gt;" style="rounded=1;whiteSpace=wrap;html=1;fillColor=#ffffff;strokeColor=#c5d0db;spacing=11;align=left;verticalAlign=top;" vertex="1" parent="1">
          <mxGeometry x="30" y="2831.9" width="755" height="225" as="geometry" />
        </mxCell>
        <mxCell id="s13" value="&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:15px&quot;&gt;&lt;b&gt;TOOLS&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:13px;line-height:1.35&quot;&gt;&amp;bull; Intent classifier — labels each query so the router can pick a subset&lt;br&gt;&amp;bull; Retrieval pipeline — the activatable RAG subsystem in the pool&lt;br&gt;&amp;bull; Knowledge graph store — the activatable relational subsystem in the pool&lt;br&gt;&amp;bull; Multi-agent runtime — the activatable planning or decomposition subsystem in the pool&lt;br&gt;&amp;bull; Tracing and cost accounting — records which subsets ran so routing can be measured and tuned&lt;/div&gt;" style="rounded=1;whiteSpace=wrap;html=1;fillColor=#ffffff;strokeColor=#c5d0db;spacing=11;align=left;verticalAlign=top;" vertex="1" parent="1">
          <mxGeometry x="815" y="2774.9" width="755" height="149" as="geometry" />
        </mxCell>
        <mxCell id="s14" value="&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:15px&quot;&gt;&lt;b&gt;FAILURE MODES&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:13px;line-height:1.35&quot;&gt;&amp;bull; Over-activation — the router defaults to a maximal subset, restoring the full-pipeline cost the pattern exists to avoid.&lt;br&gt;&amp;bull; Under-activation — the router omits a subsystem a query needed, returning a confidently wrong or incomplete answer.&lt;br&gt;&amp;bull; Layer worship — effort is poured into the most visible subsystem while the router that decides when it runs is left unimproved.&lt;/div&gt;" style="rounded=1;whiteSpace=wrap;html=1;fillColor=#ffffff;strokeColor=#c5d0db;spacing=11;align=left;verticalAlign=top;" vertex="1" parent="1">
          <mxGeometry x="30" y="3070.9" width="755" height="168" as="geometry" />
        </mxCell>
        <mxCell id="s15" value="&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:15px&quot;&gt;&lt;b&gt;EVALUATION METRICS&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:13px;line-height:1.35&quot;&gt;&amp;bull; Mean active-subset size per query — how much of the pool a typical query avoids&lt;br&gt;&amp;bull; Routing accuracy — fraction of queries activating exactly the subset their query class needs (under- and over-activation rates)&lt;br&gt;&amp;bull; Cost and latency per query vs a full-pipeline baseline — the spend the routing reclaims&lt;br&gt;&amp;bull; Failure localisation rate — fraction of failed queries traced to a single active subsystem&lt;/div&gt;" style="rounded=1;whiteSpace=wrap;html=1;fillColor=#ffffff;strokeColor=#c5d0db;spacing=11;align=left;verticalAlign=top;" vertex="1" parent="1">
          <mxGeometry x="815" y="2937.9" width="755" height="149" as="geometry" />
        </mxCell>
        <mxCell id="s16" value="&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:15px&quot;&gt;&lt;b&gt;KNOWN USES&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:13px;line-height:1.35&quot;&gt;&amp;bull; &lt;b&gt;&lt;a href=&quot;https://www.tomshw.it/business/agente-ai-non-ha-forma-percorsi-sp&quot;&gt;Tom&amp;#x27;s Hardware production-agent analysis&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/b&gt; &lt;i&gt;available&lt;/i&gt; — Field account of enterprise agents built as a router over a pool of subsystems (plain loop, RAG, knowledge graph, multi-agent, cache, memory), where the effective agent emerges query by query and design effort moves to the router.&lt;/div&gt;" style="rounded=1;whiteSpace=wrap;html=1;fillColor=#ffffff;strokeColor=#c5d0db;spacing=11;align=left;verticalAlign=top;" vertex="1" parent="1">
          <mxGeometry x="30" y="3252.9" width="755" height="130" as="geometry" />
        </mxCell>
        <mxCell id="s17" value="&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:15px&quot;&gt;&lt;b&gt;RELATED PATTERNS&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:13px;line-height:1.35&quot;&gt;&amp;bull; &lt;i&gt;complements&lt;/i&gt; &lt;b&gt;Complexity-Based Routing&lt;/b&gt; — Complexity routing binds a query to a model tier by difficulty; behavior-space routing binds it to a subset of architectural subsystems. They stack: difficulty picks the model, capability need picks the subsystems.&lt;br&gt;&amp;bull; &lt;i&gt;alternative-to&lt;/i&gt; &lt;b&gt;Dynamic Topology Routing&lt;/b&gt; — Topology routing rewires links between agents inside an all-multi-agent system; behavior-space routing selects which kinds of subsystem participate at all, and may activate zero agents.&lt;br&gt;&amp;bull; &lt;i&gt;complements&lt;/i&gt; &lt;b&gt;Modular RAG&lt;/b&gt; — Modular RAG rearranges modules inside the retrieval subsystem; behavior-space routing decides whether the retrieval subsystem is on the path for a query in the first place.&lt;br&gt;&amp;bull; &lt;i&gt;alternative-to&lt;/i&gt; &lt;b&gt;Orchestrator-Workers&lt;/b&gt; — Orchestrator-workers decomposes a task within one fixed architecture; behavior-space routing decides which architectures exist for a query before any decomposition.&lt;/div&gt;" style="rounded=1;whiteSpace=wrap;html=1;fillColor=#ffffff;strokeColor=#c5d0db;spacing=11;align=left;verticalAlign=top;" vertex="1" parent="1">
          <mxGeometry x="815" y="3100.9" width="755" height="263" as="geometry" />
        </mxCell>
        <mxCell id="s18" value="&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:15px&quot;&gt;&lt;b&gt;REFERENCES&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:13px;line-height:1.35&quot;&gt;&amp;bull; &lt;a href=&quot;https://www.tomshw.it/business/agente-ai-non-ha-forma-percorsi-sp&quot;&gt;L&amp;#x27;agente AI non ha una forma: ha dei percorsi&lt;/a&gt; — 2026&lt;br&gt;&amp;bull; &lt;a href=&quot;https://arxiv.org/abs/2403.14403&quot;&gt;Adaptive-RAG: Learning to Adapt Retrieval-Augmented Large Language Models through Question Complexity&lt;/a&gt; — Soyeong Jeong, Jinheon Baek, Sukmin Cho, Sung Ju Hwang, Jong C. Park, 2024&lt;br&gt;&amp;bull; &lt;a href=&quot;https://arxiv.org/abs/2502.00409&quot;&gt;Doing More with Less: A Survey on Routing Strategies for Resource Optimisation in Large Language Model-Based Systems&lt;/a&gt; — 2025&lt;/div&gt;" style="rounded=1;whiteSpace=wrap;html=1;fillColor=#ffffff;strokeColor=#c5d0db;spacing=11;align=left;verticalAlign=top;" vertex="1" parent="1">
          <mxGeometry x="30" y="3396.9" width="755" height="187" as="geometry" />
        </mxCell>
      </root>
    </mxGraphModel>
  </diagram>
