        <UserObject label="" mermaidId="e:R-&gt;Agent#0" mermaidBaseStyle="curved=1;startArrow=none;endArrow=block;endSize=7;strokeColor=light-dark(#333333,#cccccc);exitX=0.36;exitY=1;entryX=0.72;entryY=1;" mermaidBaseValue="" id="m13">
          <mxCell edge="1" parent="1" source="m5" style="curved=1;startArrow=none;endArrow=block;endSize=7;strokeColor=light-dark(#333333,#cccccc);exitX=0.36;exitY=1;entryX=0.72;entryY=1;" target="m0">
            <mxGeometry relative="1" as="geometry">
              <Array as="points">
                <mxPoint x="1094.2" y="615.35" />
                <mxPoint x="900.45" y="615.35" />
                <mxPoint x="705.15" y="615.35" />
                <mxPoint x="509.85" y="615.35" />
                <mxPoint x="314.55" y="615.35" />
              </Array>
            </mxGeometry>
          </mxCell>
        </UserObject>
        <mxCell id="arch-caption" value="&lt;i&gt;One task-level tool owns the call sequence and error handling; the agent never sequences the endpoints itself.&lt;/i&gt;" style="text;html=1;align=center;verticalAlign=middle;strokeColor=none;fillColor=none;fontSize=12;fontColor=#52606d;" vertex="1" parent="1">
          <mxGeometry x="65" y="567.1" width="1470" height="32" as="geometry" />
        </mxCell>
        <mxCell id="s0" value="&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:15px&quot;&gt;&lt;b&gt;INTENT&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:13px;line-height:1.35&quot;&gt;Expose one MCP tool that orchestrates several underlying API calls into a single higher-level operation, so the agent invokes a task-level capability instead of chaining many low-level endpoints.&lt;/div&gt;" style="rounded=1;whiteSpace=wrap;html=1;fillColor=#ffffff;strokeColor=#c5d0db;spacing=11;align=left;verticalAlign=top;" vertex="1" parent="1">
          <mxGeometry x="30" y="642.1" width="755" height="92" as="geometry" />
        </mxCell>
        <mxCell id="s1" value="&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:15px&quot;&gt;&lt;b&gt;CONTEXT&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:13px;line-height:1.35&quot;&gt;A team is building an MCP server over a set of fine-grained APIs, or over several different services. Agents need to accomplish a multi-step task — create a project, set its variables, then deploy it — that maps to several endpoint calls in a fixed order.&lt;/div&gt;" style="rounded=1;whiteSpace=wrap;html=1;fillColor=#ffffff;strokeColor=#c5d0db;spacing=11;align=left;verticalAlign=top;" vertex="1" parent="1">
          <mxGeometry x="815" y="642.1" width="755" height="111" as="geometry" />
        </mxCell>
        <mxCell id="s2" value="&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:15px&quot;&gt;&lt;b&gt;PROBLEM&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:13px;line-height:1.35&quot;&gt;A one-to-one endpoint-to-tool mapping pushes orchestration onto the model: it must know the right call order, thread each output into the next input, and handle partial failures across calls. This bloats the tool list, spends tokens on intermediate reasoning, and makes brittle multi-call sequences that fail in hard-to-debug ways. The team needs the agent to express intent at the level of the task, not the level of individual HTTP calls.&lt;/div&gt;" style="rounded=1;whiteSpace=wrap;html=1;fillColor=#ffffff;strokeColor=#c5d0db;spacing=11;align=left;verticalAlign=top;" vertex="1" parent="1">
          <mxGeometry x="30" y="748.1" width="755" height="149" as="geometry" />
        </mxCell>
        <mxCell id="s3" value="&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:15px&quot;&gt;&lt;b&gt;FORCES&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:13px;line-height:1.35&quot;&gt;&amp;bull; Fewer, higher-level tools are easier for the model to choose among, but each hides more logic that must be maintained on the server.&lt;br&gt;&amp;bull; Orchestration in the server is written once and testable; orchestration in the model is re-derived on every call.&lt;br&gt;&amp;bull; Aggregating endpoints couples them — a change in one underlying API can ripple into the composite tool.&lt;br&gt;&amp;bull; A composite tool must define its own error semantics for partial failure across the calls it bundles.&lt;br&gt;&amp;bull; Too much aggregation produces opaque mega-tools that are hard to debug and hard to reuse.&lt;/div&gt;" style="rounded=1;whiteSpace=wrap;html=1;fillColor=#ffffff;strokeColor=#c5d0db;spacing=11;align=left;verticalAlign=top;" vertex="1" parent="1">
          <mxGeometry x="815" y="767.1" width="755" height="225" as="geometry" />
        </mxCell>
        <mxCell id="s4" value="&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:15px&quot;&gt;&lt;b&gt;THEREFORE&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:13px;line-height:1.35&quot;&gt;Therefore: bundle a coherent sequence of underlying calls behind one MCP tool that owns the orchestration and error handling, so the agent calls a single task-level capability.&lt;/div&gt;" style="rounded=1;whiteSpace=wrap;html=1;fillColor=#ffffff;strokeColor=#c5d0db;spacing=11;align=left;verticalAlign=top;" vertex="1" parent="1">
          <mxGeometry x="30" y="911.1" width="755" height="92" as="geometry" />
        </mxCell>
        <mxCell id="s5" value="&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:15px&quot;&gt;&lt;b&gt;SOLUTION&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:13px;line-height:1.35&quot;&gt;Identify recurring multi-call workflows and expose each as one MCP tool whose handler performs the calls, threads the intermediate results, and returns a single typed result. Keep each composite cohesive — one business capability per tool, such as a deploy_project that internally creates the project, sets environment variables, and triggers the deployment — and keep the underlying integrations modular so one API&#39;s change does not cascade. Define explicit partial-failure semantics so the tool reports which underlying call failed. This is the capability-aggregation step beyond direct-api-wrapper; when the orchestration is itself better expressed as code the agent writes, see mcp-as-code-api, and when many composites need organising under one server, hierarchical-tool-selection helps the model navigate them.&lt;/div&gt;" style="rounded=1;whiteSpace=wrap;html=1;fillColor=#ffffff;strokeColor=#c5d0db;spacing=11;align=left;verticalAlign=top;" vertex="1" parent="1">
          <mxGeometry x="815" y="1006.1" width="755" height="225" as="geometry" />
        </mxCell>
        <mxCell id="s6" value="&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:15px&quot;&gt;&lt;b&gt;STRUCTURE&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:13px;line-height:1.35&quot;&gt;&lt;font face=&quot;monospace&quot;&gt;agent host -&amp;gt; [composite MCP tool] -&amp;gt; orchestrates call A + call B + call C -&amp;gt; single typed result.&lt;/font&gt;&lt;/div&gt;" style="rounded=1;whiteSpace=wrap;html=1;fillColor=#ffffff;strokeColor=#c5d0db;spacing=11;align=left;verticalAlign=top;" vertex="1" parent="1">
          <mxGeometry x="30" y="1017.1" width="755" height="92" as="geometry" />
        </mxCell>
        <mxCell id="s7" value="&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:15px&quot;&gt;&lt;b&gt;EXAMPLE SCENARIO&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:13px;line-height:1.35&quot;&gt;A platform exposes fine-grained endpoints for creating a project, setting environment variables, and starting a deployment. Instead of giving the agent three separate tools to sequence, the team ships one MCP tool, deploy_project, whose handler runs the three calls in order and returns a single result. The agent asks to deploy a project in one call, and if the environment-variable step fails the tool returns a typed error naming that step rather than leaving a half-created project behind.&lt;/div&gt;" style="rounded=1;whiteSpace=wrap;html=1;fillColor=#ffffff;strokeColor=#c5d0db;spacing=11;align=left;verticalAlign=top;" vertex="1" parent="1">
          <mxGeometry x="815" y="1245.1" width="755" height="149" as="geometry" />
        </mxCell>
        <mxCell id="s8" value="&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:15px&quot;&gt;&lt;b&gt;CONSEQUENCES&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:13px;line-height:1.35&quot;&gt;&lt;b&gt;Benefits&lt;/b&gt;&lt;br&gt;&lt;br&gt;&amp;bull; Smaller tool surface: the model chooses among task-level capabilities, not raw endpoints.&lt;br&gt;&amp;bull; Fewer model-tool round-trips and lower token cost per task.&lt;br&gt;&amp;bull; Orchestration lives on the server where it is reusable and testable.&lt;br&gt;&amp;bull; Cleaner error handling: partial failure is resolved inside the tool.&lt;br&gt;&amp;bull; Intent is expressed at the level of the task rather than the HTTP call.&lt;br&gt;&lt;br&gt;&lt;b&gt;Liabilities&lt;/b&gt;&lt;br&gt;&lt;br&gt;&amp;bull; The server holds more logic that must be maintained and versioned.&lt;br&gt;&amp;bull; Aggregated endpoints are coupled; a change in one underlying API can break the composite.&lt;br&gt;&amp;bull; Over-aggregation produces opaque mega-tools that are hard to debug.&lt;br&gt;&amp;bull; Bundled pieces are harder to reuse individually than separate tools.&lt;br&gt;&amp;bull; Partial-failure semantics across the bundled calls must be designed deliberately.&lt;/div&gt;" style="rounded=1;whiteSpace=wrap;html=1;fillColor=#ffffff;strokeColor=#c5d0db;spacing=11;align=left;verticalAlign=top;" vertex="1" parent="1">
          <mxGeometry x="30" y="1123.1" width="755" height="339" as="geometry" />
        </mxCell>
        <mxCell id="s9" value="&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:15px&quot;&gt;&lt;b&gt;WHAT THIS PATTERN CONSTRAINS&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:13px;line-height:1.35&quot;&gt;Orchestration of the bundled calls must live in the server tool, not in the model; the agent may invoke the composite capability but must not be required to sequence the underlying calls itself.&lt;/div&gt;" style="rounded=1;whiteSpace=wrap;html=1;fillColor=#ffffff;strokeColor=#c5d0db;spacing=11;align=left;verticalAlign=top;" vertex="1" parent="1">
          <mxGeometry x="815" y="1408.1" width="755" height="92" as="geometry" />
        </mxCell>
        <mxCell id="s10" value="&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:15px&quot;&gt;&lt;b&gt;USE WHEN&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:13px;line-height:1.35&quot;&gt;&amp;bull; Common tasks span several endpoints in a fixed order.&lt;br&gt;&amp;bull; A one-to-one tool surface is too large or too chatty for reliable tool selection.&lt;br&gt;&amp;bull; Orchestration logic is stable enough to own on the server and reuse across tasks.&lt;br&gt;&amp;bull; Partial-failure handling across calls should be consistent rather than re-derived by the model.&lt;/div&gt;" style="rounded=1;whiteSpace=wrap;html=1;fillColor=#ffffff;strokeColor=#c5d0db;spacing=11;align=left;verticalAlign=top;" vertex="1" parent="1">
          <mxGeometry x="30" y="1476.1" width="755" height="130" as="geometry" />
        </mxCell>
        <mxCell id="s11" value="&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:15px&quot;&gt;&lt;b&gt;DO NOT USE WHEN&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:13px;line-height:1.35&quot;&gt;&amp;bull; The underlying API is small and stable enough that one tool per operation suffices — see direct-api-wrapper.&lt;br&gt;&amp;bull; The workflow varies so much per task that any fixed composite would be wrong — let the model orchestrate, or see mcp-as-code-api.&lt;br&gt;&amp;bull; Aggregation would couple endpoints whose lifecycles are independent and likely to diverge.&lt;/div&gt;" style="rounded=1;whiteSpace=wrap;html=1;fillColor=#ffffff;strokeColor=#c5d0db;spacing=11;align=left;verticalAlign=top;" vertex="1" parent="1">
          <mxGeometry x="815" y="1514.1" width="755" height="149" as="geometry" />
        </mxCell>
        <mxCell id="s12" value="&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:15px&quot;&gt;&lt;b&gt;COMPONENTS&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:13px;line-height:1.35&quot;&gt;&amp;bull; Composite Tool — the single MCP tool that owns the multi-call workflow&lt;br&gt;&amp;bull; Orchestration Handler — sequences the underlying calls and threads intermediate results&lt;br&gt;&amp;bull; Underlying APIs — the fine-grained endpoints or services the composite bundles&lt;br&gt;&amp;bull; Error Reconciler — defines partial-failure semantics across the bundled calls&lt;/div&gt;" style="rounded=1;whiteSpace=wrap;html=1;fillColor=#ffffff;strokeColor=#c5d0db;spacing=11;align=left;verticalAlign=top;" vertex="1" parent="1">
          <mxGeometry x="30" y="1620.1" width="755" height="130" as="geometry" />
        </mxCell>
        <mxCell id="s13" value="&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:15px&quot;&gt;&lt;b&gt;TOOLS&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:13px;line-height:1.35&quot;&gt;&amp;bull; Underlying API clients — invoked in sequence inside the composite tool&#39;s handler&lt;br&gt;&amp;bull; Schema validator — checks the single typed result the composite returns to the agent&lt;/div&gt;" style="rounded=1;whiteSpace=wrap;html=1;fillColor=#ffffff;strokeColor=#c5d0db;spacing=11;align=left;verticalAlign=top;" vertex="1" parent="1">
          <mxGeometry x="815" y="1677.1" width="755" height="92" as="geometry" />
        </mxCell>
        <mxCell id="s14" value="&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:15px&quot;&gt;&lt;b&gt;GUARDRAILS&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:13px;line-height:1.35&quot;&gt;&amp;bull; Each composite tool must map to a single business capability; reject scope creep into unrelated operations.&lt;br&gt;&amp;bull; Surface partial failures as a typed error identifying which underlying call failed.&lt;/div&gt;" style="rounded=1;whiteSpace=wrap;html=1;fillColor=#ffffff;strokeColor=#c5d0db;spacing=11;align=left;verticalAlign=top;" vertex="1" parent="1">
          <mxGeometry x="30" y="1764.1" width="755" height="111" as="geometry" />
        </mxCell>
        <mxCell id="s15" value="&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:15px&quot;&gt;&lt;b&gt;FAILURE MODES&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:13px;line-height:1.35&quot;&gt;&amp;bull; Opaque mega-tool — one tool bundles too much, and when it fails nobody can tell which inner step broke.&lt;br&gt;&amp;bull; Cascade on change — one underlying API changes, and every composite that bundles it breaks at once.&lt;br&gt;&amp;bull; Undefined partial failure — two of three inner calls succeed, the tool reports a plain error, and the agent retries and creates duplicates.&lt;br&gt;&amp;bull; Missing piece — the agent needs only one step of a composite but has no separate tool for it, so it runs the whole bundle.&lt;/div&gt;" style="rounded=1;whiteSpace=wrap;html=1;fillColor=#ffffff;strokeColor=#c5d0db;spacing=11;align=left;verticalAlign=top;" vertex="1" parent="1">
          <mxGeometry x="815" y="1783.1" width="755" height="206" as="geometry" />
        </mxCell>
        <mxCell id="s16" value="&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:15px&quot;&gt;&lt;b&gt;EVALUATION METRICS&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:13px;line-height:1.35&quot;&gt;&amp;bull; Tool-surface size — number of tools the agent must choose among before versus after aggregation&lt;br&gt;&amp;bull; Round-trips per task — model-tool round-trips needed to complete the workflow&lt;br&gt;&amp;bull; Partial-failure recovery rate — fraction of composite calls that fail cleanly with a typed error&lt;br&gt;&amp;bull; Composite reuse rate — how often each composite tool is invoked across tasks&lt;/div&gt;" style="rounded=1;whiteSpace=wrap;html=1;fillColor=#ffffff;strokeColor=#c5d0db;spacing=11;align=left;verticalAlign=top;" vertex="1" parent="1">
          <mxGeometry x="30" y="1889.1" width="755" height="130" as="geometry" />
        </mxCell>
        <mxCell id="s17" value="&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:15px&quot;&gt;&lt;b&gt;KNOWN USES&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:13px;line-height:1.35&quot;&gt;&amp;bull; &lt;b&gt;&lt;a href=&quot;https://www.klavis.ai/blog/less-is-more-mcp-design-patterns-for-ai-agents&quot;&gt;Klavis AI (workflow-based design)&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/b&gt; &lt;i&gt;available&lt;/i&gt; — Consolidates granular API calls such as create_project, add_environment_variables, and create_deployment into a single atomic deploy_project tool that handles the full process internally.&lt;br&gt;&amp;bull; &lt;b&gt;&lt;a href=&quot;https://github.com/stripe/ai&quot;&gt;Stripe Agent Toolkit (MCP)&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/b&gt; &lt;i&gt;available&lt;/i&gt; — Exposes task-level tools such as create_payment_link and create_product that bundle the underlying Stripe API operations rather than mirroring every endpoint.&lt;/div&gt;" style="rounded=1;whiteSpace=wrap;html=1;fillColor=#ffffff;strokeColor=#c5d0db;spacing=11;align=left;verticalAlign=top;" vertex="1" parent="1">
          <mxGeometry x="815" y="2003.1" width="755" height="187" as="geometry" />
        </mxCell>
        <mxCell id="s18" value="&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:15px&quot;&gt;&lt;b&gt;RELATED PATTERNS&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:13px;line-height:1.35&quot;&gt;&amp;bull; &lt;i&gt;specialises&lt;/i&gt; &lt;b&gt;Model Context Protocol&lt;/b&gt;&lt;br&gt;&amp;bull; &lt;i&gt;alternative-to&lt;/i&gt; &lt;b&gt;Direct API Wrapper&lt;/b&gt; — Direct API Wrapper keeps the one-to-one surface; Composite Service aggregates several operations behind one task-level tool.&lt;br&gt;&amp;bull; &lt;i&gt;complements&lt;/i&gt; &lt;b&gt;MCP-as-Code-API&lt;/b&gt;&lt;br&gt;&amp;bull; &lt;i&gt;complements&lt;/i&gt; &lt;b&gt;Hierarchical Tool Selection&lt;/b&gt;&lt;br&gt;&amp;bull; &lt;i&gt;complements&lt;/i&gt; &lt;b&gt;MCP Server-Side Sampling&lt;/b&gt; — A composite tool that bundles several API calls can insert a sampled reasoning step between them without the server holding a model of its own.&lt;/div&gt;" style="rounded=1;whiteSpace=wrap;html=1;fillColor=#ffffff;strokeColor=#c5d0db;spacing=11;align=left;verticalAlign=top;" vertex="1" parent="1">
          <mxGeometry x="30" y="2033.1" width="755" height="187" as="geometry" />
        </mxCell>
        <mxCell id="s19" value="&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:15px&quot;&gt;&lt;b&gt;REFERENCES&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:13px;line-height:1.35&quot;&gt;&amp;bull; &lt;a href=&quot;https://www.klavis.ai/blog/less-is-more-mcp-design-patterns-for-ai-agents&quot;&gt;Less is More: design patterns for building better MCP servers (workflow-based design)&lt;/a&gt; — Klavis AI, 2025&lt;br&gt;&amp;bull; &lt;a href=&quot;https://www.scalekit.com/blog/wrap-mcp-around-existing-api&quot;&gt;Should you wrap MCP around your existing API? (capability aggregation pattern)&lt;/a&gt; — Scalekit, 2025&lt;br&gt;&amp;bull; &lt;a href=&quot;https://www.speakeasy.com/mcp/tool-design/generate-mcp-tools-from-openapi&quot;&gt;Generating MCP tools from OpenAPI: benefits, limits and best practices&lt;/a&gt; — Speakeasy&lt;/div&gt;" style="rounded=1;whiteSpace=wrap;html=1;fillColor=#ffffff;strokeColor=#c5d0db;spacing=11;align=left;verticalAlign=top;" vertex="1" parent="1">
          <mxGeometry x="815" y="2204.1" width="755" height="168" as="geometry" />
        </mxCell>
      </root>
    </mxGraphModel>
  </diagram>
