        <mxCell id="arch-caption" value="&lt;i&gt;Each agent publishes identity and capabilities as signed JSON-LD under a W3C DID; peers resolve, verify, and invoke without a central registry.&lt;/i&gt;" style="text;html=1;align=center;verticalAlign=middle;strokeColor=none;fillColor=none;fontSize=12;fontColor=#52606d;" vertex="1" parent="1">
          <mxGeometry x="65" y="875.55" width="1470" height="32" as="geometry" />
        </mxCell>
        <mxCell id="s0" value="&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:15px&quot;&gt;&lt;b&gt;INTENT&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:13px;line-height:1.35&quot;&gt;Agents publish signed DID+JSON-LD identity records so any peer can discover and verify them without a central registry — the agent equivalent of the open web.&lt;/div&gt;" style="rounded=1;whiteSpace=wrap;html=1;fillColor=#ffffff;strokeColor=#c5d0db;spacing=11;align=left;verticalAlign=top;" vertex="1" parent="1">
          <mxGeometry x="30" y="950.55" width="755" height="92" as="geometry" />
        </mxCell>
        <mxCell id="s1" value="&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:15px&quot;&gt;&lt;b&gt;CONTEXT&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:13px;line-height:1.35&quot;&gt;Agent interop protocols so far assume known endpoints. MCP exposes tools to a client that already knows where the MCP server is. A2A connects peer agents whose endpoints have been pre-shared. Both presume some bootstrapping mechanism — a directory, a marketplace, an enterprise registry — that everyone trusts. As agent populations grow across organisational boundaries and across the public internet, no single registry is going to scale or be trusted by all parties.&lt;/div&gt;" style="rounded=1;whiteSpace=wrap;html=1;fillColor=#ffffff;strokeColor=#c5d0db;spacing=11;align=left;verticalAlign=top;" vertex="1" parent="1">
          <mxGeometry x="815" y="950.55" width="755" height="149" as="geometry" />
        </mxCell>
        <mxCell id="s2" value="&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:15px&quot;&gt;&lt;b&gt;PROBLEM&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:13px;line-height:1.35&quot;&gt;Centralised agent registries do not scale across the public internet: every party must trust the registry operator, every cross-org integration requires an admin to onboard, and the registry becomes a single point of policy and failure. There is no protocol for an agent in organisation A to discover and cryptographically verify an agent in organisation B without a pre-arranged channel. Capability advertisement, identity verification, and authorisation all collapse onto the registry operator, who becomes a gatekeeper at internet scale.&lt;/div&gt;" style="rounded=1;whiteSpace=wrap;html=1;fillColor=#ffffff;strokeColor=#c5d0db;spacing=11;align=left;verticalAlign=top;" vertex="1" parent="1">
          <mxGeometry x="30" y="1056.55" width="755" height="168" as="geometry" />
        </mxCell>
        <mxCell id="s3" value="&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:15px&quot;&gt;&lt;b&gt;FORCES&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:13px;line-height:1.35&quot;&gt;&amp;bull; Open-network discovery requires identity that does not depend on a central operator.&lt;br&gt;&amp;bull; Cryptographic verification must work across organisational boundaries with no shared CA.&lt;br&gt;&amp;bull; Capability graphs need a schema everyone can parse without an out-of-band agreement.&lt;br&gt;&amp;bull; Decentralized stacks add operational complexity over a simple HTTP registry.&lt;/div&gt;" style="rounded=1;whiteSpace=wrap;html=1;fillColor=#ffffff;strokeColor=#c5d0db;spacing=11;align=left;verticalAlign=top;" vertex="1" parent="1">
          <mxGeometry x="815" y="1113.55" width="755" height="130" as="geometry" />
        </mxCell>
        <mxCell id="s4" value="&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:15px&quot;&gt;&lt;b&gt;THEREFORE&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:13px;line-height:1.35&quot;&gt;Therefore: identify each agent with a W3C Decentralized Identifier, publish its capability graph as signed JSON-LD discoverable via the DID document, and let any peer verify identity and capabilities cryptographically without consulting a central registry.&lt;/div&gt;" style="rounded=1;whiteSpace=wrap;html=1;fillColor=#ffffff;strokeColor=#c5d0db;spacing=11;align=left;verticalAlign=top;" vertex="1" parent="1">
          <mxGeometry x="30" y="1238.55" width="755" height="111" as="geometry" />
        </mxCell>
        <mxCell id="s5" value="&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:15px&quot;&gt;&lt;b&gt;SOLUTION&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:13px;line-height:1.35&quot;&gt;Assign every agent a W3C Decentralized Identifier (DID) resolvable via a DID method (DID:web, DID:key, DID:ion, etc.). Publish the agent&#39;s capability graph as JSON-LD signed by the DID&#39;s key, hosted at a location the DID document points to. A peer wanting to discover or verify the agent resolves the DID, fetches the JSON-LD capability graph, verifies the signature against the DID&#39;s published keys, and proceeds with whatever interop protocol the capabilities advertise (MCP, A2A, or domain-specific). No central registry sits in the path; trust derives from the cryptographic chain rooted in the DID method.&lt;/div&gt;" style="rounded=1;whiteSpace=wrap;html=1;fillColor=#ffffff;strokeColor=#c5d0db;spacing=11;align=left;verticalAlign=top;" vertex="1" parent="1">
          <mxGeometry x="815" y="1257.55" width="755" height="187" as="geometry" />
        </mxCell>
        <mxCell id="s6" value="&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:15px&quot;&gt;&lt;b&gt;STRUCTURE&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:13px;line-height:1.35&quot;&gt;&lt;font face=&quot;monospace&quot;&gt;Agent --publish--&amp;gt; DID document + signed JSON-LD capability graph; Peer --resolve DID--&amp;gt; DID document --fetch--&amp;gt; capability graph --verify signature against DID keys--&amp;gt; [valid: invoke advertised protocol (MCP, A2A, ...) | invalid: reject]&lt;/font&gt;&lt;/div&gt;" style="rounded=1;whiteSpace=wrap;html=1;fillColor=#ffffff;strokeColor=#c5d0db;spacing=11;align=left;verticalAlign=top;" vertex="1" parent="1">
          <mxGeometry x="30" y="1363.55" width="755" height="130" as="geometry" />
        </mxCell>
        <mxCell id="s7" value="&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:15px&quot;&gt;&lt;b&gt;EXAMPLE SCENARIO&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:13px;line-height:1.35&quot;&gt;A research assistant agent operated by organisation A wants to delegate a domain-specific subtask to an analytical agent operated by organisation B. There is no shared marketplace or directory. Under ANP, organisation B&#39;s agent has published its DID and a JSON-LD capability graph advertising the analytical service and its A2A endpoint. Organisation A&#39;s agent resolves the DID, fetches the capability graph, verifies the signature against B&#39;s published keys, and delegates the task over A2A. The first interaction required no admin onboarding on either side.&lt;/div&gt;" style="rounded=1;whiteSpace=wrap;html=1;fillColor=#ffffff;strokeColor=#c5d0db;spacing=11;align=left;verticalAlign=top;" vertex="1" parent="1">
          <mxGeometry x="815" y="1458.55" width="755" height="168" as="geometry" />
        </mxCell>
        <mxCell id="s8" value="&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:15px&quot;&gt;&lt;b&gt;CONSEQUENCES&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:13px;line-height:1.35&quot;&gt;&lt;b&gt;Benefits&lt;/b&gt;&lt;br&gt;&lt;br&gt;&amp;bull; Open-network discovery — any peer can find and verify an agent without prior arrangement.&lt;br&gt;&amp;bull; No single point of policy or failure; no registry operator to trust.&lt;br&gt;&amp;bull; Identity is cryptographic and rotatable; key compromise does not require re-onboarding.&lt;br&gt;&amp;bull; Capability graphs are machine-parseable JSON-LD, so toolchains can be generic.&lt;br&gt;&lt;br&gt;&lt;b&gt;Liabilities&lt;/b&gt;&lt;br&gt;&lt;br&gt;&amp;bull; DID method choice has its own trust and operational properties; not all DID methods are equal.&lt;br&gt;&amp;bull; Key management at scale is hard; lost keys orphan the identity.&lt;br&gt;&amp;bull; JSON-LD context resolution adds complexity over a flat schema.&lt;br&gt;&amp;bull; Adoption is thin; ecosystem of DID resolvers, verifiers, and JSON-LD tooling is still maturing.&lt;br&gt;&amp;bull; Decentralized does not mean trustless: a discovered agent can still be malicious.&lt;/div&gt;" style="rounded=1;whiteSpace=wrap;html=1;fillColor=#ffffff;strokeColor=#c5d0db;spacing=11;align=left;verticalAlign=top;" vertex="1" parent="1">
          <mxGeometry x="30" y="1507.55" width="755" height="320" as="geometry" />
        </mxCell>
        <mxCell id="s9" value="&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:15px&quot;&gt;&lt;b&gt;WHAT THIS PATTERN CONSTRAINS&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:13px;line-height:1.35&quot;&gt;Agent identity may only be asserted via the published DID; capability claims may only be trusted after JSON-LD signature verification against the DID&#39;s keys, so no in-band claim from an unverified agent is honoured.&lt;/div&gt;" style="rounded=1;whiteSpace=wrap;html=1;fillColor=#ffffff;strokeColor=#c5d0db;spacing=11;align=left;verticalAlign=top;" vertex="1" parent="1">
          <mxGeometry x="815" y="1640.55" width="755" height="111" as="geometry" />
        </mxCell>
        <mxCell id="s10" value="&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:15px&quot;&gt;&lt;b&gt;USE WHEN&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:13px;line-height:1.35&quot;&gt;&amp;bull; Agents must discover and verify each other across organisational boundaries with no shared registry.&lt;br&gt;&amp;bull; Cryptographic identity rotation is a hard requirement and centralised re-onboarding is unacceptable.&lt;br&gt;&amp;bull; Capability advertisement should be machine-parseable by generic tooling, not bespoke per vendor.&lt;br&gt;&amp;bull; The deployment is on the open internet rather than inside one enterprise.&lt;/div&gt;" style="rounded=1;whiteSpace=wrap;html=1;fillColor=#ffffff;strokeColor=#c5d0db;spacing=11;align=left;verticalAlign=top;" vertex="1" parent="1">
          <mxGeometry x="30" y="1841.55" width="755" height="168" as="geometry" />
        </mxCell>
        <mxCell id="s11" value="&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:15px&quot;&gt;&lt;b&gt;DO NOT USE WHEN&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:13px;line-height:1.35&quot;&gt;&amp;bull; All agents live inside one organisation; a central registry (see cross-domain-agent-network) is simpler.&lt;br&gt;&amp;bull; DID/JSON-LD operational maturity in the team is insufficient and a simpler protocol meets the need.&lt;br&gt;&amp;bull; Adoption thinness means no peers actually speak the protocol, defeating the discovery purpose.&lt;br&gt;&amp;bull; Latency budget cannot absorb DID resolution and signature verification on every cold discovery.&lt;/div&gt;" style="rounded=1;whiteSpace=wrap;html=1;fillColor=#ffffff;strokeColor=#c5d0db;spacing=11;align=left;verticalAlign=top;" vertex="1" parent="1">
          <mxGeometry x="815" y="1765.55" width="755" height="168" as="geometry" />
        </mxCell>
        <mxCell id="s12" value="&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:15px&quot;&gt;&lt;b&gt;COMPONENTS&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:13px;line-height:1.35&quot;&gt;&amp;bull; Decentralized Identifier — W3C DID assigned per agent, resolvable via a chosen DID method&lt;br&gt;&amp;bull; DID document — the resolution target containing the agent&#39;s public keys and service endpoints&lt;br&gt;&amp;bull; Capability graph — JSON-LD document signed by the DID&#39;s keys advertising the agent&#39;s capabilities&lt;br&gt;&amp;bull; Resolver — software that turns a DID into the DID document via the chosen method&lt;br&gt;&amp;bull; Verifier — software that validates capability-graph signatures against the resolved keys&lt;/div&gt;" style="rounded=1;whiteSpace=wrap;html=1;fillColor=#ffffff;strokeColor=#c5d0db;spacing=11;align=left;verticalAlign=top;" vertex="1" parent="1">
          <mxGeometry x="30" y="2023.55" width="755" height="149" as="geometry" />
        </mxCell>
        <mxCell id="s13" value="&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:15px&quot;&gt;&lt;b&gt;TOOLS&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:13px;line-height:1.35&quot;&gt;&amp;bull; DID resolver library — universal-resolver, did-resolver, or method-specific implementations&lt;br&gt;&amp;bull; JSON-LD processor — jsonld-signatures, json-ld.js, or equivalent&lt;br&gt;&amp;bull; Key management — HSM, KMS, or in-process for development&lt;br&gt;&amp;bull; Underlying interop protocol — MCP, A2A, or domain-specific; ANP only handles discovery and identity&lt;/div&gt;" style="rounded=1;whiteSpace=wrap;html=1;fillColor=#ffffff;strokeColor=#c5d0db;spacing=11;align=left;verticalAlign=top;" vertex="1" parent="1">
          <mxGeometry x="815" y="1947.55" width="755" height="149" as="geometry" />
        </mxCell>
        <mxCell id="s14" value="&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:15px&quot;&gt;&lt;b&gt;FAILURE MODES&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:13px;line-height:1.35&quot;&gt;&amp;bull; Lost key — an operator loses the private key behind a DID, and the agent&#39;s identity and signed capabilities can no longer be updated.&lt;br&gt;&amp;bull; Weak DID method — a team picks a DID method with poor trust properties, and peers verify signatures against keys an attacker can control.&lt;br&gt;&amp;bull; Skipped verification — a peer reads the capability graph but does not check the signature, and honours claims from an unverified agent.&lt;br&gt;&amp;bull; Context resolution failure — the JSON-LD context cannot be fetched or is parsed differently by two toolchains, and discovery breaks.&lt;/div&gt;" style="rounded=1;whiteSpace=wrap;html=1;fillColor=#ffffff;strokeColor=#c5d0db;spacing=11;align=left;verticalAlign=top;" vertex="1" parent="1">
          <mxGeometry x="30" y="2186.55" width="755" height="206" as="geometry" />
        </mxCell>
        <mxCell id="s15" value="&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:15px&quot;&gt;&lt;b&gt;EVALUATION METRICS&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:13px;line-height:1.35&quot;&gt;&amp;bull; Cold-discovery latency — DID resolution + JSON-LD fetch + signature verification, p95&lt;br&gt;&amp;bull; Discovery success rate against a corpus of advertised peers, including method-specific failures&lt;br&gt;&amp;bull; Capability-graph parsability — fraction of fetched graphs that round-trip through a generic JSON-LD parser&lt;br&gt;&amp;bull; Key rotation success rate — peers correctly handling a rotated key without re-onboarding&lt;br&gt;&amp;bull; Trust-failure rate — fraction of discovered peers that fail signature verification (poisoning detector)&lt;/div&gt;" style="rounded=1;whiteSpace=wrap;html=1;fillColor=#ffffff;strokeColor=#c5d0db;spacing=11;align=left;verticalAlign=top;" vertex="1" parent="1">
          <mxGeometry x="815" y="2110.55" width="755" height="187" as="geometry" />
        </mxCell>
        <mxCell id="s16" value="&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:15px&quot;&gt;&lt;b&gt;KNOWN USES&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:13px;line-height:1.35&quot;&gt;&amp;bull; &lt;b&gt;&lt;a href=&quot;https://agent-network-protocol.com/&quot;&gt;Agent Network Protocol (ANP) specification&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/b&gt; &lt;i&gt;available&lt;/i&gt;&lt;br&gt;&amp;bull; &lt;b&gt;&lt;a href=&quot;https://github.com/agent-network-protocol/AgentNetworkProtocol&quot;&gt;agent-network-protocol/AgentNetworkProtocol open-source reference&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/b&gt; &lt;i&gt;available&lt;/i&gt;&lt;/div&gt;" style="rounded=1;whiteSpace=wrap;html=1;fillColor=#ffffff;strokeColor=#c5d0db;spacing=11;align=left;verticalAlign=top;" vertex="1" parent="1">
          <mxGeometry x="30" y="2406.55" width="755" height="111" as="geometry" />
        </mxCell>
        <mxCell id="s17" value="&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:15px&quot;&gt;&lt;b&gt;RELATED PATTERNS&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:13px;line-height:1.35&quot;&gt;&amp;bull; &lt;i&gt;complements&lt;/i&gt; &lt;b&gt;Model Context Protocol&lt;/b&gt;&lt;br&gt;&amp;bull; &lt;i&gt;alternative-to&lt;/i&gt; &lt;b&gt;Inter-Agent Communication&lt;/b&gt;&lt;br&gt;&amp;bull; &lt;i&gt;alternative-to&lt;/i&gt; &lt;b&gt;Cross-Domain Enterprise Agent Network&lt;/b&gt;&lt;br&gt;&amp;bull; &lt;i&gt;complements&lt;/i&gt; &lt;b&gt;Tool Discovery&lt;/b&gt;&lt;br&gt;&amp;bull; &lt;i&gt;generalises&lt;/i&gt; &lt;b&gt;Decentralized Swarm Handoff&lt;/b&gt;&lt;br&gt;&amp;bull; &lt;i&gt;alternative-to&lt;/i&gt; &lt;b&gt;Cellular-Automata Agents&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/div&gt;" style="rounded=1;whiteSpace=wrap;html=1;fillColor=#ffffff;strokeColor=#c5d0db;spacing=11;align=left;verticalAlign=top;" vertex="1" parent="1">
          <mxGeometry x="815" y="2311.55" width="755" height="168" as="geometry" />
        </mxCell>
        <mxCell id="s18" value="&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:15px&quot;&gt;&lt;b&gt;REFERENCES&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:13px;line-height:1.35&quot;&gt;&amp;bull; &lt;a href=&quot;https://arxiv.org/abs/2505.02279&quot;&gt;A Survey of Agent Interoperability Protocols: MCP, ACP, A2A, ANP&lt;/a&gt; — 2025&lt;br&gt;&amp;bull; &lt;a href=&quot;https://zhuanlan.zhihu.com/p/1908175325663306451&quot;&gt;一文读懂｜大模型智能体互操作协议：MCP/ACP/A2A/ANP&lt;/a&gt;&lt;br&gt;&amp;bull; &lt;a href=&quot;https://www.w3.org/TR/did-core/&quot;&gt;W3C Decentralized Identifiers (DIDs) v1.0&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/div&gt;" style="rounded=1;whiteSpace=wrap;html=1;fillColor=#ffffff;strokeColor=#c5d0db;spacing=11;align=left;verticalAlign=top;" vertex="1" parent="1">
          <mxGeometry x="30" y="2531.55" width="755" height="130" as="geometry" />
        </mxCell>
      </root>
    </mxGraphModel>
  </diagram>
