        <UserObject label="Decoder-only transformer&#xa;next-token over interleaved sequence" mermaidId="n:TR" mermaidBaseStyle="html=1;whiteSpace=wrap;strokeWidth=1;fillColor=light-dark(#ECECFF,#1f2020);strokeColor=light-dark(#9370DB,#cccccc);fontColor=light-dark(#333333,#cccccc);fontFamily=Trebuchet MS,Verdana,Arial,sans-serif;fontSize=16;" mermaidBaseValue="Decoder-only transformer&#xa;next-token over interleaved sequence" id="m14">
          <mxCell parent="1" style="html=1;whiteSpace=wrap;strokeWidth=1;fillColor=light-dark(#ECECFF,#1f2020);strokeColor=light-dark(#9370DB,#cccccc);fontColor=light-dark(#333333,#cccccc);fontFamily=Trebuchet MS,Verdana,Arial,sans-serif;fontSize=16;" vertex="1">
            <mxGeometry height="113.15" width="516.15" x="178.15" y="785.85" as="geometry" />
          </mxCell>
        </UserObject>
        <UserObject label="" mermaidId="e:IL-&gt;TR#0" mermaidBaseStyle="curved=1;startArrow=none;endArrow=block;endSize=7;strokeColor=light-dark(#333333,#cccccc);exitX=0.5;exitY=1;entryX=0.5;entryY=0;" mermaidBaseValue="" id="m15">
          <mxCell edge="1" parent="1" source="m2" style="curved=1;startArrow=none;endArrow=block;endSize=7;strokeColor=light-dark(#333333,#cccccc);exitX=0.5;exitY=1;entryX=0.5;entryY=0;" target="m14">
            <mxGeometry relative="1" as="geometry">
              <Array as="points" />
            </mxGeometry>
          </mxCell>
        </UserObject>
        <UserObject label="shared weights" mermaidId="e:TR-&gt;M#0" mermaidBaseStyle="curved=1;dashed=1;dashPattern=2 3;fixDash=1;startArrow=none;endArrow=block;endSize=7;strokeColor=light-dark(#333333,#cccccc);html=1;fontSize=16;labelBackgroundColor=light-dark(#E8E8E88D,#2a2a2a8D);fontFamily=Trebuchet MS,Verdana,Arial,sans-serif;fontColor=light-dark(#333333,#cccccc);exitX=1;exitY=1;entryX=0.36;entryY=0;" mermaidBaseValue="shared weights" id="m16">
          <mxCell edge="1" parent="1" source="m14" style="curved=1;dashed=1;dashPattern=2 3;fixDash=1;startArrow=none;endArrow=block;endSize=7;strokeColor=light-dark(#333333,#cccccc);html=1;fontSize=16;labelBackgroundColor=light-dark(#E8E8E88D,#2a2a2a8D);fontFamily=Trebuchet MS,Verdana,Arial,sans-serif;fontColor=light-dark(#333333,#cccccc);exitX=1;exitY=1;entryX=0.36;entryY=0;" target="m10">
            <mxGeometry relative="1" as="geometry">
              <Array as="points">
                <mxPoint x="940.75" y="953.25" />
              </Array>
            </mxGeometry>
          </mxCell>
        </UserObject>
        <mxCell id="arch-caption" value="&lt;i&gt;Fixed-offset interleaving turns streaming speech into a single next-token problem with no learned read/write policy.&lt;/i&gt;" style="text;html=1;align=center;verticalAlign=middle;strokeColor=none;fillColor=none;fontSize=12;fontColor=#52606d;" vertex="1" parent="1">
          <mxGeometry x="65" y="1295.6" width="1470" height="32" as="geometry" />
        </mxCell>
        <mxCell id="s0" value="&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:15px&quot;&gt;&lt;b&gt;INTENT&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:13px;line-height:1.35&quot;&gt;Convert streaming speech tasks into a single decoder-only autoregressive problem by time-aligning the parallel input and output streams with a fixed offset in preprocessing, eliminating the learned read/write policy that cascade pipelines require.&lt;/div&gt;" style="rounded=1;whiteSpace=wrap;html=1;fillColor=#ffffff;strokeColor=#c5d0db;spacing=11;align=left;verticalAlign=top;" vertex="1" parent="1">
          <mxGeometry x="30" y="1370.6" width="755" height="111" as="geometry" />
        </mxCell>
        <mxCell id="s1" value="&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:15px&quot;&gt;&lt;b&gt;CONTEXT&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:13px;line-height:1.35&quot;&gt;A team is building a low-latency speech system — a real-time translator, a voice assistant that has to hold a conversation, or a full-duplex dialogue agent where the human and the agent can talk over each other. The conventional architecture is a cascade: a speech-to-text (STT) model transcribes the user&#39;s audio, a language model reasons about the text, and a text-to-speech (TTS) model produces the reply audio. Simultaneous-translation systems usually add a separate &quot;read/write policy&quot; that decides at each moment whether to wait for more input or emit the next chunk of output.&lt;/div&gt;" style="rounded=1;whiteSpace=wrap;html=1;fillColor=#ffffff;strokeColor=#c5d0db;spacing=11;align=left;verticalAlign=top;" vertex="1" parent="1">
          <mxGeometry x="815" y="1370.6" width="755" height="168" as="geometry" />
        </mxCell>
        <mxCell id="s2" value="&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:15px&quot;&gt;&lt;b&gt;PROBLEM&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:13px;line-height:1.35&quot;&gt;Cascading three models adds the latency of each stage to the user-perceived delay, and every handoff between them is a place where errors compound or interruptions break the pipeline. The language model cannot start reasoning until the speech-to-text stage commits to a transcription, and the text-to-speech stage cannot start speaking until the language model commits to a reply. The learned read/write policy added on top of this in simultaneous translators is itself a separate model that is hard to train, sensitive to the chosen delay budget, and has its own failure modes. None of these architectures handle full-duplex dialogue — both sides talking and listening at once — without further hacks.&lt;/div&gt;" style="rounded=1;whiteSpace=wrap;html=1;fillColor=#ffffff;strokeColor=#c5d0db;spacing=11;align=left;verticalAlign=top;" vertex="1" parent="1">
          <mxGeometry x="30" y="1495.6" width="755" height="206" as="geometry" />
        </mxCell>
        <mxCell id="s3" value="&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:15px&quot;&gt;&lt;b&gt;FORCES&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:13px;line-height:1.35&quot;&gt;&amp;bull; Streaming low-latency speech requires emitting output before input is finished.&lt;br&gt;&amp;bull; Cascade architectures accumulate latency across stages.&lt;br&gt;&amp;bull; Learned read/write policies are extra training problems with their own failure modes.&lt;br&gt;&amp;bull; A single decoder-only model is simpler to train and deploy than a cascade.&lt;br&gt;&amp;bull; Time-alignment between streams (e.g. translated speech lagging source speech by a fixed offset) can be enforced in preprocessing instead of learned at inference.&lt;/div&gt;" style="rounded=1;whiteSpace=wrap;html=1;fillColor=#ffffff;strokeColor=#c5d0db;spacing=11;align=left;verticalAlign=top;" vertex="1" parent="1">
          <mxGeometry x="815" y="1552.6" width="755" height="168" as="geometry" />
        </mxCell>
        <mxCell id="s4" value="&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:15px&quot;&gt;&lt;b&gt;THEREFORE&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:13px;line-height:1.35&quot;&gt;Therefore: time-align the input and output streams with a fixed offset during data preprocessing and train one decoder-only model that autoregressively predicts the offset-aligned target stream from the source stream, collapsing read/write policy into pure positional structure.&lt;/div&gt;" style="rounded=1;whiteSpace=wrap;html=1;fillColor=#ffffff;strokeColor=#c5d0db;spacing=11;align=left;verticalAlign=top;" vertex="1" parent="1">
          <mxGeometry x="30" y="1715.6" width="755" height="111" as="geometry" />
        </mxCell>
        <mxCell id="s5" value="&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:15px&quot;&gt;&lt;b&gt;SOLUTION&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:13px;line-height:1.35&quot;&gt;In preprocessing, represent each training example as parallel token streams (source and target) interleaved on a shared time axis, with the target stream offset by a fixed delay (the chosen latency budget, e.g. 1-3 seconds for translation, ~80ms for full-duplex dialogue). Train a standard decoder-only transformer to autoregressively predict the next interleaved token. At inference, feed source tokens as they arrive and read off target tokens at the offset position — no learned policy decides when to emit, the offset structure does. The same architecture handles speech-to-text (text stream offset behind audio), text-to-speech (audio stream offset behind text), simultaneous translation (target language offset behind source), and full-duplex dialogue (each speaker&#39;s stream offset behind the joint conversation).&lt;/div&gt;" style="rounded=1;whiteSpace=wrap;html=1;fillColor=#ffffff;strokeColor=#c5d0db;spacing=11;align=left;verticalAlign=top;" vertex="1" parent="1">
          <mxGeometry x="815" y="1734.6" width="755" height="225" as="geometry" />
        </mxCell>
        <mxCell id="s6" value="&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:15px&quot;&gt;&lt;b&gt;STRUCTURE&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:13px;line-height:1.35&quot;&gt;&lt;font face=&quot;monospace&quot;&gt;Preprocessing: source stream || target stream offset by D tokens, interleaved on shared time axis. Training: single decoder-only model, next-token prediction over interleaved sequence. Inference: source tokens stream in, target tokens stream out at fixed lag D. No separate STT, LLM, TTS, or read/write policy.&lt;/font&gt;&lt;/div&gt;" style="rounded=1;whiteSpace=wrap;html=1;fillColor=#ffffff;strokeColor=#c5d0db;spacing=11;align=left;verticalAlign=top;" vertex="1" parent="1">
          <mxGeometry x="30" y="1840.6" width="755" height="149" as="geometry" />
        </mxCell>
        <mxCell id="s7" value="&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:15px&quot;&gt;&lt;b&gt;EXAMPLE SCENARIO&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:13px;line-height:1.35&quot;&gt;A simultaneous translator app needs French speech out within two seconds of English speech in, on-device. The team trains a single delayed-streams decoder with target French audio offset 2s behind source English audio. At inference the user speaks; French tokens stream out two seconds later from the same model — no separate STT, no separate LLM, no learned read/write policy. The same architecture, retrained with a tiny offset and both speakers&#39; audio as parallel streams, powers their full-duplex dialogue assistant.&lt;/div&gt;" style="rounded=1;whiteSpace=wrap;html=1;fillColor=#ffffff;strokeColor=#c5d0db;spacing=11;align=left;verticalAlign=top;" vertex="1" parent="1">
          <mxGeometry x="815" y="1973.6" width="755" height="168" as="geometry" />
        </mxCell>
        <mxCell id="s8" value="&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:15px&quot;&gt;&lt;b&gt;CONSEQUENCES&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:13px;line-height:1.35&quot;&gt;&lt;b&gt;Benefits&lt;/b&gt;&lt;br&gt;&lt;br&gt;&amp;bull; Single model replaces a cascade; one training pipeline, one deployment target.&lt;br&gt;&amp;bull; Latency is a preprocessing knob, not a learned behaviour — easy to tune.&lt;br&gt;&amp;bull; Naturally supports full-duplex (both sides as parallel offset streams).&lt;br&gt;&amp;bull; Eliminates learned read/write policy and its failure modes.&lt;br&gt;&amp;bull; Stream alignment is interpretable: the offset is the latency.&lt;br&gt;&lt;br&gt;&lt;b&gt;Liabilities&lt;/b&gt;&lt;br&gt;&lt;br&gt;&amp;bull; Requires time-aligned paired data, which is hard to obtain for some language pairs and modalities.&lt;br&gt;&amp;bull; Fixed offset means latency cannot adapt to easy vs hard segments — a learned policy could.&lt;br&gt;&amp;bull; Single model couples STT, LLM, and TTS quality; weakness in one role is hard to isolate.&lt;br&gt;&amp;bull; Long-context behavioural shaping (instruction-following, refusals) is less clean than in a separate LLM stage.&lt;br&gt;&amp;bull; Architecture commits to streaming use; batch tasks gain little from the offset structure.&lt;/div&gt;" style="rounded=1;whiteSpace=wrap;html=1;fillColor=#ffffff;strokeColor=#c5d0db;spacing=11;align=left;verticalAlign=top;" vertex="1" parent="1">
          <mxGeometry x="30" y="2003.6" width="755" height="358" as="geometry" />
        </mxCell>
        <mxCell id="s9" value="&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:15px&quot;&gt;&lt;b&gt;WHAT THIS PATTERN CONSTRAINS&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:13px;line-height:1.35&quot;&gt;The model must not predict output tokens ahead of the configured offset — emission position is structural, not learned. The architecture forbids inserting a separate read/write policy or cascade stage; the offset is the policy.&lt;/div&gt;" style="rounded=1;whiteSpace=wrap;html=1;fillColor=#ffffff;strokeColor=#c5d0db;spacing=11;align=left;verticalAlign=top;" vertex="1" parent="1">
          <mxGeometry x="815" y="2155.6" width="755" height="111" as="geometry" />
        </mxCell>
        <mxCell id="s10" value="&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:15px&quot;&gt;&lt;b&gt;USE WHEN&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:13px;line-height:1.35&quot;&gt;&amp;bull; Latency budget is tight (sub-second to few-second).&lt;br&gt;&amp;bull; Task is naturally a stream-to-stream transduction (speech, translation, dialogue).&lt;br&gt;&amp;bull; Time-aligned paired data is available or can be synthesized.&lt;br&gt;&amp;bull; Cascade complexity (STT+LLM+TTS) is dominating engineering cost or latency.&lt;/div&gt;" style="rounded=1;whiteSpace=wrap;html=1;fillColor=#ffffff;strokeColor=#c5d0db;spacing=11;align=left;verticalAlign=top;" vertex="1" parent="1">
          <mxGeometry x="30" y="2375.6" width="755" height="130" as="geometry" />
        </mxCell>
        <mxCell id="s11" value="&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:15px&quot;&gt;&lt;b&gt;DO NOT USE WHEN&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:13px;line-height:1.35&quot;&gt;&amp;bull; Task is batch-shaped (transcribe a finished audio file).&lt;br&gt;&amp;bull; Paired time-aligned data is unobtainable.&lt;br&gt;&amp;bull; Per-stage modularity (swap the LLM independently) is a hard requirement.&lt;br&gt;&amp;bull; Variable per-segment latency (longer pause on hard sentences) is needed; fixed offset cannot do this.&lt;/div&gt;" style="rounded=1;whiteSpace=wrap;html=1;fillColor=#ffffff;strokeColor=#c5d0db;spacing=11;align=left;verticalAlign=top;" vertex="1" parent="1">
          <mxGeometry x="815" y="2280.6" width="755" height="149" as="geometry" />
        </mxCell>
        <mxCell id="s12" value="&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:15px&quot;&gt;&lt;b&gt;COMPONENTS&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:13px;line-height:1.35&quot;&gt;&amp;bull; Source stream — live input tokens (audio frames, source-language text) arriving on a shared time axis&lt;br&gt;&amp;bull; Target stream — output tokens offset by a fixed delay D and interleaved with the source&lt;br&gt;&amp;bull; Preprocessing aligner — produces the interleaved time-aligned training sequence from paired data&lt;br&gt;&amp;bull; Decoder-only transformer — single autoregressive model trained on the interleaved sequence&lt;br&gt;&amp;bull; Offset emitter — reads off target tokens at the configured lag D during inference&lt;/div&gt;" style="rounded=1;whiteSpace=wrap;html=1;fillColor=#ffffff;strokeColor=#c5d0db;spacing=11;align=left;verticalAlign=top;" vertex="1" parent="1">
          <mxGeometry x="30" y="2519.6" width="755" height="168" as="geometry" />
        </mxCell>
        <mxCell id="s13" value="&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:15px&quot;&gt;&lt;b&gt;TOOLS&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:13px;line-height:1.35&quot;&gt;&amp;bull; Decoder-only transformer framework — trains and serves next-token prediction over interleaved streams&lt;br&gt;&amp;bull; Audio codec or tokenizer — discretises speech into the token stream the model consumes&lt;br&gt;&amp;bull; Time-aligned paired corpus — source/target data alignable on a shared time axis&lt;/div&gt;" style="rounded=1;whiteSpace=wrap;html=1;fillColor=#ffffff;strokeColor=#c5d0db;spacing=11;align=left;verticalAlign=top;" vertex="1" parent="1">
          <mxGeometry x="815" y="2443.6" width="755" height="130" as="geometry" />
        </mxCell>
        <mxCell id="s14" value="&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:15px&quot;&gt;&lt;b&gt;FAILURE MODES&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:13px;line-height:1.35&quot;&gt;&amp;bull; Offset too short for hard segments — a fixed delay that suits easy speech forces the model to emit before the needed context has arrived, and output quality drops on difficult passages.&lt;br&gt;&amp;bull; Missing aligned data — a language pair or modality lacks time-aligned paired data, so the model is trained on thin or poorly aligned examples and drifts out of sync.&lt;br&gt;&amp;bull; Entangled weakness — transcription, reasoning and speech share one model, so a regression in one role shows up as a vague quality drop that the team cannot pin on a stage.&lt;br&gt;&amp;bull; Weak behaviour shaping — instruction-following or refusals tuned for a separate LLM stage do not carry over cleanly, and the joint model answers in ways a text-only stage would have blocked.&lt;/div&gt;" style="rounded=1;whiteSpace=wrap;html=1;fillColor=#ffffff;strokeColor=#c5d0db;spacing=11;align=left;verticalAlign=top;" vertex="1" parent="1">
          <mxGeometry x="30" y="2701.6" width="755" height="206" as="geometry" />
        </mxCell>
        <mxCell id="s15" value="&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:15px&quot;&gt;&lt;b&gt;EVALUATION METRICS&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:13px;line-height:1.35&quot;&gt;&amp;bull; End-to-end first-output latency — wall-clock from source onset to first emitted target token&lt;br&gt;&amp;bull; Effective offset D versus configured D — drift between intended and observed lag&lt;br&gt;&amp;bull; Task quality at offset (BLEU, WER, MOS) — does the fixed-lag constraint hold output quality&lt;br&gt;&amp;bull; Full-duplex overlap handling — fraction of overlapping-speech windows the model emits cleanly&lt;br&gt;&amp;bull; Parameter and compute cost versus cascade baseline — savings from collapsing STT+LLM+TTS into one model&lt;/div&gt;" style="rounded=1;whiteSpace=wrap;html=1;fillColor=#ffffff;strokeColor=#c5d0db;spacing=11;align=left;verticalAlign=top;" vertex="1" parent="1">
          <mxGeometry x="815" y="2587.6" width="755" height="168" as="geometry" />
        </mxCell>
        <mxCell id="s16" value="&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:15px&quot;&gt;&lt;b&gt;KNOWN USES&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:13px;line-height:1.35&quot;&gt;&amp;bull; &lt;b&gt;&lt;a href=&quot;https://kyutai.org/&quot;&gt;Kyutai Moshi&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/b&gt; &lt;i&gt;available&lt;/i&gt; — Full-duplex spoken dialogue model trained as a delayed-streams decoder.&lt;br&gt;&amp;bull; &lt;b&gt;&lt;a href=&quot;https://kyutai.org/&quot;&gt;Kyutai Hibiki&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/b&gt; &lt;i&gt;available&lt;/i&gt; — Simultaneous speech-to-speech translation via DSM.&lt;br&gt;&amp;bull; &lt;b&gt;&lt;a href=&quot;https://kyutai.org/&quot;&gt;Kyutai Unmute&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/b&gt; &lt;i&gt;available&lt;/i&gt; — Real-time speech interaction stack on DSM.&lt;/div&gt;" style="rounded=1;whiteSpace=wrap;html=1;fillColor=#ffffff;strokeColor=#c5d0db;spacing=11;align=left;verticalAlign=top;" vertex="1" parent="1">
          <mxGeometry x="30" y="2921.6" width="755" height="130" as="geometry" />
        </mxCell>
        <mxCell id="s17" value="&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:15px&quot;&gt;&lt;b&gt;RELATED PATTERNS&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:13px;line-height:1.35&quot;&gt;&amp;bull; &lt;i&gt;alternative-to&lt;/i&gt; &lt;b&gt;Streaming Typed Events&lt;/b&gt; — Streaming-typed-events is a transport-layer SSE pattern; DSM is a model-architecture pattern that produces streamable output.&lt;br&gt;&amp;bull; &lt;i&gt;alternative-to&lt;/i&gt; &lt;b&gt;Multilingual Voice Agent Stack&lt;/b&gt; — Cascade STT-&amp;gt;LLM-&amp;gt;TTS vs single-decoder offset streams; DSM trades modularity for latency and simplicity.&lt;/div&gt;" style="rounded=1;whiteSpace=wrap;html=1;fillColor=#ffffff;strokeColor=#c5d0db;spacing=11;align=left;verticalAlign=top;" vertex="1" parent="1">
          <mxGeometry x="815" y="2769.6" width="755" height="130" as="geometry" />
        </mxCell>
        <mxCell id="s18" value="&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:15px&quot;&gt;&lt;b&gt;REFERENCES&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:13px;line-height:1.35&quot;&gt;&amp;bull; &lt;a href=&quot;https://arxiv.org/abs/2509.08753&quot;&gt;Delayed Streams Modeling&lt;/a&gt; — Kyutai Labs, 2025&lt;br&gt;&amp;bull; &lt;a href=&quot;https://kyutai.org/blog/2025-02-10-hibiki&quot;&gt;Simultaneous, on-device, high fidelity speech-to-speech translation with Hibiki&lt;/a&gt; — Kyutai, 2025&lt;br&gt;&amp;bull; &lt;a href=&quot;https://github.com/kyutai-labs/delayed-streams-modeling&quot;&gt;delayed-streams-modeling&lt;/a&gt; — Kyutai Labs, 2025&lt;/div&gt;" style="rounded=1;whiteSpace=wrap;html=1;fillColor=#ffffff;strokeColor=#c5d0db;spacing=11;align=left;verticalAlign=top;" vertex="1" parent="1">
          <mxGeometry x="30" y="3065.6" width="755" height="149" as="geometry" />
        </mxCell>
      </root>
    </mxGraphModel>
  </diagram>
