        <UserObject label="yes" mermaidId="e:C-&gt;A#0" mermaidBaseStyle="curved=1;startArrow=none;endArrow=block;endSize=7;strokeColor=light-dark(#333333,#cccccc);html=1;fontSize=16;labelBackgroundColor=light-dark(#E8E8E88D,#2a2a2a8D);fontFamily=Trebuchet MS,Verdana,Arial,sans-serif;fontColor=light-dark(#333333,#cccccc);exitX=0.26;exitY=1;entryX=0.5;entryY=0;" mermaidBaseValue="yes" id="m5">
          <mxCell edge="1" parent="1" source="m1" style="curved=1;startArrow=none;endArrow=block;endSize=7;strokeColor=light-dark(#333333,#cccccc);html=1;fontSize=16;labelBackgroundColor=light-dark(#E8E8E88D,#2a2a2a8D);fontFamily=Trebuchet MS,Verdana,Arial,sans-serif;fontColor=light-dark(#333333,#cccccc);exitX=0.26;exitY=1;entryX=0.5;entryY=0;" target="m2">
            <mxGeometry relative="1" as="geometry">
              <Array as="points">
                <mxPoint x="303.7" y="1346.95" />
              </Array>
            </mxGeometry>
          </mxCell>
        </UserObject>
        <UserObject label="no" mermaidId="e:C-&gt;H#0" mermaidBaseStyle="curved=1;startArrow=none;endArrow=block;endSize=7;strokeColor=light-dark(#333333,#cccccc);html=1;fontSize=16;labelBackgroundColor=light-dark(#E8E8E88D,#2a2a2a8D);fontFamily=Trebuchet MS,Verdana,Arial,sans-serif;fontColor=light-dark(#333333,#cccccc);exitX=0.74;exitY=1;entryX=0.5;entryY=0;" mermaidBaseValue="no" id="m6">
          <mxCell edge="1" parent="1" source="m1" style="curved=1;startArrow=none;endArrow=block;endSize=7;strokeColor=light-dark(#333333,#cccccc);html=1;fontSize=16;labelBackgroundColor=light-dark(#E8E8E88D,#2a2a2a8D);fontFamily=Trebuchet MS,Verdana,Arial,sans-serif;fontColor=light-dark(#333333,#cccccc);exitX=0.74;exitY=1;entryX=0.5;entryY=0;" target="m3">
            <mxGeometry relative="1" as="geometry">
              <Array as="points">
                <mxPoint x="830.7" y="1346.95" />
              </Array>
            </mxGeometry>
          </mxCell>
        </UserObject>
        <mxCell id="arch-caption" value="&lt;i&gt;A rollback runs autonomously only when a deployment correlates with the failure onset; otherwise the agent advises and escalates.&lt;/i&gt;" style="text;html=1;align=center;verticalAlign=middle;strokeColor=none;fillColor=none;fontSize=12;fontColor=#52606d;" vertex="1" parent="1">
          <mxGeometry x="65" y="1489.35" width="1470" height="32" as="geometry" />
        </mxCell>
        <mxCell id="s0" value="&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:15px&quot;&gt;&lt;b&gt;INTENT&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:13px;line-height:1.35&quot;&gt;Gate an incident-response agent&#39;s authority to execute a rollback on whether the failure is temporally correlated with a recent deployment, unlocking autonomous rollback only on a clear deploy-to-failure link and escalating otherwise.&lt;/div&gt;" style="rounded=1;whiteSpace=wrap;html=1;fillColor=#ffffff;strokeColor=#c5d0db;spacing=11;align=left;verticalAlign=top;" vertex="1" parent="1">
          <mxGeometry x="30" y="1564.35" width="755" height="111" as="geometry" />
        </mxCell>
        <mxCell id="s1" value="&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:15px&quot;&gt;&lt;b&gt;CONTEXT&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:13px;line-height:1.35&quot;&gt;An incident-response agent watches production and can act to mitigate, including rolling back to a previous release. Some incidents start right after a deployment; others have no deployment near them at all. Rolling back is itself a change — it can fix a bad release or, applied to an unrelated incident, make things worse or destroy good state.&lt;/div&gt;" style="rounded=1;whiteSpace=wrap;html=1;fillColor=#ffffff;strokeColor=#c5d0db;spacing=11;align=left;verticalAlign=top;" vertex="1" parent="1">
          <mxGeometry x="815" y="1564.35" width="755" height="130" as="geometry" />
        </mxCell>
        <mxCell id="s2" value="&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:15px&quot;&gt;&lt;b&gt;PROBLEM&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:13px;line-height:1.35&quot;&gt;Letting the agent roll back on any incident is unsafe, because a rollback aimed at a failure a deployment did not cause is a blind change that can compound the outage. Gating purely on the agent&#39;s confidence is weak, because a model can be confidently wrong about cause. What distinguishes a safe autonomous rollback from one that needs human judgement is whether a deployment actually precedes and plausibly caused the failure — a structural fact the agent can check rather than guess.&lt;/div&gt;" style="rounded=1;whiteSpace=wrap;html=1;fillColor=#ffffff;strokeColor=#c5d0db;spacing=11;align=left;verticalAlign=top;" vertex="1" parent="1">
          <mxGeometry x="30" y="1689.35" width="755" height="149" as="geometry" />
        </mxCell>
        <mxCell id="s3" value="&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:15px&quot;&gt;&lt;b&gt;FORCES&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:13px;line-height:1.35&quot;&gt;&amp;bull; A clear deploy-to-failure temporal link makes rollback a bounded, high-confidence remedy; without it, rollback is a guess at the cause.&lt;br&gt;&amp;bull; Autonomy speeds mitigation when the cause is a recent deploy, but the same autonomy is dangerous when the cause is unknown.&lt;br&gt;&amp;bull; Confidence scores conflate &#39;the model is sure&#39; with &#39;the cause is established&#39;, so the unlock criterion should be the structural correlation, not the score.&lt;br&gt;&amp;bull; Deployment events and failure onset must both be observable and time-aligned for the correlation to be computable.&lt;/div&gt;" style="rounded=1;whiteSpace=wrap;html=1;fillColor=#ffffff;strokeColor=#c5d0db;spacing=11;align=left;verticalAlign=top;" vertex="1" parent="1">
          <mxGeometry x="815" y="1708.35" width="755" height="206" as="geometry" />
        </mxCell>
        <mxCell id="s4" value="&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:15px&quot;&gt;&lt;b&gt;THEREFORE&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:13px;line-height:1.35&quot;&gt;Therefore: make the unlock criterion a structural deploy-to-failure correlation — a deployment shortly before the failure onset, scoped to the affected service — and let the agent roll back autonomously only when that link holds, escalating to a human when it does not.&lt;/div&gt;" style="rounded=1;whiteSpace=wrap;html=1;fillColor=#ffffff;strokeColor=#c5d0db;spacing=11;align=left;verticalAlign=top;" vertex="1" parent="1">
          <mxGeometry x="30" y="1852.35" width="755" height="111" as="geometry" />
        </mxCell>
        <mxCell id="s5" value="&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:15px&quot;&gt;&lt;b&gt;SOLUTION&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:13px;line-height:1.35&quot;&gt;Give the agent a rollback action but gate it on a deployment-correlation check rather than on its confidence. When an incident fires, the gate looks for a deployment to the affected service whose timing precedes and aligns with the failure onset. If a clear correlation holds, the agent may execute the rollback of that release within its policy bound, because the change to undo is identified. If no deployment correlates — a novel failure, a dependency outage, a traffic spike — the gate keeps the agent advisory: it can recommend and gather evidence, but the rollback decision goes to a human. The correlation is computed from deployment and telemetry events, so the unlock is a checkable fact, not the agent&#39;s belief about cause.&lt;/div&gt;" style="rounded=1;whiteSpace=wrap;html=1;fillColor=#ffffff;strokeColor=#c5d0db;spacing=11;align=left;verticalAlign=top;" vertex="1" parent="1">
          <mxGeometry x="815" y="1928.35" width="755" height="206" as="geometry" />
        </mxCell>
        <mxCell id="s6" value="&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:15px&quot;&gt;&lt;b&gt;STRUCTURE&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:13px;line-height:1.35&quot;&gt;&lt;font face=&quot;monospace&quot;&gt;Incident -&amp;gt; correlation check (deploy to affected service aligned with failure onset?) -&amp;gt; yes: autonomous rollback within policy / no: advise + escalate to human&lt;/font&gt;&lt;/div&gt;" style="rounded=1;whiteSpace=wrap;html=1;fillColor=#ffffff;strokeColor=#c5d0db;spacing=11;align=left;verticalAlign=top;" vertex="1" parent="1">
          <mxGeometry x="30" y="1977.35" width="755" height="111" as="geometry" />
        </mxCell>
        <mxCell id="s7" value="&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:15px&quot;&gt;&lt;b&gt;EXAMPLE SCENARIO&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:13px;line-height:1.35&quot;&gt;Error rates on the checkout service spike at 14:02. The agent&#39;s rollback gate finds a deploy to checkout at 14:00 whose timing lines up with the spike, so it rolls back that release automatically and the errors clear. An hour later latency climbs with no deploy anywhere near it; this time the gate withholds autonomy, and the agent pages a human with its evidence instead of rolling anything back.&lt;/div&gt;" style="rounded=1;whiteSpace=wrap;html=1;fillColor=#ffffff;strokeColor=#c5d0db;spacing=11;align=left;verticalAlign=top;" vertex="1" parent="1">
          <mxGeometry x="815" y="2148.35" width="755" height="130" as="geometry" />
        </mxCell>
        <mxCell id="s8" value="&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:15px&quot;&gt;&lt;b&gt;CONSEQUENCES&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:13px;line-height:1.35&quot;&gt;&lt;b&gt;Benefits&lt;/b&gt;&lt;br&gt;&lt;br&gt;&amp;bull; Deploy-caused incidents are mitigated fast because the agent rolls back the identified release without waiting for a human.&lt;br&gt;&amp;bull; Incidents with no deploy correlation do not trigger a blind rollback; they escalate instead, limiting blast radius.&lt;br&gt;&amp;bull; The unlock rests on an observable structural fact, which is auditable after the fact, unlike a confidence threshold.&lt;br&gt;&lt;br&gt;&lt;b&gt;Liabilities&lt;/b&gt;&lt;br&gt;&lt;br&gt;&amp;bull; A correlation can be coincidental — a deploy and an unrelated failure happen close in time — so a correlated rollback can still be wrong.&lt;br&gt;&amp;bull; Missing or mis-timestamped deployment events break the correlation and either block a valid rollback or hide a real one.&lt;br&gt;&amp;bull; A noisy pipeline that floods deployment events could manufacture correlations that unlock rollbacks.&lt;/div&gt;" style="rounded=1;whiteSpace=wrap;html=1;fillColor=#ffffff;strokeColor=#c5d0db;spacing=11;align=left;verticalAlign=top;" vertex="1" parent="1">
          <mxGeometry x="30" y="2102.35" width="755" height="377" as="geometry" />
        </mxCell>
        <mxCell id="s9" value="&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:15px&quot;&gt;&lt;b&gt;WHAT THIS PATTERN CONSTRAINS&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:13px;line-height:1.35&quot;&gt;The agent may not execute a rollback autonomously unless a deployment is correlated with the failure onset for the affected service; absent that link, the rollback decision must escalate to a human rather than proceed on the agent&#39;s confidence.&lt;/div&gt;" style="rounded=1;whiteSpace=wrap;html=1;fillColor=#ffffff;strokeColor=#c5d0db;spacing=11;align=left;verticalAlign=top;" vertex="1" parent="1">
          <mxGeometry x="815" y="2292.35" width="755" height="111" as="geometry" />
        </mxCell>
        <mxCell id="s10" value="&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:15px&quot;&gt;&lt;b&gt;USE WHEN&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:13px;line-height:1.35&quot;&gt;&amp;bull; An incident-response agent can execute rollbacks and both deployment and failure events are observable and timestamped.&lt;br&gt;&amp;bull; Many incidents are deploy-caused, so deploy correlation is a meaningful unlock signal.&lt;br&gt;&amp;bull; Fast autonomous mitigation is valuable for the deploy-caused cases while unknown-cause cases should stay human-judged.&lt;/div&gt;" style="rounded=1;whiteSpace=wrap;html=1;fillColor=#ffffff;strokeColor=#c5d0db;spacing=11;align=left;verticalAlign=top;" vertex="1" parent="1">
          <mxGeometry x="30" y="2493.35" width="755" height="149" as="geometry" />
        </mxCell>
        <mxCell id="s11" value="&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:15px&quot;&gt;&lt;b&gt;DO NOT USE WHEN&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:13px;line-height:1.35&quot;&gt;&amp;bull; Deployment events are not reliably captured, so the correlation cannot be computed.&lt;br&gt;&amp;bull; Rollback is never safe to automate in this environment regardless of cause.&lt;br&gt;&amp;bull; Failures are predominantly not deploy-related, so the gate would almost always escalate and add little.&lt;/div&gt;" style="rounded=1;whiteSpace=wrap;html=1;fillColor=#ffffff;strokeColor=#c5d0db;spacing=11;align=left;verticalAlign=top;" vertex="1" parent="1">
          <mxGeometry x="815" y="2417.35" width="755" height="130" as="geometry" />
        </mxCell>
        <mxCell id="s12" value="&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:15px&quot;&gt;&lt;b&gt;COMPONENTS&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:13px;line-height:1.35&quot;&gt;&amp;bull; Deployment event feed — timestamps of releases per service, the candidate causes to correlate against&lt;br&gt;&amp;bull; Failure-onset detector — identifies when and where the incident began from telemetry&lt;br&gt;&amp;bull; Correlation gate — tests whether a deployment to the affected service aligns in time with the failure onset&lt;br&gt;&amp;bull; Rollback executor — undoes the correlated release within a policy bound when the gate unlocks&lt;br&gt;&amp;bull; Escalation path — routes uncorrelated incidents to a human with the agent&#39;s evidence&lt;/div&gt;" style="rounded=1;whiteSpace=wrap;html=1;fillColor=#ffffff;strokeColor=#c5d0db;spacing=11;align=left;verticalAlign=top;" vertex="1" parent="1">
          <mxGeometry x="30" y="2656.35" width="755" height="187" as="geometry" />
        </mxCell>
        <mxCell id="s13" value="&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:15px&quot;&gt;&lt;b&gt;TOOLS&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:13px;line-height:1.35&quot;&gt;&amp;bull; Deployment or CD system — emits the release events the correlation is computed against&lt;br&gt;&amp;bull; Observability stack — supplies the failure-onset signal from errors, latency, and saturation&lt;br&gt;&amp;bull; Rollback tooling — performs the actual revert to a previous version&lt;/div&gt;" style="rounded=1;whiteSpace=wrap;html=1;fillColor=#ffffff;strokeColor=#c5d0db;spacing=11;align=left;verticalAlign=top;" vertex="1" parent="1">
          <mxGeometry x="815" y="2561.35" width="755" height="111" as="geometry" />
        </mxCell>
        <mxCell id="s14" value="&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:15px&quot;&gt;&lt;b&gt;FAILURE MODES&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:13px;line-height:1.35&quot;&gt;&amp;bull; Spurious correlation — an unrelated deploy near the failure unlocks a rollback that does not address the cause.&lt;br&gt;&amp;bull; Missed correlation — a real deploy-caused failure is not linked because deployment telemetry is absent, so mitigation stalls in escalation.&lt;br&gt;&amp;bull; Confidence substitution — the gate quietly falls back to the agent&#39;s confidence when correlation data is missing, defeating the criterion.&lt;br&gt;&amp;bull; Rollback of good state — the correlated release also carried needed data changes, so undoing it loses more than it fixes.&lt;/div&gt;" style="rounded=1;whiteSpace=wrap;html=1;fillColor=#ffffff;strokeColor=#c5d0db;spacing=11;align=left;verticalAlign=top;" vertex="1" parent="1">
          <mxGeometry x="30" y="2857.35" width="755" height="206" as="geometry" />
        </mxCell>
        <mxCell id="s15" value="&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:15px&quot;&gt;&lt;b&gt;EVALUATION METRICS&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:13px;line-height:1.35&quot;&gt;&amp;bull; Correlated-rollback precision — share of autonomous rollbacks that actually addressed the cause&lt;br&gt;&amp;bull; Time-to-mitigate on deploy-caused incidents versus a human-gated baseline&lt;br&gt;&amp;bull; Escalation rate — fraction of incidents withheld from autonomy for lack of correlation&lt;br&gt;&amp;bull; Spurious-rollback count — autonomous rollbacks later found to target an uncorrelated cause&lt;/div&gt;" style="rounded=1;whiteSpace=wrap;html=1;fillColor=#ffffff;strokeColor=#c5d0db;spacing=11;align=left;verticalAlign=top;" vertex="1" parent="1">
          <mxGeometry x="815" y="2686.35" width="755" height="130" as="geometry" />
        </mxCell>
        <mxCell id="s16" value="&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:15px&quot;&gt;&lt;b&gt;KNOWN USES&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:13px;line-height:1.35&quot;&gt;&amp;bull; &lt;b&gt;&lt;a href=&quot;https://www.augmentcode.com/guides/ai-sre-incident-management&quot;&gt;Augment Code AI SRE&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/b&gt; &lt;i&gt;available&lt;/i&gt; — States that rollback autonomy depends on deployment correlation: a clear deploy-to-failure link lets the agent execute a rollback with high confidence, while failures without it require human judgement.&lt;br&gt;&amp;bull; &lt;b&gt;&lt;a href=&quot;https://www.jeeva.ai/blog/24-7-autonomous-devops-ai-sre-agent-implementation-plan&quot;&gt;Autonomous SRE agent implementations&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/b&gt; &lt;i&gt;available&lt;/i&gt; — Agents that correlate a severe incident with a recent deploy automatically trigger a rollback to the previous stable version.&lt;/div&gt;" style="rounded=1;whiteSpace=wrap;html=1;fillColor=#ffffff;strokeColor=#c5d0db;spacing=11;align=left;verticalAlign=top;" vertex="1" parent="1">
          <mxGeometry x="30" y="3077.35" width="755" height="168" as="geometry" />
        </mxCell>
        <mxCell id="s17" value="&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:15px&quot;&gt;&lt;b&gt;RELATED PATTERNS&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:13px;line-height:1.35&quot;&gt;&amp;bull; &lt;i&gt;alternative-to&lt;/i&gt; &lt;b&gt;Calibrated Help-Gate via Conformal Prediction&lt;/b&gt; — The conformal gate unlocks autonomy on a calibrated confidence set; this gate unlocks on a structural deploy-to-failure correlation, not on how sure the model is.&lt;br&gt;&amp;bull; &lt;i&gt;complements&lt;/i&gt; &lt;b&gt;Compensating Action&lt;/b&gt; — The rollback is the compensator for a bad release; this pattern decides when the agent may fire that compensator autonomously versus escalate.&lt;br&gt;&amp;bull; &lt;i&gt;complements&lt;/i&gt; &lt;b&gt;Risk-Tiered Action Autonomy&lt;/b&gt; — Both graduate autonomy: risk-tiered by financial materiality, this by whether a deployment correlates with the failure.&lt;br&gt;&amp;bull; &lt;i&gt;complements&lt;/i&gt; &lt;b&gt;Human-in-the-Loop&lt;/b&gt; — When no deploy correlates with the failure, the gate falls back to human judgement rather than autonomous rollback.&lt;/div&gt;" style="rounded=1;whiteSpace=wrap;html=1;fillColor=#ffffff;strokeColor=#c5d0db;spacing=11;align=left;verticalAlign=top;" vertex="1" parent="1">
          <mxGeometry x="815" y="2830.35" width="755" height="225" as="geometry" />
        </mxCell>
        <mxCell id="s18" value="&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:15px&quot;&gt;&lt;b&gt;REFERENCES&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:13px;line-height:1.35&quot;&gt;&amp;bull; &lt;a href=&quot;https://www.augmentcode.com/guides/ai-sre-incident-management&quot;&gt;AI SRE in Incident Management: How AI Agents Handle On-Call&lt;/a&gt; — 2026&lt;br&gt;&amp;bull; &lt;a href=&quot;https://www.jeeva.ai/blog/24-7-autonomous-devops-ai-sre-agent-implementation-plan&quot;&gt;Autonomous SRE Agent: AI-Driven DevOps Implementation Guide&lt;/a&gt; — 2026&lt;br&gt;&amp;bull; &lt;a href=&quot;https://arxiv.org/abs/2604.13462&quot;&gt;Learning from Change: Predictive Models for Incident Prevention in a Regulated IT Environment&lt;/a&gt; — 2026&lt;/div&gt;" style="rounded=1;whiteSpace=wrap;html=1;fillColor=#ffffff;strokeColor=#c5d0db;spacing=11;align=left;verticalAlign=top;" vertex="1" parent="1">
          <mxGeometry x="30" y="3259.35" width="755" height="168" as="geometry" />
        </mxCell>
      </root>
    </mxGraphModel>
  </diagram>
