        </UserObject>
        <UserObject label="fail" mermaidId="e:Post-&gt;Reject2#0" mermaidBaseStyle="curved=1;startArrow=none;endArrow=block;endSize=7;strokeColor=light-dark(#333333,#cccccc);html=1;fontSize=16;labelBackgroundColor=light-dark(#E8E8E88D,#2a2a2a8D);fontFamily=Trebuchet MS,Verdana,Arial,sans-serif;fontColor=light-dark(#333333,#cccccc);exitX=0.69;exitY=1;entryX=0.5;entryY=0;" mermaidBaseValue="fail" id="m12">
          <mxCell edge="1" parent="1" source="m4" style="curved=1;startArrow=none;endArrow=block;endSize=7;strokeColor=light-dark(#333333,#cccccc);html=1;fontSize=16;labelBackgroundColor=light-dark(#E8E8E88D,#2a2a2a8D);fontFamily=Trebuchet MS,Verdana,Arial,sans-serif;fontColor=light-dark(#333333,#cccccc);exitX=0.69;exitY=1;entryX=0.5;entryY=0;" target="m6">
            <mxGeometry relative="1" as="geometry">
              <Array as="points">
                <mxPoint x="437.0" y="894.35" />
              </Array>
            </mxGeometry>
          </mxCell>
        </UserObject>
        <mxCell id="s0" value="&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:15px&quot;&gt;&lt;b&gt;INTENT&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:13px;line-height:1.35&quot;&gt;Bracket every LLM call with deterministic checks on both sides.&lt;/div&gt;" style="rounded=1;whiteSpace=wrap;html=1;fillColor=#ffffff;strokeColor=#c5d0db;spacing=11;align=left;verticalAlign=top;" vertex="1" parent="1">
          <mxGeometry x="30" y="1075.75" width="755" height="78" as="geometry" />
        </mxCell>
        <mxCell id="s1" value="&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:15px&quot;&gt;&lt;b&gt;CONTEXT&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:13px;line-height:1.35&quot;&gt;A team uses a large language model at a point in the system where wrong output causes real damage: a knitting pattern with a wrong stitch count that wastes a customer&#39;s yarn, a database migration that breaks production, an insurance quote that omits a required coverage line. The model is genuinely useful at this step (it talks to the user fluently, or it transforms messy input into a tidy form) so removing it entirely is not the right answer. But every output is one hallucination away from causing harm.&lt;/div&gt;" style="rounded=1;whiteSpace=wrap;html=1;fillColor=#ffffff;strokeColor=#c5d0db;spacing=11;align=left;verticalAlign=top;" vertex="1" parent="1">
          <mxGeometry x="815" y="1075.75" width="755" height="168" as="geometry" />
        </mxCell>
        <mxCell id="s2" value="&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:15px&quot;&gt;&lt;b&gt;PROBLEM&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:13px;line-height:1.35&quot;&gt;Trusting the model&#39;s output unconditionally accepts hallucination at exactly the moment where mistakes are most expensive, and there is no signal at the boundary distinguishing a correct generation from a confidently wrong one. Banning the model entirely loses everything it was good at and forces the team back to brittle templated text. Simple downstream validation (a try/catch on the database call, for example) catches some failures but only after side effects have begun or only by failing loudly to the user. The team needs a way to keep the model in the loop while bounding what kinds of output it can land.&lt;/div&gt;" style="rounded=1;whiteSpace=wrap;html=1;fillColor=#ffffff;strokeColor=#c5d0db;spacing=11;align=left;verticalAlign=top;" vertex="1" parent="1">
          <mxGeometry x="30" y="1167.75" width="755" height="187" as="geometry" />
        </mxCell>
        <mxCell id="s3" value="&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:15px&quot;&gt;&lt;b&gt;FORCES&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:13px;line-height:1.35&quot;&gt;&amp;bull; Bracketing adds latency per call.&lt;br&gt;&amp;bull; Pre-checks must be cheap to be worth running.&lt;br&gt;&amp;bull; Post-checks must catch what the model gets wrong, not what is merely surprising.&lt;/div&gt;" style="rounded=1;whiteSpace=wrap;html=1;fillColor=#ffffff;strokeColor=#c5d0db;spacing=11;align=left;verticalAlign=top;" vertex="1" parent="1">
          <mxGeometry x="815" y="1257.75" width="755" height="111" as="geometry" />
        </mxCell>
        <mxCell id="s4" value="&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:15px&quot;&gt;&lt;b&gt;THEREFORE&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:13px;line-height:1.35&quot;&gt;Therefore: wrap the LLM call in cheap deterministic gates on both sides and only accept its output if the post-check passes, so that probabilistic generation is contained inside a verifiable envelope.&lt;/div&gt;" style="rounded=1;whiteSpace=wrap;html=1;fillColor=#ffffff;strokeColor=#c5d0db;spacing=11;align=left;verticalAlign=top;" vertex="1" parent="1">
          <mxGeometry x="30" y="1368.75" width="755" height="92" as="geometry" />
        </mxCell>
        <mxCell id="s5" value="&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:15px&quot;&gt;&lt;b&gt;SOLUTION&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:13px;line-height:1.35&quot;&gt;Three layers. Pre: deterministic check decides whether the LLM should run at all (e.g. AST parse must succeed). LLM: produces a candidate output with structured-output schema and frozen rubric. Post: deterministic re-validation (parse, type-check, run tests). If post fails, the original is returned unchanged.&lt;/div&gt;" style="rounded=1;whiteSpace=wrap;html=1;fillColor=#ffffff;strokeColor=#c5d0db;spacing=11;align=left;verticalAlign=top;" vertex="1" parent="1">
          <mxGeometry x="815" y="1382.75" width="755" height="130" as="geometry" />
        </mxCell>
        <mxCell id="s6" value="&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:15px&quot;&gt;&lt;b&gt;STRUCTURE&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:13px;line-height:1.35&quot;&gt;&lt;font face=&quot;monospace&quot;&gt;Pre(input) -&amp;gt; {pass, fail} ; if pass: LLM(input) -&amp;gt; candidate ; Post(candidate) -&amp;gt; {accept, reject}.&lt;/font&gt;&lt;/div&gt;" style="rounded=1;whiteSpace=wrap;html=1;fillColor=#ffffff;strokeColor=#c5d0db;spacing=11;align=left;verticalAlign=top;" vertex="1" parent="1">
          <mxGeometry x="30" y="1474.75" width="755" height="92" as="geometry" />
        </mxCell>
        <mxCell id="s7" value="&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:15px&quot;&gt;&lt;b&gt;EXAMPLE SCENARIO&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:13px;line-height:1.35&quot;&gt;A regulated insurance assistant generates policy quotes that occasionally include a coverage line the customer never asked for. Trusting the LLM blindly is unacceptable; banning it loses the conversational explanation users like. The team adopts a Deterministic LLM Sandwich: a deterministic step parses the user&#39;s request into a typed schema, the LLM operates only within that schema, and a deterministic post-step validates the quote against rule-engine-checked coverage limits before it&#39;s shown. The LLM still talks like an LLM, but cannot smuggle a coverage line past the brackets.&lt;/div&gt;" style="rounded=1;whiteSpace=wrap;html=1;fillColor=#ffffff;strokeColor=#c5d0db;spacing=11;align=left;verticalAlign=top;" vertex="1" parent="1">
          <mxGeometry x="815" y="1526.75" width="755" height="168" as="geometry" />
        </mxCell>
        <mxCell id="s8" value="&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:15px&quot;&gt;&lt;b&gt;CONSEQUENCES&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:13px;line-height:1.35&quot;&gt;&lt;b&gt;Benefits&lt;/b&gt;&lt;br&gt;&lt;br&gt;&amp;bull; Confidence at the correctness boundary; the model cannot land an unsafe artefact.&lt;br&gt;&amp;bull; Bug fixes go into the deterministic layer where they are testable.&lt;br&gt;&lt;br&gt;&lt;b&gt;Liabilities&lt;/b&gt;&lt;br&gt;&lt;br&gt;&amp;bull; Building the deterministic checks is itself the bulk of the work.&lt;br&gt;&amp;bull; Over-strict post-checks reject valid outputs.&lt;/div&gt;" style="rounded=1;whiteSpace=wrap;html=1;fillColor=#ffffff;strokeColor=#c5d0db;spacing=11;align=left;verticalAlign=top;" vertex="1" parent="1">
          <mxGeometry x="30" y="1580.75" width="755" height="225" as="geometry" />
        </mxCell>
        <mxCell id="s9" value="&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:15px&quot;&gt;&lt;b&gt;WHAT THIS PATTERN CONSTRAINS&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:13px;line-height:1.35&quot;&gt;An LLM-produced artefact lands only after passing the post-check; otherwise the prior state is preserved.&lt;/div&gt;" style="rounded=1;whiteSpace=wrap;html=1;fillColor=#ffffff;strokeColor=#c5d0db;spacing=11;align=left;verticalAlign=top;" vertex="1" parent="1">
          <mxGeometry x="815" y="1708.75" width="755" height="92" as="geometry" />
        </mxCell>
        <mxCell id="s10" value="&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:15px&quot;&gt;&lt;b&gt;USE WHEN&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:13px;line-height:1.35&quot;&gt;&amp;bull; LLM output must be checked deterministically before being trusted (e.g. AST parse, type-check, test run).&lt;br&gt;&amp;bull; A pre-check can decide whether the LLM should run at all.&lt;br&gt;&amp;bull; Returning the original input on post-check failure is acceptable behaviour.&lt;/div&gt;" style="rounded=1;whiteSpace=wrap;html=1;fillColor=#ffffff;strokeColor=#c5d0db;spacing=11;align=left;verticalAlign=top;" vertex="1" parent="1">
          <mxGeometry x="30" y="1819.75" width="755" height="130" as="geometry" />
        </mxCell>
        <mxCell id="s11" value="&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:15px&quot;&gt;&lt;b&gt;DO NOT USE WHEN&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:13px;line-height:1.35&quot;&gt;&amp;bull; No deterministic check exists for the output type (free-form prose, subjective ranking).&lt;br&gt;&amp;bull; Pre and post checks together cost more than the LLM call they bracket.&lt;br&gt;&amp;bull; There is no fallback when the post check fails and silent rejection would mislead.&lt;/div&gt;" style="rounded=1;whiteSpace=wrap;html=1;fillColor=#ffffff;strokeColor=#c5d0db;spacing=11;align=left;verticalAlign=top;" vertex="1" parent="1">
          <mxGeometry x="815" y="1814.75" width="755" height="111" as="geometry" />
        </mxCell>
        <mxCell id="s12" value="&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:15px&quot;&gt;&lt;b&gt;COMPONENTS&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:13px;line-height:1.35&quot;&gt;&amp;bull; Pre-check — deterministic gate that decides whether the LLM should run at all&lt;br&gt;&amp;bull; LLM call — generator constrained by structured-output schema and a frozen rubric&lt;br&gt;&amp;bull; Post-check — deterministic re-validation (parse, type-check, run tests) against rules&lt;br&gt;&amp;bull; Fallback handler — returns the original input unchanged when the post-check fails&lt;br&gt;&amp;bull; Schema and rule library — codified constraints both checks read from&lt;/div&gt;" style="rounded=1;whiteSpace=wrap;html=1;fillColor=#ffffff;strokeColor=#c5d0db;spacing=11;align=left;verticalAlign=top;" vertex="1" parent="1">
          <mxGeometry x="30" y="1963.75" width="755" height="149" as="geometry" />
        </mxCell>
        <mxCell id="s13" value="&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:15px&quot;&gt;&lt;b&gt;TOOLS&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:13px;line-height:1.35&quot;&gt;&amp;bull; Parser or AST library — runs the deterministic shape check on input and output&lt;br&gt;&amp;bull; Type-checker or schema validator — enforces the structured-output contract&lt;br&gt;&amp;bull; Test runner or rule engine — executes the post-check against domain rules&lt;br&gt;&amp;bull; LLM API with structured output — generator step bracketed by the gates&lt;/div&gt;" style="rounded=1;whiteSpace=wrap;html=1;fillColor=#ffffff;strokeColor=#c5d0db;spacing=11;align=left;verticalAlign=top;" vertex="1" parent="1">
          <mxGeometry x="815" y="1939.75" width="755" height="130" as="geometry" />
        </mxCell>
        <mxCell id="s14" value="&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:15px&quot;&gt;&lt;b&gt;FAILURE MODES&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:13px;line-height:1.35&quot;&gt;&amp;bull; Over-strict post-check — the validator rejects valid outputs, the original is always returned, and the LLM step adds cost with no effect.&lt;br&gt;&amp;bull; Weak post-check — the check only parses the output, so a well-formed but wrong change passes and lands.&lt;br&gt;&amp;bull; Pre-check skipped — a caller invokes the LLM directly for speed, and inputs that should never reach the model do.&lt;br&gt;&amp;bull; Check debt — building the deterministic checks is put off, and the sandwich ships with only one side in place.&lt;/div&gt;" style="rounded=1;whiteSpace=wrap;html=1;fillColor=#ffffff;strokeColor=#c5d0db;spacing=11;align=left;verticalAlign=top;" vertex="1" parent="1">
          <mxGeometry x="30" y="2126.75" width="755" height="206" as="geometry" />
        </mxCell>
        <mxCell id="s15" value="&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:15px&quot;&gt;&lt;b&gt;EVALUATION METRICS&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:13px;line-height:1.35&quot;&gt;&amp;bull; Post-check rejection rate — fraction of LLM outputs blocked before they land&lt;br&gt;&amp;bull; Bracket-caught defect rate — wrong outputs caught by the post-check that the LLM did not flag&lt;br&gt;&amp;bull; Pre-check skip ratio — share of inputs that never reach the LLM and the latency saved&lt;br&gt;&amp;bull; Over-strict rejection rate — valid outputs wrongly blocked by the post-check&lt;br&gt;&amp;bull; End-to-end pass-through latency overhead — added time the two gates cost per call&lt;/div&gt;" style="rounded=1;whiteSpace=wrap;html=1;fillColor=#ffffff;strokeColor=#c5d0db;spacing=11;align=left;verticalAlign=top;" vertex="1" parent="1">
          <mxGeometry x="815" y="2083.75" width="755" height="149" as="geometry" />
        </mxCell>
        <mxCell id="s16" value="&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:15px&quot;&gt;&lt;b&gt;KNOWN USES&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:13px;line-height:1.35&quot;&gt;&amp;bull; &lt;b&gt;Knitting-DSL Pipeline (Stash2Go)&lt;/b&gt; &lt;i&gt;available&lt;/i&gt; — deterministicReview.js -&amp;gt; scopedLlmFixer.js -&amp;gt; parse and revalidate.&lt;/div&gt;" style="rounded=1;whiteSpace=wrap;html=1;fillColor=#ffffff;strokeColor=#c5d0db;spacing=11;align=left;verticalAlign=top;" vertex="1" parent="1">
          <mxGeometry x="30" y="2346.75" width="755" height="92" as="geometry" />
        </mxCell>
        <mxCell id="s17" value="&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:15px&quot;&gt;&lt;b&gt;RELATED PATTERNS&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:13px;line-height:1.35&quot;&gt;&amp;bull; &lt;i&gt;uses&lt;/i&gt; &lt;b&gt;Frozen Rubric Reflection&lt;/b&gt;&lt;br&gt;&amp;bull; &lt;i&gt;uses&lt;/i&gt; &lt;b&gt;Structured Output&lt;/b&gt;&lt;br&gt;&amp;bull; &lt;i&gt;composes-with&lt;/i&gt; &lt;b&gt;Code Execution&lt;/b&gt; — Post-check often runs code (parse/test) to validate output.&lt;br&gt;&amp;bull; &lt;i&gt;composes-with&lt;/i&gt; &lt;b&gt;Frozen Rubric Reflection&lt;/b&gt;&lt;br&gt;&amp;bull; &lt;i&gt;complements&lt;/i&gt; &lt;b&gt;LLM as Periphery&lt;/b&gt;&lt;br&gt;&amp;bull; &lt;i&gt;complements&lt;/i&gt; &lt;b&gt;Hybrid Symbolic-Neural Routing&lt;/b&gt;&lt;br&gt;&amp;bull; &lt;i&gt;alternative-to&lt;/i&gt; &lt;b&gt;Workflow-Success vs Business-Validity Gap&lt;/b&gt; — Bracketing the run with a deterministic post-check against business rules is the corrective; relying on the bare exit status is the failure it prevents.&lt;br&gt;&amp;bull; &lt;i&gt;complements&lt;/i&gt; &lt;b&gt;Verify-Before-Cite Resolution Gate&lt;/b&gt; — The gate is the post-generation deterministic slice of the sandwich, specialised to resolving citations against an authority registry.&lt;br&gt;&amp;bull; &lt;i&gt;complements&lt;/i&gt; &lt;b&gt;Behavior-Pinning Test Before Agent Edit&lt;/b&gt; — The sandwich brackets each runtime call with deterministic checks; here the same deterministic-versus-model partition is applied at authoring time so pinned values are exact and model prose is compared loosely.&lt;/div&gt;" style="rounded=1;whiteSpace=wrap;html=1;fillColor=#ffffff;strokeColor=#c5d0db;spacing=11;align=left;verticalAlign=top;" vertex="1" parent="1">
          <mxGeometry x="815" y="2246.75" width="755" height="320" as="geometry" />
        </mxCell>
        <mxCell id="s18" value="&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:15px&quot;&gt;&lt;b&gt;REFERENCES&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:13px;line-height:1.35&quot;&gt;&amp;bull; &lt;a href=&quot;https://www.guardrailsai.com/docs&quot;&gt;Guardrails AI — Input and Output Guards&lt;/a&gt; — Guardrails AI, 2024&lt;/div&gt;" style="rounded=1;whiteSpace=wrap;html=1;fillColor=#ffffff;strokeColor=#c5d0db;spacing=11;align=left;verticalAlign=top;" vertex="1" parent="1">
          <mxGeometry x="30" y="2452.75" width="755" height="78" as="geometry" />
        </mxCell>
      </root>
    </mxGraphModel>
  </diagram>
