        <mxCell id="arch-caption" value="&lt;i&gt;A fixed agent pool, but the router instantiates a different communication graph for each task.&lt;/i&gt;" style="text;html=1;align=center;verticalAlign=middle;strokeColor=none;fillColor=none;fontSize=12;fontColor=#52606d;" vertex="1" parent="1">
          <mxGeometry x="65" y="1025.9" width="1470" height="32" as="geometry" />
        </mxCell>
        <mxCell id="s0" value="&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:15px&quot;&gt;&lt;b&gt;INTENT&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:13px;line-height:1.35&quot;&gt;Form and dissolve the connections between agents at runtime by matching the task to candidate collaborators, instead of committing the multi-agent system to a fixed chain, star, or mesh up front.&lt;/div&gt;" style="rounded=1;whiteSpace=wrap;html=1;fillColor=#ffffff;strokeColor=#c5d0db;spacing=11;align=left;verticalAlign=top;" vertex="1" parent="1">
          <mxGeometry x="30" y="1100.9" width="755" height="92" as="geometry" />
        </mxCell>
        <mxCell id="s1" value="&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:15px&quot;&gt;&lt;b&gt;CONTEXT&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:13px;line-height:1.35&quot;&gt;A multi-agent system has a pool of specialised agents. The classic designs wire them into a fixed topology — a sequential chain, a star around an orchestrator, or a fully connected mesh — chosen before any task arrives. Different tasks, though, want different communication structures: some need a tight pipeline, others a wide fan-out, others a small debate among three peers. A topology that fits one task wastes messages or drops needed links on another.&lt;/div&gt;" style="rounded=1;whiteSpace=wrap;html=1;fillColor=#ffffff;strokeColor=#c5d0db;spacing=11;align=left;verticalAlign=top;" vertex="1" parent="1">
          <mxGeometry x="815" y="1100.9" width="755" height="149" as="geometry" />
        </mxCell>
        <mxCell id="s2" value="&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:15px&quot;&gt;&lt;b&gt;PROBLEM&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:13px;line-height:1.35&quot;&gt;A fixed inter-agent topology is a compromise across all tasks the system will ever see. A mesh pays quadratic message and token cost even when a chain would do; a chain serialises work that could fan out; a star bottlenecks on its hub. Hard-wiring the structure at design time forces every task through the same shape, so some tasks over-communicate and others lack the links they need.&lt;/div&gt;" style="rounded=1;whiteSpace=wrap;html=1;fillColor=#ffffff;strokeColor=#c5d0db;spacing=11;align=left;verticalAlign=top;" vertex="1" parent="1">
          <mxGeometry x="30" y="1206.9" width="755" height="130" as="geometry" />
        </mxCell>
        <mxCell id="s3" value="&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:15px&quot;&gt;&lt;b&gt;FORCES&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:13px;line-height:1.35&quot;&gt;&amp;bull; Different tasks want different communication shapes; one fixed topology fits none of them well.&lt;br&gt;&amp;bull; Denser topologies raise coordination quality but cost messages and tokens quadratically.&lt;br&gt;&amp;bull; Rewiring at runtime adds a routing decision that itself can be wrong or slow.&lt;br&gt;&amp;bull; The agent pool and their competencies are known; which links matter is task-specific.&lt;br&gt;&amp;bull; An adaptive graph is harder to reason about and debug than a static one.&lt;/div&gt;" style="rounded=1;whiteSpace=wrap;html=1;fillColor=#ffffff;strokeColor=#c5d0db;spacing=11;align=left;verticalAlign=top;" vertex="1" parent="1">
          <mxGeometry x="815" y="1263.9" width="755" height="149" as="geometry" />
        </mxCell>
        <mxCell id="s4" value="&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:15px&quot;&gt;&lt;b&gt;THEREFORE&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:13px;line-height:1.35&quot;&gt;Therefore: keep the agent pool fixed but choose the edges per task at runtime, matching each subtask to the agents best placed to handle it and connecting only those, so the topology fits the work rather than the other way round.&lt;/div&gt;" style="rounded=1;whiteSpace=wrap;html=1;fillColor=#ffffff;strokeColor=#c5d0db;spacing=11;align=left;verticalAlign=top;" vertex="1" parent="1">
          <mxGeometry x="30" y="1350.9" width="755" height="111" as="geometry" />
        </mxCell>
        <mxCell id="s5" value="&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:15px&quot;&gt;&lt;b&gt;SOLUTION&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:13px;line-height:1.35&quot;&gt;Separate the agent pool from the communication graph over it. For each task (or each step), a routing layer scores candidate agents against the current subtask — by capability description, embedding similarity, or a learned router — and instantiates only the edges needed: a chain when the work is sequential, a fan-out when it is parallel, a small clique when it needs debate. As the task evolves, edges are added and dropped. Approaches range from per-step semantic matching (DyTopo) to treating the whole topology as an optimisable graph trained end to end (GPTSwarm). The static chain, star, and mesh become special cases the router can choose, not the only option.&lt;/div&gt;" style="rounded=1;whiteSpace=wrap;html=1;fillColor=#ffffff;strokeColor=#c5d0db;spacing=11;align=left;verticalAlign=top;" vertex="1" parent="1">
          <mxGeometry x="815" y="1426.9" width="755" height="187" as="geometry" />
        </mxCell>
        <mxCell id="s6" value="&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:15px&quot;&gt;&lt;b&gt;STRUCTURE&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:13px;line-height:1.35&quot;&gt;&lt;font face=&quot;monospace&quot;&gt;Agent pool (fixed) + Router (per-task/per-step) -&amp;gt; scores agents vs subtask -&amp;gt; instantiates edges (chain | fan-out | clique) -&amp;gt; agents communicate over the chosen graph -&amp;gt; edges revised as task evolves.&lt;/font&gt;&lt;/div&gt;" style="rounded=1;whiteSpace=wrap;html=1;fillColor=#ffffff;strokeColor=#c5d0db;spacing=11;align=left;verticalAlign=top;" vertex="1" parent="1">
          <mxGeometry x="30" y="1475.9" width="755" height="111" as="geometry" />
        </mxCell>
        <mxCell id="s7" value="&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:15px&quot;&gt;&lt;b&gt;EXAMPLE SCENARIO&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:13px;line-height:1.35&quot;&gt;A research platform keeps a pool of a dozen specialist agents — retrieval, code, math, critique, writing. For a simple lookup it wires a two-agent chain; for a contested claim it stands up a three-agent debate clique; for a survey it fans out to five retrievers and a synthesiser. The same pool serves all three because a router reads each task and instantiates only the edges that task needs, instead of forcing everything through one standing mesh.&lt;/div&gt;" style="rounded=1;whiteSpace=wrap;html=1;fillColor=#ffffff;strokeColor=#c5d0db;spacing=11;align=left;verticalAlign=top;" vertex="1" parent="1">
          <mxGeometry x="815" y="1627.9" width="755" height="149" as="geometry" />
        </mxCell>
        <mxCell id="s8" value="&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:15px&quot;&gt;&lt;b&gt;CONSEQUENCES&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:13px;line-height:1.35&quot;&gt;&lt;b&gt;Benefits&lt;/b&gt;&lt;br&gt;&lt;br&gt;&amp;bull; Communication cost tracks the task instead of the worst case.&lt;br&gt;&amp;bull; Each subtask reaches the agents actually suited to it.&lt;br&gt;&amp;bull; Static chain, star, and mesh remain available as router choices.&lt;br&gt;&amp;bull; An optimisable graph can be tuned for accuracy or cost over a workload.&lt;br&gt;&lt;br&gt;&lt;b&gt;Liabilities&lt;/b&gt;&lt;br&gt;&lt;br&gt;&amp;bull; The router is a new failure point: a bad routing decision wires the wrong agents.&lt;br&gt;&amp;bull; Runtime rewiring adds latency and decision cost to every task.&lt;br&gt;&amp;bull; A graph that changes shape is harder to trace and reproduce than a fixed one.&lt;br&gt;&amp;bull; Learned topologies need training data and can overfit a benchmark.&lt;br&gt;&amp;bull; Pathological routing can oscillate or rebuild edges every step.&lt;/div&gt;" style="rounded=1;whiteSpace=wrap;html=1;fillColor=#ffffff;strokeColor=#c5d0db;spacing=11;align=left;verticalAlign=top;" vertex="1" parent="1">
          <mxGeometry x="30" y="1600.9" width="755" height="320" as="geometry" />
        </mxCell>
        <mxCell id="s9" value="&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:15px&quot;&gt;&lt;b&gt;WHAT THIS PATTERN CONSTRAINS&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:13px;line-height:1.35&quot;&gt;Agents must not assume a fixed set of peers or a standing communication structure; who they talk to is decided by the routing layer per task and may change between steps. An agent must not open links the router has not granted for the current subtask.&lt;/div&gt;" style="rounded=1;whiteSpace=wrap;html=1;fillColor=#ffffff;strokeColor=#c5d0db;spacing=11;align=left;verticalAlign=top;" vertex="1" parent="1">
          <mxGeometry x="815" y="1790.9" width="755" height="111" as="geometry" />
        </mxCell>
        <mxCell id="s10" value="&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:15px&quot;&gt;&lt;b&gt;USE WHEN&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:13px;line-height:1.35&quot;&gt;&amp;bull; The system serves tasks that want genuinely different communication shapes.&lt;br&gt;&amp;bull; A fixed mesh is too costly and a fixed chain too rigid for the workload.&lt;br&gt;&amp;bull; Agents carry clear, machine-comparable capability descriptions to route against.&lt;br&gt;&amp;bull; You can afford a routing decision per task or per step.&lt;/div&gt;" style="rounded=1;whiteSpace=wrap;html=1;fillColor=#ffffff;strokeColor=#c5d0db;spacing=11;align=left;verticalAlign=top;" vertex="1" parent="1">
          <mxGeometry x="30" y="1934.9" width="755" height="130" as="geometry" />
        </mxCell>
        <mxCell id="s11" value="&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:15px&quot;&gt;&lt;b&gt;DO NOT USE WHEN&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:13px;line-height:1.35&quot;&gt;&amp;bull; A single fixed topology already fits every task the system sees.&lt;br&gt;&amp;bull; The agent pool is two or three agents where any structure is cheap.&lt;br&gt;&amp;bull; Reproducibility and easy tracing outweigh adaptivity.&lt;br&gt;&amp;bull; There is no signal to route on and the router would guess blindly.&lt;/div&gt;" style="rounded=1;whiteSpace=wrap;html=1;fillColor=#ffffff;strokeColor=#c5d0db;spacing=11;align=left;verticalAlign=top;" vertex="1" parent="1">
          <mxGeometry x="815" y="1915.9" width="755" height="130" as="geometry" />
        </mxCell>
        <mxCell id="s12" value="&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:15px&quot;&gt;&lt;b&gt;COMPONENTS&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:13px;line-height:1.35&quot;&gt;&amp;bull; Agent pool — the fixed set of specialised agents the router draws edges over&lt;br&gt;&amp;bull; Router — scores candidate agents against the current subtask and selects collaborators&lt;br&gt;&amp;bull; Topology instantiator — materialises the chosen edges (chain, fan-out, clique) for the task&lt;br&gt;&amp;bull; Capability index — descriptions or embeddings the router matches subtasks against&lt;br&gt;&amp;bull; Edge-revision loop — adds and drops links as the task progresses&lt;/div&gt;" style="rounded=1;whiteSpace=wrap;html=1;fillColor=#ffffff;strokeColor=#c5d0db;spacing=11;align=left;verticalAlign=top;" vertex="1" parent="1">
          <mxGeometry x="30" y="2078.9" width="755" height="149" as="geometry" />
        </mxCell>
        <mxCell id="s13" value="&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:15px&quot;&gt;&lt;b&gt;TOOLS&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:13px;line-height:1.35&quot;&gt;&amp;bull; Embedding model — scores subtask-to-agent similarity for semantic routing&lt;br&gt;&amp;bull; Graph runtime — represents and executes the per-task agent graph, e.g. a LangGraph-style engine&lt;br&gt;&amp;bull; Optimiser — trains the topology end to end when the graph is treated as optimisable (GPTSwarm)&lt;/div&gt;" style="rounded=1;whiteSpace=wrap;html=1;fillColor=#ffffff;strokeColor=#c5d0db;spacing=11;align=left;verticalAlign=top;" vertex="1" parent="1">
          <mxGeometry x="815" y="2059.9" width="755" height="111" as="geometry" />
        </mxCell>
        <mxCell id="s14" value="&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:15px&quot;&gt;&lt;b&gt;FAILURE MODES&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:13px;line-height:1.35&quot;&gt;&amp;bull; Wrong wiring — the router scores agents badly for a subtask and connects agents that cannot help each other.&lt;br&gt;&amp;bull; Edge thrash — the router rebuilds the communication graph every step, and time goes to rewiring instead of work.&lt;br&gt;&amp;bull; Unreproducible run — the same task produces a different graph next time, so a failure cannot be replayed.&lt;br&gt;&amp;bull; Benchmark overfit — a learned topology looks good on its training tasks and wires real tasks poorly.&lt;/div&gt;" style="rounded=1;whiteSpace=wrap;html=1;fillColor=#ffffff;strokeColor=#c5d0db;spacing=11;align=left;verticalAlign=top;" vertex="1" parent="1">
          <mxGeometry x="30" y="2241.9" width="755" height="206" as="geometry" />
        </mxCell>
        <mxCell id="s15" value="&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:15px&quot;&gt;&lt;b&gt;EVALUATION METRICS&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:13px;line-height:1.35&quot;&gt;&amp;bull; Task accuracy vs topology cost — quality per message/token compared with a fixed mesh or chain&lt;br&gt;&amp;bull; Routing precision — fraction of subtasks wired to a competent agent&lt;br&gt;&amp;bull; Messages per task — communication volume relative to the static baseline&lt;br&gt;&amp;bull; Rewiring stability — how often the graph churns without improving the outcome&lt;/div&gt;" style="rounded=1;whiteSpace=wrap;html=1;fillColor=#ffffff;strokeColor=#c5d0db;spacing=11;align=left;verticalAlign=top;" vertex="1" parent="1">
          <mxGeometry x="815" y="2184.9" width="755" height="130" as="geometry" />
        </mxCell>
        <mxCell id="s16" value="&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:15px&quot;&gt;&lt;b&gt;KNOWN USES&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:13px;line-height:1.35&quot;&gt;&amp;bull; &lt;b&gt;&lt;a href=&quot;https://github.com/metauto-ai/GPTSwarm&quot;&gt;GPTSwarm&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/b&gt; &lt;i&gt;available&lt;/i&gt; — Represents a multi-agent system as an optimisable graph and learns the edges/topology end to end, rather than fixing them by hand.&lt;br&gt;&amp;bull; &lt;b&gt;&lt;a href=&quot;https://arxiv.org/abs/2402.16823&quot;&gt;GPTSwarm (Zhuge et al., arXiv:2402.16823)&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/b&gt; &lt;i&gt;available&lt;/i&gt; — Language Agents as Optimizable Graphs: formalises agents and their connections as a graph that can be optimised for a task.&lt;br&gt;&amp;bull; &lt;b&gt;&lt;a href=&quot;https://github.com/VoltAgent/awesome-ai-agent-papers&quot;&gt;VoltAgent awesome-ai-agent-papers (DyTopo)&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/b&gt; &lt;i&gt;available&lt;/i&gt; — Catalogues Dynamic Topology Routing, which rewires agent connections at runtime via semantic matching.&lt;/div&gt;" style="rounded=1;whiteSpace=wrap;html=1;fillColor=#ffffff;strokeColor=#c5d0db;spacing=11;align=left;verticalAlign=top;" vertex="1" parent="1">
          <mxGeometry x="30" y="2461.9" width="755" height="206" as="geometry" />
        </mxCell>
        <mxCell id="s17" value="&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:15px&quot;&gt;&lt;b&gt;RELATED PATTERNS&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:13px;line-height:1.35&quot;&gt;&amp;bull; &lt;i&gt;alternative-to&lt;/i&gt; &lt;b&gt;Orchestrator-Workers&lt;/b&gt; — Orchestrator-workers fixes a star around a hub; dynamic topology routing chooses the shape per task, of which the star is one option.&lt;br&gt;&amp;bull; &lt;i&gt;complements&lt;/i&gt; &lt;b&gt;Decentralized Swarm Handoff&lt;/b&gt; — Swarm handoff transfers control along existing peer links; topology routing decides which links exist in the first place.&lt;br&gt;&amp;bull; &lt;i&gt;complements&lt;/i&gt; &lt;b&gt;Contract Net Protocol&lt;/b&gt; — Contract-net bids allocate a task to an agent; topology routing wires the resulting collaborators into a task-specific graph.&lt;br&gt;&amp;bull; &lt;i&gt;alternative-to&lt;/i&gt; &lt;b&gt;Behavior-Space Architecture&lt;/b&gt; — Topology routing rewires links between agents inside an all-multi-agent system; behavior-space routing selects which kinds of subsystem participate at all, and may activate zero agents.&lt;/div&gt;" style="rounded=1;whiteSpace=wrap;html=1;fillColor=#ffffff;strokeColor=#c5d0db;spacing=11;align=left;verticalAlign=top;" vertex="1" parent="1">
          <mxGeometry x="815" y="2328.9" width="755" height="225" as="geometry" />
        </mxCell>
        <mxCell id="s18" value="&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:15px&quot;&gt;&lt;b&gt;REFERENCES&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:13px;line-height:1.35&quot;&gt;&amp;bull; &lt;a href=&quot;https://arxiv.org/abs/2402.16823&quot;&gt;GPTSwarm: Language Agents as Optimizable Graphs&lt;/a&gt; — Mingchen Zhuge et al., 2024&lt;br&gt;&amp;bull; &lt;a href=&quot;https://github.com/VoltAgent/awesome-ai-agent-papers&quot;&gt;VoltAgent/awesome-ai-agent-papers (2026 agent papers, incl. DyTopo)&lt;/a&gt; — VoltAgent, 2026&lt;/div&gt;" style="rounded=1;whiteSpace=wrap;html=1;fillColor=#ffffff;strokeColor=#c5d0db;spacing=11;align=left;verticalAlign=top;" vertex="1" parent="1">
          <mxGeometry x="30" y="2681.9" width="755" height="130" as="geometry" />
        </mxCell>
      </root>
    </mxGraphModel>
  </diagram>
