        <UserObject label="" mermaidId="e:Exec-&gt;Out#0" mermaidBaseStyle="curved=1;startArrow=none;endArrow=block;endSize=7;strokeColor=light-dark(#333333,#cccccc);exitX=0.5;exitY=1;entryX=1;entryY=0.33;" mermaidBaseValue="" id="m17">
          <mxCell edge="1" parent="1" source="m6" style="curved=1;startArrow=none;endArrow=block;endSize=7;strokeColor=light-dark(#333333,#cccccc);exitX=0.5;exitY=1;entryX=1;entryY=0.33;" target="m7">
            <mxGeometry relative="1" as="geometry">
              <Array as="points">
                <mxPoint x="1346.72" y="924.87" />
              </Array>
            </mxGeometry>
          </mxCell>
        </UserObject>
        <mxCell id="s0" value="&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:15px&quot;&gt;&lt;b&gt;INTENT&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:13px;line-height:1.35&quot;&gt;Per query, route between a symbolic path (rule engine, knowledge graph) and a neural path (LLM), using the LLM for interpretation and the symbolic layer for exact constraints.&lt;/div&gt;" style="rounded=1;whiteSpace=wrap;html=1;fillColor=#ffffff;strokeColor=#c5d0db;spacing=11;align=left;verticalAlign=top;" vertex="1" parent="1">
          <mxGeometry x="30" y="1080.05" width="755" height="92" as="geometry" />
        </mxCell>
        <mxCell id="s1" value="&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:15px&quot;&gt;&lt;b&gt;CONTEXT&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:13px;line-height:1.35&quot;&gt;An agent serves a mixed workload: some queries are inherently logical (tax rules, dosage limits, schema validation, eligibility checks) where a wrong answer is unacceptable; other queries are inherently interpretive (free-text intent, summarization, ranking) where exact rules do not exist. Sending everything to the LLM costs accuracy on the logical queries; sending everything to a rule engine is impossible for the interpretive ones.&lt;/div&gt;" style="rounded=1;whiteSpace=wrap;html=1;fillColor=#ffffff;strokeColor=#c5d0db;spacing=11;align=left;verticalAlign=top;" vertex="1" parent="1">
          <mxGeometry x="815" y="1080.05" width="755" height="149" as="geometry" />
        </mxCell>
        <mxCell id="s2" value="&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:15px&quot;&gt;&lt;b&gt;PROBLEM&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:13px;line-height:1.35&quot;&gt;LLMs are bad at exact constraint satisfaction at scale — they confabulate edge cases, lose track of conjunctions, and silently round numbers. Rule engines are bad at interpretation — they cannot handle free text. Yet most real workloads need both. A single path forces one of two losses: confabulated rule violations from the LLM path, or brittle template-only coverage from the symbolic path. Recent practitioner write-ups (Japanese Qiita, Anthropic-style architecture posts) and the Nov 2025 arXiv preprint &#39;Bridging Symbolic Control and Neural Reasoning in LLM Agents&#39; converge on per-query routing as the resolution: estimate complexity, decide where the query belongs, and only blend the two when neither pure path suffices.&lt;/div&gt;" style="rounded=1;whiteSpace=wrap;html=1;fillColor=#ffffff;strokeColor=#c5d0db;spacing=11;align=left;verticalAlign=top;" vertex="1" parent="1">
          <mxGeometry x="30" y="1186.05" width="755" height="206" as="geometry" />
        </mxCell>
        <mxCell id="s3" value="&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:15px&quot;&gt;&lt;b&gt;FORCES&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:13px;line-height:1.35&quot;&gt;&amp;bull; Hard rules need verifiable execution; LLMs cannot give that guarantee without external enforcement.&lt;br&gt;&amp;bull; Interpretive queries need free-text understanding; rule engines cannot give that.&lt;br&gt;&amp;bull; Per-query routing is itself a model — a bad router collapses to either pure-LLM or pure-symbolic.&lt;br&gt;&amp;bull; Maintaining two stacks (LLM + symbolic) doubles the surface for drift; the routing decision is also the boundary that has to be kept current.&lt;/div&gt;" style="rounded=1;whiteSpace=wrap;html=1;fillColor=#ffffff;strokeColor=#c5d0db;spacing=11;align=left;verticalAlign=top;" vertex="1" parent="1">
          <mxGeometry x="815" y="1243.05" width="755" height="168" as="geometry" />
        </mxCell>
        <mxCell id="s4" value="&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:15px&quot;&gt;&lt;b&gt;THEREFORE&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:13px;line-height:1.35&quot;&gt;Therefore: introduce an explicit router that classifies each query as symbolic, neural, or hybrid, runs it through the matching path, and is itself tested with both rule-satisfaction and interpretation benchmarks so the boundary does not silently move.&lt;/div&gt;" style="rounded=1;whiteSpace=wrap;html=1;fillColor=#ffffff;strokeColor=#c5d0db;spacing=11;align=left;verticalAlign=top;" vertex="1" parent="1">
          <mxGeometry x="30" y="1406.05" width="755" height="111" as="geometry" />
        </mxCell>
        <mxCell id="s5" value="&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:15px&quot;&gt;&lt;b&gt;SOLUTION&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:13px;line-height:1.35&quot;&gt;Build three first-class components: (a) a symbolic path holding the rules, ontologies, and constraint solvers; (b) a neural path holding the LLM with retrieval, tools, and synthesis; (c) a router that estimates per-query complexity and resource needs and dispatches. For genuinely hybrid queries, the LLM proposes a plan that the symbolic layer validates and executes — the LLM never asserts the answer alone. Track router accuracy as a first-class metric; treat boundary drift as a regression.&lt;/div&gt;" style="rounded=1;whiteSpace=wrap;html=1;fillColor=#ffffff;strokeColor=#c5d0db;spacing=11;align=left;verticalAlign=top;" vertex="1" parent="1">
          <mxGeometry x="815" y="1425.05" width="755" height="149" as="geometry" />
        </mxCell>
        <mxCell id="s6" value="&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:15px&quot;&gt;&lt;b&gt;STRUCTURE&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:13px;line-height:1.35&quot;&gt;&lt;font face=&quot;monospace&quot;&gt;Query --&amp;gt; Router (estimate kind and complexity) --exact rule--&amp;gt; Symbolic path (KG / rules / solver) --&amp;gt; Answer | --interpretive--&amp;gt; Neural path (LLM + retrieval + tools) --&amp;gt; Answer | --hybrid--&amp;gt; LLM proposes plan --&amp;gt; Symbolic validator --ok--&amp;gt; Execute --&amp;gt; Answer; --reject--&amp;gt; LLM revises&lt;/font&gt;&lt;/div&gt;" style="rounded=1;whiteSpace=wrap;html=1;fillColor=#ffffff;strokeColor=#c5d0db;spacing=11;align=left;verticalAlign=top;" vertex="1" parent="1">
          <mxGeometry x="30" y="1531.05" width="755" height="130" as="geometry" />
        </mxCell>
        <mxCell id="s7" value="&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:15px&quot;&gt;&lt;b&gt;EXAMPLE SCENARIO&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:13px;line-height:1.35&quot;&gt;A medication-recommendation assistant takes free-text clinician queries. The router classifies each query: &#39;is amoxicillin contraindicated with X?&#39; → symbolic path (drug-interaction graph, deterministic answer). &#39;Summarize this patient&#39;s last three visits&#39; → neural path (LLM with retrieval). &#39;Patient is allergic to penicillin and on warfarin — what should I avoid?&#39; → hybrid: LLM proposes candidate drugs, symbolic layer validates each against allergy + interaction rules and returns the filtered set. Router accuracy is tracked weekly; a regression on hard-rule queries triggers a rule-base refresh.&lt;/div&gt;" style="rounded=1;whiteSpace=wrap;html=1;fillColor=#ffffff;strokeColor=#c5d0db;spacing=11;align=left;verticalAlign=top;" vertex="1" parent="1">
          <mxGeometry x="815" y="1588.05" width="755" height="168" as="geometry" />
        </mxCell>
        <mxCell id="s8" value="&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:15px&quot;&gt;&lt;b&gt;CONSEQUENCES&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:13px;line-height:1.35&quot;&gt;&lt;b&gt;Benefits&lt;/b&gt;&lt;br&gt;&lt;br&gt;&amp;bull; Hard constraints stay verifiable: violations are caught by the symbolic layer regardless of LLM phrasing.&lt;br&gt;&amp;bull; Free-text and ambiguous inputs still flow; the LLM is not removed, just contained.&lt;br&gt;&amp;bull; Cost can drop because the symbolic path is dramatically cheaper than an LLM call for queries that fit it.&lt;br&gt;&amp;bull; Failure modes become legible: a wrong answer is either a symbolic-rule miss or an LLM confabulation, not &#39;something happened&#39;.&lt;br&gt;&lt;br&gt;&lt;b&gt;Liabilities&lt;/b&gt;&lt;br&gt;&lt;br&gt;&amp;bull; Router accuracy becomes a load-bearing component; misrouting either confabulates rules or fails interpretation.&lt;br&gt;&amp;bull; Two stacks must be kept in sync; rule changes and prompt/tool changes both move the boundary.&lt;br&gt;&amp;bull; Hybrid queries (LLM-proposes, symbolic-validates) introduce latency and a new failure mode — the LLM proposing plans the symbolic layer cannot represent.&lt;/div&gt;" style="rounded=1;whiteSpace=wrap;html=1;fillColor=#ffffff;strokeColor=#c5d0db;spacing=11;align=left;verticalAlign=top;" vertex="1" parent="1">
          <mxGeometry x="30" y="1675.05" width="755" height="377" as="geometry" />
        </mxCell>
        <mxCell id="s9" value="&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:15px&quot;&gt;&lt;b&gt;WHAT THIS PATTERN CONSTRAINS&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:13px;line-height:1.35&quot;&gt;Forbids the LLM from asserting outputs that fall under the symbolic path&#39;s jurisdiction without symbolic validation. The router and symbolic layer together restrict the LLM&#39;s freedom to ungoverned interpretive and synthesis tasks.&lt;/div&gt;" style="rounded=1;whiteSpace=wrap;html=1;fillColor=#ffffff;strokeColor=#c5d0db;spacing=11;align=left;verticalAlign=top;" vertex="1" parent="1">
          <mxGeometry x="815" y="1770.05" width="755" height="111" as="geometry" />
        </mxCell>
        <mxCell id="s10" value="&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:15px&quot;&gt;&lt;b&gt;USE WHEN&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:13px;line-height:1.35&quot;&gt;&amp;bull; Workloads that mix hard-rule queries (tax, dosage, eligibility, schema) with free-text/interpretive queries.&lt;br&gt;&amp;bull; Domains where a single wrong rule-application is unacceptable and the rules can be represented symbolically.&lt;br&gt;&amp;bull; Cost regimes where the symbolic path is materially cheaper per query than the LLM path.&lt;br&gt;&amp;bull; Settings where a knowledge graph or rule base already exists and is maintained.&lt;/div&gt;" style="rounded=1;whiteSpace=wrap;html=1;fillColor=#ffffff;strokeColor=#c5d0db;spacing=11;align=left;verticalAlign=top;" vertex="1" parent="1">
          <mxGeometry x="30" y="2066.05" width="755" height="168" as="geometry" />
        </mxCell>
        <mxCell id="s11" value="&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:15px&quot;&gt;&lt;b&gt;DO NOT USE WHEN&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:13px;line-height:1.35&quot;&gt;&amp;bull; Pure interpretive workloads (summarization, chat) where no symbolic representation of the rules exists.&lt;br&gt;&amp;bull; Workloads where the rules change too fast for the symbolic representation to keep up.&lt;br&gt;&amp;bull; Small deployments where maintaining two stacks costs more than the accuracy gain.&lt;br&gt;&amp;bull; Settings where router-quality cannot be measured — the routing decision becomes silent risk.&lt;/div&gt;" style="rounded=1;whiteSpace=wrap;html=1;fillColor=#ffffff;strokeColor=#c5d0db;spacing=11;align=left;verticalAlign=top;" vertex="1" parent="1">
          <mxGeometry x="815" y="1895.05" width="755" height="149" as="geometry" />
        </mxCell>
        <mxCell id="s12" value="&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:15px&quot;&gt;&lt;b&gt;COMPONENTS&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:13px;line-height:1.35&quot;&gt;&amp;bull; Router — per-query classifier that picks symbolic / neural / hybrid path&lt;br&gt;&amp;bull; Symbolic path — rule engine, knowledge graph, constraint solver, ontology&lt;br&gt;&amp;bull; Neural path — LLM with retrieval, tools, synthesis&lt;br&gt;&amp;bull; Validator — symbolic check on LLM-proposed plans in the hybrid case&lt;br&gt;&amp;bull; Boundary monitor — flags drift between what the router sends symbolic vs what the symbolic layer can actually handle&lt;/div&gt;" style="rounded=1;whiteSpace=wrap;html=1;fillColor=#ffffff;strokeColor=#c5d0db;spacing=11;align=left;verticalAlign=top;" vertex="1" parent="1">
          <mxGeometry x="30" y="2248.05" width="755" height="168" as="geometry" />
        </mxCell>
        <mxCell id="s13" value="&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:15px&quot;&gt;&lt;b&gt;TOOLS&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:13px;line-height:1.35&quot;&gt;&amp;bull; Rule engine — Prolog, Answer Set Programming, Drools, custom DSL&lt;br&gt;&amp;bull; Knowledge graph store — Neo4j, RDF triple store, in-memory graph&lt;br&gt;&amp;bull; LLM with tool-use — the neural path&lt;br&gt;&amp;bull; Router classifier — small model or heuristic that scores per-query&lt;br&gt;&amp;bull; Router-accuracy harness — held-out labelled queries used to track router drift&lt;/div&gt;" style="rounded=1;whiteSpace=wrap;html=1;fillColor=#ffffff;strokeColor=#c5d0db;spacing=11;align=left;verticalAlign=top;" vertex="1" parent="1">
          <mxGeometry x="815" y="2058.05" width="755" height="149" as="geometry" />
        </mxCell>
        <mxCell id="s14" value="&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:15px&quot;&gt;&lt;b&gt;FAILURE MODES&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:13px;line-height:1.35&quot;&gt;&amp;bull; Misrouted constraint query — the router sends an exact-rule question to the neural path, and the LLM confabulates an edge case the rule engine would have caught.&lt;br&gt;&amp;bull; Misrouted free text — an interpretive query lands on the symbolic path, and the rule engine fails or returns a template answer that misses the point.&lt;br&gt;&amp;bull; Unrepresentable plan — in the hybrid case the LLM proposes plans the symbolic layer cannot express, and the propose-validate loop keeps rejecting without converging.&lt;br&gt;&amp;bull; Boundary drift — rules or prompts change on one stack only, and the router keeps sending queries to a path that can no longer handle them.&lt;/div&gt;" style="rounded=1;whiteSpace=wrap;html=1;fillColor=#ffffff;strokeColor=#c5d0db;spacing=11;align=left;verticalAlign=top;" vertex="1" parent="1">
          <mxGeometry x="30" y="2430.05" width="755" height="206" as="geometry" />
        </mxCell>
        <mxCell id="s15" value="&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:15px&quot;&gt;&lt;b&gt;EVALUATION METRICS&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:13px;line-height:1.35&quot;&gt;&amp;bull; Router accuracy — share of queries routed to the path that produces the highest-quality answer on a held-out set&lt;br&gt;&amp;bull; Hard-rule violation rate — symbolic violations escaping past the validator&lt;br&gt;&amp;bull; Cost per query by path — should show meaningful savings on the symbolic path&lt;br&gt;&amp;bull; Hybrid-validation rejection rate — share of LLM-proposed plans rejected by the symbolic layer (high = LLM-symbolic mismatch growing)&lt;br&gt;&amp;bull; Boundary-drift rate — change over time in what the router classifies as symbolic vs neural for the same input class&lt;/div&gt;" style="rounded=1;whiteSpace=wrap;html=1;fillColor=#ffffff;strokeColor=#c5d0db;spacing=11;align=left;verticalAlign=top;" vertex="1" parent="1">
          <mxGeometry x="815" y="2221.05" width="755" height="206" as="geometry" />
        </mxCell>
        <mxCell id="s16" value="&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:15px&quot;&gt;&lt;b&gt;KNOWN USES&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:13px;line-height:1.35&quot;&gt;&amp;bull; &lt;b&gt;&lt;a href=&quot;https://arxiv.org/pdf/2511.17673&quot;&gt;NeSyC — neuro-symbolic hypothesis induction with symbolic validation and continual trajectory monitoring&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/b&gt; &lt;i&gt;available&lt;/i&gt;&lt;br&gt;&amp;bull; &lt;b&gt;&lt;a href=&quot;https://arxiv.org/abs/2511.17673&quot;&gt;Structured Cognitive Loop (Kim, 2025) — five-module R-CCAM design bridging expert-system principles with LLM capabilities&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/b&gt; &lt;i&gt;available&lt;/i&gt;&lt;br&gt;&amp;bull; &lt;b&gt;&lt;a href=&quot;https://arxiv.org/abs/2504.13190&quot;&gt;Cellular-X — retrieval-augmented, tool-centric agent with modular symbolic/neural routing for configuration generation&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/b&gt; &lt;i&gt;available&lt;/i&gt;&lt;br&gt;&amp;bull; &lt;b&gt;&lt;a href=&quot;https://qiita.com/syukan3/items/174e43235bde8a1a0694&quot;&gt;Reported in Japanese practitioner write-ups (Qiita syukan3 22-pattern survey, note.com makokon failure-modes analysis) as one of the operational design patterns under active use&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/b&gt; &lt;i&gt;available&lt;/i&gt;&lt;/div&gt;" style="rounded=1;whiteSpace=wrap;html=1;fillColor=#ffffff;strokeColor=#c5d0db;spacing=11;align=left;verticalAlign=top;" vertex="1" parent="1">
          <mxGeometry x="30" y="2650.05" width="755" height="225" as="geometry" />
        </mxCell>
        <mxCell id="s17" value="&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:15px&quot;&gt;&lt;b&gt;RELATED PATTERNS&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:13px;line-height:1.35&quot;&gt;&amp;bull; &lt;i&gt;specialises&lt;/i&gt; &lt;b&gt;Routing&lt;/b&gt;&lt;br&gt;&amp;bull; &lt;i&gt;complements&lt;/i&gt; &lt;b&gt;Multi-Model Routing&lt;/b&gt;&lt;br&gt;&amp;bull; &lt;i&gt;complements&lt;/i&gt; &lt;b&gt;Deterministic-LLM Sandwich&lt;/b&gt; — the sandwich is one specific implementation when the symbolic layer brackets the LLM call&lt;br&gt;&amp;bull; &lt;i&gt;complements&lt;/i&gt; &lt;b&gt;World Model as Tool&lt;/b&gt; — world-model-as-tool gives the LLM a callable simulator; here the symbolic layer is non-callable and authoritative&lt;br&gt;&amp;bull; &lt;i&gt;complements&lt;/i&gt; &lt;b&gt;Policy-as-Code Gate&lt;/b&gt;&lt;br&gt;&amp;bull; &lt;i&gt;uses&lt;/i&gt; &lt;b&gt;Knowledge Graph Memory&lt;/b&gt; — the symbolic path often reads from a knowledge graph&lt;br&gt;&amp;bull; &lt;i&gt;complements&lt;/i&gt; &lt;b&gt;Hybrid HTN + Generative Agent&lt;/b&gt;&lt;br&gt;&amp;bull; &lt;i&gt;complements&lt;/i&gt; &lt;b&gt;BPMN/DMN Deterministic Shell Around Agent&lt;/b&gt;&lt;br&gt;&amp;bull; &lt;i&gt;generalises&lt;/i&gt; &lt;b&gt;MRKL Systems (Modular Neuro-Symbolic)&lt;/b&gt;&lt;br&gt;&amp;bull; &lt;i&gt;complements&lt;/i&gt; &lt;b&gt;Agent-to-Rule Distillation&lt;/b&gt; — The hybrid router is the runtime seat of the pattern: promoted rules populate the symbolic path that short-circuits the neural one for matching inputs.&lt;br&gt;&amp;bull; &lt;i&gt;complements&lt;/i&gt; &lt;b&gt;Solver-Ready Formulation Handoff&lt;/b&gt; — That picks a symbolic or a neural path per query; here both always run, in a fixed producer-and-decider order, with the model structurally barred from the decision.&lt;br&gt;&amp;bull; &lt;i&gt;complements&lt;/i&gt; &lt;b&gt;Semantic Decision Node&lt;/b&gt; — Symbolic rules handle the conditions that can be written down; semantic decision nodes handle the rest inside the same code-owned structure.&lt;/div&gt;" style="rounded=1;whiteSpace=wrap;html=1;fillColor=#ffffff;strokeColor=#c5d0db;spacing=11;align=left;verticalAlign=top;" vertex="1" parent="1">
          <mxGeometry x="815" y="2441.05" width="755" height="396" as="geometry" />
        </mxCell>
        <mxCell id="s18" value="&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:15px&quot;&gt;&lt;b&gt;REFERENCES&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:13px;line-height:1.35&quot;&gt;&amp;bull; &lt;a href=&quot;https://arxiv.org/pdf/2511.17673&quot;&gt;Bridging Symbolic Control and Neural Reasoning in LLM Agents&lt;/a&gt; — 2025&lt;br&gt;&amp;bull; &lt;a href=&quot;https://qiita.com/syukan3/items/174e43235bde8a1a0694&quot;&gt;多様な AI エージェント設計パターン22選を比較&lt;/a&gt; — 2025&lt;br&gt;&amp;bull; &lt;a href=&quot;https://note.com/makokon/n/ne9b86a4cc82b&quot;&gt;LLMエージェントはなぜ失敗するのか？ 自律型AIのデバッグと改善手法&lt;/a&gt; — 2025&lt;/div&gt;" style="rounded=1;whiteSpace=wrap;html=1;fillColor=#ffffff;strokeColor=#c5d0db;spacing=11;align=left;verticalAlign=top;" vertex="1" parent="1">
          <mxGeometry x="30" y="2889.05" width="755" height="130" as="geometry" />
        </mxCell>
      </root>
    </mxGraphModel>
  </diagram>
