        <UserObject label="" mermaidId="e:MS-&gt;ExtCl3#0" mermaidBaseStyle="curved=1;startArrow=none;endArrow=block;endSize=7;strokeColor=light-dark(#333333,#cccccc);exitX=1;exitY=0.91;entryX=0.5;entryY=0;" mermaidBaseValue="" id="m14">
          <mxCell edge="1" parent="1" source="m4" style="curved=1;startArrow=none;endArrow=block;endSize=7;strokeColor=light-dark(#333333,#cccccc);exitX=1;exitY=0.91;entryX=0.5;entryY=0;" target="m7">
            <mxGeometry relative="1" as="geometry">
              <Array as="points">
                <mxPoint x="1021.35" y="832.35" />
              </Array>
            </mxGeometry>
          </mxCell>
        </UserObject>
        <mxCell id="s0" value="&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:15px&quot;&gt;&lt;b&gt;INTENT&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:13px;line-height:1.35&quot;&gt;Run a framework as both MCP client (consuming external MCP servers as tools) and MCP server (publishing its own agents, tools, and workflows back over MCP) so capabilities flow both directions across the protocol boundary.&lt;/div&gt;" style="rounded=1;whiteSpace=wrap;html=1;fillColor=#ffffff;strokeColor=#c5d0db;spacing=11;align=left;verticalAlign=top;" vertex="1" parent="1">
          <mxGeometry x="30" y="1029.25" width="755" height="111" as="geometry" />
        </mxCell>
        <mxCell id="s1" value="&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:15px&quot;&gt;&lt;b&gt;CONTEXT&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:13px;line-height:1.35&quot;&gt;An organisation operates in a heterogeneous agent ecosystem where the Model Context Protocol (MCP) has become the common contract between tools, agents, and hosts. The team is choosing or building a framework that will both use external MCP services and offer its own agents and workflows to other MCP-speaking systems.&lt;/div&gt;" style="rounded=1;whiteSpace=wrap;html=1;fillColor=#ffffff;strokeColor=#c5d0db;spacing=11;align=left;verticalAlign=top;" vertex="1" parent="1">
          <mxGeometry x="815" y="1029.25" width="755" height="130" as="geometry" />
        </mxCell>
        <mxCell id="s2" value="&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:15px&quot;&gt;&lt;b&gt;PROBLEM&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:13px;line-height:1.35&quot;&gt;A framework that only acts as an MCP client can consume external capabilities but cannot expose its own agents and workflows to peers, locking its value inside its own runtime. A framework that only acts as an MCP server can be called from outside but cannot integrate external MCP tools without writing per-vendor adapters. Either asymmetry forces teams to commit to one framework and rewrite integrations whenever they want to combine its agents with another system, defeating the point of having a shared protocol.&lt;/div&gt;" style="rounded=1;whiteSpace=wrap;html=1;fillColor=#ffffff;strokeColor=#c5d0db;spacing=11;align=left;verticalAlign=top;" vertex="1" parent="1">
          <mxGeometry x="30" y="1154.25" width="755" height="168" as="geometry" />
        </mxCell>
        <mxCell id="s3" value="&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:15px&quot;&gt;&lt;b&gt;FORCES&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:13px;line-height:1.35&quot;&gt;&amp;bull; MCP is rapidly becoming the cross-framework tool contract; participating only on one side limits composability.&lt;br&gt;&amp;bull; Exposing internal agents as MCP servers requires careful contract design — schemas, auth, lifecycle, elicitation.&lt;br&gt;&amp;bull; A framework can expose at multiple granularities: a tool, an agent, a workflow, a prompt, a resource.&lt;br&gt;&amp;bull; Permission and credential management is non-trivial when the framework is both client and server.&lt;br&gt;&amp;bull; MCP-as-Code-API (where the agent writes code that calls MCP tools as imports) is a useful third axis.&lt;/div&gt;" style="rounded=1;whiteSpace=wrap;html=1;fillColor=#ffffff;strokeColor=#c5d0db;spacing=11;align=left;verticalAlign=top;" vertex="1" parent="1">
          <mxGeometry x="815" y="1173.25" width="755" height="225" as="geometry" />
        </mxCell>
        <mxCell id="s4" value="&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:15px&quot;&gt;&lt;b&gt;THEREFORE&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:13px;line-height:1.35&quot;&gt;Therefore: implement both the MCP client surface (consume external servers as tools) and the MCP server surface (publish your own agents, tools, and workflows over MCP), so that capabilities can flow in either direction and the framework is composable with other MCP-speaking systems.&lt;/div&gt;" style="rounded=1;whiteSpace=wrap;html=1;fillColor=#ffffff;strokeColor=#c5d0db;spacing=11;align=left;verticalAlign=top;" vertex="1" parent="1">
          <mxGeometry x="30" y="1336.25" width="755" height="111" as="geometry" />
        </mxCell>
        <mxCell id="s5" value="&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:15px&quot;&gt;&lt;b&gt;SOLUTION&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:13px;line-height:1.35&quot;&gt;Build the framework with two symmetric MCP modules: a client module that lets agents call external MCP servers as tools (with auth, schema validation, and elicitation handling), and a server module that publishes internal artefacts — typically agents, tools, workflows, prompts, and resources — over MCP for external consumers. Treat the two as one architectural decision, not two: the same registry should describe both what the framework consumes and what it offers. Pair with mcp (the underlying protocol), mcp-as-code-api (code-as-import variant), and tool-agent-registry. The bridge is also a useful anti-lock-in stance — see vendor-lock-in.&lt;/div&gt;" style="rounded=1;whiteSpace=wrap;html=1;fillColor=#ffffff;strokeColor=#c5d0db;spacing=11;align=left;verticalAlign=top;" vertex="1" parent="1">
          <mxGeometry x="815" y="1412.25" width="755" height="187" as="geometry" />
        </mxCell>
        <mxCell id="s6" value="&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:15px&quot;&gt;&lt;b&gt;STRUCTURE&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:13px;line-height:1.35&quot;&gt;&lt;font face=&quot;monospace&quot;&gt;External MCP servers ↔ [framework MCP client] ↔ framework runtime ↔ [framework MCP server] ↔ external MCP clients.&lt;/font&gt;&lt;/div&gt;" style="rounded=1;whiteSpace=wrap;html=1;fillColor=#ffffff;strokeColor=#c5d0db;spacing=11;align=left;verticalAlign=top;" vertex="1" parent="1">
          <mxGeometry x="30" y="1461.25" width="755" height="111" as="geometry" />
        </mxCell>
        <mxCell id="s7" value="&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:15px&quot;&gt;&lt;b&gt;EXAMPLE SCENARIO&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:13px;line-height:1.35&quot;&gt;A platform team picks Mastra as its agent framework. On the client side, Mastra connects to external MCP servers — GitHub, Slack, an internal Postgres MCP — so agents can use those tools. On the server side, Mastra publishes the team&#39;s internal agents and workflows over MCP so the company&#39;s other tools (a Pydantic-AI service, a Dify dashboard, Claude Desktop, an n8n workflow) can call them directly without an HTTP wrapper. When the team later evaluates Pydantic-AI for one new product line, the integration is a configuration change rather than a rewrite — both frameworks already speak MCP both ways.&lt;/div&gt;" style="rounded=1;whiteSpace=wrap;html=1;fillColor=#ffffff;strokeColor=#c5d0db;spacing=11;align=left;verticalAlign=top;" vertex="1" parent="1">
          <mxGeometry x="815" y="1613.25" width="755" height="187" as="geometry" />
        </mxCell>
        <mxCell id="s8" value="&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:15px&quot;&gt;&lt;b&gt;CONSEQUENCES&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:13px;line-height:1.35&quot;&gt;&lt;b&gt;Benefits&lt;/b&gt;&lt;br&gt;&lt;br&gt;&amp;bull; Capabilities flow both directions across the protocol boundary.&lt;br&gt;&amp;bull; Internal artefacts (agents, workflows, prompts) become reusable by any MCP-speaking peer.&lt;br&gt;&amp;bull; Switching framework on either side becomes a configuration choice.&lt;br&gt;&amp;bull; Composition with other MCP-speaking systems is straightforward.&lt;br&gt;&lt;br&gt;&lt;b&gt;Liabilities&lt;/b&gt;&lt;br&gt;&lt;br&gt;&amp;bull; Double the surface area of the MCP integration — schemas, auth, lifecycle on both sides.&lt;br&gt;&amp;bull; Permission and credential boundary is harder to reason about when the framework is both ends.&lt;br&gt;&amp;bull; Versioning of exposed artefacts is now a public contract.&lt;/div&gt;" style="rounded=1;whiteSpace=wrap;html=1;fillColor=#ffffff;strokeColor=#c5d0db;spacing=11;align=left;verticalAlign=top;" vertex="1" parent="1">
          <mxGeometry x="30" y="1586.25" width="755" height="282" as="geometry" />
        </mxCell>
        <mxCell id="s9" value="&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:15px&quot;&gt;&lt;b&gt;WHAT THIS PATTERN CONSTRAINS&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:13px;line-height:1.35&quot;&gt;External capabilities must arrive through the MCP client surface and internal artefacts must be published through the MCP server surface; the framework&#39;s value is not allowed to be locked behind a non-MCP boundary that peers cannot cross.&lt;/div&gt;" style="rounded=1;whiteSpace=wrap;html=1;fillColor=#ffffff;strokeColor=#c5d0db;spacing=11;align=left;verticalAlign=top;" vertex="1" parent="1">
          <mxGeometry x="815" y="1814.25" width="755" height="111" as="geometry" />
        </mxCell>
        <mxCell id="s10" value="&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:15px&quot;&gt;&lt;b&gt;USE WHEN&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:13px;line-height:1.35&quot;&gt;&amp;bull; The framework participates in a heterogeneous MCP ecosystem.&lt;br&gt;&amp;bull; Internal artefacts (agents, workflows, prompts) should be reusable by external MCP clients.&lt;br&gt;&amp;bull; Anti-lock-in stance is part of the product positioning.&lt;br&gt;&amp;bull; External capabilities arrive through MCP rather than vendor SDKs.&lt;/div&gt;" style="rounded=1;whiteSpace=wrap;html=1;fillColor=#ffffff;strokeColor=#c5d0db;spacing=11;align=left;verticalAlign=top;" vertex="1" parent="1">
          <mxGeometry x="30" y="1882.25" width="755" height="130" as="geometry" />
        </mxCell>
        <mxCell id="s11" value="&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:15px&quot;&gt;&lt;b&gt;DO NOT USE WHEN&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:13px;line-height:1.35&quot;&gt;&amp;bull; The framework is a single-vendor stack with no peer interoperability requirement.&lt;br&gt;&amp;bull; Publishing artefacts as MCP servers would expose internals that the team is not ready to support as a public contract.&lt;br&gt;&amp;bull; Credential and permission boundaries cannot be cleanly maintained across both surfaces.&lt;/div&gt;" style="rounded=1;whiteSpace=wrap;html=1;fillColor=#ffffff;strokeColor=#c5d0db;spacing=11;align=left;verticalAlign=top;" vertex="1" parent="1">
          <mxGeometry x="815" y="1939.25" width="755" height="130" as="geometry" />
        </mxCell>
        <mxCell id="s12" value="&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:15px&quot;&gt;&lt;b&gt;COMPONENTS&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:13px;line-height:1.35&quot;&gt;&amp;bull; MCP Client Module — consumes external MCP servers as tools, with auth, schema validation, and elicitation&lt;br&gt;&amp;bull; MCP Server Module — publishes the framework&#39;s own agents, tools, workflows, prompts, and resources to peers&lt;br&gt;&amp;bull; Internal Registry — single source of truth for capabilities that the server module advertises&lt;br&gt;&amp;bull; Framework Runtime — hosts agents and workflows that both consume external tools and serve internal ones&lt;br&gt;&amp;bull; Auth Bridge — maps credentials across the client and server boundaries&lt;/div&gt;" style="rounded=1;whiteSpace=wrap;html=1;fillColor=#ffffff;strokeColor=#c5d0db;spacing=11;align=left;verticalAlign=top;" vertex="1" parent="1">
          <mxGeometry x="30" y="2026.25" width="755" height="206" as="geometry" />
        </mxCell>
        <mxCell id="s13" value="&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:15px&quot;&gt;&lt;b&gt;TOOLS&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:13px;line-height:1.35&quot;&gt;&amp;bull; MCP server and client SDKs — implement both protocol roles inside the same process&lt;br&gt;&amp;bull; Schema validator — checks payloads in both directions against published tool schemas&lt;br&gt;&amp;bull; Elicitation handler — prompts the surrounding user when an external server requires additional input&lt;/div&gt;" style="rounded=1;whiteSpace=wrap;html=1;fillColor=#ffffff;strokeColor=#c5d0db;spacing=11;align=left;verticalAlign=top;" vertex="1" parent="1">
          <mxGeometry x="815" y="2083.25" width="755" height="130" as="geometry" />
        </mxCell>
        <mxCell id="s14" value="&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:15px&quot;&gt;&lt;b&gt;FAILURE MODES&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:13px;line-height:1.35&quot;&gt;&amp;bull; Credential bleed — a token the framework holds as a client is reachable from a request that arrived through its server side, and an outside caller gets access it was never granted.&lt;br&gt;&amp;bull; Broken public contract — an internal agent or workflow is renamed or reshaped, and external peers that call it over MCP break without warning.&lt;br&gt;&amp;bull; Split registry — consumed and published capabilities are tracked in two places, so the framework advertises tools it can no longer reach.&lt;br&gt;&amp;bull; Call loop — the framework calls a peer over MCP, the peer calls back into the framework&#39;s server side, and the request circles until it times out.&lt;/div&gt;" style="rounded=1;whiteSpace=wrap;html=1;fillColor=#ffffff;strokeColor=#c5d0db;spacing=11;align=left;verticalAlign=top;" vertex="1" parent="1">
          <mxGeometry x="30" y="2246.25" width="755" height="206" as="geometry" />
        </mxCell>
        <mxCell id="s15" value="&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:15px&quot;&gt;&lt;b&gt;EVALUATION METRICS&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:13px;line-height:1.35&quot;&gt;&amp;bull; External tool consumption rate — number and diversity of MCP servers the client module integrates&lt;br&gt;&amp;bull; Internal capability publication rate — count of framework artefacts exposed over the server module&lt;br&gt;&amp;bull; Bidirectional call success rate — fraction of cross-boundary calls returning a typed non-error result&lt;br&gt;&amp;bull; Registry-to-MCP drift incidents — mismatches between internal artefacts and what the server advertises&lt;br&gt;&amp;bull; Auth-bridge failure rate — share of calls failing at the credential mapping step&lt;/div&gt;" style="rounded=1;whiteSpace=wrap;html=1;fillColor=#ffffff;strokeColor=#c5d0db;spacing=11;align=left;verticalAlign=top;" vertex="1" parent="1">
          <mxGeometry x="815" y="2227.25" width="755" height="187" as="geometry" />
        </mxCell>
        <mxCell id="s16" value="&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:15px&quot;&gt;&lt;b&gt;KNOWN USES&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:13px;line-height:1.35&quot;&gt;&amp;bull; &lt;b&gt;&lt;a href=&quot;https://mastra.ai/docs/mcp/overview&quot;&gt;Mastra (MCPClient + MCPServer)&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/b&gt; &lt;i&gt;available&lt;/i&gt; — Mastra ships an MCPClient for consuming external servers and an MCPServer for exposing Mastra tools, agents, workflows, prompts, and resources.&lt;br&gt;&amp;bull; &lt;b&gt;&lt;a href=&quot;https://pydantic.dev/docs/ai/mcp/overview/&quot;&gt;Pydantic-AI (MCP client + Agents-as-MCP-servers)&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/b&gt; &lt;i&gt;available&lt;/i&gt; — Pydantic-AI documents both directions — agents can connect to MCP servers and agents can be used within MCP servers.&lt;br&gt;&amp;bull; &lt;b&gt;&lt;a href=&quot;https://docs.n8n.io/integrations/builtin/core-nodes/n8n-nodes-langchain.mcptrigger/&quot;&gt;n8n (MCP Server Trigger + MCP Client node)&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/b&gt; &lt;i&gt;available&lt;/i&gt; — n8n exposes workflows as MCP servers via the MCP Server Trigger node and consumes MCP via the MCP Client node.&lt;br&gt;&amp;bull; &lt;b&gt;&lt;a href=&quot;https://github.com/langgenius/dify&quot;&gt;Dify (MCP tools consumption + apps as MCP servers)&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/b&gt; &lt;i&gt;available&lt;/i&gt; — Dify consumes MCP tools and can publish apps as MCP servers.&lt;br&gt;&amp;bull; &lt;b&gt;&lt;a href=&quot;https://pypi.org/project/llama-index-tools-mcp/&quot;&gt;LlamaIndex (MCP client + workflows as MCP)&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/b&gt; &lt;i&gt;available&lt;/i&gt; — LlamaIndex ships both directions in one package: a client that connects to MCP servers so an agent can call their tools, and a helper that converts a Workflow into an MCP app.&lt;/div&gt;" style="rounded=1;whiteSpace=wrap;html=1;fillColor=#ffffff;strokeColor=#c5d0db;spacing=11;align=left;verticalAlign=top;" vertex="1" parent="1">
          <mxGeometry x="30" y="2466.25" width="755" height="320" as="geometry" />
        </mxCell>
        <mxCell id="s17" value="&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:15px&quot;&gt;&lt;b&gt;RELATED PATTERNS&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:13px;line-height:1.35&quot;&gt;&amp;bull; &lt;i&gt;specialises&lt;/i&gt; &lt;b&gt;Model Context Protocol&lt;/b&gt;&lt;br&gt;&amp;bull; &lt;i&gt;complements&lt;/i&gt; &lt;b&gt;MCP-as-Code-API&lt;/b&gt;&lt;br&gt;&amp;bull; &lt;i&gt;complements&lt;/i&gt; &lt;b&gt;Tool/Agent Registry&lt;/b&gt;&lt;br&gt;&amp;bull; &lt;i&gt;alternative-to&lt;/i&gt; &lt;b&gt;Vendor Lock-In&lt;/b&gt;&lt;br&gt;&amp;bull; &lt;i&gt;complements&lt;/i&gt; &lt;b&gt;Performative Message&lt;/b&gt;&lt;br&gt;&amp;bull; &lt;i&gt;complements&lt;/i&gt; &lt;b&gt;Hierarchical Tool Selection&lt;/b&gt;&lt;br&gt;&amp;bull; &lt;i&gt;composes-with&lt;/i&gt; &lt;b&gt;MCP Server-Side Sampling&lt;/b&gt; — The bridge makes a framework both client and server as a topology; server-side sampling is the per-call reasoning inversion that flows over the server-to-host surface that topology opens.&lt;/div&gt;" style="rounded=1;whiteSpace=wrap;html=1;fillColor=#ffffff;strokeColor=#c5d0db;spacing=11;align=left;verticalAlign=top;" vertex="1" parent="1">
          <mxGeometry x="815" y="2428.25" width="755" height="225" as="geometry" />
        </mxCell>
        <mxCell id="s18" value="&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:15px&quot;&gt;&lt;b&gt;REFERENCES&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:13px;line-height:1.35&quot;&gt;&amp;bull; &lt;a href=&quot;https://mastra.ai/docs/mcp/overview&quot;&gt;Mastra — MCP Overview&lt;/a&gt; — Mastra&lt;br&gt;&amp;bull; &lt;a href=&quot;https://pydantic.dev/docs/ai/mcp/overview/&quot;&gt;Pydantic-AI — MCP Overview&lt;/a&gt; — Pydantic&lt;/div&gt;" style="rounded=1;whiteSpace=wrap;html=1;fillColor=#ffffff;strokeColor=#c5d0db;spacing=11;align=left;verticalAlign=top;" vertex="1" parent="1">
          <mxGeometry x="30" y="2800.25" width="755" height="92" as="geometry" />
        </mxCell>
      </root>
    </mxGraphModel>
  </diagram>
