        <mxCell id="arch-caption" value="&lt;i&gt;Without a feasibility check a confidently-phrased but physically impossible command reaches the actuator; a grounding or simulation gate discards it instead.&lt;/i&gt;" style="text;html=1;align=center;verticalAlign=middle;strokeColor=none;fillColor=none;fontSize=12;fontColor=#52606d;" vertex="1" parent="1">
          <mxGeometry x="65" y="1197.95" width="1470" height="32" as="geometry" />
        </mxCell>
        <mxCell id="s0" value="&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:15px&quot;&gt;&lt;b&gt;INTENT&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:13px;line-height:1.35&quot;&gt;Anti-pattern: an embodied or process-control agent issues a confidently-phrased command that is syntactically valid but physically infeasible or unsafe, because nothing checks it against geometry, dynamics, or actual plant state before actuation.&lt;/div&gt;" style="rounded=1;whiteSpace=wrap;html=1;fillColor=#ffffff;strokeColor=#c5d0db;spacing=11;align=left;verticalAlign=top;" vertex="1" parent="1">
          <mxGeometry x="30" y="1272.95" width="755" height="111" as="geometry" />
        </mxCell>
        <mxCell id="s1" value="&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:15px&quot;&gt;&lt;b&gt;CONTEXT&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:13px;line-height:1.35&quot;&gt;An agent drives an embodied or physical system — a robot arm, a mobile robot, a process-control loop — by emitting commands to actuators or controllers. The model produces those commands from a language or vision-language policy, the same way it produces text, choosing the most plausible next action given the goal. The physical world, unlike a text channel, has hard constraints: reachable poses, dynamic limits, collision geometry, and a current plant state the command must be consistent with.&lt;/div&gt;" style="rounded=1;whiteSpace=wrap;html=1;fillColor=#ffffff;strokeColor=#c5d0db;spacing=11;align=left;verticalAlign=top;" vertex="1" parent="1">
          <mxGeometry x="815" y="1272.95" width="755" height="149" as="geometry" />
        </mxCell>
        <mxCell id="s2" value="&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:15px&quot;&gt;&lt;b&gt;PROBLEM&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:13px;line-height:1.35&quot;&gt;The model has no faithful internal model of physics, so it can emit a command that reads as correct and is phrased with full confidence yet cannot be executed. Examples are an unreachable arm pose, a dynamically infeasible motion, a path through an obstacle, or a setpoint inconsistent with the plant&#39;s current state. Because the command is syntactically valid and confident, downstream systems that trust the policy pass it through to actuation, where it fails, damages equipment, or creates a hazard. The failure is grounded in the physical world the model cannot perceive faithfully, not in missing facts or tools.&lt;/div&gt;" style="rounded=1;whiteSpace=wrap;html=1;fillColor=#ffffff;strokeColor=#c5d0db;spacing=11;align=left;verticalAlign=top;" vertex="1" parent="1">
          <mxGeometry x="30" y="1397.95" width="755" height="187" as="geometry" />
        </mxCell>
        <mxCell id="s3" value="&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:15px&quot;&gt;&lt;b&gt;FORCES&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:13px;line-height:1.35&quot;&gt;&amp;bull; A language or vision-language policy generates the most plausible command, and plausibility is not feasibility — a fluent command can be physically impossible.&lt;br&gt;&amp;bull; Physical constraints (reach, dynamics, collision, plant state) are not legible in the token stream, so the policy is not penalised for violating them at generation time.&lt;br&gt;&amp;bull; A confidently-phrased command invites trust from a controller that has no independent feasibility check.&lt;br&gt;&amp;bull; Adding a grounding or simulation gate before actuation costs latency and engineering that a direct policy-to-actuator path avoids.&lt;/div&gt;" style="rounded=1;whiteSpace=wrap;html=1;fillColor=#ffffff;strokeColor=#c5d0db;spacing=11;align=left;verticalAlign=top;" vertex="1" parent="1">
          <mxGeometry x="815" y="1435.95" width="755" height="206" as="geometry" />
        </mxCell>
        <mxCell id="s4" value="&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:15px&quot;&gt;&lt;b&gt;THEREFORE&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:13px;line-height:1.35&quot;&gt;Therefore: do not let a policy command reach an actuator on its confidence alone; ground each candidate action against the current scene and the system&#39;s physical limits, or simulate it, and discard or correct any command the physical world cannot support before it executes.&lt;/div&gt;" style="rounded=1;whiteSpace=wrap;html=1;fillColor=#ffffff;strokeColor=#c5d0db;spacing=11;align=left;verticalAlign=top;" vertex="1" parent="1">
          <mxGeometry x="30" y="1598.95" width="755" height="111" as="geometry" />
        </mxCell>
        <mxCell id="s5" value="&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:15px&quot;&gt;&lt;b&gt;SOLUTION&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:13px;line-height:1.35&quot;&gt;Treat every command from the policy as a proposal to be checked against physics, not an instruction to execute. Insert a feasibility stage between policy and actuator that grounds the candidate action in the current scene and the system&#39;s kinematic, dynamic, and state limits. That stage can predict the action&#39;s affordance from perception, or roll it out in a simulator or world model, and it rejects or repairs any action the environment cannot support. Only feasible, in-state commands reach the controller; infeasible ones are discarded, re-planned, or escalated. The check is independent of the policy&#39;s own confidence, so a fluent but impossible command cannot pass merely by being well-phrased.&lt;/div&gt;" style="rounded=1;whiteSpace=wrap;html=1;fillColor=#ffffff;strokeColor=#c5d0db;spacing=11;align=left;verticalAlign=top;" vertex="1" parent="1">
          <mxGeometry x="815" y="1655.95" width="755" height="206" as="geometry" />
        </mxCell>
        <mxCell id="s6" value="&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:15px&quot;&gt;&lt;b&gt;STRUCTURE&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:13px;line-height:1.35&quot;&gt;&lt;font face=&quot;monospace&quot;&gt;Policy --confident command--&amp;gt; [no feasibility check] --&amp;gt; actuator (BROKEN: impossible/unsafe command executes) ; Corrected: Policy --candidate--&amp;gt; feasibility/affordance/simulation gate --&amp;gt; feasible? --&amp;gt; actuator / discard + re-plan&lt;/font&gt;&lt;/div&gt;" style="rounded=1;whiteSpace=wrap;html=1;fillColor=#ffffff;strokeColor=#c5d0db;spacing=11;align=left;verticalAlign=top;" vertex="1" parent="1">
          <mxGeometry x="30" y="1723.95" width="755" height="130" as="geometry" />
        </mxCell>
        <mxCell id="s7" value="&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:15px&quot;&gt;&lt;b&gt;EXAMPLE SCENARIO&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:13px;line-height:1.35&quot;&gt;A warehouse robot is told to place a box on the top shelf. The vision-language policy confidently emits an arm pose to reach it, but the pose is outside the arm&#39;s workspace; with no feasibility check the controller accepts it, the arm jams against its limit, and the motion faults. A grounding gate that predicted the pose was unreachable would have rejected the command before the arm ever moved.&lt;/div&gt;" style="rounded=1;whiteSpace=wrap;html=1;fillColor=#ffffff;strokeColor=#c5d0db;spacing=11;align=left;verticalAlign=top;" vertex="1" parent="1">
          <mxGeometry x="815" y="1875.95" width="755" height="130" as="geometry" />
        </mxCell>
        <mxCell id="s8" value="&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:15px&quot;&gt;&lt;b&gt;CONSEQUENCES&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:13px;line-height:1.35&quot;&gt;&lt;b&gt;Benefits&lt;/b&gt;&lt;br&gt;&lt;br&gt;&amp;bull; Short-term: sending policy commands straight to the controller keeps the loop fast and looks fine while the commands happen to be feasible.&lt;br&gt;&amp;bull; Naming it lets a team insert a physics and plant-state feasibility check between policy and actuator as a standard stage.&lt;br&gt;&lt;br&gt;&lt;b&gt;Liabilities&lt;/b&gt;&lt;br&gt;&lt;br&gt;&amp;bull; An infeasible command sent to an actuator can damage hardware, collide with the environment, or create a safety hazard.&lt;br&gt;&amp;bull; A setpoint inconsistent with plant state can push a process outside safe operating bounds.&lt;br&gt;&amp;bull; Because the command was confident and valid, the failure surfaces at execution, where it is costly and sometimes dangerous to diagnose.&lt;br&gt;&amp;bull; Operators lose trust in the autonomy once it issues commands the physical system visibly cannot perform.&lt;/div&gt;" style="rounded=1;whiteSpace=wrap;html=1;fillColor=#ffffff;strokeColor=#c5d0db;spacing=11;align=left;verticalAlign=top;" vertex="1" parent="1">
          <mxGeometry x="30" y="1867.95" width="755" height="358" as="geometry" />
        </mxCell>
        <mxCell id="s9" value="&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:15px&quot;&gt;&lt;b&gt;WHAT THIS PATTERN CONSTRAINS&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:13px;line-height:1.35&quot;&gt;A policy-generated command must not reach an actuator on its confidence alone; it has to pass an independent feasibility check against the current scene and the system&#39;s physical limits before execution, and an infeasible command is discarded rather than issued.&lt;/div&gt;" style="rounded=1;whiteSpace=wrap;html=1;fillColor=#ffffff;strokeColor=#c5d0db;spacing=11;align=left;verticalAlign=top;" vertex="1" parent="1">
          <mxGeometry x="815" y="2019.95" width="755" height="111" as="geometry" />
        </mxCell>
        <mxCell id="s10" value="&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:15px&quot;&gt;&lt;b&gt;USE WHEN&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:13px;line-height:1.35&quot;&gt;&amp;bull; Recognising this failure when an embodied or process-control agent issues commands the physical system cannot actually perform.&lt;br&gt;&amp;bull; Reviewing a policy-to-actuator path that has no feasibility or affordance check between the model and the hardware.&lt;br&gt;&amp;bull; Diagnosing actuation faults, collisions, or unsafe setpoints that trace back to confidently-phrased but impossible commands.&lt;/div&gt;" style="rounded=1;whiteSpace=wrap;html=1;fillColor=#ffffff;strokeColor=#c5d0db;spacing=11;align=left;verticalAlign=top;" vertex="1" parent="1">
          <mxGeometry x="30" y="2239.95" width="755" height="168" as="geometry" />
        </mxCell>
        <mxCell id="s11" value="&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:15px&quot;&gt;&lt;b&gt;DO NOT USE WHEN&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:13px;line-height:1.35&quot;&gt;&amp;bull; The agent only produces text or advisory output with no actuation, so there is no physical command to be infeasible.&lt;br&gt;&amp;bull; A grounding, affordance, or simulation gate already checks every command against physical limits before it executes.&lt;br&gt;&amp;bull; Actuators hard-reject infeasible commands at a lower layer the agent cannot bypass.&lt;/div&gt;" style="rounded=1;whiteSpace=wrap;html=1;fillColor=#ffffff;strokeColor=#c5d0db;spacing=11;align=left;verticalAlign=top;" vertex="1" parent="1">
          <mxGeometry x="815" y="2144.95" width="755" height="149" as="geometry" />
        </mxCell>
        <mxCell id="s12" value="&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:15px&quot;&gt;&lt;b&gt;COMPONENTS&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:13px;line-height:1.35&quot;&gt;&amp;bull; Policy — the language or vision-language model that emits action commands from the goal&lt;br&gt;&amp;bull; Command channel — the path from policy to actuator that this anti-pattern leaves unchecked&lt;br&gt;&amp;bull; Actuator or controller — the hardware interface that executes whatever command it receives&lt;br&gt;&amp;bull; Missing feasibility gate — the absent affordance or simulation check against scene and physical limits&lt;br&gt;&amp;bull; Physical environment — the geometry, dynamics, and plant state the command must be consistent with&lt;/div&gt;" style="rounded=1;whiteSpace=wrap;html=1;fillColor=#ffffff;strokeColor=#c5d0db;spacing=11;align=left;verticalAlign=top;" vertex="1" parent="1">
          <mxGeometry x="30" y="2421.95" width="755" height="168" as="geometry" />
        </mxCell>
        <mxCell id="s13" value="&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:15px&quot;&gt;&lt;b&gt;TOOLS&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:13px;line-height:1.35&quot;&gt;&amp;bull; Vision-language policy — proposes the actions, sometimes physically infeasible ones&lt;br&gt;&amp;bull; Affordance or feasibility predictor — the corrective check that grounds an action against the scene&lt;br&gt;&amp;bull; Simulator or world model — rolls out a candidate command to test feasibility before actuation&lt;/div&gt;" style="rounded=1;whiteSpace=wrap;html=1;fillColor=#ffffff;strokeColor=#c5d0db;spacing=11;align=left;verticalAlign=top;" vertex="1" parent="1">
          <mxGeometry x="815" y="2307.95" width="755" height="130" as="geometry" />
        </mxCell>
        <mxCell id="s14" value="&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:15px&quot;&gt;&lt;b&gt;FAILURE MODES&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:13px;line-height:1.35&quot;&gt;&amp;bull; Unreachable pose — the policy commands an arm configuration outside the manipulator&#39;s workspace.&lt;br&gt;&amp;bull; Dynamically infeasible motion — the commanded trajectory exceeds velocity, acceleration, or torque limits.&lt;br&gt;&amp;bull; Collision path — the command routes the body through an obstacle the policy did not account for.&lt;br&gt;&amp;bull; State-inconsistent setpoint — a process command assumes a plant state that does not currently hold.&lt;/div&gt;" style="rounded=1;whiteSpace=wrap;html=1;fillColor=#ffffff;strokeColor=#c5d0db;spacing=11;align=left;verticalAlign=top;" vertex="1" parent="1">
          <mxGeometry x="30" y="2603.95" width="755" height="168" as="geometry" />
        </mxCell>
        <mxCell id="s15" value="&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:15px&quot;&gt;&lt;b&gt;EVALUATION METRICS&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:13px;line-height:1.35&quot;&gt;&amp;bull; Infeasible-command rate — fraction of issued commands the physical system could not execute&lt;br&gt;&amp;bull; Actuation-fault incidents — faults, collisions, or unsafe setpoints traced to impossible commands&lt;br&gt;&amp;bull; Feasibility-gate catch rate — share of infeasible commands rejected before reaching the actuator&lt;br&gt;&amp;bull; Confidence-feasibility gap — how often high-confidence commands were physically impossible&lt;/div&gt;" style="rounded=1;whiteSpace=wrap;html=1;fillColor=#ffffff;strokeColor=#c5d0db;spacing=11;align=left;verticalAlign=top;" vertex="1" parent="1">
          <mxGeometry x="815" y="2451.95" width="755" height="130" as="geometry" />
        </mxCell>
        <mxCell id="s16" value="&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:15px&quot;&gt;&lt;b&gt;KNOWN USES&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:13px;line-height:1.35&quot;&gt;&amp;bull; &lt;b&gt;&lt;a href=&quot;https://arxiv.org/pdf/2506.15065&quot;&gt;HEAL embodied-hallucination study&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/b&gt; &lt;i&gt;available&lt;/i&gt; — Empirical study finding embodied-agent hallucinations are qualitatively different from conversational ones, stemming from a failure to ground instructions in the observed physical environment.&lt;br&gt;&amp;bull; &lt;b&gt;&lt;a href=&quot;https://www.mdpi.com/2227-9717/14/2/322&quot;&gt;Neuro-symbolic verification for process control&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/b&gt; &lt;i&gt;available&lt;/i&gt; — Documents hallucinated control commands that are syntactically valid and confident yet physically infeasible or inconsistent with plant state, and adds a verification layer.&lt;br&gt;&amp;bull; &lt;b&gt;&lt;a href=&quot;https://arxiv.org/pdf/2505.19933&quot;&gt;Embodied physical-safety diagnosis for LLM decision-making&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/b&gt; &lt;i&gt;available&lt;/i&gt; — Framework showing embodied agents overlook hazardous effects of actions under unsafe preconditions or execute safe actions in an unsafe order.&lt;/div&gt;" style="rounded=1;whiteSpace=wrap;html=1;fillColor=#ffffff;strokeColor=#c5d0db;spacing=11;align=left;verticalAlign=top;" vertex="1" parent="1">
          <mxGeometry x="30" y="2785.95" width="755" height="225" as="geometry" />
        </mxCell>
        <mxCell id="s17" value="&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:15px&quot;&gt;&lt;b&gt;RELATED PATTERNS&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:13px;line-height:1.35&quot;&gt;&amp;bull; &lt;i&gt;alternative-to&lt;/i&gt; &lt;b&gt;Affordance Grounding Before Action&lt;/b&gt; — Affordance grounding is the corrective — discard actions the scene cannot physically support before the controller; physical hallucination is the failure when no such gate exists.&lt;br&gt;&amp;bull; &lt;i&gt;complements&lt;/i&gt; &lt;b&gt;Simulate Before Actuate&lt;/b&gt; — Simulating an irreversible action before issuing it catches exactly the physically-infeasible command this anti-pattern lets through.&lt;br&gt;&amp;bull; &lt;i&gt;complements&lt;/i&gt; &lt;b&gt;Hallucinated Tools&lt;/b&gt; — Both are hallucination at the action boundary; hallucinated-tools invents a nonexistent tool, physical hallucination issues a real-tool command the physical world cannot execute.&lt;br&gt;&amp;bull; &lt;i&gt;complements&lt;/i&gt; &lt;b&gt;Phantom Action Completion&lt;/b&gt; — Phantom completion narrates an action as done that never ran; physical hallucination actually issues a command that is physically impossible to carry out.&lt;br&gt;&amp;bull; &lt;i&gt;complements&lt;/i&gt; &lt;b&gt;Mental-Model-In-The-Loop Simulator&lt;/b&gt; — Running candidate strategies in an internal simulator before committing is one way to reject the infeasible commands this anti-pattern produces.&lt;br&gt;&amp;bull; &lt;i&gt;complements&lt;/i&gt; &lt;b&gt;Physical-Evidence Progress Gate&lt;/b&gt; — That anti-pattern is a physically infeasible command reaching an actuator unchecked; here the command was feasible and was issued, and the omission is the check on whether it worked.&lt;/div&gt;" style="rounded=1;whiteSpace=wrap;html=1;fillColor=#ffffff;strokeColor=#c5d0db;spacing=11;align=left;verticalAlign=top;" vertex="1" parent="1">
          <mxGeometry x="815" y="2595.95" width="755" height="339" as="geometry" />
        </mxCell>
        <mxCell id="s18" value="&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:15px&quot;&gt;&lt;b&gt;REFERENCES&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:13px;line-height:1.35&quot;&gt;&amp;bull; &lt;a href=&quot;https://arxiv.org/pdf/2506.15065&quot;&gt;HEAL: An Empirical Study on Hallucinations in Embodied Agents Driven by Large Language Models&lt;/a&gt; — 2025&lt;br&gt;&amp;bull; &lt;a href=&quot;https://www.mdpi.com/2227-9717/14/2/322&quot;&gt;Neuro-Symbolic Verification for Preventing LLM Hallucinations in Process Control&lt;/a&gt; — 2026&lt;br&gt;&amp;bull; &lt;a href=&quot;https://arxiv.org/pdf/2505.19933&quot;&gt;Subtle Risks, Critical Failures: A Framework for Diagnosing Physical Safety of LLMs for Embodied Decision Making&lt;/a&gt; — 2025&lt;br&gt;&amp;bull; &lt;a href=&quot;https://semiengineering.com/llms-add-safety-risks-to-physical-ai/&quot;&gt;LLMs Add Safety Risks To Physical AI&lt;/a&gt; — 2026&lt;/div&gt;" style="rounded=1;whiteSpace=wrap;html=1;fillColor=#ffffff;strokeColor=#c5d0db;spacing=11;align=left;verticalAlign=top;" vertex="1" parent="1">
          <mxGeometry x="30" y="3024.95" width="755" height="206" as="geometry" />
        </mxCell>
      </root>
    </mxGraphModel>
  </diagram>
