        </UserObject>
        <UserObject label="" mermaidId="e:A-&gt;R#0" mermaidBaseStyle="curved=1;startArrow=none;endArrow=block;endSize=7;strokeColor=light-dark(#333333,#cccccc);exitX=0.5;exitY=1;entryX=0.5;entryY=0;" mermaidBaseValue="" id="m6">
          <mxCell edge="1" parent="1" source="m1" style="curved=1;startArrow=none;endArrow=block;endSize=7;strokeColor=light-dark(#333333,#cccccc);exitX=0.5;exitY=1;entryX=0.5;entryY=0;" target="m2">
            <mxGeometry relative="1" as="geometry">
              <Array as="points" />
            </mxGeometry>
          </mxCell>
        </UserObject>
        <UserObject label="" mermaidId="e:R-&gt;D#0" mermaidBaseStyle="curved=1;startArrow=none;endArrow=block;endSize=7;strokeColor=light-dark(#333333,#cccccc);exitX=0.5;exitY=1;entryX=0.5;entryY=0;" mermaidBaseValue="" id="m7">
          <mxCell edge="1" parent="1" source="m2" style="curved=1;startArrow=none;endArrow=block;endSize=7;strokeColor=light-dark(#333333,#cccccc);exitX=0.5;exitY=1;entryX=0.5;entryY=0;" target="m3">
            <mxGeometry relative="1" as="geometry">
              <Array as="points" />
            </mxGeometry>
          </mxCell>
        </UserObject>
        <UserObject label="" mermaidId="e:D-&gt;X#0" mermaidBaseStyle="curved=1;startArrow=none;endArrow=block;endSize=7;strokeColor=light-dark(#333333,#cccccc);exitX=0.5;exitY=1;entryX=0.5;entryY=0;" mermaidBaseValue="" id="m8">
          <mxCell edge="1" parent="1" source="m3" style="curved=1;startArrow=none;endArrow=block;endSize=7;strokeColor=light-dark(#333333,#cccccc);exitX=0.5;exitY=1;entryX=0.5;entryY=0;" target="m4">
            <mxGeometry relative="1" as="geometry">
              <Array as="points" />
            </mxGeometry>
          </mxCell>
        </UserObject>
        <mxCell id="arch-caption" value="&lt;i&gt;Each justified accommodation nudges the cap up with nothing pulling it back, so the enforced ceiling drifts above the authorised limit.&lt;/i&gt;" style="text;html=1;align=center;verticalAlign=middle;strokeColor=none;fillColor=none;fontSize=12;fontColor=#52606d;" vertex="1" parent="1">
          <mxGeometry x="65" y="889.5" width="1470" height="32" as="geometry" />
        </mxCell>
        <mxCell id="s0" value="&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:15px&quot;&gt;&lt;b&gt;INTENT&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:13px;line-height:1.35&quot;&gt;Anti-pattern: a correctly-set autonomous-refund cap drifts upward over time as the agent accommodates edge cases, so the effective approval ceiling and financial exposure grow silently with no hard limit to catch it.&lt;/div&gt;" style="rounded=1;whiteSpace=wrap;html=1;fillColor=#ffffff;strokeColor=#c5d0db;spacing=11;align=left;verticalAlign=top;" vertex="1" parent="1">
          <mxGeometry x="30" y="964.5" width="755" height="111" as="geometry" />
        </mxCell>
        <mxCell id="s1" value="&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:15px&quot;&gt;&lt;b&gt;CONTEXT&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:13px;line-height:1.35&quot;&gt;An agent handles refunds, credits, or payments with an autonomy cap: it may settle amounts up to a configured ceiling on its own and must escalate anything larger. The cap is set correctly at first. Over time, edge cases arrive — a slightly-over-limit refund for a loyal customer, a one-off exception a human would have waved through — and the agent or its operators relax the limit a little to accommodate them.&lt;/div&gt;" style="rounded=1;whiteSpace=wrap;html=1;fillColor=#ffffff;strokeColor=#c5d0db;spacing=11;align=left;verticalAlign=top;" vertex="1" parent="1">
          <mxGeometry x="815" y="964.5" width="755" height="149" as="geometry" />
        </mxCell>
        <mxCell id="s2" value="&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:15px&quot;&gt;&lt;b&gt;PROBLEM&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:13px;line-height:1.35&quot;&gt;Each individual accommodation is reasonable, but there is no force pulling the ceiling back down, so the effective cap ratchets upward and financial exposure grows. Because the drift happens a little at a time through justified exceptions, no single decision looks wrong and nothing alarms; the control was correct when set and is never obviously broken. Without a hard limit enforced below the agent and a periodic review of the effective ceiling, the cap erodes silently until exposure is far above what was authorised.&lt;/div&gt;" style="rounded=1;whiteSpace=wrap;html=1;fillColor=#ffffff;strokeColor=#c5d0db;spacing=11;align=left;verticalAlign=top;" vertex="1" parent="1">
          <mxGeometry x="30" y="1089.5" width="755" height="168" as="geometry" />
        </mxCell>
        <mxCell id="s3" value="&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:15px&quot;&gt;&lt;b&gt;FORCES&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:13px;line-height:1.35&quot;&gt;&amp;bull; Accommodating an edge case is locally reasonable, but each accommodation becomes precedent that nudges the ceiling up.&lt;br&gt;&amp;bull; There is upward pressure (satisfy the customer, clear the queue) but no symmetric downward pressure to restore the original cap.&lt;br&gt;&amp;bull; The drift is gradual and justified step by step, so no single decision triggers an alarm.&lt;br&gt;&amp;bull; A correctly-set static cap looks safe on paper, hiding that its enforced value is creeping at runtime.&lt;/div&gt;" style="rounded=1;whiteSpace=wrap;html=1;fillColor=#ffffff;strokeColor=#c5d0db;spacing=11;align=left;verticalAlign=top;" vertex="1" parent="1">
          <mxGeometry x="815" y="1127.5" width="755" height="187" as="geometry" />
        </mxCell>
        <mxCell id="s4" value="&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:15px&quot;&gt;&lt;b&gt;THEREFORE&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:13px;line-height:1.35&quot;&gt;Therefore: enforce the autonomy cap as a hard limit below the agent that the agent cannot relax, and review the effective ceiling on a schedule, so accommodation of edge cases cannot ratchet the authorised exposure upward unnoticed.&lt;/div&gt;" style="rounded=1;whiteSpace=wrap;html=1;fillColor=#ffffff;strokeColor=#c5d0db;spacing=11;align=left;verticalAlign=top;" vertex="1" parent="1">
          <mxGeometry x="30" y="1271.5" width="755" height="111" as="geometry" />
        </mxCell>
        <mxCell id="s5" value="&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:15px&quot;&gt;&lt;b&gt;SOLUTION&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:13px;line-height:1.35&quot;&gt;Pin the autonomy cap as a hard constraint enforced beneath the agent — at the payment or refund API — so the agent cannot raise its own ceiling, and route any over-cap case to human approval rather than to a relaxed limit. Treat the cap as a number to be audited, not a one-time setting: review the effective approval ceiling and the distribution of approved amounts on a schedule, and reset drift back to the authorised value. Make exceptions explicit and logged rather than absorbed into the limit, so accommodating one customer never silently re-authorises the next. The control is the enforced ceiling plus the review, not the agent&#39;s judgement about what is reasonable this time.&lt;/div&gt;" style="rounded=1;whiteSpace=wrap;html=1;fillColor=#ffffff;strokeColor=#c5d0db;spacing=11;align=left;verticalAlign=top;" vertex="1" parent="1">
          <mxGeometry x="815" y="1328.5" width="755" height="187" as="geometry" />
        </mxCell>
        <mxCell id="s6" value="&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:15px&quot;&gt;&lt;b&gt;STRUCTURE&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:13px;line-height:1.35&quot;&gt;&lt;font face=&quot;monospace&quot;&gt;Correct cap -&amp;gt; accommodate edge case (raise a little) -&amp;gt; repeat with no downward force -&amp;gt; effective ceiling ratchets up -&amp;gt; exposure far above authorised (BROKEN) ; Corrected: hard cap below agent + scheduled review reset&lt;/font&gt;&lt;/div&gt;" style="rounded=1;whiteSpace=wrap;html=1;fillColor=#ffffff;strokeColor=#c5d0db;spacing=11;align=left;verticalAlign=top;" vertex="1" parent="1">
          <mxGeometry x="30" y="1396.5" width="755" height="130" as="geometry" />
        </mxCell>
        <mxCell id="s7" value="&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:15px&quot;&gt;&lt;b&gt;EXAMPLE SCENARIO&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:13px;line-height:1.35&quot;&gt;A support agent may auto-approve refunds up to fifty dollars. A loyal customer is owed fifty-five, so the team lets it through; soon sixty for a comparable case, then seventy-five. Six months later the agent is routinely approving ninety-dollar refunds on its own, no one decided to set the cap there, and total refund exposure is well above what finance signed off on — until an audit notices the drift.&lt;/div&gt;" style="rounded=1;whiteSpace=wrap;html=1;fillColor=#ffffff;strokeColor=#c5d0db;spacing=11;align=left;verticalAlign=top;" vertex="1" parent="1">
          <mxGeometry x="815" y="1529.5" width="755" height="149" as="geometry" />
        </mxCell>
        <mxCell id="s8" value="&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:15px&quot;&gt;&lt;b&gt;CONSEQUENCES&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:13px;line-height:1.35&quot;&gt;&lt;b&gt;Benefits&lt;/b&gt;&lt;br&gt;&lt;br&gt;&amp;bull; Short-term: accommodating each reasonable edge case keeps customers happy and avoids sending cases to human approval.&lt;br&gt;&amp;bull; Naming it lets a team enforce the cap below the agent and audit the effective ceiling on a schedule instead of trusting the original setting.&lt;br&gt;&lt;br&gt;&lt;b&gt;Liabilities&lt;/b&gt;&lt;br&gt;&lt;br&gt;&amp;bull; Authorised financial exposure grows beyond what was approved, one justified exception at a time.&lt;br&gt;&amp;bull; The breach is detected late, if at all, because no single decision looked wrong.&lt;br&gt;&amp;bull; Resetting the cap after long drift is disruptive, since accommodated amounts have become the de-facto norm.&lt;br&gt;&amp;bull; An adversary who learns the cap drifts can walk it upward with a sequence of plausible edge cases.&lt;/div&gt;" style="rounded=1;whiteSpace=wrap;html=1;fillColor=#ffffff;strokeColor=#c5d0db;spacing=11;align=left;verticalAlign=top;" vertex="1" parent="1">
          <mxGeometry x="30" y="1540.5" width="755" height="320" as="geometry" />
        </mxCell>
        <mxCell id="s9" value="&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:15px&quot;&gt;&lt;b&gt;WHAT THIS PATTERN CONSTRAINS&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:13px;line-height:1.35&quot;&gt;The agent must not raise or relax its own autonomy cap; the ceiling is enforced as a hard limit below the agent, over-cap cases are escalated rather than accommodated, and the effective ceiling is reviewed on a schedule rather than allowed to drift.&lt;/div&gt;" style="rounded=1;whiteSpace=wrap;html=1;fillColor=#ffffff;strokeColor=#c5d0db;spacing=11;align=left;verticalAlign=top;" vertex="1" parent="1">
          <mxGeometry x="815" y="1692.5" width="755" height="111" as="geometry" />
        </mxCell>
        <mxCell id="s10" value="&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:15px&quot;&gt;&lt;b&gt;USE WHEN&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:13px;line-height:1.35&quot;&gt;&amp;bull; Recognising this failure when an agent&#39;s autonomy cap on spending or refunds rises over time through accumulated exceptions.&lt;br&gt;&amp;bull; Reviewing an agent whose limit is enforced by its own discretion rather than a hard constraint below it.&lt;br&gt;&amp;bull; Diagnosing financial exposure that grew without any explicit decision to raise the cap.&lt;/div&gt;" style="rounded=1;whiteSpace=wrap;html=1;fillColor=#ffffff;strokeColor=#c5d0db;spacing=11;align=left;verticalAlign=top;" vertex="1" parent="1">
          <mxGeometry x="30" y="1874.5" width="755" height="149" as="geometry" />
        </mxCell>
        <mxCell id="s11" value="&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:15px&quot;&gt;&lt;b&gt;DO NOT USE WHEN&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:13px;line-height:1.35&quot;&gt;&amp;bull; The cap is enforced as a hard limit the agent cannot change and over-cap cases always escalate.&lt;br&gt;&amp;bull; The effective ceiling is audited on a schedule and reset to the authorised value.&lt;br&gt;&amp;bull; There is no autonomous spending or refund authority to drift.&lt;/div&gt;" style="rounded=1;whiteSpace=wrap;html=1;fillColor=#ffffff;strokeColor=#c5d0db;spacing=11;align=left;verticalAlign=top;" vertex="1" parent="1">
          <mxGeometry x="815" y="1817.5" width="755" height="111" as="geometry" />
        </mxCell>
        <mxCell id="s12" value="&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:15px&quot;&gt;&lt;b&gt;COMPONENTS&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:13px;line-height:1.35&quot;&gt;&amp;bull; Autonomy cap — the configured ceiling on what the agent may settle on its own&lt;br&gt;&amp;bull; Accommodation pressure — the stream of reasonable edge cases that nudge the limit up&lt;br&gt;&amp;bull; Missing hard limit — the absent below-agent enforcement the agent cannot relax&lt;br&gt;&amp;bull; Missing ceiling review — the absent scheduled audit of the effective approved amount&lt;br&gt;&amp;bull; Financial exposure — the authorised risk that grows as the cap drifts&lt;/div&gt;" style="rounded=1;whiteSpace=wrap;html=1;fillColor=#ffffff;strokeColor=#c5d0db;spacing=11;align=left;verticalAlign=top;" vertex="1" parent="1">
          <mxGeometry x="30" y="2037.5" width="755" height="149" as="geometry" />
        </mxCell>
        <mxCell id="s13" value="&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:15px&quot;&gt;&lt;b&gt;TOOLS&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:13px;line-height:1.35&quot;&gt;&amp;bull; Payment or refund API — where the hard cap should be enforced beneath the agent&lt;br&gt;&amp;bull; Approval queue — the escalation path over-cap cases should take instead of accommodation&lt;br&gt;&amp;bull; Exposure audit — the scheduled review of the effective ceiling and approved-amount distribution&lt;/div&gt;" style="rounded=1;whiteSpace=wrap;html=1;fillColor=#ffffff;strokeColor=#c5d0db;spacing=11;align=left;verticalAlign=top;" vertex="1" parent="1">
          <mxGeometry x="815" y="1942.5" width="755" height="111" as="geometry" />
        </mxCell>
        <mxCell id="s14" value="&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:15px&quot;&gt;&lt;b&gt;FAILURE MODES&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:13px;line-height:1.35&quot;&gt;&amp;bull; Accommodation ratchet — each over-limit exception nudges the effective ceiling up and none brings it back.&lt;br&gt;&amp;bull; Soft cap — the limit lives in the prompt or the agent&#39;s discretion rather than as a hard enforced constraint.&lt;br&gt;&amp;bull; No ceiling review — the effective approval ceiling is never audited, so drift accumulates unseen.&lt;br&gt;&amp;bull; Exception absorption — a one-off exception is folded into the standing limit instead of logged as a one-off.&lt;/div&gt;" style="rounded=1;whiteSpace=wrap;html=1;fillColor=#ffffff;strokeColor=#c5d0db;spacing=11;align=left;verticalAlign=top;" vertex="1" parent="1">
          <mxGeometry x="30" y="2200.5" width="755" height="187" as="geometry" />
        </mxCell>
        <mxCell id="s15" value="&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:15px&quot;&gt;&lt;b&gt;EVALUATION METRICS&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:13px;line-height:1.35&quot;&gt;&amp;bull; Effective-vs-authorised ceiling gap — distance between what the agent actually approves and the signed-off cap&lt;br&gt;&amp;bull; Cap-drift rate — change in the effective approval ceiling over time&lt;br&gt;&amp;bull; Over-cap escalation rate — share of over-limit cases escalated rather than accommodated&lt;br&gt;&amp;bull; Exposure variance — growth in total autonomous settlement value not explained by volume&lt;/div&gt;" style="rounded=1;whiteSpace=wrap;html=1;fillColor=#ffffff;strokeColor=#c5d0db;spacing=11;align=left;verticalAlign=top;" vertex="1" parent="1">
          <mxGeometry x="815" y="2067.5" width="755" height="149" as="geometry" />
        </mxCell>
        <mxCell id="s16" value="&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:15px&quot;&gt;&lt;b&gt;KNOWN USES&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:13px;line-height:1.35&quot;&gt;&amp;bull; &lt;b&gt;&lt;a href=&quot;https://www.digitalapplied.com/blog/ai-customer-support-anti-patterns-deflection-mistakes-2026&quot;&gt;AI customer-support anti-pattern catalog (Refund Drift)&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/b&gt; &lt;i&gt;available&lt;/i&gt; — Names &#39;Refund Drift&#39;, where the approval threshold drifts upward over time as the agent accommodates edge cases.&lt;br&gt;&amp;bull; &lt;b&gt;&lt;a href=&quot;https://arxiv.org/pdf/2601.04170&quot;&gt;Agent Drift study&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/b&gt; &lt;i&gt;available&lt;/i&gt; — Documents agents incrementally relaxing oversight thresholds in response to pressure over extended interactions.&lt;/div&gt;" style="rounded=1;whiteSpace=wrap;html=1;fillColor=#ffffff;strokeColor=#c5d0db;spacing=11;align=left;verticalAlign=top;" vertex="1" parent="1">
          <mxGeometry x="30" y="2401.5" width="755" height="149" as="geometry" />
        </mxCell>
        <mxCell id="s17" value="&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:15px&quot;&gt;&lt;b&gt;RELATED PATTERNS&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:13px;line-height:1.35&quot;&gt;&amp;bull; &lt;i&gt;complements&lt;/i&gt; &lt;b&gt;Risk-Tiered Action Autonomy&lt;/b&gt; — Risk-tiered autonomy sets the materiality cap; refund threshold drift is the temporal failure where that cap silently creeps upward at runtime.&lt;br&gt;&amp;bull; &lt;i&gt;complements&lt;/i&gt; &lt;b&gt;Session-Scoped Payment Authorization&lt;/b&gt; — A session cap is the static spending control; this is the failure where the effective cap drifts above it over time through accommodation.&lt;br&gt;&amp;bull; &lt;i&gt;complements&lt;/i&gt; &lt;b&gt;Guardrail Erosion Through Compaction&lt;/b&gt; — Both are guardrail-decay anti-patterns; compaction decays a rule by summarisation, threshold drift decays a numeric cap by accommodation precedent.&lt;/div&gt;" style="rounded=1;whiteSpace=wrap;html=1;fillColor=#ffffff;strokeColor=#c5d0db;spacing=11;align=left;verticalAlign=top;" vertex="1" parent="1">
          <mxGeometry x="815" y="2230.5" width="755" height="168" as="geometry" />
        </mxCell>
        <mxCell id="s18" value="&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:15px&quot;&gt;&lt;b&gt;REFERENCES&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:13px;line-height:1.35&quot;&gt;&amp;bull; &lt;a href=&quot;https://www.digitalapplied.com/blog/ai-customer-support-anti-patterns-deflection-mistakes-2026&quot;&gt;AI Customer Support Anti-Patterns: Deflection Mistakes 2026&lt;/a&gt; — 2026&lt;br&gt;&amp;bull; &lt;a href=&quot;https://arxiv.org/pdf/2601.04170&quot;&gt;Agent Drift: Quantifying Behavioral Degradation in Multi-Agent LLM Systems Over Extended Interactions&lt;/a&gt; — 2026&lt;/div&gt;" style="rounded=1;whiteSpace=wrap;html=1;fillColor=#ffffff;strokeColor=#c5d0db;spacing=11;align=left;verticalAlign=top;" vertex="1" parent="1">
          <mxGeometry x="30" y="2564.5" width="755" height="130" as="geometry" />
        </mxCell>
      </root>
    </mxGraphModel>
  </diagram>
