        <mxCell id="arch-caption" value="&lt;i&gt;A small RL-trained conductor dispatches sub-tasks across frozen frontier workers; only the conductor learns.&lt;/i&gt;" style="text;html=1;align=center;verticalAlign=middle;strokeColor=none;fillColor=none;fontSize=12;fontColor=#52606d;" vertex="1" parent="1">
          <mxGeometry x="65" y="883.32" width="1470" height="32" as="geometry" />
        </mxCell>
        <mxCell id="s0" value="&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:15px&quot;&gt;&lt;b&gt;INTENT&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:13px;line-height:1.35&quot;&gt;Train a small meta-model with reinforcement learning to dispatch sub-tasks across a pool of frontier LLM workers, learning the communication topology end-to-end and allowing the conductor to recursively invoke itself as a worker.&lt;/div&gt;" style="rounded=1;whiteSpace=wrap;html=1;fillColor=#ffffff;strokeColor=#c5d0db;spacing=11;align=left;verticalAlign=top;" vertex="1" parent="1">
          <mxGeometry x="30" y="958.32" width="755" height="111" as="geometry" />
        </mxCell>
        <mxCell id="s1" value="&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:15px&quot;&gt;&lt;b&gt;CONTEXT&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:13px;line-height:1.35&quot;&gt;A team operates a production multi-agent stack that dispatches sub-tasks across a heterogeneous pool of frontier large language models from different vendors — one strong at long-context summarisation, one at code synthesis, one at image understanding — plus a set of tools. The routing logic between them is usually a hand-written tree of if-this-then-that rules with prompt-time hints. Tasks span many domains and the pool of workers keeps changing as vendors release and deprecate models.&lt;/div&gt;" style="rounded=1;whiteSpace=wrap;html=1;fillColor=#ffffff;strokeColor=#c5d0db;spacing=11;align=left;verticalAlign=top;" vertex="1" parent="1">
          <mxGeometry x="815" y="958.32" width="755" height="149" as="geometry" />
        </mxCell>
        <mxCell id="s2" value="&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:15px&quot;&gt;&lt;b&gt;PROBLEM&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:13px;line-height:1.35&quot;&gt;Hand-coded orchestrator logic does not generalise across the breadth of incoming tasks: static heuristics for which model gets which sub-task miss the task-specific signals that actually predict the right routing, and the rules grow stale every time the worker pool changes. Using a frontier model itself as the orchestrator is expensive on every dispatch step and still does not learn from the reward signal that finished tasks provide. There is no obvious place for the system to improve its own decomposition strategy from experience, so every gain in routing quality requires another round of human rule editing.&lt;/div&gt;" style="rounded=1;whiteSpace=wrap;html=1;fillColor=#ffffff;strokeColor=#c5d0db;spacing=11;align=left;verticalAlign=top;" vertex="1" parent="1">
          <mxGeometry x="30" y="1083.32" width="755" height="187" as="geometry" />
        </mxCell>
        <mxCell id="s3" value="&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:15px&quot;&gt;&lt;b&gt;FORCES&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:13px;line-height:1.35&quot;&gt;&amp;bull; Routing decisions are task-dependent and the right worker for a sub-task is not knowable from static rules alone.&lt;br&gt;&amp;bull; Frontier models are expensive to use as the always-on orchestrator on every dispatch step.&lt;br&gt;&amp;bull; The worker pool changes — new models arrive, old ones are deprecated — and hand-coded routing must be rewritten each time.&lt;br&gt;&amp;bull; Reward signal from task outcomes is available but unused by static orchestration.&lt;br&gt;&amp;bull; Some sub-tasks are themselves decomposable, so the orchestrator must be able to recurse without infinite expansion.&lt;/div&gt;" style="rounded=1;whiteSpace=wrap;html=1;fillColor=#ffffff;strokeColor=#c5d0db;spacing=11;align=left;verticalAlign=top;" vertex="1" parent="1">
          <mxGeometry x="815" y="1121.32" width="755" height="206" as="geometry" />
        </mxCell>
        <mxCell id="s4" value="&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:15px&quot;&gt;&lt;b&gt;THEREFORE&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:13px;line-height:1.35&quot;&gt;Therefore: train a small meta-model end-to-end with reinforcement learning to emit natural-language sub-task instructions, choose a worker from the pool for each instruction, and recursively call itself when a sub-task is itself decomposable, so the communication topology is learned from task rewards rather than hand-coded.&lt;/div&gt;" style="rounded=1;whiteSpace=wrap;html=1;fillColor=#ffffff;strokeColor=#c5d0db;spacing=11;align=left;verticalAlign=top;" vertex="1" parent="1">
          <mxGeometry x="30" y="1284.32" width="755" height="130" as="geometry" />
        </mxCell>
        <mxCell id="s5" value="&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:15px&quot;&gt;&lt;b&gt;SOLUTION&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:13px;line-height:1.35&quot;&gt;A small conductor model (often in the 7B–13B range) sits in front of a pool of worker LLMs and tools. On each step the conductor emits a natural-language sub-task instruction and a worker selection; the worker is run, its output returned, and the conductor decides the next move. The conductor is trained with reinforcement learning against final task rewards: it learns which workers handle which sub-task shapes, how to phrase the hand-off, when to stop, and when to recursively dispatch a sub-task back to itself as a worker. Recursion is bounded by a depth limit and a step budget. Workers remain frozen frontier models; only the conductor is trained.&lt;/div&gt;" style="rounded=1;whiteSpace=wrap;html=1;fillColor=#ffffff;strokeColor=#c5d0db;spacing=11;align=left;verticalAlign=top;" vertex="1" parent="1">
          <mxGeometry x="815" y="1341.32" width="755" height="187" as="geometry" />
        </mxCell>
        <mxCell id="s6" value="&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:15px&quot;&gt;&lt;b&gt;STRUCTURE&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:13px;line-height:1.35&quot;&gt;&lt;font face=&quot;monospace&quot;&gt;User task -&amp;gt; Conductor (small RL-trained meta-model) -&amp;gt; (sub-task instruction, worker id) -&amp;gt; Worker pool {frontier LLMs, tools, conductor-as-worker} -&amp;gt; worker output -&amp;gt; Conductor next step ... -&amp;gt; final answer. Reward from task outcome flows back into the conductor&amp;#x27;s policy only.&lt;/font&gt;&lt;/div&gt;" style="rounded=1;whiteSpace=wrap;html=1;fillColor=#ffffff;strokeColor=#c5d0db;spacing=11;align=left;verticalAlign=top;" vertex="1" parent="1">
          <mxGeometry x="30" y="1428.32" width="755" height="130" as="geometry" />
        </mxCell>
        <mxCell id="s7" value="&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:15px&quot;&gt;&lt;b&gt;EXAMPLE SCENARIO&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:13px;line-height:1.35&quot;&gt;A product routes user tasks across four frontier models plus a code-execution tool. The team replaces its rule-based router with a 7B conductor trained on six months of task outcomes. The conductor learns that long-context summarisation goes to one vendor, code synthesis to another, image understanding to a third, and that some research tasks should be broken into three sub-tasks where the conductor recursively calls itself as the second-level planner. Average cost-per-task drops, and routing improves without anyone editing rules.&lt;/div&gt;" style="rounded=1;whiteSpace=wrap;html=1;fillColor=#ffffff;strokeColor=#c5d0db;spacing=11;align=left;verticalAlign=top;" vertex="1" parent="1">
          <mxGeometry x="815" y="1542.32" width="755" height="168" as="geometry" />
        </mxCell>
        <mxCell id="s8" value="&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:15px&quot;&gt;&lt;b&gt;CONSEQUENCES&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:13px;line-height:1.35&quot;&gt;&lt;b&gt;Benefits&lt;/b&gt;&lt;br&gt;&lt;br&gt;&amp;bull; Routing improves from experience instead of by hand-editing rules.&lt;br&gt;&amp;bull; Cheap meta-model on the hot path; frontier models are only called as workers when the conductor selects them.&lt;br&gt;&amp;bull; Recursive self-dispatch handles decomposable sub-tasks without a separate planner agent.&lt;br&gt;&amp;bull; Worker pool churn is absorbed by retraining the conductor rather than rewriting routing logic.&lt;br&gt;&lt;br&gt;&lt;b&gt;Liabilities&lt;/b&gt;&lt;br&gt;&lt;br&gt;&amp;bull; Requires a reward signal and an RL training pipeline, which most teams do not have in-house.&lt;br&gt;&amp;bull; Conductor policy can be opaque; a learned routing tree is harder to audit than a written one.&lt;br&gt;&amp;bull; Recursive self-dispatch needs strict depth and budget caps or it can fan out aggressively.&lt;br&gt;&amp;bull; Worker drift (a vendor updates a model) silently changes the policy&#39;s effective action semantics.&lt;/div&gt;" style="rounded=1;whiteSpace=wrap;html=1;fillColor=#ffffff;strokeColor=#c5d0db;spacing=11;align=left;verticalAlign=top;" vertex="1" parent="1">
          <mxGeometry x="30" y="1572.32" width="755" height="320" as="geometry" />
        </mxCell>
        <mxCell id="s9" value="&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:15px&quot;&gt;&lt;b&gt;WHAT THIS PATTERN CONSTRAINS&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:13px;line-height:1.35&quot;&gt;The conductor must respect a hard recursion-depth cap and a step budget on every task, must emit explicit sub-task instructions and worker selections rather than free-form thoughts, and must not invoke workers outside the registered pool — including its own untrained ancestor models.&lt;/div&gt;" style="rounded=1;whiteSpace=wrap;html=1;fillColor=#ffffff;strokeColor=#c5d0db;spacing=11;align=left;verticalAlign=top;" vertex="1" parent="1">
          <mxGeometry x="815" y="1724.32" width="755" height="111" as="geometry" />
        </mxCell>
        <mxCell id="s10" value="&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:15px&quot;&gt;&lt;b&gt;USE WHEN&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:13px;line-height:1.35&quot;&gt;&amp;bull; A heterogeneous frontier-model worker pool is in production and routing matters.&lt;br&gt;&amp;bull; Task-outcome rewards are observable at scale.&lt;br&gt;&amp;bull; An RL training pipeline (or partner) is available.&lt;/div&gt;" style="rounded=1;whiteSpace=wrap;html=1;fillColor=#ffffff;strokeColor=#c5d0db;spacing=11;align=left;verticalAlign=top;" vertex="1" parent="1">
          <mxGeometry x="30" y="1906.32" width="755" height="111" as="geometry" />
        </mxCell>
        <mxCell id="s11" value="&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:15px&quot;&gt;&lt;b&gt;DO NOT USE WHEN&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:13px;line-height:1.35&quot;&gt;&amp;bull; Routing is dominated by one model and a static cascade suffices.&lt;br&gt;&amp;bull; Reward signal is not available or is too noisy to learn from.&lt;br&gt;&amp;bull; Audit and explainability requirements demand human-readable routing rules.&lt;/div&gt;" style="rounded=1;whiteSpace=wrap;html=1;fillColor=#ffffff;strokeColor=#c5d0db;spacing=11;align=left;verticalAlign=top;" vertex="1" parent="1">
          <mxGeometry x="815" y="1849.32" width="755" height="111" as="geometry" />
        </mxCell>
        <mxCell id="s12" value="&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:15px&quot;&gt;&lt;b&gt;COMPONENTS&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:13px;line-height:1.35&quot;&gt;&amp;bull; RL-trained conductor — small 7B-13B meta-model that emits sub-task instructions and picks a worker&lt;br&gt;&amp;bull; Worker pool — heterogeneous frozen frontier LLMs and tools, plus the conductor invocable as a worker&lt;br&gt;&amp;bull; Reward signal — end-of-task outcome score that updates the conductor&#39;s policy only&lt;br&gt;&amp;bull; Recursion-depth cap — hard limit preventing infinite self-dispatch fan-out&lt;br&gt;&amp;bull; Step budget — global cap on conductor steps per task&lt;/div&gt;" style="rounded=1;whiteSpace=wrap;html=1;fillColor=#ffffff;strokeColor=#c5d0db;spacing=11;align=left;verticalAlign=top;" vertex="1" parent="1">
          <mxGeometry x="30" y="2031.32" width="755" height="168" as="geometry" />
        </mxCell>
        <mxCell id="s13" value="&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:15px&quot;&gt;&lt;b&gt;TOOLS&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:13px;line-height:1.35&quot;&gt;&amp;bull; Multiple frontier LLM APIs — heterogeneous workers (one strong at long-context, one at code, one at vision)&lt;br&gt;&amp;bull; RL training pipeline — off-policy or on-policy trainer that updates the conductor against task rewards&lt;br&gt;&amp;bull; Tool catalogue — non-LLM tools (code execution, search) registered as worker actions&lt;br&gt;&amp;bull; Trace logger — per-task record of dispatches, worker outputs, and rewards for training&lt;/div&gt;" style="rounded=1;whiteSpace=wrap;html=1;fillColor=#ffffff;strokeColor=#c5d0db;spacing=11;align=left;verticalAlign=top;" vertex="1" parent="1">
          <mxGeometry x="815" y="1974.32" width="755" height="168" as="geometry" />
        </mxCell>
        <mxCell id="s14" value="&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:15px&quot;&gt;&lt;b&gt;FAILURE MODES&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:13px;line-height:1.35&quot;&gt;&amp;bull; Recursive fan-out — the conductor keeps dispatching sub-tasks back to itself, and without strict caps the task spreads across many calls.&lt;br&gt;&amp;bull; Silent worker drift — a vendor updates a worker model, and the learned routing now sends sub-tasks to a worker that behaves differently.&lt;br&gt;&amp;bull; Unauditable routing — a bad dispatch occurs, and nobody can read the learned policy to see why that worker was chosen.&lt;br&gt;&amp;bull; Reward hacking — a weak final-task reward teaches the conductor routes that score well but do not solve the task.&lt;/div&gt;" style="rounded=1;whiteSpace=wrap;html=1;fillColor=#ffffff;strokeColor=#c5d0db;spacing=11;align=left;verticalAlign=top;" vertex="1" parent="1">
          <mxGeometry x="30" y="2213.32" width="755" height="206" as="geometry" />
        </mxCell>
        <mxCell id="s15" value="&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:15px&quot;&gt;&lt;b&gt;EVALUATION METRICS&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:13px;line-height:1.35&quot;&gt;&amp;bull; Average cost-per-task — drop after switching from rule-based router to trained conductor&lt;br&gt;&amp;bull; Routing-accuracy lift — share of sub-tasks sent to the right worker versus the hand-coded baseline&lt;br&gt;&amp;bull; Recursion-depth distribution — how often self-dispatch is used and against the cap&lt;br&gt;&amp;bull; Reward improvement curve — policy improvement over training iterations&lt;br&gt;&amp;bull; Worker-drift sensitivity — outcome degradation when an upstream vendor silently updates a worker model&lt;/div&gt;" style="rounded=1;whiteSpace=wrap;html=1;fillColor=#ffffff;strokeColor=#c5d0db;spacing=11;align=left;verticalAlign=top;" vertex="1" parent="1">
          <mxGeometry x="815" y="2156.32" width="755" height="168" as="geometry" />
        </mxCell>
        <mxCell id="s16" value="&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:15px&quot;&gt;&lt;b&gt;KNOWN USES&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:13px;line-height:1.35&quot;&gt;&amp;bull; &lt;b&gt;&lt;a href=&quot;https://sakana.ai/fugu-beta/&quot;&gt;Sakana AI Fugu&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/b&gt; &lt;i&gt;available&lt;/i&gt; — Commercial RL-trained conductor orchestrating across frontier LLM workers; beta announced April 2026.&lt;br&gt;&amp;bull; &lt;b&gt;&lt;a href=&quot;https://sakana.ai/learning-to-orchestrate/&quot;&gt;Sakana AI Conductor + Trinity&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/b&gt; &lt;i&gt;available&lt;/i&gt; — Research line on learning to orchestrate, accepted at ICLR 2026.&lt;/div&gt;" style="rounded=1;whiteSpace=wrap;html=1;fillColor=#ffffff;strokeColor=#c5d0db;spacing=11;align=left;verticalAlign=top;" vertex="1" parent="1">
          <mxGeometry x="30" y="2433.32" width="755" height="130" as="geometry" />
        </mxCell>
        <mxCell id="s17" value="&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:15px&quot;&gt;&lt;b&gt;RELATED PATTERNS&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:13px;line-height:1.35&quot;&gt;&amp;bull; &lt;i&gt;specialises&lt;/i&gt; &lt;b&gt;Orchestrator-Workers&lt;/b&gt; — Specialises orchestrator-workers with an RL-trained meta-model instead of rule-based routing.&lt;br&gt;&amp;bull; &lt;i&gt;alternative-to&lt;/i&gt; &lt;b&gt;Multi-Model Routing&lt;/b&gt; — Multi-model-routing uses static cascades or heuristics; this pattern learns the routing policy.&lt;br&gt;&amp;bull; &lt;i&gt;alternative-to&lt;/i&gt; &lt;b&gt;Mixture of Experts Routing&lt;/b&gt; — MoE routing selects experts inside one model; this pattern routes across whole frontier models.&lt;br&gt;&amp;bull; &lt;i&gt;complements&lt;/i&gt; &lt;b&gt;Agent-as-Tool Embedding&lt;/b&gt; — Workers in the pool may themselves be agents wrapped as tools.&lt;br&gt;&amp;bull; &lt;i&gt;complements&lt;/i&gt; &lt;b&gt;Served-Policy Anchoring&lt;/b&gt; — An orchestrator trained with reinforcement learning inherits exactly this seam, since its rollouts come from an inference engine and its gradients from a training engine.&lt;/div&gt;" style="rounded=1;whiteSpace=wrap;html=1;fillColor=#ffffff;strokeColor=#c5d0db;spacing=11;align=left;verticalAlign=top;" vertex="1" parent="1">
          <mxGeometry x="815" y="2338.32" width="755" height="263" as="geometry" />
        </mxCell>
        <mxCell id="s18" value="&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:15px&quot;&gt;&lt;b&gt;REFERENCES&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:13px;line-height:1.35&quot;&gt;&amp;bull; &lt;a href=&quot;https://sakana.ai/learning-to-orchestrate/&quot;&gt;Learning to Orchestrate&lt;/a&gt; — Sakana AI, 2025&lt;br&gt;&amp;bull; &lt;a href=&quot;https://sakana.ai/fugu-beta/&quot;&gt;Fugu beta&lt;/a&gt; — Sakana AI, 2026&lt;/div&gt;" style="rounded=1;whiteSpace=wrap;html=1;fillColor=#ffffff;strokeColor=#c5d0db;spacing=11;align=left;verticalAlign=top;" vertex="1" parent="1">
          <mxGeometry x="30" y="2577.32" width="755" height="92" as="geometry" />
        </mxCell>
      </root>
    </mxGraphModel>
  </diagram>
