        <UserObject label="fi" mermaidId="e:R-&gt;GP#0" mermaidBaseStyle="curved=1;dashed=1;dashPattern=2 3;fixDash=1;startArrow=cross;endArrow=block;endSize=7;strokeColor=light-dark(#333333,#cccccc);html=1;fontSize=16;labelBackgroundColor=light-dark(#E8E8E88D,#2a2a2a8D);fontFamily=Trebuchet MS,Verdana,Arial,sans-serif;fontColor=light-dark(#333333,#cccccc);exitX=1;exitY=0.57;entryX=0.5;entryY=0;" mermaidBaseValue="fi" id="m16">
          <mxCell edge="1" parent="1" source="m1" style="curved=1;dashed=1;dashPattern=2 3;fixDash=1;startArrow=cross;endArrow=block;endSize=7;strokeColor=light-dark(#333333,#cccccc);html=1;fontSize=16;labelBackgroundColor=light-dark(#E8E8E88D,#2a2a2a8D);fontFamily=Trebuchet MS,Verdana,Arial,sans-serif;fontColor=light-dark(#333333,#cccccc);exitX=1;exitY=0.57;entryX=0.5;entryY=0;" target="m7">
            <mxGeometry relative="1" as="geometry">
              <Array as="points">
                <mxPoint x="1215.84" y="409.39" />
              </Array>
            </mxGeometry>
          </mxCell>
        </UserObject>
        <UserObject label="" mermaidId="e:GP-&gt;Many#0" mermaidBaseStyle="curved=1;startArrow=none;endArrow=block;endSize=7;strokeColor=light-dark(#333333,#cccccc);exitX=0.14;exitY=1;entryX=0.5;entryY=0;" mermaidBaseValue="" id="m17">
          <mxCell edge="1" parent="1" source="m7" style="curved=1;startArrow=none;endArrow=block;endSize=7;strokeColor=light-dark(#333333,#cccccc);exitX=0.14;exitY=1;entryX=0.5;entryY=0;" target="m8">
            <mxGeometry relative="1" as="geometry">
              <Array as="points">
                <mxPoint x="1048.75" y="516.4" />
              </Array>
            </mxGeometry>
          </mxCell>
        </UserObject>
        <UserObject label="" mermaidId="e:GP-&gt;Overlay#0" mermaidBaseStyle="curved=1;startArrow=none;endArrow=block;endSize=7;strokeColor=light-dark(#333333,#cccccc);exitX=0.86;exitY=1;entryX=0.5;entryY=0;" mermaidBaseValue="" id="m18">
          <mxCell edge="1" parent="1" source="m7" style="curved=1;startArrow=none;endArrow=block;endSize=7;strokeColor=light-dark(#333333,#cccccc);exitX=0.86;exitY=1;entryX=0.5;entryY=0;" target="m9">
            <mxGeometry relative="1" as="geometry">
              <Array as="points">
                <mxPoint x="1382.93" y="516.4" />
              </Array>
            </mxGeometry>
          </mxCell>
        </UserObject>
        <mxCell id="s0" value="&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:15px&quot;&gt;&lt;b&gt;INTENT&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:13px;line-height:1.35&quot;&gt;Anti-pattern: pre-allocate roles (manager, coder, designer, researcher) across a fixed set of typed sub-agents and route tasks to them by role label.&lt;/div&gt;" style="rounded=1;whiteSpace=wrap;html=1;fillColor=#ffffff;strokeColor=#c5d0db;spacing=11;align=left;verticalAlign=top;" vertex="1" parent="1">
          <mxGeometry x="30" y="635.56" width="755" height="92" as="geometry" />
        </mxCell>
        <mxCell id="s1" value="&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:15px&quot;&gt;&lt;b&gt;CONTEXT&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:13px;line-height:1.35&quot;&gt;A team is designing a multi-agent system and, before seeing real workloads, decides on a fixed set of roles — typically manager, researcher, coder, designer, reviewer — and gives each role its own narrow system prompt and restricted tool palette. The orchestrator routes each task to a sub-agent by matching the task to a role label. The architecture diagram looks like clean separation of concerns, and each specialist agent is cheaper per call than a general-purpose one.&lt;/div&gt;" style="rounded=1;whiteSpace=wrap;html=1;fillColor=#ffffff;strokeColor=#c5d0db;spacing=11;align=left;verticalAlign=top;" vertex="1" parent="1">
          <mxGeometry x="815" y="635.56" width="755" height="149" as="geometry" />
        </mxCell>
        <mxCell id="s2" value="&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:15px&quot;&gt;&lt;b&gt;PROBLEM&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:13px;line-height:1.35&quot;&gt;Real workloads do not partition cleanly into the roles the architect imagined in advance. Tasks that fall between two roles get squeezed into whichever label is closest, and the chosen specialist underperforms because its tool palette is missing what the task actually needs. Adding a new role means changing the architecture rather than parameters, and capability-equal parallelism — running many fully capable, identical sub-agents in parallel on the same subtask — is structurally impossible because no sub-agent has the full tool set.&lt;/div&gt;" style="rounded=1;whiteSpace=wrap;html=1;fillColor=#ffffff;strokeColor=#c5d0db;spacing=11;align=left;verticalAlign=top;" vertex="1" parent="1">
          <mxGeometry x="30" y="741.56" width="755" height="168" as="geometry" />
        </mxCell>
        <mxCell id="s3" value="&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:15px&quot;&gt;&lt;b&gt;FORCES&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:13px;line-height:1.35&quot;&gt;&amp;bull; Role labels make the architecture diagrammable and look like sound separation of concerns.&lt;br&gt;&amp;bull; Cheaper per-call specialised prompts can outperform a single generalist on narrow tasks.&lt;br&gt;&amp;bull; Real workloads do not partition cleanly into the roles named in advance.&lt;br&gt;&amp;bull; Capability-equal fan-out (clone-fan-out-research) requires general-purpose sub-agents, which a typed role table forbids.&lt;/div&gt;" style="rounded=1;whiteSpace=wrap;html=1;fillColor=#ffffff;strokeColor=#c5d0db;spacing=11;align=left;verticalAlign=top;" vertex="1" parent="1">
          <mxGeometry x="815" y="798.56" width="755" height="149" as="geometry" />
        </mxCell>
        <mxCell id="s4" value="&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:15px&quot;&gt;&lt;b&gt;THEREFORE&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:13px;line-height:1.35&quot;&gt;Therefore: prefer general-purpose sub-agents with the full tool palette over a fixed role table, so that the system can decompose tasks the architect did not anticipate and can run capability-equal parallel instances against the same subtask.&lt;/div&gt;" style="rounded=1;whiteSpace=wrap;html=1;fillColor=#ffffff;strokeColor=#c5d0db;spacing=11;align=left;verticalAlign=top;" vertex="1" parent="1">
          <mxGeometry x="30" y="923.56" width="755" height="111" as="geometry" />
        </mxCell>
        <mxCell id="s5" value="&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:15px&quot;&gt;&lt;b&gt;SOLUTION&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:13px;line-height:1.35&quot;&gt;Don&#39;t bake role types into the architecture. Use one general-purpose sub-agent shape with the full tool palette and let the orchestrator route by task content, not role label. When specialisation pays, scope it per-call (system-prompt overlay, tool subset for this task) rather than per-agent-type. For wide tasks, prefer capability-equal fan-out over typed crews. See clone-fan-out-research, role-assignment (the valid form: per-call persona, not per-agent type), supervisor.&lt;/div&gt;" style="rounded=1;whiteSpace=wrap;html=1;fillColor=#ffffff;strokeColor=#c5d0db;spacing=11;align=left;verticalAlign=top;" vertex="1" parent="1">
          <mxGeometry x="815" y="961.56" width="755" height="149" as="geometry" />
        </mxCell>
        <mxCell id="s6" value="&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:15px&quot;&gt;&lt;b&gt;STRUCTURE&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:13px;line-height:1.35&quot;&gt;&lt;font face=&quot;monospace&quot;&gt;Anti-pattern: task -&amp;gt; route by role label -&amp;gt; fixed typed sub-agent with partial tool palette -&amp;gt; mismatch. Corrected: task -&amp;gt; orchestrator routes by content -&amp;gt; general sub-agent with full palette + per-call prompt overlay or tool subset -&amp;gt; capability-equal fan-out for wide tasks.&lt;/font&gt;&lt;/div&gt;" style="rounded=1;whiteSpace=wrap;html=1;fillColor=#ffffff;strokeColor=#c5d0db;spacing=11;align=left;verticalAlign=top;" vertex="1" parent="1">
          <mxGeometry x="30" y="1048.56" width="755" height="130" as="geometry" />
        </mxCell>
        <mxCell id="s7" value="&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:15px&quot;&gt;&lt;b&gt;EXAMPLE SCENARIO&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:13px;line-height:1.35&quot;&gt;A team builds a multi-agent product with manager, researcher, coder, and designer sub-agents, each with its own tightly scoped tool palette. A new task type — multilingual marketing copy with light data analysis — fits none of the roles, so it is forced into the researcher role and underperforms. The team rewrites the system: one general-purpose sub-agent shape with the full tool palette, and a per-call system-prompt overlay when specialisation pays. The marketing task now succeeds; a later wide-research task fans out twenty capability-equal instances against the same prompt, which the typed crew could not have done.&lt;/div&gt;" style="rounded=1;whiteSpace=wrap;html=1;fillColor=#ffffff;strokeColor=#c5d0db;spacing=11;align=left;verticalAlign=top;" vertex="1" parent="1">
          <mxGeometry x="815" y="1124.56" width="755" height="187" as="geometry" />
        </mxCell>
        <mxCell id="s8" value="&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:15px&quot;&gt;&lt;b&gt;CONSEQUENCES&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:13px;line-height:1.35&quot;&gt;&lt;b&gt;Benefits&lt;/b&gt;&lt;br&gt;&lt;br&gt;&amp;bull; Short-term: a crew of named roles is easy to explain and mirrors a human team chart.&lt;br&gt;&amp;bull; Naming it lets a team scope specialisation per call instead of per agent type.&lt;br&gt;&lt;br&gt;&lt;b&gt;Liabilities&lt;/b&gt;&lt;br&gt;&lt;br&gt;&amp;bull; Tasks outside the foreseen role table get squeezed into the nearest label.&lt;br&gt;&amp;bull; Capability-equal parallelism is impossible by construction.&lt;br&gt;&amp;bull; Adding a new role requires re-architecting rather than parameter changes.&lt;br&gt;&amp;bull; The role labels invite team boundaries (the coder agent&#39;s team, the designer agent&#39;s team) that ossify the system.&lt;/div&gt;" style="rounded=1;whiteSpace=wrap;html=1;fillColor=#ffffff;strokeColor=#c5d0db;spacing=11;align=left;verticalAlign=top;" vertex="1" parent="1">
          <mxGeometry x="30" y="1192.56" width="755" height="282" as="geometry" />
        </mxCell>
        <mxCell id="s9" value="&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:15px&quot;&gt;&lt;b&gt;WHAT THIS PATTERN CONSTRAINS&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:13px;line-height:1.35&quot;&gt;Avoiding it forbids baking the org chart into the architecture: sub-agent capability must not be fixed by role label; specialisation belongs in per-call overlays, not in a static typology the orchestrator routes by.&lt;/div&gt;" style="rounded=1;whiteSpace=wrap;html=1;fillColor=#ffffff;strokeColor=#c5d0db;spacing=11;align=left;verticalAlign=top;" vertex="1" parent="1">
          <mxGeometry x="815" y="1325.56" width="755" height="111" as="geometry" />
        </mxCell>
        <mxCell id="s10" value="&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:15px&quot;&gt;&lt;b&gt;USE WHEN&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:13px;line-height:1.35&quot;&gt;&amp;bull; Cite this entry when a design pre-allocates manager/coder/designer/researcher agents and routes by label.&lt;br&gt;&amp;bull; You are already here if tasks outside the anticipated role table have nowhere to go, or role agents idle while one is the bottleneck.&lt;br&gt;&amp;bull; Use one general-purpose sub-agent shape and scope specialisation per call (system-prompt overlay, tool subset).&lt;/div&gt;" style="rounded=1;whiteSpace=wrap;html=1;fillColor=#ffffff;strokeColor=#c5d0db;spacing=11;align=left;verticalAlign=top;" vertex="1" parent="1">
          <mxGeometry x="30" y="1488.56" width="755" height="168" as="geometry" />
        </mxCell>
        <mxCell id="s11" value="&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:15px&quot;&gt;&lt;b&gt;DO NOT USE WHEN&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:13px;line-height:1.35&quot;&gt;&amp;bull; The task space is well-understood and stable across roles — even then, per-call overlays beat baked-in role types.&lt;br&gt;&amp;bull; Capability-equal parallelism (many identical agents working in parallel) is on the roadmap.&lt;br&gt;&amp;bull; The architecture is expected to grow new task shapes over time.&lt;/div&gt;" style="rounded=1;whiteSpace=wrap;html=1;fillColor=#ffffff;strokeColor=#c5d0db;spacing=11;align=left;verticalAlign=top;" vertex="1" parent="1">
          <mxGeometry x="815" y="1450.56" width="755" height="130" as="geometry" />
        </mxCell>
        <mxCell id="s12" value="&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:15px&quot;&gt;&lt;b&gt;COMPONENTS&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:13px;line-height:1.35&quot;&gt;&amp;bull; Fixed role table — manager, researcher, coder, designer baked into architecture before workloads were seen&lt;br&gt;&amp;bull; Per-role narrow prompt and tool subset — each specialist missing what neighbouring tasks need&lt;br&gt;&amp;bull; Orchestrator router — matches tasks to roles by label rather than by content&lt;br&gt;&amp;bull; Missing general-purpose sub-agent shape — would carry the full tool palette and accept per-call overlays&lt;/div&gt;" style="rounded=1;whiteSpace=wrap;html=1;fillColor=#ffffff;strokeColor=#c5d0db;spacing=11;align=left;verticalAlign=top;" vertex="1" parent="1">
          <mxGeometry x="30" y="1670.56" width="755" height="168" as="geometry" />
        </mxCell>
        <mxCell id="s13" value="&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:15px&quot;&gt;&lt;b&gt;TOOLS&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:13px;line-height:1.35&quot;&gt;&amp;bull; Per-role restricted tool palette — forbids capability-equal parallel fan-out by construction&lt;br&gt;&amp;bull; Per-call persona overlay — missing alternative that would specialise without baking the role into the agent type&lt;/div&gt;" style="rounded=1;whiteSpace=wrap;html=1;fillColor=#ffffff;strokeColor=#c5d0db;spacing=11;align=left;verticalAlign=top;" vertex="1" parent="1">
          <mxGeometry x="815" y="1594.56" width="755" height="111" as="geometry" />
        </mxCell>
        <mxCell id="s14" value="&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:15px&quot;&gt;&lt;b&gt;FAILURE MODES&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:13px;line-height:1.35&quot;&gt;&amp;bull; Squeezed task — a task that falls between two roles is sent to the closest label, and that specialist lacks the tools it needs.&lt;br&gt;&amp;bull; No equal fan-out — the team wants many identical capable sub-agents on one subtask, and the typed crew cannot provide it.&lt;br&gt;&amp;bull; Re-architecture for a new role — a new kind of work needs a new agent type and architecture changes, not a parameter change.&lt;/div&gt;" style="rounded=1;whiteSpace=wrap;html=1;fillColor=#ffffff;strokeColor=#c5d0db;spacing=11;align=left;verticalAlign=top;" vertex="1" parent="1">
          <mxGeometry x="30" y="1852.56" width="755" height="168" as="geometry" />
        </mxCell>
        <mxCell id="s15" value="&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:15px&quot;&gt;&lt;b&gt;EVALUATION METRICS&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:13px;line-height:1.35&quot;&gt;&amp;bull; Cross-role task placement error rate — share of tasks forced into the nearest role label&lt;br&gt;&amp;bull; Capability-equal fan-out feasibility — binary indicator that twenty identical sub-agents can run the same subtask&lt;br&gt;&amp;bull; Architecture-change frequency per new task type — counts how often adding a role required structural change&lt;br&gt;&amp;bull; Specialist underperformance gap — accuracy delta between the routed specialist and a generalist on the same task&lt;/div&gt;" style="rounded=1;whiteSpace=wrap;html=1;fillColor=#ffffff;strokeColor=#c5d0db;spacing=11;align=left;verticalAlign=top;" vertex="1" parent="1">
          <mxGeometry x="815" y="1719.56" width="755" height="187" as="geometry" />
        </mxCell>
        <mxCell id="s16" value="&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:15px&quot;&gt;&lt;b&gt;KNOWN USES&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:13px;line-height:1.35&quot;&gt;&amp;bull; &lt;b&gt;&lt;a href=&quot;https://manus.im/blog/introducing-wide-research&quot;&gt;Manus Wide Research (named as a deliberate design rejection)&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/b&gt; &lt;i&gt;available&lt;/i&gt; — Manus contrasts Wide Research with traditional predefined-roles multi-agent systems; every Wide Research sub-agent is a fully capable, general-purpose Manus instance.&lt;/div&gt;" style="rounded=1;whiteSpace=wrap;html=1;fillColor=#ffffff;strokeColor=#c5d0db;spacing=11;align=left;verticalAlign=top;" vertex="1" parent="1">
          <mxGeometry x="30" y="2034.56" width="755" height="111" as="geometry" />
        </mxCell>
        <mxCell id="s17" value="&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:15px&quot;&gt;&lt;b&gt;RELATED PATTERNS&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:13px;line-height:1.35&quot;&gt;&amp;bull; &lt;i&gt;alternative-to&lt;/i&gt; &lt;b&gt;Clone Fan-Out Research&lt;/b&gt;&lt;br&gt;&amp;bull; &lt;i&gt;alternative-to&lt;/i&gt; &lt;b&gt;Role Assignment&lt;/b&gt;&lt;br&gt;&amp;bull; &lt;i&gt;alternative-to&lt;/i&gt; &lt;b&gt;Supervisor&lt;/b&gt;&lt;br&gt;&amp;bull; &lt;i&gt;complements&lt;/i&gt; &lt;b&gt;Orchestrator-Workers&lt;/b&gt;&lt;br&gt;&amp;bull; &lt;i&gt;alternative-to&lt;/i&gt; &lt;b&gt;Personality Variant Overlay&lt;/b&gt;&lt;br&gt;&amp;bull; &lt;i&gt;complements&lt;/i&gt; &lt;b&gt;Supervisor Cognitive Overload&lt;/b&gt; — Role-typed sub-agents without an aggregation layer push their interactions onto one overloaded supervisor.&lt;/div&gt;" style="rounded=1;whiteSpace=wrap;html=1;fillColor=#ffffff;strokeColor=#c5d0db;spacing=11;align=left;verticalAlign=top;" vertex="1" parent="1">
          <mxGeometry x="815" y="1920.56" width="755" height="187" as="geometry" />
        </mxCell>
        <mxCell id="s18" value="&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:15px&quot;&gt;&lt;b&gt;REFERENCES&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:13px;line-height:1.35&quot;&gt;&amp;bull; &lt;a href=&quot;https://manus.im/blog/introducing-wide-research&quot;&gt;Introducing Wide Research&lt;/a&gt; — Manus, 2025&lt;/div&gt;" style="rounded=1;whiteSpace=wrap;html=1;fillColor=#ffffff;strokeColor=#c5d0db;spacing=11;align=left;verticalAlign=top;" vertex="1" parent="1">
