        <mxCell id="arch-caption" value="&lt;i&gt;Each rollout is compressed to a structured summary; selection and reuse run over the summaries, not the raw trajectories.&lt;/i&gt;" style="text;html=1;align=center;verticalAlign=middle;strokeColor=none;fillColor=none;fontSize=12;fontColor=#52606d;" vertex="1" parent="1">
          <mxGeometry x="65" y="889.5" width="1470" height="32" as="geometry" />
        </mxCell>
        <mxCell id="s0" value="&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:15px&quot;&gt;&lt;b&gt;INTENT&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:13px;line-height:1.35&quot;&gt;When an agent&#39;s outputs are extended action-observation trajectories rather than short answers, scale test-time compute by compressing each rollout into a structured summary and selecting or reusing across those summaries instead of raw traces.&lt;/div&gt;" style="rounded=1;whiteSpace=wrap;html=1;fillColor=#ffffff;strokeColor=#c5d0db;spacing=11;align=left;verticalAlign=top;" vertex="1" parent="1">
          <mxGeometry x="30" y="964.5" width="755" height="111" as="geometry" />
        </mxCell>
        <mxCell id="s1" value="&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:15px&quot;&gt;&lt;b&gt;CONTEXT&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:13px;line-height:1.35&quot;&gt;Test-time scaling improves quality by spending more inference compute — sampling several attempts, voting, or searching — and it works cleanly when each attempt is a short, directly comparable answer. Long-horizon agents break that assumption: a single attempt is a trajectory of dozens of tool calls and observations, far too long to compare verbatim and too noisy to majority-vote token by token. There is still good reason to spend more compute to raise success rates on these long tasks.&lt;/div&gt;" style="rounded=1;whiteSpace=wrap;html=1;fillColor=#ffffff;strokeColor=#c5d0db;spacing=11;align=left;verticalAlign=top;" vertex="1" parent="1">
          <mxGeometry x="815" y="964.5" width="755" height="149" as="geometry" />
        </mxCell>
        <mxCell id="s2" value="&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:15px&quot;&gt;&lt;b&gt;PROBLEM&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:13px;line-height:1.35&quot;&gt;Best-of-N and self-consistency assume the candidates are short outputs that a reward model can rank or a vote can aggregate. A hundred-step trajectory is neither: its salient content — the hypotheses tried, the progress made, the dead ends — is buried in low-signal trace detail, so naive ranking compares noise and naive voting has nothing to count. Without a comparable representation, extra rollouts add cost but not a reliable way to pick or combine the best one.&lt;/div&gt;" style="rounded=1;whiteSpace=wrap;html=1;fillColor=#ffffff;strokeColor=#c5d0db;spacing=11;align=left;verticalAlign=top;" vertex="1" parent="1">
          <mxGeometry x="30" y="1089.5" width="755" height="149" as="geometry" />
        </mxCell>
        <mxCell id="s3" value="&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:15px&quot;&gt;&lt;b&gt;FORCES&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:13px;line-height:1.35&quot;&gt;&amp;bull; Spending more rollouts raises the chance one of them succeeds, but only if there is a reliable way to identify or combine the good ones.&lt;br&gt;&amp;bull; A trajectory&#39;s decision-relevant content is a small fraction of its tokens, so comparing or voting over raw traces drowns the signal.&lt;br&gt;&amp;bull; Compressing a rollout to a summary risks discarding the very detail that distinguishes a good attempt from a plausible-looking bad one.&lt;br&gt;&amp;bull; Sequential reuse (re-rolling conditioned on prior summaries) and parallel selection (voting across summaries) need the same summary representation but spend compute differently.&lt;/div&gt;" style="rounded=1;whiteSpace=wrap;html=1;fillColor=#ffffff;strokeColor=#c5d0db;spacing=11;align=left;verticalAlign=top;" vertex="1" parent="1">
          <mxGeometry x="815" y="1127.5" width="755" height="206" as="geometry" />
        </mxCell>
        <mxCell id="s4" value="&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:15px&quot;&gt;&lt;b&gt;THEREFORE&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:13px;line-height:1.35&quot;&gt;Therefore: make the unit of test-time scaling a structured summary of each rollout — its hypotheses, progress, and failures — and select across summaries by tournament voting or condition new rollouts on them, rather than ranking or voting over raw trajectories.&lt;/div&gt;" style="rounded=1;whiteSpace=wrap;html=1;fillColor=#ffffff;strokeColor=#c5d0db;spacing=11;align=left;verticalAlign=top;" vertex="1" parent="1">
          <mxGeometry x="30" y="1252.5" width="755" height="111" as="geometry" />
        </mxCell>
        <mxCell id="s5" value="&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:15px&quot;&gt;&lt;b&gt;SOLUTION&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:13px;line-height:1.35&quot;&gt;Run several rollouts of the long task, and convert each into a structured summary that keeps its salient hypotheses, progress, and failure modes while shedding low-signal trace detail. Scale in two directions over these summaries. For parallel scaling, compare summaries against each other — for example by recursive tournament voting — to select the strongest attempt without ever diffing raw traces. For sequential scaling, feed the summaries of earlier rollouts back in as conditioning so a fresh rollout starts from what previous attempts learned. The summary, not the trajectory, is the object that gets ranked, voted on, and carried forward, which makes long-horizon outputs comparable at a fraction of their token cost.&lt;/div&gt;" style="rounded=1;whiteSpace=wrap;html=1;fillColor=#ffffff;strokeColor=#c5d0db;spacing=11;align=left;verticalAlign=top;" vertex="1" parent="1">
          <mxGeometry x="815" y="1347.5" width="755" height="206" as="geometry" />
        </mxCell>
        <mxCell id="s6" value="&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:15px&quot;&gt;&lt;b&gt;STRUCTURE&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:13px;line-height:1.35&quot;&gt;&lt;font face=&quot;monospace&quot;&gt;N rollouts --summarise each--&amp;gt; structured summaries --(parallel) tournament vote / (sequential) condition re-rollout--&amp;gt; selected or improved result&lt;/font&gt;&lt;/div&gt;" style="rounded=1;whiteSpace=wrap;html=1;fillColor=#ffffff;strokeColor=#c5d0db;spacing=11;align=left;verticalAlign=top;" vertex="1" parent="1">
          <mxGeometry x="30" y="1377.5" width="755" height="111" as="geometry" />
        </mxCell>
        <mxCell id="s7" value="&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:15px&quot;&gt;&lt;b&gt;EXAMPLE SCENARIO&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:13px;line-height:1.35&quot;&gt;A coding agent gets eight attempts at a hard multi-file bug. Each attempt is about eighty tool calls long, so the attempts cannot be compared directly. The system summarises each as &#39;tried X, fixed the import but tests still fail on the date parser&#39; and runs a tournament over the summaries; the winner — the one whose summary shows passing tests — is returned, and its summary also seeds a ninth attempt that starts where it left off.&lt;/div&gt;" style="rounded=1;whiteSpace=wrap;html=1;fillColor=#ffffff;strokeColor=#c5d0db;spacing=11;align=left;verticalAlign=top;" vertex="1" parent="1">
          <mxGeometry x="815" y="1567.5" width="755" height="149" as="geometry" />
        </mxCell>
        <mxCell id="s8" value="&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:15px&quot;&gt;&lt;b&gt;CONSEQUENCES&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:13px;line-height:1.35&quot;&gt;&lt;b&gt;Benefits&lt;/b&gt;&lt;br&gt;&lt;br&gt;&amp;bull; Long-horizon tasks gain the success-rate lift of test-time scaling that short-answer methods could not provide on trajectories.&lt;br&gt;&amp;bull; Comparing summaries instead of raw traces makes selection cheap enough to run many rollouts under a fixed budget.&lt;br&gt;&amp;bull; The same summary representation serves both parallel selection and sequential reuse, so one mechanism covers both scaling modes.&lt;br&gt;&lt;br&gt;&lt;b&gt;Liabilities&lt;/b&gt;&lt;br&gt;&lt;br&gt;&amp;bull; A summary that omits a load-bearing detail can rank a worse attempt above a better one, so summary quality bounds the whole method.&lt;br&gt;&amp;bull; Producing structured summaries adds an extra model pass per rollout, spending some of the compute the scaling is meant to buy.&lt;br&gt;&amp;bull; Tournament or conditioning logic adds orchestration the short-answer baselines do not need.&lt;/div&gt;" style="rounded=1;whiteSpace=wrap;html=1;fillColor=#ffffff;strokeColor=#c5d0db;spacing=11;align=left;verticalAlign=top;" vertex="1" parent="1">
          <mxGeometry x="30" y="1502.5" width="755" height="358" as="geometry" />
        </mxCell>
        <mxCell id="s9" value="&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:15px&quot;&gt;&lt;b&gt;WHAT THIS PATTERN CONSTRAINS&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:13px;line-height:1.35&quot;&gt;Test-time selection and reuse operate only on the structured rollout summaries, never on the raw trajectories directly; a rollout cannot be ranked, voted on, or carried forward until it has been compressed into the summary representation.&lt;/div&gt;" style="rounded=1;whiteSpace=wrap;html=1;fillColor=#ffffff;strokeColor=#c5d0db;spacing=11;align=left;verticalAlign=top;" vertex="1" parent="1">
          <mxGeometry x="815" y="1730.5" width="755" height="111" as="geometry" />
        </mxCell>
        <mxCell id="s10" value="&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:15px&quot;&gt;&lt;b&gt;USE WHEN&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:13px;line-height:1.35&quot;&gt;&amp;bull; The task is long-horizon, so each attempt is a trajectory too long to compare or vote on verbatim.&lt;br&gt;&amp;bull; More inference compute is available and expected to raise success if the good attempts can be identified.&lt;br&gt;&amp;bull; A reliable summariser can capture an attempt&#39;s hypotheses, progress, and failures.&lt;/div&gt;" style="rounded=1;whiteSpace=wrap;html=1;fillColor=#ffffff;strokeColor=#c5d0db;spacing=11;align=left;verticalAlign=top;" vertex="1" parent="1">
          <mxGeometry x="30" y="1874.5" width="755" height="130" as="geometry" />
        </mxCell>
        <mxCell id="s11" value="&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:15px&quot;&gt;&lt;b&gt;DO NOT USE WHEN&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:13px;line-height:1.35&quot;&gt;&amp;bull; Outputs are short, directly comparable answers, where best-of-N or self-consistency already apply.&lt;br&gt;&amp;bull; A single rollout reliably solves the task, so extra attempts add cost without benefit.&lt;br&gt;&amp;bull; No summariser can compress trajectories without dropping the detail that distinguishes success from failure.&lt;/div&gt;" style="rounded=1;whiteSpace=wrap;html=1;fillColor=#ffffff;strokeColor=#c5d0db;spacing=11;align=left;verticalAlign=top;" vertex="1" parent="1">
          <mxGeometry x="815" y="1855.5" width="755" height="130" as="geometry" />
        </mxCell>
        <mxCell id="s12" value="&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:15px&quot;&gt;&lt;b&gt;COMPONENTS&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:13px;line-height:1.35&quot;&gt;&amp;bull; Rollout generator — produces several full trajectories for the long task&lt;br&gt;&amp;bull; Rollout summariser — compresses each trajectory into a structured summary of hypotheses, progress, and failures&lt;br&gt;&amp;bull; Tournament selector — compares summaries against each other to pick the strongest attempt for parallel scaling&lt;br&gt;&amp;bull; Conditioning loop — feeds earlier summaries back in to seed a fresh rollout for sequential scaling&lt;br&gt;&amp;bull; Budget controller — splits the compute between extra rollouts and the summarisation passes they require&lt;/div&gt;" style="rounded=1;whiteSpace=wrap;html=1;fillColor=#ffffff;strokeColor=#c5d0db;spacing=11;align=left;verticalAlign=top;" vertex="1" parent="1">
          <mxGeometry x="30" y="2018.5" width="755" height="206" as="geometry" />
        </mxCell>
        <mxCell id="s13" value="&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:15px&quot;&gt;&lt;b&gt;TOOLS&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:13px;line-height:1.35&quot;&gt;&amp;bull; Tool-calling LLM — runs the rollouts and produces the summaries&lt;br&gt;&amp;bull; Summary scorer or judge — ranks summaries during tournament voting&lt;br&gt;&amp;bull; Compute-budget accounting — tracks rollout and summarisation spend against the budget&lt;/div&gt;" style="rounded=1;whiteSpace=wrap;html=1;fillColor=#ffffff;strokeColor=#c5d0db;spacing=11;align=left;verticalAlign=top;" vertex="1" parent="1">
          <mxGeometry x="815" y="1999.5" width="755" height="111" as="geometry" />
        </mxCell>
        <mxCell id="s14" value="&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:15px&quot;&gt;&lt;b&gt;FAILURE MODES&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:13px;line-height:1.35&quot;&gt;&amp;bull; Lossy summary — the compression drops the detail that distinguished the best rollout, so selection picks a weaker one.&lt;br&gt;&amp;bull; Summary homogenisation — distinct trajectories compress to near-identical summaries, collapsing the signal the vote needs.&lt;br&gt;&amp;bull; Conditioning lock-in — sequential reuse anchors every new rollout to an early summary&#39;s mistake.&lt;br&gt;&amp;bull; Budget inversion — the summarisation passes cost more than the extra rollouts gain.&lt;/div&gt;" style="rounded=1;whiteSpace=wrap;html=1;fillColor=#ffffff;strokeColor=#c5d0db;spacing=11;align=left;verticalAlign=top;" vertex="1" parent="1">
          <mxGeometry x="30" y="2238.5" width="755" height="168" as="geometry" />
        </mxCell>
        <mxCell id="s15" value="&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:15px&quot;&gt;&lt;b&gt;EVALUATION METRICS&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:13px;line-height:1.35&quot;&gt;&amp;bull; Long-horizon success rate vs a single-rollout baseline at equal compute — what the scaling buys&lt;br&gt;&amp;bull; Selection accuracy — how often the tournament picks the genuinely best rollout against an oracle check&lt;br&gt;&amp;bull; Summary overhead fraction — share of the compute spent on summarisation rather than rollouts&lt;br&gt;&amp;bull; Sequential lift — success gain from conditioning re-rollouts on prior summaries&lt;/div&gt;" style="rounded=1;whiteSpace=wrap;html=1;fillColor=#ffffff;strokeColor=#c5d0db;spacing=11;align=left;verticalAlign=top;" vertex="1" parent="1">
          <mxGeometry x="815" y="2124.5" width="755" height="149" as="geometry" />
        </mxCell>
        <mxCell id="s16" value="&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:15px&quot;&gt;&lt;b&gt;KNOWN USES&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:13px;line-height:1.35&quot;&gt;&amp;bull; &lt;b&gt;&lt;a href=&quot;https://arxiv.org/abs/2604.16529&quot;&gt;Scaling Test-Time Compute for Agentic Coding&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/b&gt; &lt;i&gt;pure-future&lt;/i&gt; — Converts each long coding rollout into a structured summary and selects via recursive tournament voting rather than diffing raw traces.&lt;br&gt;&amp;bull; &lt;b&gt;Memex(RL)&lt;/b&gt; &lt;i&gt;pure-future&lt;/i&gt; — Frames long-horizon test-time scaling as represent-select-reuse over compressed rollout representations.&lt;/div&gt;" style="rounded=1;whiteSpace=wrap;html=1;fillColor=#ffffff;strokeColor=#c5d0db;spacing=11;align=left;verticalAlign=top;" vertex="1" parent="1">
          <mxGeometry x="30" y="2420.5" width="755" height="149" as="geometry" />
        </mxCell>
        <mxCell id="s17" value="&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:15px&quot;&gt;&lt;b&gt;RELATED PATTERNS&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:13px;line-height:1.35&quot;&gt;&amp;bull; &lt;i&gt;specialises&lt;/i&gt; &lt;b&gt;Test-Time Compute Scaling&lt;/b&gt; — The general principle is spend-more-inference-compute; this specialises it for outputs that are long trajectories rather than short answers.&lt;br&gt;&amp;bull; &lt;i&gt;alternative-to&lt;/i&gt; &lt;b&gt;Best-of-N Sampling&lt;/b&gt; — Best-of-N ranks raw candidate outputs and picks one; here the ranked unit is a compressed trajectory summary, and reuse can re-roll from it.&lt;br&gt;&amp;bull; &lt;i&gt;alternative-to&lt;/i&gt; &lt;b&gt;Self-Consistency&lt;/b&gt; — Self-consistency votes over short comparable answers; trajectories are not comparable verbatim, so the vote runs over their summaries.&lt;br&gt;&amp;bull; &lt;i&gt;complements&lt;/i&gt; &lt;b&gt;Episodic Summaries&lt;/b&gt; — Both compress a trajectory to a summary; episodic-summaries does it to save memory cost, this does it to make rollouts selectable.&lt;br&gt;&amp;bull; &lt;i&gt;complements&lt;/i&gt; &lt;b&gt;Reasoning Trace Carry-Forward&lt;/b&gt; — Carry-forward keeps a single trace within an episode; this carries a summary of a whole rollout across attempts for sequential scaling.&lt;/div&gt;" style="rounded=1;whiteSpace=wrap;html=1;fillColor=#ffffff;strokeColor=#c5d0db;spacing=11;align=left;verticalAlign=top;" vertex="1" parent="1">
          <mxGeometry x="815" y="2287.5" width="755" height="244" as="geometry" />
        </mxCell>
        <mxCell id="s18" value="&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:15px&quot;&gt;&lt;b&gt;REFERENCES&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:13px;line-height:1.35&quot;&gt;&amp;bull; &lt;a href=&quot;https://arxiv.org/abs/2604.16529&quot;&gt;Scaling Test-Time Compute for Agentic Coding&lt;/a&gt; — 2026&lt;/div&gt;" style="rounded=1;whiteSpace=wrap;html=1;fillColor=#ffffff;strokeColor=#c5d0db;spacing=11;align=left;verticalAlign=top;" vertex="1" parent="1">
          <mxGeometry x="30" y="2583.5" width="755" height="78" as="geometry" />
        </mxCell>
      </root>
    </mxGraphModel>
  </diagram>
