        </UserObject>
        <UserObject label="" mermaidId="e:E-&gt;O#0" mermaidBaseStyle="curved=1;startArrow=none;endArrow=block;endSize=7;strokeColor=light-dark(#333333,#cccccc);exitX=0.5;exitY=1;entryX=0.5;entryY=0;" mermaidBaseValue="" id="m15">
          <mxCell edge="1" parent="1" source="m6" style="curved=1;startArrow=none;endArrow=block;endSize=7;strokeColor=light-dark(#333333,#cccccc);exitX=0.5;exitY=1;entryX=0.5;entryY=0;" target="m7">
            <mxGeometry relative="1" as="geometry">
              <Array as="points" />
            </mxGeometry>
          </mxCell>
        </UserObject>
        <UserObject label="forbidden" mermaidId="e:A-&gt;S#0" mermaidBaseStyle="curved=1;dashed=1;dashPattern=2 3;fixDash=1;startArrow=none;endArrow=block;endSize=7;strokeColor=light-dark(#333333,#cccccc);html=1;fontSize=16;labelBackgroundColor=light-dark(#E8E8E88D,#2a2a2a8D);fontFamily=Trebuchet MS,Verdana,Arial,sans-serif;fontColor=light-dark(#333333,#cccccc);exitX=0.63;exitY=1;entryX=0.5;entryY=0;" mermaidBaseValue="forbidden" id="m16">
          <mxCell edge="1" parent="1" source="m1" style="curved=1;dashed=1;dashPattern=2 3;fixDash=1;startArrow=none;endArrow=block;endSize=7;strokeColor=light-dark(#333333,#cccccc);html=1;fontSize=16;labelBackgroundColor=light-dark(#E8E8E88D,#2a2a2a8D);fontFamily=Trebuchet MS,Verdana,Arial,sans-serif;fontColor=light-dark(#333333,#cccccc);exitX=0.63;exitY=1;entryX=0.5;entryY=0;" target="m8">
            <mxGeometry relative="1" as="geometry">
              <Array as="points">
                <mxPoint x="1112.8" y="454.15" />
              </Array>
            </mxGeometry>
          </mxCell>
        </UserObject>
        <mxCell id="arch-caption" value="&lt;i&gt;The agent selects and parameterises vetted metrics; the semantic layer, not the model, compiles and runs the SQL. Authoring raw SQL is forbidden.&lt;/i&gt;" style="text;html=1;align=center;verticalAlign=middle;strokeColor=none;fillColor=none;fontSize=12;fontColor=#52606d;" vertex="1" parent="1">
          <mxGeometry x="65" y="1531.2" width="1470" height="32" as="geometry" />
        </mxCell>
        <mxCell id="s0" value="&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:15px&quot;&gt;&lt;b&gt;INTENT&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:13px;line-height:1.35&quot;&gt;Route natural-language data questions through a curated semantic layer so the model selects and parameterises vetted metrics and dimensions instead of free-authoring raw SQL against production data.&lt;/div&gt;" style="rounded=1;whiteSpace=wrap;html=1;fillColor=#ffffff;strokeColor=#c5d0db;spacing=11;align=left;verticalAlign=top;" vertex="1" parent="1">
          <mxGeometry x="30" y="1606.2" width="755" height="92" as="geometry" />
        </mxCell>
        <mxCell id="s1" value="&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:15px&quot;&gt;&lt;b&gt;CONTEXT&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:13px;line-height:1.35&quot;&gt;An agent answers natural-language questions over a production data warehouse — revenue last quarter, active users by region, churn for a cohort. Free-form text-to-SQL is the obvious mechanism, but in practice it rarely survives contact with a real schema: column names are cryptic, business terms are organisation-specific, and the same word means different things to different teams. A separate semantic layer — a metric store such as a dbt Semantic Layer, LookML, or Cube model — already encodes the vetted definitions of each metric and dimension and can compile a metric request into correct SQL on its own.&lt;/div&gt;" style="rounded=1;whiteSpace=wrap;html=1;fillColor=#ffffff;strokeColor=#c5d0db;spacing=11;align=left;verticalAlign=top;" vertex="1" parent="1">
          <mxGeometry x="815" y="1606.2" width="755" height="187" as="geometry" />
        </mxCell>
        <mxCell id="s2" value="&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:15px&quot;&gt;&lt;b&gt;PROBLEM&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:13px;line-height:1.35&quot;&gt;Natural language is ambiguous and business terminology is domain-specific, so the model does not know what a particular organisation means by revenue or active user, and a text-to-SQL system that lets the model author raw SQL will silently pick the wrong column, the wrong join, or the wrong filter and return a confidently incorrect number. Letting the model write arbitrary SQL against production also widens the action surface to every table the credential can reach. The system needs the model&#39;s language understanding to map the question onto a metric, without granting it the authority to define what that metric is or to author the query that computes it.&lt;/div&gt;" style="rounded=1;whiteSpace=wrap;html=1;fillColor=#ffffff;strokeColor=#c5d0db;spacing=11;align=left;verticalAlign=top;" vertex="1" parent="1">
          <mxGeometry x="30" y="1712.2" width="755" height="187" as="geometry" />
        </mxCell>
        <mxCell id="s3" value="&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:15px&quot;&gt;&lt;b&gt;FORCES&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:13px;line-height:1.35&quot;&gt;&amp;bull; Free-form text-to-SQL covers any question the schema can express, but a single misread column or join yields a wrong answer that looks authoritative and is hard to catch.&lt;br&gt;&amp;bull; Business definitions such as revenue or active user are organisation-specific and change over time; encoding them once in a shared layer is correct, while re-deriving them in each generated query is fragile.&lt;br&gt;&amp;bull; Arbitrary model-authored SQL against production widens the blast radius to every table the credential can read; a fixed catalogue of metrics narrows it.&lt;br&gt;&amp;bull; A semantic layer must be modelled and maintained up front, which is real work and cannot answer a question whose metric nobody has defined yet.&lt;/div&gt;" style="rounded=1;whiteSpace=wrap;html=1;fillColor=#ffffff;strokeColor=#c5d0db;spacing=11;align=left;verticalAlign=top;" vertex="1" parent="1">
          <mxGeometry x="815" y="1807.2" width="755" height="225" as="geometry" />
        </mxCell>
        <mxCell id="s4" value="&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:15px&quot;&gt;&lt;b&gt;THEREFORE&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:13px;line-height:1.35&quot;&gt;Therefore: place a curated semantic layer between the question and execution so the model maps the question onto a vetted metric and its parameters, and the layer — not the model — deterministically compiles and runs the SQL.&lt;/div&gt;" style="rounded=1;whiteSpace=wrap;html=1;fillColor=#ffffff;strokeColor=#c5d0db;spacing=11;align=left;verticalAlign=top;" vertex="1" parent="1">
          <mxGeometry x="30" y="1913.2" width="755" height="111" as="geometry" />
        </mxCell>
        <mxCell id="s5" value="&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:15px&quot;&gt;&lt;b&gt;SOLUTION&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:13px;line-height:1.35&quot;&gt;Model the metrics, dimensions, and business definitions once in a semantic layer and expose querying it as the agent&#39;s only data tool. The agent&#39;s job shrinks from authoring SQL to selecting: it maps the natural-language question onto one or more pre-defined metrics, picks the dimensions to group by, and fills in time grain and filter parameters, emitting a structured metric request rather than a SQL string. The semantic layer validates that request against its schema and deterministically compiles it to SQL — resolving the canonical column, join path, and definition behind each metric — then executes it and returns the result. Because the definitions live in the layer, every consumer computes revenue the same way; because the agent cannot author raw SQL, it cannot reach a table outside the modelled catalogue or invent a definition. A question whose metric is not yet modelled is refused or escalated to extend the layer rather than answered by improvised SQL.&lt;/div&gt;" style="rounded=1;whiteSpace=wrap;html=1;fillColor=#ffffff;strokeColor=#c5d0db;spacing=11;align=left;verticalAlign=top;" vertex="1" parent="1">
          <mxGeometry x="815" y="2046.2" width="755" height="244" as="geometry" />
        </mxCell>
        <mxCell id="s6" value="&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:15px&quot;&gt;&lt;b&gt;STRUCTURE&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:13px;line-height:1.35&quot;&gt;&lt;font face=&quot;monospace&quot;&gt;Question --model maps to metric+params--&amp;gt; Structured metric request --validate--&amp;gt; Semantic layer compiles to SQL deterministically --execute--&amp;gt; Result returned to agent&lt;/font&gt;&lt;/div&gt;" style="rounded=1;whiteSpace=wrap;html=1;fillColor=#ffffff;strokeColor=#c5d0db;spacing=11;align=left;verticalAlign=top;" vertex="1" parent="1">
          <mxGeometry x="30" y="2038.2" width="755" height="111" as="geometry" />
        </mxCell>
        <mxCell id="s7" value="&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:15px&quot;&gt;&lt;b&gt;EXAMPLE SCENARIO&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:13px;line-height:1.35&quot;&gt;A business-intelligence agent is asked &#39;what was active-user revenue in EMEA last quarter?&#39;. Instead of writing SQL, it maps the question onto two pre-defined metrics — active_users and revenue — sets the region dimension to EMEA and the time grain to the prior quarter, and emits a structured metric request. The dbt Semantic Layer validates the request, compiles it to the correct SQL using the team&#39;s vetted definitions of active user and revenue, runs it, and returns the figures. When someone later asks for a metric nobody has modelled, the agent reports that the metric is undefined rather than improvising a query.&lt;/div&gt;" style="rounded=1;whiteSpace=wrap;html=1;fillColor=#ffffff;strokeColor=#c5d0db;spacing=11;align=left;verticalAlign=top;" vertex="1" parent="1">
          <mxGeometry x="815" y="2304.2" width="755" height="187" as="geometry" />
        </mxCell>
        <mxCell id="s8" value="&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:15px&quot;&gt;&lt;b&gt;CONSEQUENCES&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:13px;line-height:1.35&quot;&gt;&lt;b&gt;Benefits&lt;/b&gt;&lt;br&gt;&lt;br&gt;&amp;bull; Every answer uses the organisation&#39;s vetted definition of each metric, so the same question returns the same number regardless of which agent or consumer asked it.&lt;br&gt;&amp;bull; The model cannot reach a table outside the modelled catalogue or invent a business definition, so the data action surface is bounded to vetted metrics.&lt;br&gt;&amp;bull; SQL compilation is deterministic and owned by the layer, so a correct metric selection cannot be undermined by a model-authored join or filter error.&lt;br&gt;&amp;bull; Metric definitions are maintained in one place, so a change to how revenue is computed propagates to every agent answer without re-prompting.&lt;br&gt;&lt;br&gt;&lt;b&gt;Liabilities&lt;/b&gt;&lt;br&gt;&lt;br&gt;&amp;bull; A question whose metric has not been modelled cannot be answered until someone extends the semantic layer, so coverage lags ad-hoc text-to-SQL.&lt;br&gt;&amp;bull; The semantic layer is upfront modelling and ongoing maintenance work that a small or fast-moving schema may not justify.&lt;br&gt;&amp;bull; Selection is not free of error: the model can still map a question onto the wrong vetted metric or the wrong grouping, returning a precise answer to the wrong question.&lt;/div&gt;" style="rounded=1;whiteSpace=wrap;html=1;fillColor=#ffffff;strokeColor=#c5d0db;spacing=11;align=left;verticalAlign=top;" vertex="1" parent="1">
          <mxGeometry x="30" y="2163.2" width="755" height="415" as="geometry" />
        </mxCell>
        <mxCell id="s9" value="&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:15px&quot;&gt;&lt;b&gt;WHAT THIS PATTERN CONSTRAINS&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:13px;line-height:1.35&quot;&gt;The agent may not author or execute raw SQL against production data; it may only select from and parameterise metrics and dimensions defined in the semantic layer, and a question whose metric is not modelled must be refused or escalated rather than answered by improvised SQL.&lt;/div&gt;" style="rounded=1;whiteSpace=wrap;html=1;fillColor=#ffffff;strokeColor=#c5d0db;spacing=11;align=left;verticalAlign=top;" vertex="1" parent="1">
          <mxGeometry x="815" y="2505.2" width="755" height="111" as="geometry" />
        </mxCell>
        <mxCell id="s10" value="&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:15px&quot;&gt;&lt;b&gt;USE WHEN&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:13px;line-height:1.35&quot;&gt;&amp;bull; An agent answers natural-language questions over a production warehouse where business terms such as revenue or active user have organisation-specific definitions the model cannot infer.&lt;br&gt;&amp;bull; A semantic or metric layer (dbt Semantic Layer, Cube, LookML) already encodes vetted metric and dimension definitions, or one is worth building.&lt;br&gt;&amp;bull; Wrong answers from a mis-authored query are costly and consistency of definitions across consumers matters more than answering every possible ad-hoc question.&lt;/div&gt;" style="rounded=1;whiteSpace=wrap;html=1;fillColor=#ffffff;strokeColor=#c5d0db;spacing=11;align=left;verticalAlign=top;" vertex="1" parent="1">
          <mxGeometry x="30" y="2592.2" width="755" height="168" as="geometry" />
        </mxCell>
        <mxCell id="s11" value="&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:15px&quot;&gt;&lt;b&gt;DO NOT USE WHEN&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:13px;line-height:1.35&quot;&gt;&amp;bull; The questions are too varied or exploratory to pre-model, so a fixed metric catalogue would refuse more questions than it answers.&lt;br&gt;&amp;bull; No semantic layer exists and the schema is too small or fast-moving to justify modelling one.&lt;br&gt;&amp;bull; The question genuinely needs per-row semantic judgement over free text that no pre-defined metric captures, where table-augmented generation fits better.&lt;/div&gt;" style="rounded=1;whiteSpace=wrap;html=1;fillColor=#ffffff;strokeColor=#c5d0db;spacing=11;align=left;verticalAlign=top;" vertex="1" parent="1">
          <mxGeometry x="815" y="2630.2" width="755" height="149" as="geometry" />
        </mxCell>
        <mxCell id="s12" value="&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:15px&quot;&gt;&lt;b&gt;COMPONENTS&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:13px;line-height:1.35&quot;&gt;&amp;bull; Semantic layer — the metric store that holds vetted metric, dimension, and business definitions and owns SQL compilation&lt;br&gt;&amp;bull; Metric catalogue — the enumerable set of pre-defined metrics and dimensions the agent may select from&lt;br&gt;&amp;bull; Metric-selection agent — maps the natural-language question onto metrics, dimensions, time grain, and filter parameters&lt;br&gt;&amp;bull; Structured metric request — the validated request object the agent emits instead of a SQL string&lt;br&gt;&amp;bull; Deterministic compiler — resolves each metric to its canonical columns and join path and generates the SQL&lt;br&gt;&amp;bull; Query executor — runs the compiled SQL against the warehouse and returns the result&lt;br&gt;&amp;bull; Coverage gate — refuses or escalates a question whose metric is not modelled rather than improvising SQL&lt;/div&gt;" style="rounded=1;whiteSpace=wrap;html=1;fillColor=#ffffff;strokeColor=#c5d0db;spacing=11;align=left;verticalAlign=top;" vertex="1" parent="1">
          <mxGeometry x="30" y="2774.2" width="755" height="282" as="geometry" />
        </mxCell>
        <mxCell id="s13" value="&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:15px&quot;&gt;&lt;b&gt;TOOLS&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:13px;line-height:1.35&quot;&gt;&amp;bull; Semantic-layer / metric store (for example dbt Semantic Layer, Cube, LookML) — defines metrics and compiles requests to SQL&lt;br&gt;&amp;bull; Metric query API — accepts a structured metric request and returns results without exposing raw SQL authoring&lt;br&gt;&amp;bull; Tool-calling LLM — maps the question onto vetted metrics and fills in dimensions and parameters&lt;br&gt;&amp;bull; Data warehouse — executes the layer-compiled SQL over production tables&lt;/div&gt;" style="rounded=1;whiteSpace=wrap;html=1;fillColor=#ffffff;strokeColor=#c5d0db;spacing=11;align=left;verticalAlign=top;" vertex="1" parent="1">
          <mxGeometry x="815" y="2793.2" width="755" height="168" as="geometry" />
        </mxCell>
        <mxCell id="s14" value="&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:15px&quot;&gt;&lt;b&gt;FAILURE MODES&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:13px;line-height:1.35&quot;&gt;&amp;bull; Metric mis-selection — the model maps the question onto a plausible but wrong vetted metric, so the layer computes the correct SQL for the wrong measure.&lt;br&gt;&amp;bull; Parameter drift — the model fills in the wrong time grain, filter, or grouping dimension, returning a precise answer that does not match the question&#39;s intent.&lt;br&gt;&amp;bull; Coverage gap masking — a question whose metric is unmodelled is forced onto the nearest defined metric instead of being refused, hiding that no vetted answer exists.&lt;br&gt;&amp;bull; Stale definition — the layer&#39;s metric definition lags a business change, so every agent answer is consistently and silently out of date.&lt;/div&gt;" style="rounded=1;whiteSpace=wrap;html=1;fillColor=#ffffff;strokeColor=#c5d0db;spacing=11;align=left;verticalAlign=top;" vertex="1" parent="1">
          <mxGeometry x="30" y="3070.2" width="755" height="206" as="geometry" />
        </mxCell>
        <mxCell id="s15" value="&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:15px&quot;&gt;&lt;b&gt;EVALUATION METRICS&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:13px;line-height:1.35&quot;&gt;&amp;bull; Metric-selection accuracy — fraction of questions mapped onto the correct vetted metric and grouping&lt;br&gt;&amp;bull; Definition-consistency rate — share of answers that match the canonical metric definition across agents and consumers&lt;br&gt;&amp;bull; Coverage / refusal rate — fraction of questions answerable from modelled metrics vs correctly refused for being unmodelled&lt;br&gt;&amp;bull; Raw-SQL escape rate — count of queries that reach production outside the semantic layer (target zero)&lt;br&gt;&amp;bull; Answer-correctness vs a free-form text-to-SQL baseline on the same question set&lt;/div&gt;" style="rounded=1;whiteSpace=wrap;html=1;fillColor=#ffffff;strokeColor=#c5d0db;spacing=11;align=left;verticalAlign=top;" vertex="1" parent="1">
          <mxGeometry x="815" y="2975.2" width="755" height="225" as="geometry" />
        </mxCell>
        <mxCell id="s16" value="&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:15px&quot;&gt;&lt;b&gt;KNOWN USES&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:13px;line-height:1.35&quot;&gt;&amp;bull; &lt;b&gt;&lt;a href=&quot;https://docs.getdbt.com/docs/build/about-metricflow&quot;&gt;dbt Semantic Layer (MetricFlow)&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/b&gt; &lt;i&gt;available&lt;/i&gt; — Metrics and dimensions are defined once in YAML semantic models; MetricFlow handles SQL query construction, compiling a metric request into the correct SQL across join paths so consumers query vetted metrics rather than authoring SQL.&lt;br&gt;&amp;bull; &lt;b&gt;&lt;a href=&quot;https://cube.dev/&quot;&gt;Cube&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/b&gt; &lt;i&gt;available&lt;/i&gt; — Semantic layer that exposes modelled measures and dimensions through a query API and an AI/agent endpoint, so an agent selects vetted metrics and the layer generates the SQL.&lt;br&gt;&amp;bull; &lt;b&gt;&lt;a href=&quot;https://docs.cloud.google.com/looker/docs/lookml-terms-and-concepts&quot;&gt;Looker (LookML)&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/b&gt; &lt;i&gt;available&lt;/i&gt; — LookML models dimensions and measures once per view, and Looker generates the SQL from that model, so consumers query vetted metrics rather than authoring raw SQL against the warehouse.&lt;br&gt;&amp;bull; &lt;b&gt;&lt;a href=&quot;https://docs.databricks.com/aws/en/genie/&quot;&gt;Databricks AI/BI Genie&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/b&gt; &lt;i&gt;available&lt;/i&gt; — Genie Spaces are curated by data analysts with Unity Catalog datasets, example SQL queries, and SQL expressions for business semantics, so natural-language questions resolve against vetted definitions instead of free-form SQL.&lt;br&gt;&amp;bull; &lt;b&gt;&lt;a href=&quot;https://www.atscale.com/&quot;&gt;AtScale&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/b&gt; &lt;i&gt;available&lt;/i&gt; — Universal semantic layer that centralises metric and dimension definitions so BI tools and agents query consistent, vetted metrics that the layer compiles to SQL.&lt;/div&gt;" style="rounded=1;whiteSpace=wrap;html=1;fillColor=#ffffff;strokeColor=#c5d0db;spacing=11;align=left;verticalAlign=top;" vertex="1" parent="1">
          <mxGeometry x="30" y="3290.2" width="755" height="377" as="geometry" />
        </mxCell>
        <mxCell id="s17" value="&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:15px&quot;&gt;&lt;b&gt;RELATED PATTERNS&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:13px;line-height:1.35&quot;&gt;&amp;bull; &lt;i&gt;alternative-to&lt;/i&gt; &lt;b&gt;Table-Augmented Generation&lt;/b&gt; — TAG keeps the model authoring an executable query and embeds model calls inside its execution for semantic predicates; this pattern removes the model&#39;s authority to author SQL entirely, routing the question through pre-defined metrics the layer compiles deterministically.&lt;br&gt;&amp;bull; &lt;i&gt;complements&lt;/i&gt; &lt;b&gt;Canonical-Entity Grounding&lt;/b&gt; — Grounding replaces model-emitted identifiers with authoritative lookups; this pattern replaces model-authored metric definitions and SQL with vetted ones, so the two together bound both the entities and the measures an agent&#39;s data query may reference.&lt;br&gt;&amp;bull; &lt;i&gt;complements&lt;/i&gt; &lt;b&gt;Risk-Tiered Action Autonomy&lt;/b&gt; — Narrowing read queries to a curated metric catalogue is the read-side analogue of tiering write autonomy: both shrink the agent&#39;s action surface against production to a vetted, bounded set.&lt;br&gt;&amp;bull; &lt;i&gt;complements&lt;/i&gt; &lt;b&gt;Tool Discovery&lt;/b&gt; — The semantic layer presents the catalogue of vetted metrics and dimensions as the agent&#39;s selectable surface, so the agent discovers and picks from modelled metrics rather than free-authoring against the raw schema.&lt;br&gt;&amp;bull; &lt;i&gt;complements&lt;/i&gt; &lt;b&gt;Solver-Ready Formulation Handoff&lt;/b&gt; — The same discipline on the analytics side — the model parameterises a vetted structure and a deterministic compiler produces the executable artefact.&lt;br&gt;&amp;bull; &lt;i&gt;complements&lt;/i&gt; &lt;b&gt;Cumulative Disclosure Accounting&lt;/b&gt; — Constrains which query may be issued at all; the account constrains how many permitted queries may be answered before their results compose.&lt;/div&gt;" style="rounded=1;whiteSpace=wrap;html=1;fillColor=#ffffff;strokeColor=#c5d0db;spacing=11;align=left;verticalAlign=top;" vertex="1" parent="1">
          <mxGeometry x="815" y="3214.2" width="755" height="377" as="geometry" />
        </mxCell>
        <mxCell id="s18" value="&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:15px&quot;&gt;&lt;b&gt;REFERENCES&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:13px;line-height:1.35&quot;&gt;&amp;bull; &lt;a href=&quot;https://ihower.tw/blog/13513-agent-design-is-still-hard-2025&quot;&gt;AI Agent 產品開發仍然不簡單（2025）&lt;/a&gt; — ihower (Wen-Tien Chang), 2025&lt;br&gt;&amp;bull; &lt;a href=&quot;https://docs.getdbt.com/docs/build/about-metricflow&quot;&gt;About MetricFlow — dbt Semantic Layer&lt;/a&gt; — 2025&lt;br&gt;&amp;bull; &lt;a href=&quot;https://docs.getdbt.com/docs/use-dbt-semantic-layer/dbt-sl&quot;&gt;About the dbt Semantic Layer&lt;/a&gt; — 2025&lt;br&gt;&amp;bull; &lt;a href=&quot;https://arxiv.org/abs/2403.15879&quot;&gt;TrustSQL: Benchmarking Text-to-SQL Reliability with Penalty-Based Scoring&lt;/a&gt; — Gyubok Lee, Woosog Chay, Seonhee Cho, Edward Choi, 2024&lt;br&gt;&amp;bull; &lt;a href=&quot;https://arxiv.org/abs/2410.06011&quot;&gt;Large Language Model Enhanced Text-to-SQL Generation: A Survey&lt;/a&gt; — 2024&lt;/div&gt;" style="rounded=1;whiteSpace=wrap;html=1;fillColor=#ffffff;strokeColor=#c5d0db;spacing=11;align=left;verticalAlign=top;" vertex="1" parent="1">
          <mxGeometry x="30" y="3681.2" width="755" height="206" as="geometry" />
        </mxCell>
      </root>
    </mxGraphModel>
  </diagram>
  <diagram id="semantic-memory" name="Semantic Memory">
    <mxGraphModel dx="1600" dy="2701.3594394064303" grid="1" gridSize="10" guides="1" tooltips="1" connect="1" arrows="1" fold="1" page="1" pageScale="1" pageWidth="1600" pageHeight="2701.3594394064303" math="0" shadow="0">
      <root>
        <mxCell id="0" />
        <mxCell id="1" parent="0" />
        <mxCell id="title" value="&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:24px&quot;&gt;&lt;b&gt;Semantic Memory&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:12px;color:#52606d&quot;&gt;Memory &amp;nbsp; | &amp;nbsp; emerging &amp;nbsp; | &amp;nbsp; Also known as: Fact Memory, Agent Knowledge Store, Knowledge Memory&lt;/div&gt;" style="text;html=1;align=left;verticalAlign=middle;whiteSpace=wrap;strokeColor=none;fillColor=none;" vertex="1" parent="1">
          <mxGeometry x="30" y="20" width="1540" height="58" as="geometry" />
        </mxCell>
        <mxCell id="arch-panel" value="" style="rounded=1;whiteSpace=wrap;html=1;fillColor=#f5f8fc;strokeColor=#b6c6d8;strokeWidth=1;arcSize=12;" vertex="1" parent="1">
          <mxGeometry x="30" y="95" width="1540" height="688.36" as="geometry" />
        </mxCell>
