          </mxCell>
        </UserObject>
        <mxCell id="arch-caption" value="&lt;i&gt;Routing every agent to one human saturates the supervisor; an aggregation layer restores a bounded oversight surface.&lt;/i&gt;" style="text;html=1;align=center;verticalAlign=middle;strokeColor=none;fillColor=none;fontSize=12;fontColor=#52606d;" vertex="1" parent="1">
          <mxGeometry x="65" y="633.11" width="1470" height="32" as="geometry" />
        </mxCell>
        <mxCell id="s0" value="&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:15px&quot;&gt;&lt;b&gt;INTENT&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:13px;line-height:1.35&quot;&gt;Name the failure where a human must converse with and steer every parallel sub-agent individually, so oversight saturates the supervisor and the human becomes the bottleneck the multi-agent design meant to remove.&lt;/div&gt;" style="rounded=1;whiteSpace=wrap;html=1;fillColor=#ffffff;strokeColor=#c5d0db;spacing=11;align=left;verticalAlign=top;" vertex="1" parent="1">
          <mxGeometry x="30" y="708.11" width="755" height="111" as="geometry" />
        </mxCell>
        <mxCell id="s1" value="&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:15px&quot;&gt;&lt;b&gt;CONTEXT&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:13px;line-height:1.35&quot;&gt;A team adopts a multi-agent setup to parallelise work — several sub-agents each handling a slice of a larger task. To keep them on track, the design routes every sub-agent&#39;s questions, clarifications, and approvals back to one human. As the number of concurrent agents grows, that human is expected to hold context on all of them at once and reply to each.&lt;/div&gt;" style="rounded=1;whiteSpace=wrap;html=1;fillColor=#ffffff;strokeColor=#c5d0db;spacing=11;align=left;verticalAlign=top;" vertex="1" parent="1">
          <mxGeometry x="815" y="708.11" width="755" height="130" as="geometry" />
        </mxCell>
        <mxCell id="s2" value="&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:15px&quot;&gt;&lt;b&gt;PROBLEM&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:13px;line-height:1.35&quot;&gt;Parallel agents multiply the supervision surface faster than a human can absorb it. Each agent needs context-setting, mid-task clarification, and review; when all of that lands on one person simultaneously, the supervisor thrashes between agents, loses track of which said what, and either rubber-stamps to keep up or becomes the slowest part of the system. The parallelism that was supposed to speed things up is throttled by a single human&#39;s working memory.&lt;/div&gt;" style="rounded=1;whiteSpace=wrap;html=1;fillColor=#ffffff;strokeColor=#c5d0db;spacing=11;align=left;verticalAlign=top;" vertex="1" parent="1">
          <mxGeometry x="30" y="833.11" width="755" height="149" as="geometry" />
        </mxCell>
        <mxCell id="s3" value="&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:15px&quot;&gt;&lt;b&gt;FORCES&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:13px;line-height:1.35&quot;&gt;&amp;bull; More concurrent agents mean more parallel demands on one supervisor&#39;s attention.&lt;br&gt;&amp;bull; Human working memory and context-switching capacity are fixed and small.&lt;br&gt;&amp;bull; Sub-agents still need steering, so oversight cannot simply be removed.&lt;br&gt;&amp;bull; Rubber-stamping to keep pace defeats the purpose of having a human in the loop.&lt;br&gt;&amp;bull; Aggregating agent state for the human costs design effort that is easy to skip.&lt;/div&gt;" style="rounded=1;whiteSpace=wrap;html=1;fillColor=#ffffff;strokeColor=#c5d0db;spacing=11;align=left;verticalAlign=top;" vertex="1" parent="1">
          <mxGeometry x="815" y="852.11" width="755" height="149" as="geometry" />
        </mxCell>
        <mxCell id="s4" value="&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:15px&quot;&gt;&lt;b&gt;THEREFORE&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:13px;line-height:1.35&quot;&gt;Therefore (avoidance): do not route every sub-agent&#39;s interaction to one human; aggregate, summarise, and escalate selectively so the supervisor sees a manageable, prioritised surface rather than N live conversations.&lt;/div&gt;" style="rounded=1;whiteSpace=wrap;html=1;fillColor=#ffffff;strokeColor=#c5d0db;spacing=11;align=left;verticalAlign=top;" vertex="1" parent="1">
          <mxGeometry x="30" y="996.11" width="755" height="111" as="geometry" />
        </mxCell>
        <mxCell id="s5" value="&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:15px&quot;&gt;&lt;b&gt;SOLUTION&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:13px;line-height:1.35&quot;&gt;Recognise the anti-pattern and redesign the oversight surface. Insert an aggregation layer between the agents and the human: batch and summarise sub-agent status, surface only the decisions that genuinely need a human, and let a lead agent or orchestrator absorb routine clarifications. Cap the number of agents one person supervises, or move from per-agent conversation to a single dashboard with prioritised exceptions. The corrective patterns are selective escalation and a coordinating layer, not more human bandwidth. Japanese practitioner reports flag this directly: when the human must talk to every agent, cognitive load becomes the limiting factor.&lt;/div&gt;" style="rounded=1;whiteSpace=wrap;html=1;fillColor=#ffffff;strokeColor=#c5d0db;spacing=11;align=left;verticalAlign=top;" vertex="1" parent="1">
          <mxGeometry x="815" y="1015.11" width="755" height="187" as="geometry" />
        </mxCell>
        <mxCell id="s6" value="&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:15px&quot;&gt;&lt;b&gt;STRUCTURE&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:13px;line-height:1.35&quot;&gt;&lt;font face=&quot;monospace&quot;&gt;Anti-pattern: N agents -&amp;gt; N live conversations -&amp;gt; 1 human (saturated). Corrected: N agents -&amp;gt; aggregator/orchestrator -&amp;gt; summarised status + prioritised exceptions -&amp;gt; 1 human (bounded surface).&lt;/font&gt;&lt;/div&gt;" style="rounded=1;whiteSpace=wrap;html=1;fillColor=#ffffff;strokeColor=#c5d0db;spacing=11;align=left;verticalAlign=top;" vertex="1" parent="1">
          <mxGeometry x="30" y="1121.11" width="755" height="111" as="geometry" />
        </mxCell>
        <mxCell id="s7" value="&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:15px&quot;&gt;&lt;b&gt;EXAMPLE SCENARIO&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:13px;line-height:1.35&quot;&gt;A developer runs a spec-driven setup that spawns five sub-agents, each pinging them for clarification on its slice of the build. Within an hour they are switching between five chat threads, losing track of which agent was told what, and approving diffs they have not really read just to keep all five moving. The five-way parallelism that promised speed has collapsed into one exhausted human serialising everything through their own attention.&lt;/div&gt;" style="rounded=1;whiteSpace=wrap;html=1;fillColor=#ffffff;strokeColor=#c5d0db;spacing=11;align=left;verticalAlign=top;" vertex="1" parent="1">
          <mxGeometry x="815" y="1216.11" width="755" height="149" as="geometry" />
        </mxCell>
        <mxCell id="s8" value="&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:15px&quot;&gt;&lt;b&gt;CONSEQUENCES&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:13px;line-height:1.35&quot;&gt;&lt;b&gt;Benefits&lt;/b&gt;&lt;br&gt;&lt;br&gt;&amp;bull; Short-term: talking to each agent directly needs no extra tooling and gives the supervisor a feeling of close control.&lt;br&gt;&amp;bull; Naming it lets a team cap agents per supervisor and add an aggregation layer before the oversight load breaks.&lt;br&gt;&lt;br&gt;&lt;b&gt;Liabilities&lt;/b&gt;&lt;br&gt;&lt;br&gt;&amp;bull; Oversight quality collapses as the supervisor thrashes between agents.&lt;br&gt;&amp;bull; Rubber-stamping creeps in to keep pace, nullifying the human check.&lt;br&gt;&amp;bull; The human becomes the throughput bottleneck, erasing the parallelism gain.&lt;br&gt;&amp;bull; Errors slip through because no one holds full context on any single agent.&lt;br&gt;&amp;bull; Supervisor fatigue and burnout make the arrangement unsustainable.&lt;/div&gt;" style="rounded=1;whiteSpace=wrap;html=1;fillColor=#ffffff;strokeColor=#c5d0db;spacing=11;align=left;verticalAlign=top;" vertex="1" parent="1">
          <mxGeometry x="30" y="1246.11" width="755" height="320" as="geometry" />
        </mxCell>
        <mxCell id="s9" value="&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:15px&quot;&gt;&lt;b&gt;WHAT THIS PATTERN CONSTRAINS&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:13px;line-height:1.35&quot;&gt;The system must not require a human to hold live context on, and individually converse with, every concurrent sub-agent; supervision has to be aggregated and selectively escalated so the human&#39;s attention surface stays bounded as the agent count grows.&lt;/div&gt;" style="rounded=1;whiteSpace=wrap;html=1;fillColor=#ffffff;strokeColor=#c5d0db;spacing=11;align=left;verticalAlign=top;" vertex="1" parent="1">
          <mxGeometry x="815" y="1379.11" width="755" height="111" as="geometry" />
        </mxCell>
        <mxCell id="s10" value="&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:15px&quot;&gt;&lt;b&gt;USE WHEN&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:13px;line-height:1.35&quot;&gt;&amp;bull; You are reviewing a multi-agent design where every agent reports to one human.&lt;br&gt;&amp;bull; Supervisors report thrashing or falling behind as agent count grows.&lt;br&gt;&amp;bull; Approvals are being rubber-stamped to keep pace.&lt;br&gt;&amp;bull; You are deciding how many agents one person can realistically oversee.&lt;/div&gt;" style="rounded=1;whiteSpace=wrap;html=1;fillColor=#ffffff;strokeColor=#c5d0db;spacing=11;align=left;verticalAlign=top;" vertex="1" parent="1">
          <mxGeometry x="30" y="1580.11" width="755" height="130" as="geometry" />
        </mxCell>
        <mxCell id="s11" value="&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:15px&quot;&gt;&lt;b&gt;DO NOT USE WHEN&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:13px;line-height:1.35&quot;&gt;&amp;bull; Only one or two agents run and the human comfortably tracks both.&lt;br&gt;&amp;bull; An aggregation and selective-escalation layer already bounds the human&#39;s surface.&lt;br&gt;&amp;bull; Agents are fully autonomous and no per-agent human steering is required.&lt;br&gt;&amp;bull; Supervision is already distributed across multiple people or a lead agent.&lt;/div&gt;" style="rounded=1;whiteSpace=wrap;html=1;fillColor=#ffffff;strokeColor=#c5d0db;spacing=11;align=left;verticalAlign=top;" vertex="1" parent="1">
          <mxGeometry x="815" y="1504.11" width="755" height="130" as="geometry" />
        </mxCell>
        <mxCell id="s12" value="&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:15px&quot;&gt;&lt;b&gt;COMPONENTS&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:13px;line-height:1.35&quot;&gt;&amp;bull; Concurrent sub-agents — the parallel workers each generating supervision demand&lt;br&gt;&amp;bull; Overloaded supervisor — the single human expected to steer all of them&lt;br&gt;&amp;bull; Missing aggregation layer — the absent orchestrator/summariser whose lack causes the saturation&lt;br&gt;&amp;bull; Escalation policy — the selective filter that, when present, keeps the human&#39;s surface bounded&lt;/div&gt;" style="rounded=1;whiteSpace=wrap;html=1;fillColor=#ffffff;strokeColor=#c5d0db;spacing=11;align=left;verticalAlign=top;" vertex="1" parent="1">
          <mxGeometry x="30" y="1724.11" width="755" height="130" as="geometry" />
        </mxCell>
        <mxCell id="s13" value="&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:15px&quot;&gt;&lt;b&gt;TOOLS&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:13px;line-height:1.35&quot;&gt;&amp;bull; Status dashboard — aggregates sub-agent state so the human sees one prioritised surface instead of N chats&lt;br&gt;&amp;bull; Orchestrator/lead agent — absorbs routine clarifications that would otherwise reach the human&lt;br&gt;&amp;bull; Escalation router — forwards only decisions that genuinely need human judgement&lt;/div&gt;" style="rounded=1;whiteSpace=wrap;html=1;fillColor=#ffffff;strokeColor=#c5d0db;spacing=11;align=left;verticalAlign=top;" vertex="1" parent="1">
          <mxGeometry x="815" y="1648.11" width="755" height="130" as="geometry" />
        </mxCell>
        <mxCell id="s14" value="&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:15px&quot;&gt;&lt;b&gt;FAILURE MODES&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:13px;line-height:1.35&quot;&gt;&amp;bull; Context thrash — the supervisor switches between agent conversations, forgets which agent said what, and gives one agent instructions meant for another.&lt;br&gt;&amp;bull; Rubber-stamp creep — to keep pace with the queue of agent questions, the human approves outputs without reading them, and the oversight check becomes empty.&lt;br&gt;&amp;bull; Human bottleneck — agents sit idle waiting for clarification from one person, so adding agents adds waiting time instead of throughput.&lt;/div&gt;" style="rounded=1;whiteSpace=wrap;html=1;fillColor=#ffffff;strokeColor=#c5d0db;spacing=11;align=left;verticalAlign=top;" vertex="1" parent="1">
          <mxGeometry x="30" y="1868.11" width="755" height="168" as="geometry" />
        </mxCell>
        <mxCell id="s15" value="&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:15px&quot;&gt;&lt;b&gt;EVALUATION METRICS&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:13px;line-height:1.35&quot;&gt;&amp;bull; Agents-per-supervisor — concurrent agents one human is expected to steer&lt;br&gt;&amp;bull; Approval dwell time — how long agents wait on the saturated human&lt;br&gt;&amp;bull; Rubber-stamp rate — approvals granted without substantive review&lt;br&gt;&amp;bull; Escaped-error rate — mistakes that passed because no one held full context&lt;/div&gt;" style="rounded=1;whiteSpace=wrap;html=1;fillColor=#ffffff;strokeColor=#c5d0db;spacing=11;align=left;verticalAlign=top;" vertex="1" parent="1">
          <mxGeometry x="815" y="1792.11" width="755" height="130" as="geometry" />
        </mxCell>
        <mxCell id="s16" value="&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:15px&quot;&gt;&lt;b&gt;KNOWN USES&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:13px;line-height:1.35&quot;&gt;&amp;bull; &lt;b&gt;&lt;a href=&quot;https://zenn.dev/ryo369/articles/d02561ddaacc62&quot;&gt;AIエージェント時代、正直しんどい話 (Zenn)&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/b&gt; &lt;i&gt;available&lt;/i&gt; — Reports that spec-driven sub-agent delegation forces the human to converse with all the agents, and identifies the resulting cognitive load as the limiting problem.&lt;br&gt;&amp;bull; &lt;b&gt;&lt;a href=&quot;https://www.anthropic.com/engineering/effective-context-engineering-for-ai-agents&quot;&gt;Anthropic — Effective context engineering for AI agents&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/b&gt; &lt;i&gt;available&lt;/i&gt; — Motivates orchestrator/sub-agent layering and selective surfacing so a human is not in the loop on every parallel agent&#39;s full context.&lt;/div&gt;" style="rounded=1;whiteSpace=wrap;html=1;fillColor=#ffffff;strokeColor=#c5d0db;spacing=11;align=left;verticalAlign=top;" vertex="1" parent="1">
          <mxGeometry x="30" y="2050.11" width="755" height="168" as="geometry" />
        </mxCell>
        <mxCell id="s17" value="&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:15px&quot;&gt;&lt;b&gt;RELATED PATTERNS&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:13px;line-height:1.35&quot;&gt;&amp;bull; &lt;i&gt;complements&lt;/i&gt; &lt;b&gt;Orchestrator as Bottleneck&lt;/b&gt; — Orchestrator-as-bottleneck saturates a coordinating agent; supervisor cognitive overload saturates the coordinating human. Same shape, different node.&lt;br&gt;&amp;bull; &lt;i&gt;complements&lt;/i&gt; &lt;b&gt;Unbounded Subagent Spawn&lt;/b&gt; — Unbounded spawning is a common trigger: the more sub-agents created, the heavier the human supervision surface becomes.&lt;br&gt;&amp;bull; &lt;i&gt;complements&lt;/i&gt; &lt;b&gt;Role-Typed Subagents&lt;/b&gt; — Role-typed sub-agents without an aggregation layer push their individual interactions straight onto one overloaded supervisor.&lt;br&gt;&amp;bull; &lt;i&gt;complements&lt;/i&gt; &lt;b&gt;Agent Output Alert Fatigue&lt;/b&gt; — Overload exceeds supervisor capacity; alert fatigue desensitises the control within capacity.&lt;br&gt;&amp;bull; &lt;i&gt;complements&lt;/i&gt; &lt;b&gt;Oversight as Legitimation&lt;/b&gt; — Overload is attention saturating across parallel sub-agents; legitimation can occur with a single case per day, since the missing ingredient is the ability to detect an error rather than the capacity to look.&lt;/div&gt;" style="rounded=1;whiteSpace=wrap;html=1;fillColor=#ffffff;strokeColor=#c5d0db;spacing=11;align=left;verticalAlign=top;" vertex="1" parent="1">
          <mxGeometry x="815" y="1936.11" width="755" height="263" as="geometry" />
        </mxCell>
        <mxCell id="s18" value="&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:15px&quot;&gt;&lt;b&gt;REFERENCES&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:13px;line-height:1.35&quot;&gt;&amp;bull; &lt;a href=&quot;https://zenn.dev/ryo369/articles/d02561ddaacc62&quot;&gt;AIエージェント時代、正直しんどい話&lt;/a&gt; — ryo369 (Zenn), 2026&lt;br&gt;&amp;bull; &lt;a href=&quot;https://www.anthropic.com/engineering/effective-context-engineering-for-ai-agents&quot;&gt;Effective context engineering for AI agents&lt;/a&gt; — Anthropic, 2025&lt;/div&gt;" style="rounded=1;whiteSpace=wrap;html=1;fillColor=#ffffff;strokeColor=#c5d0db;spacing=11;align=left;verticalAlign=top;" vertex="1" parent="1">
          <mxGeometry x="30" y="2232.11" width="755" height="111" as="geometry" />
        </mxCell>
      </root>
    </mxGraphModel>
  </diagram>
