        <mxCell id="arch-caption" value="&lt;i&gt;Tenant scope is bound from the authenticated request in the tool layer; the model supplies business arguments only and cannot change which tenant a call reads.&lt;/i&gt;" style="text;html=1;align=center;verticalAlign=middle;strokeColor=none;fillColor=none;fontSize=12;fontColor=#52606d;" vertex="1" parent="1">
          <mxGeometry x="65" y="692.99" width="1470" height="32" as="geometry" />
        </mxCell>
        <mxCell id="s0" value="&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:15px&quot;&gt;&lt;b&gt;INTENT&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:13px;line-height:1.35&quot;&gt;Bind every tool call and retrieval to the active tenant in code at the execution layer, so a multi-tenant agent can never be talked into reading or writing another tenant&#39;s data.&lt;/div&gt;" style="rounded=1;whiteSpace=wrap;html=1;fillColor=#ffffff;strokeColor=#c5d0db;spacing=11;align=left;verticalAlign=top;" vertex="1" parent="1">
          <mxGeometry x="30" y="767.99" width="755" height="92" as="geometry" />
        </mxCell>
        <mxCell id="s1" value="&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:15px&quot;&gt;&lt;b&gt;CONTEXT&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:13px;line-height:1.35&quot;&gt;A business runs one agent over a multi-tenant SaaS or business platform where every customer organisation sees only its own records. The same model, prompt, tools, and vector store serve all tenants; the only thing that differs between two requests is which organisation the caller belongs to. Tool calls — database reads, API writes, document retrieval — must be confined to the calling tenant&#39;s slice of the data, and the cost of getting that wrong is one customer reading another customer&#39;s records.&lt;/div&gt;" style="rounded=1;whiteSpace=wrap;html=1;fillColor=#ffffff;strokeColor=#c5d0db;spacing=11;align=left;verticalAlign=top;" vertex="1" parent="1">
          <mxGeometry x="815" y="767.99" width="755" height="168" as="geometry" />
        </mxCell>
        <mxCell id="s2" value="&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:15px&quot;&gt;&lt;b&gt;PROBLEM&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:13px;line-height:1.35&quot;&gt;If the tenant boundary is expressed only as an instruction in the system prompt — telling the model to act for organisation 42 and refuse anything else — the boundary depends on the model continuing to honour that instruction under adversarial input. A retrieved document, a tool result, or a crafted user message can pull the model into emitting a tool argument scoped to a different organisation, and the model will sometimes comply. Prompt-level scoping also leaves no enforceable contract at the data layer: a single missing filter in one tool silently exposes every tenant&#39;s rows. The boundary that matters most for trust is the one the model is least reliable at holding.&lt;/div&gt;" style="rounded=1;whiteSpace=wrap;html=1;fillColor=#ffffff;strokeColor=#c5d0db;spacing=11;align=left;verticalAlign=top;" vertex="1" parent="1">
          <mxGeometry x="30" y="873.99" width="755" height="187" as="geometry" />
        </mxCell>
        <mxCell id="s3" value="&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:15px&quot;&gt;&lt;b&gt;FORCES&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:13px;line-height:1.35&quot;&gt;&amp;bull; The model is convenient for routing a request but is not a trustworthy place to enforce an authorization boundary, because its output is shaped by untrusted retrieved content and user text.&lt;br&gt;&amp;bull; Carrying the tenant id as a tool argument the model fills in means the model can fill in the wrong one; carrying it out of band means the tool layer must thread it through every call.&lt;br&gt;&amp;bull; A shared vector store and shared database make pooling cheap, but pooling is exactly what makes a single missing tenant filter catastrophic.&lt;br&gt;&amp;bull; Per-tenant physical isolation — a database or index per customer — is the safest option but the most expensive to operate at scale.&lt;br&gt;&amp;bull; Tenant scope must be enforced on every retrieval and every tool call, yet developers add new tools faster than they remember to re-apply the filter.&lt;/div&gt;" style="rounded=1;whiteSpace=wrap;html=1;fillColor=#ffffff;strokeColor=#c5d0db;spacing=11;align=left;verticalAlign=top;" vertex="1" parent="1">
          <mxGeometry x="815" y="949.99" width="755" height="244" as="geometry" />
        </mxCell>
        <mxCell id="s4" value="&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:15px&quot;&gt;&lt;b&gt;THEREFORE&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:13px;line-height:1.35&quot;&gt;Therefore: resolve the tenant from the authenticated request context and enforce it in the tool and retrieval layer — as a query filter, a row-level-security predicate, or a per-tenant namespace bound from the caller&#39;s token — so the model never supplies, and can never override, which tenant&#39;s data a call touches.&lt;/div&gt;" style="rounded=1;whiteSpace=wrap;html=1;fillColor=#ffffff;strokeColor=#c5d0db;spacing=11;align=left;verticalAlign=top;" vertex="1" parent="1">
          <mxGeometry x="30" y="1074.99" width="755" height="130" as="geometry" />
        </mxCell>
        <mxCell id="s5" value="&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:15px&quot;&gt;&lt;b&gt;SOLUTION&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:13px;line-height:1.35&quot;&gt;Derive the tenant identifier from the authenticated session or token at the trust boundary, not from anything the model produces. Pass it out of band into the tool-execution layer through a request-scoped context, closure, or middleware, and have every tool and retriever apply it as a mandatory predicate: a WHERE tenant_id = ? filter, a Postgres row-level-security policy keyed on the connection&#39;s tenant, or a vector-store namespace selected from the token rather than from a model argument. Tools accept business arguments only; the tenant scope is injected beneath them and is not part of the model&#39;s action schema. Retrieval requests carry the tenant id before they reach the index, so a query can only ever match the calling tenant&#39;s partition. Make the scoped accessor the only path to tenant data, so a newly added tool inherits the boundary by construction instead of by the author remembering to add a filter.&lt;/div&gt;" style="rounded=1;whiteSpace=wrap;html=1;fillColor=#ffffff;strokeColor=#c5d0db;spacing=11;align=left;verticalAlign=top;" vertex="1" parent="1">
          <mxGeometry x="815" y="1207.99" width="755" height="244" as="geometry" />
        </mxCell>
        <mxCell id="s6" value="&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:15px&quot;&gt;&lt;b&gt;STRUCTURE&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:13px;line-height:1.35&quot;&gt;&lt;font face=&quot;monospace&quot;&gt;Auth boundary --(verified tenant_id)--&amp;gt; request-scoped context --&amp;gt; tool/retriever middleware --(mandatory tenant predicate)--&amp;gt; data store. Model --(business args only, no tenant_id)--&amp;gt; tool. Vector store partitioned by tenant namespace selected from the token, not from model output.&lt;/font&gt;&lt;/div&gt;" style="rounded=1;whiteSpace=wrap;html=1;fillColor=#ffffff;strokeColor=#c5d0db;spacing=11;align=left;verticalAlign=top;" vertex="1" parent="1">
          <mxGeometry x="30" y="1218.99" width="755" height="130" as="geometry" />
        </mxCell>
        <mxCell id="s7" value="&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:15px&quot;&gt;&lt;b&gt;EXAMPLE SCENARIO&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:13px;line-height:1.35&quot;&gt;A B2B analytics SaaS gives every customer an in-app assistant that answers questions over that customer&#39;s data. Early on the tenant is named in the system prompt — the assistant is told it serves Acme Corp — until a pasted support email containing instructions about a different account makes it fetch and summarise the wrong customer&#39;s invoices. The team moves the tenant id out of the prompt: it is read from the signed session token, threaded into a request-scoped context, and applied as a row-level-security predicate on every query and as the vector-store namespace for every retrieval. The assistant&#39;s tools now accept only business arguments, and no prompt or pasted text can change which organisation a call reads.&lt;/div&gt;" style="rounded=1;whiteSpace=wrap;html=1;fillColor=#ffffff;strokeColor=#c5d0db;spacing=11;align=left;verticalAlign=top;" vertex="1" parent="1">
          <mxGeometry x="815" y="1465.99" width="755" height="206" as="geometry" />
        </mxCell>
        <mxCell id="s8" value="&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:15px&quot;&gt;&lt;b&gt;CONSEQUENCES&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:13px;line-height:1.35&quot;&gt;&lt;b&gt;Benefits&lt;/b&gt;&lt;br&gt;&lt;br&gt;&amp;bull; A compromised or misled model cannot widen its data scope, because the scope is not in its hands.&lt;br&gt;&amp;bull; The tenant boundary is enforced in one place per data store and inherited by every tool, rather than re-implemented per tool.&lt;br&gt;&amp;bull; Audits and tests can assert the predicate is always applied, independent of model behaviour.&lt;br&gt;&amp;bull; Pooled storage stays cheap because isolation is logical and enforced, not bought through per-tenant infrastructure.&lt;br&gt;&lt;br&gt;&lt;b&gt;Liabilities&lt;/b&gt;&lt;br&gt;&lt;br&gt;&amp;bull; Threading a request-scoped tenant id through every tool and data path adds plumbing and discipline a prototype may not have.&lt;br&gt;&amp;bull; Row-level security and per-tenant namespaces must be configured correctly once; a misconfigured policy fails open as silently as a missing filter.&lt;br&gt;&amp;bull; Cross-tenant features such as admin views or benchmarking need an explicit, separately-audited escape from the default scope.&lt;br&gt;&amp;bull; Logs, caches, and embeddings derived from tenant data inherit the same isolation requirement and are easy to overlook.&lt;/div&gt;" style="rounded=1;whiteSpace=wrap;html=1;fillColor=#ffffff;strokeColor=#c5d0db;spacing=11;align=left;verticalAlign=top;" vertex="1" parent="1">
          <mxGeometry x="30" y="1362.99" width="755" height="415" as="geometry" />
        </mxCell>
        <mxCell id="s9" value="&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:15px&quot;&gt;&lt;b&gt;WHAT THIS PATTERN CONSTRAINS&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:13px;line-height:1.35&quot;&gt;The model must not be the authority for which tenant a tool call or retrieval targets: the tenant scope is bound from the authenticated request context in code, is not exposed as a model-supplied tool argument, and a tool with no resolvable tenant scope must refuse to execute rather than fall back to an unscoped query.&lt;/div&gt;" style="rounded=1;whiteSpace=wrap;html=1;fillColor=#ffffff;strokeColor=#c5d0db;spacing=11;align=left;verticalAlign=top;" vertex="1" parent="1">
          <mxGeometry x="815" y="1685.99" width="755" height="130" as="geometry" />
        </mxCell>
        <mxCell id="s10" value="&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:15px&quot;&gt;&lt;b&gt;USE WHEN&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:13px;line-height:1.35&quot;&gt;&amp;bull; One agent serves many customer organisations over shared storage and the data must stay strictly per-tenant.&lt;br&gt;&amp;bull; Untrusted content (retrieved documents, user messages, tool results) can reach the model and could influence a tool argument.&lt;br&gt;&amp;bull; A data leak across tenants is a serious commercial or regulatory event.&lt;br&gt;&amp;bull; New tools and data paths are added often and must inherit the boundary automatically.&lt;/div&gt;" style="rounded=1;whiteSpace=wrap;html=1;fillColor=#ffffff;strokeColor=#c5d0db;spacing=11;align=left;verticalAlign=top;" vertex="1" parent="1">
          <mxGeometry x="30" y="1791.99" width="755" height="168" as="geometry" />
        </mxCell>
        <mxCell id="s11" value="&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:15px&quot;&gt;&lt;b&gt;DO NOT USE WHEN&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:13px;line-height:1.35&quot;&gt;&amp;bull; The deployment is single-tenant, so there is no other tenant to leak to.&lt;br&gt;&amp;bull; Every tenant already has fully isolated infrastructure and no code path is shared.&lt;br&gt;&amp;bull; The agent only reads data the caller could see anyway, with no cross-customer surface.&lt;/div&gt;" style="rounded=1;whiteSpace=wrap;html=1;fillColor=#ffffff;strokeColor=#c5d0db;spacing=11;align=left;verticalAlign=top;" vertex="1" parent="1">
          <mxGeometry x="815" y="1829.99" width="755" height="111" as="geometry" />
        </mxCell>
        <mxCell id="s12" value="&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:15px&quot;&gt;&lt;b&gt;COMPONENTS&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:13px;line-height:1.35&quot;&gt;&amp;bull; Auth boundary — verifies the caller and extracts the tenant id from the session or token&lt;br&gt;&amp;bull; Request-scoped tenant context — carries the verified tenant id out of band to every tool and retriever&lt;br&gt;&amp;bull; Tool/retriever middleware — applies the tenant id as a mandatory query predicate or namespace before any data access&lt;br&gt;&amp;bull; Scoped data accessor — the only path to tenant data, so new tools inherit the boundary by construction&lt;br&gt;&amp;bull; Cross-tenant escape — an explicit, separately-audited accessor for admin or aggregate views&lt;/div&gt;" style="rounded=1;whiteSpace=wrap;html=1;fillColor=#ffffff;strokeColor=#c5d0db;spacing=11;align=left;verticalAlign=top;" vertex="1" parent="1">
          <mxGeometry x="30" y="1973.99" width="755" height="206" as="geometry" />
        </mxCell>
        <mxCell id="s13" value="&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:15px&quot;&gt;&lt;b&gt;TOOLS&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:13px;line-height:1.35&quot;&gt;&amp;bull; Row-level security (Postgres RLS or equivalent) — database-enforced per-tenant row filtering&lt;br&gt;&amp;bull; Vector store with per-tenant namespaces — retrieval partitioned so a query matches only the caller&#39;s tenant&lt;br&gt;&amp;bull; Identity provider / JWT — supplies the authenticated tenant claim used to bind scope&lt;/div&gt;" style="rounded=1;whiteSpace=wrap;html=1;fillColor=#ffffff;strokeColor=#c5d0db;spacing=11;align=left;verticalAlign=top;" vertex="1" parent="1">
          <mxGeometry x="815" y="1954.99" width="755" height="130" as="geometry" />
        </mxCell>
        <mxCell id="s14" value="&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:15px&quot;&gt;&lt;b&gt;GUARDRAILS&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:13px;line-height:1.35&quot;&gt;&amp;bull; Default-deny accessor — a tool with no resolvable tenant scope refuses rather than running unscoped&lt;br&gt;&amp;bull; Isolation tests in CI — automated assertions that every data path applies the tenant predicate&lt;/div&gt;" style="rounded=1;whiteSpace=wrap;html=1;fillColor=#ffffff;strokeColor=#c5d0db;spacing=11;align=left;verticalAlign=top;" vertex="1" parent="1">
          <mxGeometry x="30" y="2193.99" width="755" height="111" as="geometry" />
        </mxCell>
        <mxCell id="s15" value="&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:15px&quot;&gt;&lt;b&gt;FAILURE MODES&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:13px;line-height:1.35&quot;&gt;&amp;bull; Fail-open policy — a row-level-security policy is misconfigured, returns rows from every tenant, and nothing errors.&lt;br&gt;&amp;bull; Unscoped side path — a cache, log, or embedding index built from tenant data lacks the tenant key, and one tenant&#39;s content surfaces for another.&lt;br&gt;&amp;bull; Unaudited escape — an admin or benchmarking feature bypasses the default scope, and the agent reaches it through a normal tool path.&lt;/div&gt;" style="rounded=1;whiteSpace=wrap;html=1;fillColor=#ffffff;strokeColor=#c5d0db;spacing=11;align=left;verticalAlign=top;" vertex="1" parent="1">
          <mxGeometry x="815" y="2098.99" width="755" height="168" as="geometry" />
        </mxCell>
        <mxCell id="s16" value="&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:15px&quot;&gt;&lt;b&gt;EVALUATION METRICS&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:13px;line-height:1.35&quot;&gt;&amp;bull; Cross-tenant access rate — requests that returned another tenant&#39;s data; the target is zero&lt;br&gt;&amp;bull; Unscoped-query escape rate — fraction of tool calls reaching a data store without the tenant predicate applied&lt;br&gt;&amp;bull; Red-team isolation pass-rate — share of prompt-injection attempts that fail to widen tenant scope&lt;br&gt;&amp;bull; New-tool coverage — share of tools reaching tenant data exclusively through the scoped accessor&lt;/div&gt;" style="rounded=1;whiteSpace=wrap;html=1;fillColor=#ffffff;strokeColor=#c5d0db;spacing=11;align=left;verticalAlign=top;" vertex="1" parent="1">
          <mxGeometry x="30" y="2318.99" width="755" height="149" as="geometry" />
        </mxCell>
        <mxCell id="s17" value="&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:15px&quot;&gt;&lt;b&gt;KNOWN USES&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:13px;line-height:1.35&quot;&gt;&amp;bull; &lt;b&gt;&lt;a href=&quot;https://aws.amazon.com/blogs/machine-learning/building-multi-tenant-agents-with-amazon-bedrock-agentcore/&quot;&gt;Amazon Bedrock AgentCore&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/b&gt; &lt;i&gt;available&lt;/i&gt; — Multi-tenant agent deployments isolate each tenant&#39;s data and resources below the agent rather than via the prompt.&lt;br&gt;&amp;bull; &lt;b&gt;&lt;a href=&quot;https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/architecture/ai-ml/guide/secure-multitenant-rag&quot;&gt;Azure Architecture Center — Secure Multitenant RAG&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/b&gt; &lt;i&gt;available&lt;/i&gt; — Reference architecture enforcing tenant isolation at the retrieval and vector layer, treating model-level access control as insufficient.&lt;br&gt;&amp;bull; &lt;b&gt;&lt;a href=&quot;https://www.scalekit.com/blog/access-control-multi-tenant-ai-agents&quot;&gt;Scalekit&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/b&gt; &lt;i&gt;available&lt;/i&gt; — Identity and isolation layer for multi-tenant agents that scopes tool access by the authenticated tenant.&lt;/div&gt;" style="rounded=1;whiteSpace=wrap;html=1;fillColor=#ffffff;strokeColor=#c5d0db;spacing=11;align=left;verticalAlign=top;" vertex="1" parent="1">
          <mxGeometry x="815" y="2280.99" width="755" height="206" as="geometry" />
        </mxCell>
        <mxCell id="s18" value="&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:15px&quot;&gt;&lt;b&gt;RELATED PATTERNS&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:13px;line-height:1.35&quot;&gt;&amp;bull; &lt;i&gt;complements&lt;/i&gt; &lt;b&gt;Session Isolation&lt;/b&gt; — Session isolation separates one conversation&#39;s state from another&#39;s; tenant binding separates one customer&#39;s data from another&#39;s. Both isolate, on different axes.&lt;br&gt;&amp;bull; &lt;i&gt;complements&lt;/i&gt; &lt;b&gt;Tool Over-Broad Scope&lt;/b&gt; — Over-broad tool scope is the failure this pattern narrows: the tenant predicate bounds what an otherwise broad tool can reach.&lt;br&gt;&amp;bull; &lt;i&gt;complements&lt;/i&gt; &lt;b&gt;Policy-as-Code Gate&lt;/b&gt; — A policy gate decides whether an action is allowed; tenant binding decides which tenant&#39;s data it may touch. The two stack.&lt;br&gt;&amp;bull; &lt;i&gt;complements&lt;/i&gt; &lt;b&gt;Agentic RAG&lt;/b&gt; — Retrieval must carry the tenant id before it reaches the index so a query can only match the caller&#39;s partition.&lt;br&gt;&amp;bull; &lt;i&gt;complements&lt;/i&gt; &lt;b&gt;Delegated Agent Authorization&lt;/b&gt; — Delegated authorization scopes what the agent may do on a principal&#39;s behalf; tenant binding scopes which tenant&#39;s records it sees.&lt;br&gt;&amp;bull; &lt;i&gt;complements&lt;/i&gt; &lt;b&gt;Risk-Tiered Action Autonomy&lt;/b&gt;&lt;br&gt;&amp;bull; &lt;i&gt;complements&lt;/i&gt; &lt;b&gt;Canonical-Entity Grounding&lt;/b&gt; — The resolver that grounds identifiers must itself be tenant-scoped, or it leaks the existence of other tenants&#39; entities.&lt;/div&gt;" style="rounded=1;whiteSpace=wrap;html=1;fillColor=#ffffff;strokeColor=#c5d0db;spacing=11;align=left;verticalAlign=top;" vertex="1" parent="1">
          <mxGeometry x="30" y="2481.99" width="755" height="301" as="geometry" />
        </mxCell>
        <mxCell id="s19" value="&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:15px&quot;&gt;&lt;b&gt;REFERENCES&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:13px;line-height:1.35&quot;&gt;&amp;bull; &lt;a href=&quot;https://zenn.dev/geekplus/articles/e9ea7d8e183eb5&quot;&gt;マルチテナントSaaSにAIエージェントを組み込むとき、テナント分離をどう設計するか&lt;/a&gt; — 2026&lt;br&gt;&amp;bull; &lt;a href=&quot;https://docs.aws.amazon.com/prescriptive-guidance/latest/agentic-ai-multitenant/enforcing-tenant-isolation.html&quot;&gt;Enforcing tenant isolation — AWS Prescriptive Guidance (Agentic AI multitenant)&lt;/a&gt; — 2026&lt;br&gt;&amp;bull; &lt;a href=&quot;https://learn.microsoft.com/en-us/azure/architecture/ai-ml/guide/secure-multitenant-rag&quot;&gt;Design a Secure Multitenant RAG Inferencing Solution — Azure Architecture Center&lt;/a&gt; — 2026&lt;br&gt;&amp;bull; &lt;a href=&quot;https://aws.amazon.com/blogs/machine-learning/multi-tenant-rag-implementation-with-amazon-bedrock-and-amazon-opensearch-service-for-saas-using-jwt/&quot;&gt;Multi-tenant RAG implementation with Amazon Bedrock and Amazon OpenSearch Service for SaaS using JWT&lt;/a&gt; — AWS Machine Learning Blog, 2026&lt;/div&gt;" style="rounded=1;whiteSpace=wrap;html=1;fillColor=#ffffff;strokeColor=#c5d0db;spacing=11;align=left;verticalAlign=top;" vertex="1" parent="1">
          <mxGeometry x="815" y="2500.99" width="755" height="206" as="geometry" />
        </mxCell>
      </root>
    </mxGraphModel>
  </diagram>
  <diagram id="test-time-compute-scaling" name="Test-Time Compute Scaling">
    <mxGraphModel dx="1600" dy="2712.05" grid="1" gridSize="10" guides="1" tooltips="1" connect="1" arrows="1" fold="1" page="1" pageScale="1" pageWidth="1600" pageHeight="2712.05" math="0" shadow="0">
      <root>
        <mxCell id="0" />
