        <UserObject label="" mermaidId="e:R-&gt;A#0" mermaidBaseStyle="curved=1;startArrow=none;endArrow=block;endSize=7;strokeColor=light-dark(#333333,#cccccc);exitX=0.5;exitY=1;entryX=0.5;entryY=0;" mermaidBaseValue="" id="m12">
          <mxCell edge="1" parent="1" source="m5" style="curved=1;startArrow=none;endArrow=block;endSize=7;strokeColor=light-dark(#333333,#cccccc);exitX=0.5;exitY=1;entryX=0.5;entryY=0;" target="m6">
            <mxGeometry relative="1" as="geometry">
              <Array as="points" />
            </mxGeometry>
          </mxCell>
        </UserObject>
        <mxCell id="arch-caption" value="&lt;i&gt;The tool returns the data correctly; the failure is computing the aggregate in-head instead of offloading it to a deterministic step and reading the result back.&lt;/i&gt;" style="text;html=1;align=center;verticalAlign=middle;strokeColor=none;fillColor=none;fontSize=12;fontColor=#52606d;" vertex="1" parent="1">
          <mxGeometry x="65" y="1438.2" width="1470" height="32" as="geometry" />
        </mxCell>
        <mxCell id="s0" value="&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:15px&quot;&gt;&lt;b&gt;INTENT&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:13px;line-height:1.35&quot;&gt;Anti-pattern: the agent compares, ranks, or sums correctly returned tool data in its own head instead of offloading the computation to a deterministic tool, emitting confident wrong aggregates.&lt;/div&gt;" style="rounded=1;whiteSpace=wrap;html=1;fillColor=#ffffff;strokeColor=#c5d0db;spacing=11;align=left;verticalAlign=top;" vertex="1" parent="1">
          <mxGeometry x="30" y="1513.2" width="755" height="92" as="geometry" />
        </mxCell>
        <mxCell id="s1" value="&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:15px&quot;&gt;&lt;b&gt;CONTEXT&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:13px;line-height:1.35&quot;&gt;An agent gathers data through tools — search hits with scores, rows of prices, durations, line items, or counts — and then has to combine those values to answer the user. The tool returns the data correctly; the remaining work is ordinary computation over it, such as finding the cheapest option, ranking results, summing a column, or comparing two totals. Because the data is already in the context window, treating the next step as more free-form text feels natural to the model.&lt;/div&gt;" style="rounded=1;whiteSpace=wrap;html=1;fillColor=#ffffff;strokeColor=#c5d0db;spacing=11;align=left;verticalAlign=top;" vertex="1" parent="1">
          <mxGeometry x="815" y="1513.2" width="755" height="149" as="geometry" />
        </mxCell>
        <mxCell id="s2" value="&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:15px&quot;&gt;&lt;b&gt;PROBLEM&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:13px;line-height:1.35&quot;&gt;Token-by-token generation is not arithmetic. When the model performs comparison, ranking, or addition over tool data inside its own reasoning rather than in a deterministic step, it produces answers that read as authoritative but are numerically wrong: a mis-sorted ranking, a total that is off, a wrong cheapest pick. The data was right and the tool was right, so nothing in the trace flags the error, and the confident wrong aggregate flows straight to the user or into the next decision.&lt;/div&gt;" style="rounded=1;whiteSpace=wrap;html=1;fillColor=#ffffff;strokeColor=#c5d0db;spacing=11;align=left;verticalAlign=top;" vertex="1" parent="1">
          <mxGeometry x="30" y="1619.2" width="755" height="149" as="geometry" />
        </mxCell>
        <mxCell id="s3" value="&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:15px&quot;&gt;&lt;b&gt;FORCES&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:13px;line-height:1.35&quot;&gt;&amp;bull; The tool already returned the values into context, so re-using a separate compute step feels redundant even though free-form generation is unreliable at exact arithmetic.&lt;br&gt;&amp;bull; Small inputs (a handful of rows) look easy enough to do in-head, but error rate rises silently with the number of items and the depth of the comparison.&lt;br&gt;&amp;bull; A wrong aggregate is indistinguishable in tone from a right one; there is no refusal or error to catch it, so the failure is silent.&lt;br&gt;&amp;bull; Forcing every comparison through a deterministic tool adds a call and a round-trip the agent would rather skip.&lt;/div&gt;" style="rounded=1;whiteSpace=wrap;html=1;fillColor=#ffffff;strokeColor=#c5d0db;spacing=11;align=left;verticalAlign=top;" vertex="1" parent="1">
          <mxGeometry x="815" y="1676.2" width="755" height="206" as="geometry" />
        </mxCell>
        <mxCell id="s4" value="&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:15px&quot;&gt;&lt;b&gt;THEREFORE&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:13px;line-height:1.35&quot;&gt;Therefore: avoid it by treating comparison, ranking, and arithmetic over tool data as computation to be offloaded to a deterministic tool, never as text the model generates from the values in its window.&lt;/div&gt;" style="rounded=1;whiteSpace=wrap;html=1;fillColor=#ffffff;strokeColor=#c5d0db;spacing=11;align=left;verticalAlign=top;" vertex="1" parent="1">
          <mxGeometry x="30" y="1782.2" width="755" height="111" as="geometry" />
        </mxCell>
        <mxCell id="s5" value="&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:15px&quot;&gt;&lt;b&gt;SOLUTION&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:13px;line-height:1.35&quot;&gt;The corrected stance is to route every aggregate over tool data through a deterministic step rather than the model&#39;s free-form output. After a tool returns rows, the agent passes them to a calculator, a code-execution sandbox, a sort or filter primitive, or a query, and reads back the computed result; the model&#39;s job is to choose what to compute and how to phrase the answer, not to be the adder or the comparator. Where a single deterministic step is impractical, the aggregate is at least recomputed and cross-checked before it is reported, so a numeric claim never rests solely on token generation.&lt;/div&gt;" style="rounded=1;whiteSpace=wrap;html=1;fillColor=#ffffff;strokeColor=#c5d0db;spacing=11;align=left;verticalAlign=top;" vertex="1" parent="1">
          <mxGeometry x="815" y="1896.2" width="755" height="187" as="geometry" />
        </mxCell>
        <mxCell id="s6" value="&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:15px&quot;&gt;&lt;b&gt;STRUCTURE&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:13px;line-height:1.35&quot;&gt;&lt;font face=&quot;monospace&quot;&gt;Tool returns data --&amp;gt; [ANTI: model computes aggregate in-head] --&amp;gt; confident wrong total&lt;br&gt;             \--&amp;gt; [FIX: deterministic tool computes aggregate] --&amp;gt; read-back verified result&lt;/font&gt;&lt;/div&gt;" style="rounded=1;whiteSpace=wrap;html=1;fillColor=#ffffff;strokeColor=#c5d0db;spacing=11;align=left;verticalAlign=top;" vertex="1" parent="1">
          <mxGeometry x="30" y="1907.2" width="755" height="111" as="geometry" />
        </mxCell>
        <mxCell id="s7" value="&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:15px&quot;&gt;&lt;b&gt;EXAMPLE SCENARIO&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:13px;line-height:1.35&quot;&gt;A travel agent calls a flights tool that correctly returns five fares. Asked for the cheapest, the agent eyeballs the list in its reasoning and confidently names a flight that is not actually the lowest fare. The tool was right and the data was right, so nothing flags the mistake, and the user books a pricier flight than they were told was cheapest.&lt;/div&gt;" style="rounded=1;whiteSpace=wrap;html=1;fillColor=#ffffff;strokeColor=#c5d0db;spacing=11;align=left;verticalAlign=top;" vertex="1" parent="1">
          <mxGeometry x="815" y="2097.2" width="755" height="130" as="geometry" />
        </mxCell>
        <mxCell id="s8" value="&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:15px&quot;&gt;&lt;b&gt;CONSEQUENCES&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:13px;line-height:1.35&quot;&gt;&lt;b&gt;Benefits&lt;/b&gt;&lt;br&gt;&lt;br&gt;&amp;bull; Short-term: letting the model sum or rank the rows directly saves a tool call and returns an answer that reads as authoritative.&lt;br&gt;&amp;bull; Naming it lets a team route every aggregate over tool data through a deterministic step and keep the model to choosing what to compute.&lt;br&gt;&lt;br&gt;&lt;b&gt;Liabilities&lt;/b&gt;&lt;br&gt;&lt;br&gt;&amp;bull; Confident wrong aggregates — a mis-ranked list, an off-by-some total, or a wrong cheapest pick — reach the user with no warning.&lt;br&gt;&amp;bull; The error is invisible in the trace because both the tool call and its returned data are correct; only the in-head step is wrong.&lt;br&gt;&amp;bull; Downstream steps that branch on the bad aggregate compound the mistake into a wrong action.&lt;br&gt;&amp;bull; Error rate scales with the number of items and the comparison depth, so the failure surfaces exactly on the larger inputs that matter most.&lt;/div&gt;" style="rounded=1;whiteSpace=wrap;html=1;fillColor=#ffffff;strokeColor=#c5d0db;spacing=11;align=left;verticalAlign=top;" vertex="1" parent="1">
          <mxGeometry x="30" y="2032.2" width="755" height="358" as="geometry" />
        </mxCell>
        <mxCell id="s9" value="&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:15px&quot;&gt;&lt;b&gt;WHAT THIS PATTERN CONSTRAINS&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:13px;line-height:1.35&quot;&gt;Avoiding it forbids the agent from computing aggregates over tool data itself: comparison, ranking, and arithmetic must be offloaded to a deterministic tool, and a numeric claim is never reported until it has been read back from that computation.&lt;/div&gt;" style="rounded=1;whiteSpace=wrap;html=1;fillColor=#ffffff;strokeColor=#c5d0db;spacing=11;align=left;verticalAlign=top;" vertex="1" parent="1">
          <mxGeometry x="815" y="2241.2" width="755" height="111" as="geometry" />
        </mxCell>
        <mxCell id="s10" value="&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:15px&quot;&gt;&lt;b&gt;USE WHEN&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:13px;line-height:1.35&quot;&gt;&amp;bull; Watch for this when an agent answers with totals, rankings, or comparisons derived from tool data but no deterministic compute step appears in the trace.&lt;br&gt;&amp;bull; Watch for it when answers are correct on small inputs but degrade on larger result sets.&lt;br&gt;&amp;bull; Watch for it when a numeric recommendation drives a downstream action and there is no read-back of the computed value.&lt;/div&gt;" style="rounded=1;whiteSpace=wrap;html=1;fillColor=#ffffff;strokeColor=#c5d0db;spacing=11;align=left;verticalAlign=top;" vertex="1" parent="1">
          <mxGeometry x="30" y="2404.2" width="755" height="149" as="geometry" />
        </mxCell>
        <mxCell id="s11" value="&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:15px&quot;&gt;&lt;b&gt;DO NOT USE WHEN&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:13px;line-height:1.35&quot;&gt;&amp;bull; The agent already offloads every aggregate to a calculator, code sandbox, sort, or query and reads the result back — the anti-pattern does not apply.&lt;br&gt;&amp;bull; The answer is purely qualitative with no comparison, ranking, or arithmetic over the returned values.&lt;/div&gt;" style="rounded=1;whiteSpace=wrap;html=1;fillColor=#ffffff;strokeColor=#c5d0db;spacing=11;align=left;verticalAlign=top;" vertex="1" parent="1">
          <mxGeometry x="815" y="2366.2" width="755" height="130" as="geometry" />
        </mxCell>
        <mxCell id="s12" value="&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:15px&quot;&gt;&lt;b&gt;COMPONENTS&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:13px;line-height:1.35&quot;&gt;&amp;bull; Tool layer — returns the raw data (scores, prices, counts, durations) that the aggregate is computed over&lt;br&gt;&amp;bull; Aggregation step — where comparison, ranking, or arithmetic happens; the anti-pattern lets the model do this in free-form generation&lt;br&gt;&amp;bull; Deterministic compute tool — the calculator, code sandbox, sort/filter primitive, or query that should perform the aggregate instead of the model&lt;br&gt;&amp;bull; Read-back check — re-reads the computed value so a numeric claim never rests on token generation alone&lt;br&gt;&amp;bull; Reporting step — phrases the answer; in the corrected form it states only values produced by the deterministic step&lt;/div&gt;" style="rounded=1;whiteSpace=wrap;html=1;fillColor=#ffffff;strokeColor=#c5d0db;spacing=11;align=left;verticalAlign=top;" vertex="1" parent="1">
          <mxGeometry x="30" y="2567.2" width="755" height="244" as="geometry" />
        </mxCell>
        <mxCell id="s13" value="&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:15px&quot;&gt;&lt;b&gt;TOOLS&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:13px;line-height:1.35&quot;&gt;&amp;bull; Code-execution sandbox — runs the comparison or arithmetic deterministically and returns the exact result&lt;br&gt;&amp;bull; Calculator / math tool — offloads sums, totals, and numeric comparison out of the model&#39;s free-form output&lt;br&gt;&amp;bull; Sort, filter, and query primitives — produce rankings and selections (cheapest, top-k) deterministically over the returned rows&lt;/div&gt;" style="rounded=1;whiteSpace=wrap;html=1;fillColor=#ffffff;strokeColor=#c5d0db;spacing=11;align=left;verticalAlign=top;" vertex="1" parent="1">
          <mxGeometry x="815" y="2510.2" width="755" height="168" as="geometry" />
        </mxCell>
        <mxCell id="s14" value="&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:15px&quot;&gt;&lt;b&gt;FAILURE MODES&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:13px;line-height:1.35&quot;&gt;&amp;bull; Silent miscount — the model sums or tallies a column of tool values and reports a total that is simply wrong.&lt;br&gt;&amp;bull; Mis-ranking — the model orders results by a returned score or price and gets the order wrong, recommending a worse option as the best.&lt;br&gt;&amp;bull; Wrong comparison — the model judges which of two tool-returned totals is larger and picks the wrong one, then acts on it.&lt;br&gt;&amp;bull; Over-trust on scale — small inputs happen to come out right, masking the failure until a larger input set produces a confident error.&lt;/div&gt;" style="rounded=1;whiteSpace=wrap;html=1;fillColor=#ffffff;strokeColor=#c5d0db;spacing=11;align=left;verticalAlign=top;" vertex="1" parent="1">
          <mxGeometry x="30" y="2825.2" width="755" height="206" as="geometry" />
        </mxCell>
        <mxCell id="s15" value="&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:15px&quot;&gt;&lt;b&gt;EVALUATION METRICS&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:13px;line-height:1.35&quot;&gt;&amp;bull; Aggregate accuracy — fraction of reported totals, rankings, and comparisons that match a ground-truth deterministic computation&lt;br&gt;&amp;bull; Offload rate — fraction of aggregates over tool data that are routed through a deterministic step rather than generated in-head&lt;br&gt;&amp;bull; Accuracy-vs-input-size curve — whether aggregate accuracy degrades as the number of items or comparison depth grows&lt;br&gt;&amp;bull; Silent-error rate — fraction of wrong aggregates that reach the user with no flag, refusal, or read-back&lt;/div&gt;" style="rounded=1;whiteSpace=wrap;html=1;fillColor=#ffffff;strokeColor=#c5d0db;spacing=11;align=left;verticalAlign=top;" vertex="1" parent="1">
          <mxGeometry x="815" y="2692.2" width="755" height="206" as="geometry" />
        </mxCell>
        <mxCell id="s16" value="&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:15px&quot;&gt;&lt;b&gt;KNOWN USES&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:13px;line-height:1.35&quot;&gt;&amp;bull; &lt;b&gt;&lt;a href=&quot;https://arxiv.org/abs/2508.19504&quot;&gt;Aegis (agent-environment failure taxonomy)&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/b&gt; &lt;i&gt;pure-future&lt;/i&gt; — Names Tool Output Processing Failure — the agent makes computational errors such as comparisons and ranking when processing correctly-returned tool outputs — as a high-frequency failure class in its annotated data.&lt;br&gt;&amp;bull; &lt;b&gt;Code-execution / tool-calculator offloading&lt;/b&gt; &lt;i&gt;available&lt;/i&gt; — Production agent stacks mitigate the failure by routing arithmetic and comparison through a code sandbox or calculator tool rather than the model&#39;s free-form output.&lt;br&gt;&amp;bull; &lt;b&gt;&lt;a href=&quot;https://developers.openai.com/api/docs/guides/tools-code-interpreter&quot;&gt;OpenAI Code Interpreter (Advanced Data Analysis)&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/b&gt; &lt;i&gt;available&lt;/i&gt; — Routes arithmetic, comparison, and data aggregation to a sandboxed Python runtime and reads back the computed result, the corrected offload this anti-pattern skips.&lt;/div&gt;" style="rounded=1;whiteSpace=wrap;html=1;fillColor=#ffffff;strokeColor=#c5d0db;spacing=11;align=left;verticalAlign=top;" vertex="1" parent="1">
          <mxGeometry x="30" y="3045.2" width="755" height="244" as="geometry" />
        </mxCell>
        <mxCell id="s17" value="&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:15px&quot;&gt;&lt;b&gt;RELATED PATTERNS&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:13px;line-height:1.35&quot;&gt;&amp;bull; &lt;i&gt;alternative-to&lt;/i&gt; &lt;b&gt;Code Execution&lt;/b&gt; — Code Execution is the corrected counterpart: it runs the computation in a sandbox and treats the run as the answer, which is exactly the offload this anti-pattern omits.&lt;br&gt;&amp;bull; &lt;i&gt;alternative-to&lt;/i&gt; &lt;b&gt;MRKL Systems (Modular Neuro-Symbolic)&lt;/b&gt; — MRKL routes computation to a symbolic expert (a calculator) instead of asking one model to do the math; this anti-pattern is what happens when that routing is skipped for aggregates over tool data.&lt;br&gt;&amp;bull; &lt;i&gt;complements&lt;/i&gt; &lt;b&gt;Tool Output Trusted Verbatim&lt;/b&gt; — Inverse failure on the same boundary: trusted-verbatim is about over-trusting the tool&#39;s content and safety, while this is the agent mis-computing over content the tool returned correctly.&lt;br&gt;&amp;bull; &lt;i&gt;complements&lt;/i&gt; &lt;b&gt;Premature Closure&lt;/b&gt; — Both are confidence-without-checking failures; premature closure skips constraints during answer generation, this skips deterministic computation over already-correct data.&lt;br&gt;&amp;bull; &lt;i&gt;complements&lt;/i&gt; &lt;b&gt;False Confidence Syndrome&lt;/b&gt; — The wrong aggregate is reported in the same authoritative tone as a correct one, which is the false-confidence surface that hides this failure from review.&lt;br&gt;&amp;bull; &lt;i&gt;complements&lt;/i&gt; &lt;b&gt;Solver-Ready Formulation Handoff&lt;/b&gt; — That anti-pattern is the agent computing over tool data in its head; this pattern moves the whole decision, not just the arithmetic, into a deterministic tool.&lt;/div&gt;" style="rounded=1;whiteSpace=wrap;html=1;fillColor=#ffffff;strokeColor=#c5d0db;spacing=11;align=left;verticalAlign=top;" vertex="1" parent="1">
          <mxGeometry x="815" y="2912.2" width="755" height="377" as="geometry" />
        </mxCell>
        <mxCell id="s18" value="&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:15px&quot;&gt;&lt;b&gt;REFERENCES&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:13px;line-height:1.35&quot;&gt;&amp;bull; &lt;a href=&quot;https://arxiv.org/abs/2508.19504&quot;&gt;Aegis: Taxonomy and Optimizations for Overcoming Agent-Environment Failures in LLM Agents&lt;/a&gt; — 2025&lt;br&gt;&amp;bull; &lt;a href=&quot;https://arxiv.org/abs/2211.10435&quot;&gt;PAL: Program-aided Language Models&lt;/a&gt; — Luyu Gao, Aman Madaan, Shuyan Zhou, Uri Alon, Pengfei Liu, Yiming Yang, Jamie Callan, Graham Neubig, 2022&lt;br&gt;&amp;bull; &lt;a href=&quot;https://arxiv.org/abs/2211.12588&quot;&gt;Program of Thoughts Prompting: Disentangling Computation from Reasoning for Numerical Reasoning Tasks&lt;/a&gt; — Wenhu Chen, Xueguang Ma, Xinyi Wang, William W. Cohen, 2022&lt;br&gt;&amp;bull; &lt;a href=&quot;https://arxiv.org/abs/2302.04761&quot;&gt;Toolformer: Language Models Can Teach Themselves to Use Tools&lt;/a&gt; — Timo Schick, Jane Dwivedi-Yu, Roberto Dessì, Roberta Raileanu, Maria Lomeli, Luke Zettlemoyer, Nicola Cancedda, Thomas Scialom, 2023&lt;/div&gt;" style="rounded=1;whiteSpace=wrap;html=1;fillColor=#ffffff;strokeColor=#c5d0db;spacing=11;align=left;verticalAlign=top;" vertex="1" parent="1">
          <mxGeometry x="30" y="3303.2" width="755" height="225" as="geometry" />
        </mxCell>
      </root>
    </mxGraphModel>
  </diagram>
  <diagram id="tool-output-poisoning" name="Tool Output Poisoning Defense">
    <mxGraphModel dx="1600" dy="2854.55" grid="1" gridSize="10" guides="1" tooltips="1" connect="1" arrows="1" fold="1" page="1" pageScale="1" pageWidth="1600" pageHeight="2854.55" math="0" shadow="0">
      <root>
        <mxCell id="0" />
        <mxCell id="1" parent="0" />
        <mxCell id="title" value="&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:24px&quot;&gt;&lt;b&gt;Tool Output Poisoning Defense&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:12px;color:#52606d&quot;&gt;Safety &amp;amp; Control &amp;nbsp; | &amp;nbsp; emerging &amp;nbsp; | &amp;nbsp; Also known as: Indirect Prompt Injection (Tools), Untrusted Tool Output&lt;/div&gt;" style="text;html=1;align=left;verticalAlign=middle;whiteSpace=wrap;strokeColor=none;fillColor=none;" vertex="1" parent="1">
          <mxGeometry x="30" y="20" width="1540" height="58" as="geometry" />
        </mxCell>
        <mxCell id="arch-panel" value="" style="rounded=1;whiteSpace=wrap;html=1;fillColor=#f5f8fc;strokeColor=#b6c6d8;strokeWidth=1;arcSize=12;" vertex="1" parent="1">
          <mxGeometry x="30" y="95" width="1540" height="1197.55" as="geometry" />
        </mxCell>
        <mxCell id="arch-heading" value="&lt;b&gt;ARCHITECTURE&lt;/b&gt; &amp;nbsp; &lt;span style=&quot;color:#52606d&quot;&gt;Editable diagram imported from the pattern source&lt;/span&gt;" style="text;html=1;align=left;verticalAlign=middle;strokeColor=none;fillColor=none;fontSize=14;" vertex="1" parent="1">
          <mxGeometry x="48" y="109" width="1504" height="28" as="geometry" />
