        <mxCell id="arch-caption" value="&lt;i&gt;All guards format through one helper; downstream parses once and triages mechanically by code.&lt;/i&gt;" style="text;html=1;align=center;verticalAlign=middle;strokeColor=none;fillColor=none;fontSize=12;fontColor=#52606d;" vertex="1" parent="1">
          <mxGeometry x="65" y="583.57" width="1470" height="32" as="geometry" />
        </mxCell>
        <mxCell id="s0" value="&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:15px&quot;&gt;&lt;b&gt;INTENT&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:13px;line-height:1.35&quot;&gt;Define a single source of truth for machine-readable refusal codes across all guard surfaces, so refusals can be triaged mechanically rather than by string-grepping ad-hoc human-readable messages.&lt;/div&gt;" style="rounded=1;whiteSpace=wrap;html=1;fillColor=#ffffff;strokeColor=#c5d0db;spacing=11;align=left;verticalAlign=top;" vertex="1" parent="1">
          <mxGeometry x="30" y="658.57" width="755" height="92" as="geometry" />
        </mxCell>
        <mxCell id="s1" value="&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:15px&quot;&gt;&lt;b&gt;CONTEXT&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:13px;line-height:1.35&quot;&gt;A mature agent stack accumulates many guard surfaces: a tool-loop guard, a skill-scanner that refuses risky imports, a post-compaction guard that rejects suspicious context restorations, an RCE backstop, an input/output guardrail. Each was added at a different time and emits its own refusal string in a different shape. Downstream observability — logs, audits, dashboards, on-call triage — has to grep through human-readable strings to count and classify refusals, and small wording changes silently break the dashboards.&lt;/div&gt;" style="rounded=1;whiteSpace=wrap;html=1;fillColor=#ffffff;strokeColor=#c5d0db;spacing=11;align=left;verticalAlign=top;" vertex="1" parent="1">
          <mxGeometry x="815" y="658.57" width="755" height="168" as="geometry" />
        </mxCell>
        <mxCell id="s2" value="&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:15px&quot;&gt;&lt;b&gt;PROBLEM&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:13px;line-height:1.35&quot;&gt;Refusals are the single most important class of events to triage cleanly: they are the boundary between policy-aligned behaviour and policy-violating behaviour. When every guard formats its own refusal string by hand, the audit story collapses. Counts of &#39;how many refusals last week, of what kind&#39; depend on regexes that break when one guard&#39;s author rephrases the message; legacy guards that pre-dated a category cannot be retrofitted without text-search risk; downstream consumers (a Slack alert, a dashboard, a fine-tuning negative example pipeline) all build their own ad-hoc parser. A single source of truth for refusal codes is the obvious lever; the team rarely pulls it because each guard feels self-contained.&lt;/div&gt;" style="rounded=1;whiteSpace=wrap;html=1;fillColor=#ffffff;strokeColor=#c5d0db;spacing=11;align=left;verticalAlign=top;" vertex="1" parent="1">
          <mxGeometry x="30" y="764.57" width="755" height="206" as="geometry" />
        </mxCell>
        <mxCell id="s3" value="&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:15px&quot;&gt;&lt;b&gt;FORCES&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:13px;line-height:1.35&quot;&gt;&amp;bull; Many independent guard surfaces emit refusals; centralisation is non-trivial.&lt;br&gt;&amp;bull; Codes must be machine-readable (enum-style) and human-readable in one string.&lt;br&gt;&amp;bull; Legacy refusal phrasings must keep working or existing dashboards break.&lt;br&gt;&amp;bull; New codes appear over time; the enum must be extensible without breaking parsers.&lt;br&gt;&amp;bull; Parsing must be cheap; refusal events fire on the hot path.&lt;/div&gt;" style="rounded=1;whiteSpace=wrap;html=1;fillColor=#ffffff;strokeColor=#c5d0db;spacing=11;align=left;verticalAlign=top;" vertex="1" parent="1">
          <mxGeometry x="815" y="840.57" width="755" height="149" as="geometry" />
        </mxCell>
        <mxCell id="s4" value="&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:15px&quot;&gt;&lt;b&gt;THEREFORE&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:13px;line-height:1.35&quot;&gt;Therefore: define a single ReasonCode enum with format and parse helpers, format every refusal across every guard as &#39;REFUSED: CODE: detail&#39;, preserve known legacy substrings as code aliases, and treat unknown codes as a parse miss rather than a crash, so refusal events become uniformly typed across the whole stack while legacy consumers keep working.&lt;/div&gt;" style="rounded=1;whiteSpace=wrap;html=1;fillColor=#ffffff;strokeColor=#c5d0db;spacing=11;align=left;verticalAlign=top;" vertex="1" parent="1">
          <mxGeometry x="30" y="984.57" width="755" height="130" as="geometry" />
        </mxCell>
        <mxCell id="s5" value="&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:15px&quot;&gt;&lt;b&gt;SOLUTION&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:13px;line-height:1.35&quot;&gt;Maintain a single module that exports: a ReasonCode enum (e.g. POLICY_VIOLATION, RATE_LIMIT, UNVERIFIED_TOOL, RCE_RISK, LOOP_DETECTED, INTEGRITY_FAILURE, CONTEXT_INJECTION, ...); a format_refusal(code, detail) helper returning &#39;REFUSED: CODE: detail&#39;; a parse_refusal(string) helper that returns (code, detail) or None; and a KNOWN_CODES constant for consumers to validate against. Every guard surface in the system uses format_refusal exclusively. Legacy substrings (&#39;cannot comply&#39;, &#39;blocked by policy&#39;, etc.) are recognised by parse_refusal as code aliases so old logs keep parsing. Unknown codes return None from the parser rather than throwing. Downstream tooling depends only on the parser, never on raw strings.&lt;/div&gt;" style="rounded=1;whiteSpace=wrap;html=1;fillColor=#ffffff;strokeColor=#c5d0db;spacing=11;align=left;verticalAlign=top;" vertex="1" parent="1">
          <mxGeometry x="815" y="1003.57" width="755" height="187" as="geometry" />
        </mxCell>
        <mxCell id="s6" value="&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:15px&quot;&gt;&lt;b&gt;STRUCTURE&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:13px;line-height:1.35&quot;&gt;&lt;font face=&quot;monospace&quot;&gt;Each guard --format_refusal(code, detail)--&amp;gt; &amp;#x27;REFUSED: CODE: detail&amp;#x27; --&amp;gt; parse_refusal (also maps legacy aliases; unknown -&amp;gt; None) --&amp;gt; (code, detail) --&amp;gt; Audit log / dashboards / alerts&lt;/font&gt;&lt;/div&gt;" style="rounded=1;whiteSpace=wrap;html=1;fillColor=#ffffff;strokeColor=#c5d0db;spacing=11;align=left;verticalAlign=top;" vertex="1" parent="1">
          <mxGeometry x="30" y="1128.57" width="755" height="111" as="geometry" />
        </mxCell>
        <mxCell id="s7" value="&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:15px&quot;&gt;&lt;b&gt;EXAMPLE SCENARIO&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:13px;line-height:1.35&quot;&gt;An agent stack has five places that can emit a refusal: a tool-loop guard, a skill-scanner that refuses risky imports, a post-compaction integrity check, an RCE backstop, and a top-level input/output guardrail. Without centralisation, each emits its own string (&#39;I cannot help with that&#39;, &#39;blocked by policy&#39;, &#39;unsupported tool&#39;, etc.), and the dashboard parses these with brittle regex. After centralisation, every surface emits &#39;REFUSED: POLICY_VIOLATION: vendor block on this domain&#39; or &#39;REFUSED: LOOP_DETECTED: same tool called 7x in 12s&#39;. The dashboard groups by code, the on-call channel alerts on RCE_RISK and INTEGRITY_FAILURE, and the legacy substrings still parse because they are recognised as aliases.&lt;/div&gt;" style="rounded=1;whiteSpace=wrap;html=1;fillColor=#ffffff;strokeColor=#c5d0db;spacing=11;align=left;verticalAlign=top;" vertex="1" parent="1">
          <mxGeometry x="815" y="1204.57" width="755" height="206" as="geometry" />
        </mxCell>
        <mxCell id="s8" value="&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:15px&quot;&gt;&lt;b&gt;CONSEQUENCES&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:13px;line-height:1.35&quot;&gt;&lt;b&gt;Benefits&lt;/b&gt;&lt;br&gt;&lt;br&gt;&amp;bull; Refusal triage becomes mechanical: count by code, group by surface, alert by category.&lt;br&gt;&amp;bull; New guards inherit the audit story for free.&lt;br&gt;&amp;bull; Legacy substrings remain parseable, so existing dashboards keep working.&lt;br&gt;&lt;br&gt;&lt;b&gt;Liabilities&lt;/b&gt;&lt;br&gt;&lt;br&gt;&amp;bull; Centralisation is upfront work that pays back only after several guard surfaces exist.&lt;br&gt;&amp;bull; The enum becomes a contract; renaming a code is a breaking change for consumers.&lt;br&gt;&amp;bull; Detail strings remain human-authored; useful detail is still author-discipline-dependent.&lt;/div&gt;" style="rounded=1;whiteSpace=wrap;html=1;fillColor=#ffffff;strokeColor=#c5d0db;spacing=11;align=left;verticalAlign=top;" vertex="1" parent="1">
          <mxGeometry x="30" y="1253.57" width="755" height="263" as="geometry" />
        </mxCell>
        <mxCell id="s9" value="&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:15px&quot;&gt;&lt;b&gt;WHAT THIS PATTERN CONSTRAINS&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:13px;line-height:1.35&quot;&gt;No guard surface in the stack may emit a refusal string by hand; every refusal must flow through format_refusal so the code field is machine-readable and the detail string is the only free-form portion.&lt;/div&gt;" style="rounded=1;whiteSpace=wrap;html=1;fillColor=#ffffff;strokeColor=#c5d0db;spacing=11;align=left;verticalAlign=top;" vertex="1" parent="1">
          <mxGeometry x="815" y="1424.57" width="755" height="111" as="geometry" />
        </mxCell>
        <mxCell id="s10" value="&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:15px&quot;&gt;&lt;b&gt;USE WHEN&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:13px;line-height:1.35&quot;&gt;&amp;bull; The stack has three or more guard surfaces that each emit refusals.&lt;br&gt;&amp;bull; Downstream observability depends on counting or alerting on refusal categories.&lt;br&gt;&amp;bull; Legacy refusal phrasings already exist and must keep parsing.&lt;/div&gt;" style="rounded=1;whiteSpace=wrap;html=1;fillColor=#ffffff;strokeColor=#c5d0db;spacing=11;align=left;verticalAlign=top;" vertex="1" parent="1">
          <mxGeometry x="30" y="1530.57" width="755" height="111" as="geometry" />
        </mxCell>
        <mxCell id="s11" value="&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:15px&quot;&gt;&lt;b&gt;DO NOT USE WHEN&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:13px;line-height:1.35&quot;&gt;&amp;bull; The agent has exactly one refusal surface; centralisation is over-engineered.&lt;br&gt;&amp;bull; Refusals are not audited downstream and the enum would be pure ceremony.&lt;br&gt;&amp;bull; The team cannot enforce that all surfaces use the shared formatter.&lt;/div&gt;" style="rounded=1;whiteSpace=wrap;html=1;fillColor=#ffffff;strokeColor=#c5d0db;spacing=11;align=left;verticalAlign=top;" vertex="1" parent="1">
          <mxGeometry x="815" y="1549.57" width="755" height="111" as="geometry" />
        </mxCell>
        <mxCell id="s12" value="&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:15px&quot;&gt;&lt;b&gt;COMPONENTS&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:13px;line-height:1.35&quot;&gt;&amp;bull; ReasonCode enum — single source of truth for refusal categories across guards&lt;br&gt;&amp;bull; format_refusal helper — formatter that every guard surface uses to emit refusals&lt;br&gt;&amp;bull; parse_refusal helper — reverse parser returning (code, detail) or None&lt;br&gt;&amp;bull; Legacy-alias matcher — recogniser for old refusal substrings as code aliases&lt;br&gt;&amp;bull; Guard surfaces — tool-loop, skill-scanner, compaction, RCE, I/O guards that emit through the formatter&lt;/div&gt;" style="rounded=1;whiteSpace=wrap;html=1;fillColor=#ffffff;strokeColor=#c5d0db;spacing=11;align=left;verticalAlign=top;" vertex="1" parent="1">
          <mxGeometry x="30" y="1655.57" width="755" height="168" as="geometry" />
        </mxCell>
        <mxCell id="s13" value="&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:15px&quot;&gt;&lt;b&gt;TOOLS&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:13px;line-height:1.35&quot;&gt;&amp;bull; Shared module containing enum and helpers — imported by every guard surface in the stack&lt;br&gt;&amp;bull; Linter rule — forbids hand-rolled refusal strings outside the helper&lt;br&gt;&amp;bull; Audit dashboard — consumes parse_refusal output to count and group refusals by code&lt;/div&gt;" style="rounded=1;whiteSpace=wrap;html=1;fillColor=#ffffff;strokeColor=#c5d0db;spacing=11;align=left;verticalAlign=top;" vertex="1" parent="1">
          <mxGeometry x="815" y="1674.57" width="755" height="111" as="geometry" />
        </mxCell>
        <mxCell id="s14" value="&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:15px&quot;&gt;&lt;b&gt;FAILURE MODES&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:13px;line-height:1.35&quot;&gt;&amp;bull; Bypassed helper — one guard formats its refusal by hand, and its events are missing from every dashboard that counts by code.&lt;br&gt;&amp;bull; Breaking rename — a code is renamed in the enum, and downstream alerts keyed on the old name stop firing.&lt;br&gt;&amp;bull; Empty detail — the code is correct but the detail string says nothing useful, so triage still needs the raw logs.&lt;/div&gt;" style="rounded=1;whiteSpace=wrap;html=1;fillColor=#ffffff;strokeColor=#c5d0db;spacing=11;align=left;verticalAlign=top;" vertex="1" parent="1">
          <mxGeometry x="30" y="1837.57" width="755" height="168" as="geometry" />
        </mxCell>
        <mxCell id="s15" value="&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:15px&quot;&gt;&lt;b&gt;EVALUATION METRICS&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:13px;line-height:1.35&quot;&gt;&amp;bull; Refusal-code coverage — share of refusal events that parsed cleanly into a known code&lt;br&gt;&amp;bull; Hand-rolled-refusal occurrence rate — guards still emitting strings without the helper&lt;br&gt;&amp;bull; Legacy-alias parse hits — old phrasings preserved by the alias matcher over time&lt;br&gt;&amp;bull; Enum-churn rate — frequency of code renames and the breakage they caused downstream&lt;/div&gt;" style="rounded=1;whiteSpace=wrap;html=1;fillColor=#ffffff;strokeColor=#c5d0db;spacing=11;align=left;verticalAlign=top;" vertex="1" parent="1">
          <mxGeometry x="815" y="1799.57" width="755" height="130" as="geometry" />
        </mxCell>
        <mxCell id="s16" value="&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:15px&quot;&gt;&lt;b&gt;KNOWN USES&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:13px;line-height:1.35&quot;&gt;&amp;bull; &lt;b&gt;Author&#39;s long-running personal agent (single private deployment)&lt;/b&gt; &lt;i&gt;available&lt;/i&gt; — Single-source evidence: one private deployment by the catalog author; no independently documented use yet.&lt;/div&gt;" style="rounded=1;whiteSpace=wrap;html=1;fillColor=#ffffff;strokeColor=#c5d0db;spacing=11;align=left;verticalAlign=top;" vertex="1" parent="1">
          <mxGeometry x="30" y="2019.57" width="755" height="92" as="geometry" />
        </mxCell>
        <mxCell id="s17" value="&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:15px&quot;&gt;&lt;b&gt;RELATED PATTERNS&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:13px;line-height:1.35&quot;&gt;&amp;bull; &lt;i&gt;complements&lt;/i&gt; &lt;b&gt;Refusal&lt;/b&gt; — Refusal is the policy decision; typed-refusal-codes is the format the decision takes on the wire.&lt;br&gt;&amp;bull; &lt;i&gt;complements&lt;/i&gt; &lt;b&gt;Input/Output Guardrails&lt;/b&gt;&lt;br&gt;&amp;bull; &lt;i&gt;complements&lt;/i&gt; &lt;b&gt;Policy-as-Code Gate&lt;/b&gt;&lt;br&gt;&amp;bull; &lt;i&gt;complements&lt;/i&gt; &lt;b&gt;Decision Log&lt;/b&gt; — Typed codes are how refusals enter the decision log without grep fragility.&lt;br&gt;&amp;bull; &lt;i&gt;complements&lt;/i&gt; &lt;b&gt;Stochastic-Deterministic Boundary (SDB)&lt;/b&gt;&lt;br&gt;&amp;bull; &lt;i&gt;complements&lt;/i&gt; &lt;b&gt;Supervisor-Plus-Gate&lt;/b&gt;&lt;br&gt;&amp;bull; &lt;i&gt;complements&lt;/i&gt; &lt;b&gt;Reflexive Metacognitive Agent&lt;/b&gt;&lt;br&gt;&amp;bull; &lt;i&gt;complements&lt;/i&gt; &lt;b&gt;Production Failure Triage Loop&lt;/b&gt; — Machine-readable categories are what make mechanical triage possible; typed codes feed the classifier directly instead of string-grepping human-readable messages.&lt;br&gt;&amp;bull; &lt;i&gt;complements&lt;/i&gt; &lt;b&gt;Machine-Checkable Tool Contract&lt;/b&gt; — The same machine-readable discipline applied to guard-surface refusals rather than to tool failures.&lt;br&gt;&amp;bull; &lt;i&gt;complements&lt;/i&gt; &lt;b&gt;Action-Admissibility Tiering&lt;/b&gt; — Gives the block a machine-readable reason so the failing tier and the failing check reach the caller instead of an unexplained rejection.&lt;br&gt;&amp;bull; &lt;i&gt;complements&lt;/i&gt; &lt;b&gt;Feasibility-Negotiated Intent Contract&lt;/b&gt; — Supplies the machine-readable vocabulary a refusal needs; this pattern says what the refusal must carry, namely the binding constraint that made the stated goal unreachable.&lt;/div&gt;" style="rounded=1;whiteSpace=wrap;html=1;fillColor=#ffffff;strokeColor=#c5d0db;spacing=11;align=left;verticalAlign=top;" vertex="1" parent="1">
          <mxGeometry x="815" y="1943.57" width="755" height="415" as="geometry" />
        </mxCell>
        <mxCell id="s18" value="&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:15px&quot;&gt;&lt;b&gt;REFERENCES&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:13px;line-height:1.35&quot;&gt;&amp;bull; &lt;a href=&quot;https://platform.openai.com/docs/guides/moderation&quot;&gt;OpenAI Moderation API — typed category outputs&lt;/a&gt;&lt;br&gt;&amp;bull; &lt;a href=&quot;https://datatracker.ietf.org/doc/html/rfc9110&quot;&gt;HTTP Semantics (RFC 9110) — status codes as typed reasons&lt;/a&gt; — IETF, 2022&lt;/div&gt;" style="rounded=1;whiteSpace=wrap;html=1;fillColor=#ffffff;strokeColor=#c5d0db;spacing=11;align=left;verticalAlign=top;" vertex="1" parent="1">
          <mxGeometry x="30" y="2125.57" width="755" height="111" as="geometry" />
        </mxCell>
      </root>
    </mxGraphModel>
  </diagram>
  <diagram id="typed-tool-loop-detector" name="Typed Tool-Loop Failure Detector">
    <mxGraphModel dx="1600" dy="4666.6" grid="1" gridSize="10" guides="1" tooltips="1" connect="1" arrows="1" fold="1" page="1" pageScale="1" pageWidth="1600" pageHeight="4666.6" math="0" shadow="0">
      <root>
        <mxCell id="0" />
        <mxCell id="1" parent="0" />
        <mxCell id="title" value="&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:24px&quot;&gt;&lt;b&gt;Typed Tool-Loop Failure Detector&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:12px;color:#52606d&quot;&gt;Cognition &amp;amp; Introspection &amp;nbsp; | &amp;nbsp; emerging &amp;nbsp; | &amp;nbsp; Also known as: Dispatch-Boundary Veto, Five-Mode Loop Guard, Tool-Call Pattern Detector&lt;/div&gt;" style="text;html=1;align=left;verticalAlign=middle;whiteSpace=wrap;strokeColor=none;fillColor=none;" vertex="1" parent="1">
          <mxGeometry x="30" y="20" width="1540" height="58" as="geometry" />
        </mxCell>
        <mxCell id="arch-panel" value="" style="rounded=1;whiteSpace=wrap;html=1;fillColor=#f5f8fc;strokeColor=#b6c6d8;strokeWidth=1;arcSize=12;" vertex="1" parent="1">
          <mxGeometry x="30" y="95" width="1540" height="2862.6" as="geometry" />
        </mxCell>
        <mxCell id="arch-heading" value="&lt;b&gt;ARCHITECTURE&lt;/b&gt; &amp;nbsp; &lt;span style=&quot;color:#52606d&quot;&gt;Editable diagram imported from the pattern source&lt;/span&gt;" style="text;html=1;align=left;verticalAlign=middle;strokeColor=none;fillColor=none;fontSize=14;" vertex="1" parent="1">
          <mxGeometry x="48" y="109" width="1504" height="28" as="geometry" />
        </mxCell>
