        <UserObject label="" mermaidId="e:Refuse-&gt;Obs#0" mermaidBaseStyle="curved=1;startArrow=none;endArrow=block;endSize=7;strokeColor=light-dark(#333333,#cccccc);exitX=0.5;exitY=1;entryX=0.5;entryY=0;" mermaidBaseValue="" id="m22">
          <mxCell edge="1" parent="1" source="m3" style="curved=1;startArrow=none;endArrow=block;endSize=7;strokeColor=light-dark(#333333,#cccccc);exitX=0.5;exitY=1;entryX=0.5;entryY=0;" target="m9">
            <mxGeometry relative="1" as="geometry">
              <Array as="points" />
            </mxGeometry>
          </mxCell>
        </UserObject>
        <mxCell id="arch-caption" value="&lt;i&gt;Five typed modes run at the dispatch boundary; a trip returns a formatted refusal so the model adjusts in its next turn.&lt;/i&gt;" style="text;html=1;align=center;verticalAlign=middle;strokeColor=none;fillColor=none;fontSize=12;fontColor=#52606d;" vertex="1" parent="1">
          <mxGeometry x="65" y="2907.6" width="1470" height="32" as="geometry" />
        </mxCell>
        <mxCell id="s0" value="&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:15px&quot;&gt;&lt;b&gt;INTENT&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:13px;line-height:1.35&quot;&gt;Lift tool-loop detection from prompt-level rules to a mechanical dispatch-boundary veto with typed failure modes and per-tool caps that returns a formatted refusal the model must consume.&lt;/div&gt;" style="rounded=1;whiteSpace=wrap;html=1;fillColor=#ffffff;strokeColor=#c5d0db;spacing=11;align=left;verticalAlign=top;" vertex="1" parent="1">
          <mxGeometry x="30" y="2982.6" width="755" height="92" as="geometry" />
        </mxCell>
        <mxCell id="s1" value="&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:15px&quot;&gt;&lt;b&gt;CONTEXT&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:13px;line-height:1.35&quot;&gt;A team is running an agent with a rich tool palette in which loop bugs — the agent calling the same tool over and over, or cycling through a small subset of tools without progress — can eat substantial budget before any safety net trips. Prompt-level instructions telling the model &#39;do not call X more than three times&#39; are not actually enforced: the model can simply ignore them. A single global circuit-breaker on total tool calls catches the most extreme cases but hides the specific shape of the failure when it does fire.&lt;/div&gt;" style="rounded=1;whiteSpace=wrap;html=1;fillColor=#ffffff;strokeColor=#c5d0db;spacing=11;align=left;verticalAlign=top;" vertex="1" parent="1">
          <mxGeometry x="815" y="2982.6" width="755" height="168" as="geometry" />
        </mxCell>
        <mxCell id="s2" value="&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:15px&quot;&gt;&lt;b&gt;PROBLEM&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:13px;line-height:1.35&quot;&gt;Tool-explosion is named elsewhere in the catalogue as an anti-pattern, but naming it provides no mechanism to catch it. A single global circuit-breaker misses the shape of the underlying failure: a thirty-call canvas-action burst looks identical to thirty healthy file reads under a flat global counter, so the breaker either trips too often on legitimate bursts or too late on real failures. Prompt-level rules are advisory only, so the model can ignore them when it is most stuck. The team needs detection lifted from the prompt to a mechanical check at the dispatch boundary, with typed failure modes and per-tool caps that emit a refusal the model is forced to consume rather than silently retry.&lt;/div&gt;" style="rounded=1;whiteSpace=wrap;html=1;fillColor=#ffffff;strokeColor=#c5d0db;spacing=11;align=left;verticalAlign=top;" vertex="1" parent="1">
          <mxGeometry x="30" y="3088.6" width="755" height="187" as="geometry" />
        </mxCell>
        <mxCell id="s3" value="&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:15px&quot;&gt;&lt;b&gt;FORCES&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:13px;line-height:1.35&quot;&gt;&amp;bull; Per-tool caps are noisy without good defaults.&lt;br&gt;&amp;bull; A typed refusal must be formatted so the model can consume it as input rather than silently retry.&lt;br&gt;&amp;bull; Global breaker is the backstop but should be the last to fire.&lt;br&gt;&amp;bull; Detection windows must be tunable; too short trips legit work, too long drains money before tripping.&lt;/div&gt;" style="rounded=1;whiteSpace=wrap;html=1;fillColor=#ffffff;strokeColor=#c5d0db;spacing=11;align=left;verticalAlign=top;" vertex="1" parent="1">
          <mxGeometry x="815" y="3164.6" width="755" height="149" as="geometry" />
        </mxCell>
        <mxCell id="s4" value="&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:15px&quot;&gt;&lt;b&gt;THEREFORE&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:13px;line-height:1.35&quot;&gt;Therefore: at the dispatch boundary classify every tool call against a small set of typed failure modes (generic repeat, unknown-tool repeat, no-progress poll, ping-pong, global breaker) with per-tool caps and return a formatted refusal when a mode trips, so the next observation forces the model to react instead of silently looping.&lt;/div&gt;" style="rounded=1;whiteSpace=wrap;html=1;fillColor=#ffffff;strokeColor=#c5d0db;spacing=11;align=left;verticalAlign=top;" vertex="1" parent="1">
          <mxGeometry x="30" y="3289.6" width="755" height="130" as="geometry" />
        </mxCell>
        <mxCell id="s5" value="&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:15px&quot;&gt;&lt;b&gt;SOLUTION&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:13px;line-height:1.35&quot;&gt;A dispatcher pre-check function. On each tool call, append &lt;font face=&quot;monospace&quot;&gt;(timestamp, tool_name, hash(args))&lt;/font&gt; to a bounded rolling window. Evaluate five rules: (1) generic-repeat: same &lt;font face=&quot;monospace&quot;&gt;(tool, arg-hash)&lt;/font&gt; at least N times in window; (2) unknown-tool-repeat: call to unregistered tool at least M times; (3) poll-no-progress: same tool with no state change at least K times; (4) ping-pong: alternating between two tools at least J cycles; (5) global-circuit-breaker: total tool calls in window at least G. Each rule has per-tool overrides (for example a known-bursty tool capped lower than the default). On trip, the dispatcher returns &lt;font face=&quot;monospace&quot;&gt;{error: &#39;tool_loop_detected&#39;, mode: &amp;lt;id&amp;gt;, observed: &amp;lt;stats&amp;gt;}&lt;/font&gt; as the tool result. The model sees this in its next turn and must adjust.&lt;/div&gt;" style="rounded=1;whiteSpace=wrap;html=1;fillColor=#ffffff;strokeColor=#c5d0db;spacing=11;align=left;verticalAlign=top;" vertex="1" parent="1">
          <mxGeometry x="815" y="3327.6" width="755" height="206" as="geometry" />
        </mxCell>
        <mxCell id="s6" value="&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:15px&quot;&gt;&lt;b&gt;STRUCTURE&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:13px;line-height:1.35&quot;&gt;&lt;font face=&quot;monospace&quot;&gt;Tool call --&amp;gt; append (timestamp, tool, arg-hash) to rolling window --&amp;gt; rules with per-tool caps (generic-repeat, unknown-tool-repeat, poll-no-progress, ping-pong, global breaker) --any trip--&amp;gt; {error: &amp;#x27;tool_loop_detected&amp;#x27;, mode, observed} as tool result --&amp;gt; next model turn | --no trip--&amp;gt; dispatch normally&lt;/font&gt;&lt;/div&gt;" style="rounded=1;whiteSpace=wrap;html=1;fillColor=#ffffff;strokeColor=#c5d0db;spacing=11;align=left;verticalAlign=top;" vertex="1" parent="1">
          <mxGeometry x="30" y="3433.6" width="755" height="130" as="geometry" />
        </mxCell>
        <mxCell id="s7" value="&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:15px&quot;&gt;&lt;b&gt;EXAMPLE SCENARIO&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:13px;line-height:1.35&quot;&gt;A long-running personal agent has a canvas-action tool that occasionally enters a thirty-call burst when an interaction goes wrong. The global step-budget catches it eventually but only after thousands of tokens. The team adds a Typed Tool-Loop Failure Detector with per-tool caps: canvas-action is capped at four calls in a sixty-second window. When the burst starts, the fifth call returns a typed refusal &lt;font face=&quot;monospace&quot;&gt;{mode: &#39;generic_repeat&#39;, observed: {...}}&lt;/font&gt;. The model sees the refusal in its next observation and shifts to a different approach instead of pounding the same tool.&lt;/div&gt;" style="rounded=1;whiteSpace=wrap;html=1;fillColor=#ffffff;strokeColor=#c5d0db;spacing=11;align=left;verticalAlign=top;" vertex="1" parent="1">
          <mxGeometry x="815" y="3547.6" width="755" height="168" as="geometry" />
        </mxCell>
        <mxCell id="s8" value="&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:15px&quot;&gt;&lt;b&gt;CONSEQUENCES&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:13px;line-height:1.35&quot;&gt;&lt;b&gt;Benefits&lt;/b&gt;&lt;br&gt;&lt;br&gt;&amp;bull; Loop failures are caught at the dispatch boundary, not in prompt-text-the-model-may-ignore.&lt;br&gt;&amp;bull; Typed modes make triage and per-tool tuning meaningful.&lt;br&gt;&amp;bull; Formatted refusal as a tool result keeps the model in-loop rather than crashing.&lt;br&gt;&lt;br&gt;&lt;b&gt;Liabilities&lt;/b&gt;&lt;br&gt;&lt;br&gt;&amp;bull; Per-tool caps must be calibrated or legit work trips.&lt;br&gt;&amp;bull; Five modes is more state to maintain than a single breaker.&lt;br&gt;&amp;bull; A determined model can still loop on tools that the cap missed.&lt;/div&gt;" style="rounded=1;whiteSpace=wrap;html=1;fillColor=#ffffff;strokeColor=#c5d0db;spacing=11;align=left;verticalAlign=top;" vertex="1" parent="1">
          <mxGeometry x="30" y="3577.6" width="755" height="263" as="geometry" />
        </mxCell>
        <mxCell id="s9" value="&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:15px&quot;&gt;&lt;b&gt;WHAT THIS PATTERN CONSTRAINS&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:13px;line-height:1.35&quot;&gt;No tool call may bypass the dispatch-boundary loop check; a tripped detector blocks that specific call and returns a typed refusal that becomes the next observation, and the per-tool cap cannot be raised mid-session by the model.&lt;/div&gt;" style="rounded=1;whiteSpace=wrap;html=1;fillColor=#ffffff;strokeColor=#c5d0db;spacing=11;align=left;verticalAlign=top;" vertex="1" parent="1">
          <mxGeometry x="815" y="3729.6" width="755" height="111" as="geometry" />
        </mxCell>
        <mxCell id="s10" value="&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:15px&quot;&gt;&lt;b&gt;USE WHEN&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:13px;line-height:1.35&quot;&gt;&amp;bull; Tool palette is rich enough that prompt-level rules are not reliably followed.&lt;br&gt;&amp;bull; Loop bugs are observable in telemetry and have wasted budget historically.&lt;br&gt;&amp;bull; Per-tool calibration is feasible (known-bursty tools have caps tuned individually).&lt;/div&gt;" style="rounded=1;whiteSpace=wrap;html=1;fillColor=#ffffff;strokeColor=#c5d0db;spacing=11;align=left;verticalAlign=top;" vertex="1" parent="1">
          <mxGeometry x="30" y="3854.6" width="755" height="111" as="geometry" />
        </mxCell>
        <mxCell id="s11" value="&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:15px&quot;&gt;&lt;b&gt;DO NOT USE WHEN&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:13px;line-height:1.35&quot;&gt;&amp;bull; Tool palette is tiny and prompt-level rules suffice.&lt;br&gt;&amp;bull; Per-tool caps cannot be calibrated without churning legit workflows.&lt;br&gt;&amp;bull; A single global circuit-breaker already catches all observed failure shapes.&lt;/div&gt;" style="rounded=1;whiteSpace=wrap;html=1;fillColor=#ffffff;strokeColor=#c5d0db;spacing=11;align=left;verticalAlign=top;" vertex="1" parent="1">
          <mxGeometry x="815" y="3854.6" width="755" height="111" as="geometry" />
        </mxCell>
        <mxCell id="s12" value="&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:15px&quot;&gt;&lt;b&gt;COMPONENTS&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:13px;line-height:1.35&quot;&gt;&amp;bull; Dispatcher Pre-Check — runs on every tool call before dispatch&lt;br&gt;&amp;bull; Rolling Call Window — bounded list of timestamp, tool, arg-hash tuples&lt;br&gt;&amp;bull; Five Typed Rules — generic-repeat, unknown-tool-repeat, poll-no-progress, ping-pong, global breaker&lt;br&gt;&amp;bull; Per-Tool Cap Overrides — tighter limits on known-bursty tools&lt;br&gt;&amp;bull; Typed Refusal — formatted tool-result the model consumes as the next observation&lt;/div&gt;" style="rounded=1;whiteSpace=wrap;html=1;fillColor=#ffffff;strokeColor=#c5d0db;spacing=11;align=left;verticalAlign=top;" vertex="1" parent="1">
          <mxGeometry x="30" y="3979.6" width="755" height="168" as="geometry" />
        </mxCell>
        <mxCell id="s13" value="&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:15px&quot;&gt;&lt;b&gt;TOOLS&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:13px;line-height:1.35&quot;&gt;&amp;bull; Tool-dispatcher hook — interception point at the dispatch boundary&lt;br&gt;&amp;bull; Structured JSON store — rolling call window and per-tool cap config&lt;/div&gt;" style="rounded=1;whiteSpace=wrap;html=1;fillColor=#ffffff;strokeColor=#c5d0db;spacing=11;align=left;verticalAlign=top;" vertex="1" parent="1">
          <mxGeometry x="815" y="3979.6" width="755" height="92" as="geometry" />
        </mxCell>
        <mxCell id="s14" value="&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:15px&quot;&gt;&lt;b&gt;FAILURE MODES&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:13px;line-height:1.35&quot;&gt;&amp;bull; Blocked legitimate burst — per-tool caps are set too tight, and a real batch of file reads trips the generic-repeat rule.&lt;br&gt;&amp;bull; Missed loop — a loop varies its arguments slightly each call, so the arg-hash differs and generic-repeat never fires.&lt;br&gt;&amp;bull; Ignored refusal — the model reads the typed refusal and retries a sibling tool with the same intent.&lt;br&gt;&amp;bull; Bypass path — a tool route skips the dispatcher pre-check, and the loop runs unchecked there.&lt;/div&gt;" style="rounded=1;whiteSpace=wrap;html=1;fillColor=#ffffff;strokeColor=#c5d0db;spacing=11;align=left;verticalAlign=top;" vertex="1" parent="1">
          <mxGeometry x="30" y="4161.6" width="755" height="187" as="geometry" />
        </mxCell>
        <mxCell id="s15" value="&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:15px&quot;&gt;&lt;b&gt;EVALUATION METRICS&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:13px;line-height:1.35&quot;&gt;&amp;bull; Loop-detection hit rate — trips per 1000 tool calls, broken down by mode&lt;br&gt;&amp;bull; False-trip rate — trips on calls a retrospective audit considered legitimate&lt;br&gt;&amp;bull; Per-mode distribution — share of trips by each of the five rules, surfacing tuning gaps&lt;br&gt;&amp;bull; Post-refusal recovery rate — share of refusals followed by a productive shift rather than a retry&lt;/div&gt;" style="rounded=1;whiteSpace=wrap;html=1;fillColor=#ffffff;strokeColor=#c5d0db;spacing=11;align=left;verticalAlign=top;" vertex="1" parent="1">
          <mxGeometry x="815" y="4085.6" width="755" height="130" as="geometry" />
        </mxCell>
        <mxCell id="s16" value="&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:15px&quot;&gt;&lt;b&gt;KNOWN USES&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:13px;line-height:1.35&quot;&gt;&amp;bull; &lt;b&gt;&lt;a href=&quot;https://marco-nissen.com/sparrot/&quot;&gt;Sparrot (author&amp;#x27;s long-running personal agent; single private deployment)&lt;/a&gt;&lt;/b&gt; &lt;i&gt;available&lt;/i&gt; — Tool-loop detection is mechanical at the dispatch boundary with five typed failure modes (repeat, unknown, poll, ping-pong, circuit-breaker) and per-tool caps, returning a structured refusal the model must consume rather than a prompt-level reminder. Single-source evidence: one private deployment by the catalog author.&lt;/div&gt;" style="rounded=1;whiteSpace=wrap;html=1;fillColor=#ffffff;strokeColor=#c5d0db;spacing=11;align=left;verticalAlign=top;" vertex="1" parent="1">
          <mxGeometry x="30" y="4362.6" width="755" height="149" as="geometry" />
        </mxCell>
        <mxCell id="s17" value="&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:15px&quot;&gt;&lt;b&gt;RELATED PATTERNS&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:13px;line-height:1.35&quot;&gt;&amp;bull; &lt;i&gt;specialises&lt;/i&gt; &lt;b&gt;Circuit Breaker&lt;/b&gt;&lt;br&gt;&amp;bull; &lt;i&gt;complements&lt;/i&gt; &lt;b&gt;Step Budget&lt;/b&gt;&lt;br&gt;&amp;bull; &lt;i&gt;complements&lt;/i&gt; &lt;b&gt;Pre-Generative Loop Gate&lt;/b&gt;&lt;br&gt;&amp;bull; &lt;i&gt;alternative-to&lt;/i&gt; &lt;b&gt;Trajectory Anomaly Monitor&lt;/b&gt; — Loop detection catches repetition by shape; the trajectory monitor catches task-misalignment across the sequence.&lt;/div&gt;" style="rounded=1;whiteSpace=wrap;html=1;fillColor=#ffffff;strokeColor=#c5d0db;spacing=11;align=left;verticalAlign=top;" vertex="1" parent="1">
          <mxGeometry x="815" y="4229.6" width="755" height="149" as="geometry" />
        </mxCell>
        <mxCell id="s18" value="&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:15px&quot;&gt;&lt;b&gt;REFERENCES&lt;/b&gt;&lt;/div&gt;&lt;div style=&quot;font-size:13px;line-height:1.35&quot;&gt;&amp;bull; &lt;a href=&quot;https://pragprog.com/titles/mnee2/release-it-second-edition/&quot;&gt;Release It! Design and Deploy Production-Ready Software (circuit breaker chapter)&lt;/a&gt; — Michael T. Nygard, 2018&lt;/div&gt;" style="rounded=1;whiteSpace=wrap;html=1;fillColor=#ffffff;strokeColor=#c5d0db;spacing=11;align=left;verticalAlign=top;" vertex="1" parent="1">
          <mxGeometry x="30" y="4525.6" width="755" height="92" as="geometry" />
        </mxCell>
      </root>
    </mxGraphModel>
  </diagram>
